window.SpecialistATAR = [{"id":"mas11-logic","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Geometry","title":"Statements, quantifiers and logical equivalence","outcomes":["1.1.1","1.1.3","1.1.4"],"icon":"📐","scope":"A statement has a truth value · Quantifiers determine what must be shown · Implication is a conditional relationship · Equivalence requires both directions","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Statements, quantifiers and logical equivalence</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>A statement has a truth value</li><li>Quantifiers determine what must be shown</li><li>Implication is a conditional relationship</li><li>Equivalence requires both directions</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"A statement has a truth value","text":"A mathematical statement is an assertion that is true or false within its specified setting. An open sentence such as $x^2=4$ becomes a statement only when $x$ is specified or quantified. Specify the domain: an assertion about all real numbers can differ from the same assertion about integers. Definitions establish meaning, axioms or accepted results supply starting facts, and a proof connects those facts logically. A numerical calculation illustrating one case is useful evidence for that case, but does not establish an unrestricted universal claim.","diagram":null,"html":"A mathematical statement is an assertion that is true or false within its specified setting. An open sentence such as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2=4</annotation></semantics></math></span> becomes a statement only when <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span> is specified or quantified. Specify the domain: an assertion about all real numbers can differ from the same assertion about integers. Definitions establish meaning, axioms or accepted results supply starting facts, and a proof connects those facts logically. A numerical calculation illustrating one case is useful evidence for that case, but does not establish an unrestricted universal claim."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Quantifiers determine what must be shown","text":"The statement $\\forall x\\in D,\\ P(x)$ asserts the property for every member of the domain, whereas $\\exists x\\in D,\\ P(x)$ needs at least one suitable member. A universal claim is disproved by one counterexample; an existential claim is proved by one valid witness. To disprove an existential assertion, show that no member works. Negation reverses the quantifier and negates the property. Thus the negation of every student passed is at least one student did not pass, not every student failed.","diagram":"logic","html":"The statement <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall x\\in D,\\ P(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span> asserts the property for every member of the domain, whereas <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists x\\in D,\\ P(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span> needs at least one suitable member. A universal claim is disproved by one counterexample; an existential claim is proved by one valid witness. To disprove an existential assertion, show that no member works. Negation reverses the quantifier and negates the property. Thus the negation of every student passed is at least one student did not pass, not every student failed."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Implication is a conditional relationship","text":"$P\\Rightarrow Q$ says that whenever $P$ holds, $Q$ follows. It does not assert that $P$ actually occurs or that $Q$ cannot occur for another reason. An implication is false precisely when $P$ is true and $Q$ false. For example, a square is a rectangle does not imply every rectangle is a square. Mathematical use of if must be interpreted through the conditional structure rather than a guess about everyday causal wording. Keep each assumption attached to the conclusion it supports.","diagram":null,"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\Rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> says that whenever <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math></span> holds, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> follows. It does not assert that <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math></span> actually occurs or that <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> cannot occur for another reason. An implication is false precisely when <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math></span> is true and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> false. For example, a square is a rectangle does not imply every rectangle is a square. Mathematical use of if must be interpreted through the conditional structure rather than a guess about everyday causal wording. Keep each assumption attached to the conclusion it supports."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Equivalence requires both directions","text":"$P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ asserts $P\\Rightarrow Q$ and $Q\\Rightarrow P$. A proof of only one direction cannot establish if and only if. Equivalence is useful when solving equations, but an operation such as squaring can introduce extra possibilities if the reverse direction is not justified. Record domains and side conditions before replacing one statement by another. A clear argument names the direction being proved, states an arbitrary relevant object where necessary and ends with the exact quantified conclusion requested.","diagram":null,"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⇔</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\Leftrightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> asserts <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\Rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\Rightarrow P</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A proof of only one direction cannot establish if and only if. Equivalence is useful when solving equations, but an operation such as squaring can introduce extra possibilities if the reverse direction is not justified. Record domains and side conditions before replacing one statement by another. A clear argument names the direction being proved, states an arbitrary relevant object where necessary and ends with the exact quantified conclusion requested."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-logic-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Negate $\\forall x\\in\\mathbb R,\\ x^2>0$ and determine whether the original assertion is true. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Change the universal quantifier to an existential one.","[1] Negate the strict inequality: $\\exists x\\in\\mathbb R,\\ x^2\\le0$.","[1] Choose the valid real counterexample $x=0$.","[1] Since $0^2=0$, the original universal assertion is false."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Negate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall x\\in\\mathbb R,\\ x^2&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and determine whether the original assertion is true. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Change the universal quantifier to an existential one.","[1] Negate the strict inequality: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists x\\in\\mathbb R,\\ x^2\\le0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Choose the valid real counterexample <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Since <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0^2=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the original universal assertion is false."]},{"id":"mas11-logic-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"For real $x$, decide whether $x=2\\Rightarrow x^2=4$ and whether the reverse implication holds. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] If $x=2$, substitution gives $x^2=4$, proving the forward implication.","[1] To test the reverse, solve $x^2=4$ as $x=2$ or $x=-2$.","[1] The value $x=-2$ satisfies the antecedent of the reverse but not $x=2$.","[1] Therefore the forward statement is true and the reverse is false; the statements are not equivalent over all reals."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For real <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, decide whether <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2\\Rightarrow x^2=4</annotation></semantics></math></span> and whether the reverse implication holds. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] If <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, substitution gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2=4</annotation></semantics></math></span>, proving the forward implication.","[1] To test the reverse, solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2=4</annotation></semantics></math></span> as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The value <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-2</annotation></semantics></math></span> satisfies the antecedent of the reverse but not <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Therefore the forward statement is true and the reverse is false; the statements are not equivalent over all reals."]},{"id":"mas11-logic-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Negate $\\exists n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ n^2=2$. Explain which kind of argument would prove the negation. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Replace exists by for all.","[1] Negate equality: $\\forall n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ n^2\\ne2$.","[1] The negation needs an argument ruling out every integer, not merely checking several.","[1] For example, $|n|\\le1$ gives $n^2\\le1$ while $|n|\\ge2$ gives $n^2\\ge4$, so no integer works."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Negate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ n^2=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Explain which kind of argument would prove the negation. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Replace exists by for all.","[1] Negate equality: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ n^2\\ne2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The negation needs an argument ruling out every integer, not merely checking several.","[1] For example, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|n|\\le1</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2\\le1</annotation></semantics></math></span> while <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|n|\\ge2</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2\\ge4</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so no integer works."]},{"id":"mas11-logic-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Prove $\\exists x\\in\\mathbb R$ such that $x^2-5x+6=0$ and explain why this is not a proof that every real number satisfies the equation. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] A witness is sufficient for the existential claim.","[1] Choose $x=2$ and calculate $4-10+6=0$.","[1] Thus at least one valid real solution exists.","[1] A universal claim would require every real value; $x=0$ gives 6 and disproves that different assertion."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists x\\in\\mathbb R</annotation></semantics></math></span> such that <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2-5x+6=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and explain why this is not a proof that every real number satisfies the equation. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] A witness is sufficient for the existential claim.","[1] Choose <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and calculate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-10+6=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus at least one valid real solution exists.","[1] A universal claim would require every real value; <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives 6 and disproves that different assertion."]},{"id":"mas11-logic-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"For real $x$, prove $x>3\\Rightarrow x^2>9$. Is the converse true? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] From $x>3$, both $x-3$ and $x+3$ are positive.","[1] Hence $x^2-9=(x-3)(x+3)>0$, establishing the implication.","[1] The converse would assert $x^2>9\\Rightarrow x>3$.","[1] $x=-4$ gives 16 greater than 9 but is not greater than 3, so the converse is false."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For real <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;3\\Rightarrow x^2&gt;9</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Is the converse true? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] From <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, both <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-3</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+3</annotation></semantics></math></span> are positive.","[1] Hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2-9=(x-3)(x+3)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, establishing the implication.","[1] The converse would assert <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2&gt;9\\Rightarrow x&gt;3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-4</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives 16 greater than 9 but is not greater than 3, so the converse is false."]},{"id":"mas11-logic-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Assess the claim $x^2=9\\Leftrightarrow x=3$ first on $\\mathbb R$, then on the domain $x\\ge0$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Over the reals, $x^2=9$ gives $x=\\pm3$.","[1] $x=-3$ disproves the proposed forward direction to $x=3$.","[1] On $x\\ge0$, only $x=3$ remains and $x=3$ also gives $x^2=9$.","[1] Both directions hold on the restricted domain, so the equivalence is valid there."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Assess the claim <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>⇔</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2=9\\Leftrightarrow x=3</annotation></semantics></math></span> first on <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb R</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then on the domain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Over the reals, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2=9</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pm3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-3</annotation></semantics></math></span> disproves the proposed forward direction to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] On <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, only <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3</annotation></semantics></math></span> remains and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3</annotation></semantics></math></span> also gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2=9</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both directions hold on the restricted domain, so the equivalence is valid there."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What disproves a universal assertion on its stated domain?","o":["One valid counterexample.","One supporting example.","A witness proving a different existential claim.","Changing the domain without stating it."],"a":0,"why":"A universal claim must hold for every domain member; a single failure is decisive.","qHtml":"What disproves a universal assertion on its stated domain?","oHtml":["One valid counterexample.","One supporting example.","A witness proving a different existential claim.","Changing the domain without stating it."],"whyHtml":"A universal claim must hold for every domain member; a single failure is decisive."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-counterexamples","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Geometry","title":"Converse, inverse, contrapositive and counterexamples","outcomes":["1.1.1","1.1.5"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Name the four related statements · Use contrapositive when it simplifies the mathematics · Find a counterexample that satisfies the hypothesis · Negate conjunctions and inequalities carefully","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Converse, inverse, contrapositive and counterexamples</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Name the four related statements</li><li>Use contrapositive when it simplifies the mathematics</li><li>Find a counterexample that satisfies the hypothesis</li><li>Negate conjunctions and inequalities carefully</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Name the four related statements","text":"For $P\\Rightarrow Q$, the converse is $Q\\Rightarrow P$, the inverse is $\\neg P\\Rightarrow\\neg Q$ and the contrapositive is $\\neg Q\\Rightarrow\\neg P$. The original and contrapositive are logically equivalent. The converse and inverse are equivalent to each other, but need not be equivalent to the original. Write these symbolically before translating a complicated verbal claim; reversing and negating are separate operations and easy to confuse.","diagram":null,"html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\Rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the converse is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\Rightarrow P</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the inverse is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg P\\Rightarrow\\neg Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the contrapositive is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg Q\\Rightarrow\\neg P</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The original and contrapositive are logically equivalent. The converse and inverse are equivalent to each other, but need not be equivalent to the original. Write these symbolically before translating a complicated verbal claim; reversing and negating are separate operations and easy to confuse."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use contrapositive when it simplifies the mathematics","text":"To prove if an integer has an odd square then the integer is odd, it is convenient to prove that an even integer has an even square. Write $n=2k$ for an integer $k$, then $n^2=4k^2=2(2k^2)$ is even. This proves the contrapositive and therefore the original statement. It does not require testing every odd square separately. Explicitly identify the domain and conclude why the transformed argument proves the requested implication.","diagram":"contrapositive","html":"To prove if an integer has an odd square then the integer is odd, it is convenient to prove that an even integer has an even square. Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2k</annotation></semantics></math></span> for an integer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2=4k^2=2(2k^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is even. This proves the contrapositive and therefore the original statement. It does not require testing every odd square separately. Explicitly identify the domain and conclude why the transformed argument proves the requested implication."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Find a counterexample that satisfies the hypothesis","text":"A counterexample to $P\\Rightarrow Q$ must make $P$ true and $Q$ false. An example where $P$ is false cannot refute the implication, however surprising its conclusion is. For all rectangles are squares, a rectangle of side lengths 2 and 3 has the required rectangle property but lacks equal sides. One counterexample is sufficient, while thousands of supportive examples do not establish the universal conditional without a general argument.","diagram":null,"html":"A counterexample to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\Rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> must make <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math></span> true and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> false. An example where <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math></span> is false cannot refute the implication, however surprising its conclusion is. For all rectangles are squares, a rectangle of side lengths 2 and 3 has the required rectangle property but lacks equal sides. One counterexample is sufficient, while thousands of supportive examples do not establish the universal conditional without a general argument."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Negate conjunctions and inequalities carefully","text":"Negating $P\\land Q$ gives $\\neg P\\lor\\neg Q$; negating $P\\lor Q$ gives $\\neg P\\land\\neg Q$. Negating $x>2$ gives $x\\le2$, including the boundary, rather than $x<2$. An argument can fail through a missed equality case even if the rest of its algebra is sound. When conditions include nonzero denominators, integer parity or positive lengths, preserve those restrictions during every transformation and distinguish a logical counterexample from an inadmissible value.","diagram":null,"html":"Negating <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\land Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg P\\lor\\neg Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>; negating <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\lor Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg P\\land\\neg Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Negating <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\le2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, including the boundary, rather than <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. An argument can fail through a missed equality case even if the rest of its algebra is sound. When conditions include nonzero denominators, integer parity or positive lengths, preserve those restrictions during every transformation and distinguish a logical counterexample from an inadmissible value."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-counterexamples-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Write the converse, inverse and contrapositive of: if an integer is divisible by 4, it is even. Decide which is equivalent to the original. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Converse: if an integer is even, it is divisible by 4.","[1] Inverse: if an integer is not divisible by 4, it is not even.","[1] Contrapositive: if an integer is not even, it is not divisible by 4.","[1] The contrapositive is equivalent to the true original; $n=2$ disproves the converse and inverse."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Write the converse, inverse and contrapositive of: if an integer is divisible by 4, it is even. Decide which is equivalent to the original. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Converse: if an integer is even, it is divisible by 4.","[1] Inverse: if an integer is not divisible by 4, it is not even.","[1] Contrapositive: if an integer is not even, it is not divisible by 4.","[1] The contrapositive is equivalent to the true original; <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2</annotation></semantics></math></span> disproves the converse and inverse."]},{"id":"mas11-counterexamples-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Prove for integer $n$: if $n^2$ is odd, then $n$ is odd. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use the contrapositive: if $n$ is even, then $n^2$ is even.","[1] Write $n=2k$ with $k\\in\\mathbb Z$.","[1] Then $n^2=4k^2=2(2k^2)$ and $2k^2$ is an integer.","[1] Thus the contrapositive holds, proving the original implication."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove for integer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span>: if <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is odd, then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> is odd. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use the contrapositive: if <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> is even, then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is even.","[1] Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2k</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\in\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2=4k^2=2(2k^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2k^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is an integer.","[1] Thus the contrapositive holds, proving the original implication."]},{"id":"mas11-counterexamples-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Give a valid counterexample to: for real $x$, if $x^2>1$, then $x>1$. State both required checks. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Choose $x=-2$, a real number in the stated domain.","[1] The hypothesis is true because $x^2=4>1$.","[1] The conclusion is false because $-2$ is not greater than 1.","[1] Hence this is a valid counterexample to the implication."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Give a valid counterexample to: for real <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, if <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. State both required checks. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Choose <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, a real number in the stated domain.","[1] The hypothesis is true because <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2=4&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The conclusion is false because <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is not greater than 1.","[1] Hence this is a valid counterexample to the implication."]},{"id":"mas11-counterexamples-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Negate: $x>0$ and $y>0$. Then negate: $x>0$ or $y>0$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Negation of a conjunction uses or.","[1] The first negation is $x\\le0$ or $y\\le0$.","[1] Negation of a disjunction uses and.","[1] The second negation is $x\\le0$ and $y\\le0$, including equality boundaries."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Negate: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Then negate: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Negation of a conjunction uses or.","[1] The first negation is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\le0</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\le0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Negation of a disjunction uses and.","[1] The second negation is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\le0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\le0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, including equality boundaries."]},{"id":"mas11-counterexamples-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Assess the proposed converse: if a quadrilateral has perpendicular diagonals, it is a rhombus. Give a coordinate counterexample. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use vertices $(2,0),(0,1),(-1,0),(0,-1)$ in order.","[1] The diagonals lie on the coordinate axes, so are perpendicular.","[1] Adjacent side lengths include $\\sqrt5$ and $\\sqrt2$, so all four sides are not equal.","[1] This quadrilateral satisfies the hypothesis but is not a rhombus; the converse needs an additional condition such as parallelogram."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Assess the proposed converse: if a quadrilateral has perpendicular diagonals, it is a rhombus. Give a coordinate counterexample. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use vertices <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,0),(0,1),(-1,0),(0,-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> in order.","[1] The diagonals lie on the coordinate axes, so are perpendicular.","[1] Adjacent side lengths include <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt5</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so all four sides are not equal.","[1] This quadrilateral satisfies the hypothesis but is not a rhombus; the converse needs an additional condition such as parallelogram."]},{"id":"mas11-counterexamples-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Prove for integers $a,b$: if $ab$ is odd, then both $a$ and $b$ are odd, using a contrapositive. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Contrapositive: if $a$ or $b$ is even, then $ab$ is even.","[1] If $a=2k$, then $ab=2(kb)$ is even.","[1] If $b=2m$, then $ab=2(am)$ is even.","[1] Either case establishes the contrapositive, hence an odd product requires both factors odd."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove for integers <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b</annotation></semantics></math></span>: if <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ab</annotation></semantics></math></span> is odd, then both <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math></span> are odd, using a contrapositive. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Contrapositive: if <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math></span> is even, then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ab</annotation></semantics></math></span> is even.","[1] If <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=2k</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ab=2(kb)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is even.","[1] If <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=2m</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ab=2(am)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is even.","[1] Either case establishes the contrapositive, hence an odd product requires both factors odd."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which statement is logically equivalent to $P\\Rightarrow Q$?","o":["$\\neg Q\\Rightarrow\\neg P$.","$Q\\Rightarrow P$.","$\\neg P\\Rightarrow\\neg Q$.","$P\\Rightarrow\\neg Q$."],"a":0,"why":"The contrapositive preserves the truth conditions of the original implication.","qHtml":"Which statement is logically equivalent to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\Rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg Q\\Rightarrow\\neg P</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\Rightarrow P</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg P\\Rightarrow\\neg Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\Rightarrow\\neg Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>."],"whyHtml":"The contrapositive preserves the truth conditions of the original implication."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-contradiction","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Geometry","title":"Proof by contradiction and rigorous conclusions","outcomes":["1.1.2","1.1.5"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Begin with the negation of the required result · Track where each assumption is used · Contradiction and contrapositive are related but distinct · Finish with the requested conclusion","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Proof by contradiction and rigorous conclusions</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Begin with the negation of the required result</li><li>Track where each assumption is used</li><li>Contradiction and contrapositive are related but distinct</li><li>Finish with the requested conclusion</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Begin with the negation of the required result","text":"A contradiction proof assumes the requested conclusion is false, combines that assumption with accepted facts and derives an impossibility. The impossibility might be both an integer and non-integer value, a number both even and odd, or an inequality inconsistent with a known bound. Identify the precise statement being negated. Assuming a result that is merely stronger than the negation is not a valid substitute, because disproving that stronger statement need not prove the original claim.","diagram":null,"html":"A contradiction proof assumes the requested conclusion is false, combines that assumption with accepted facts and derives an impossibility. The impossibility might be both an integer and non-integer value, a number both even and odd, or an inequality inconsistent with a known bound. Identify the precise statement being negated. Assuming a result that is merely stronger than the negation is not a valid substitute, because disproving that stronger statement need not prove the original claim."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Track where each assumption is used","text":"State the domain and any representation justified by it. If a rational number is written $a/b$, require integers $a,b$ with $b\\ne0$, and if the argument uses coprimality explicitly choose a fraction in lowest terms. Each algebraic operation must be valid; division by an expression that might vanish can invalidate an otherwise convincing chain. When an assumption is contradicted, name it rather than announcing a contradiction with no reference to the incompatible facts.","diagram":"contrapositive","html":"State the domain and any representation justified by it. If a rational number is written <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a/b</annotation></semantics></math></span>, require integers <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and if the argument uses coprimality explicitly choose a fraction in lowest terms. Each algebraic operation must be valid; division by an expression that might vanish can invalidate an otherwise convincing chain. When an assumption is contradicted, name it rather than announcing a contradiction with no reference to the incompatible facts."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Contradiction and contrapositive are related but distinct","text":"A contrapositive proof establishes $\\neg Q\\Rightarrow\\neg P$ for an original implication. A contradiction proof for that implication can assume both $P$ and $\\neg Q$, then derive an impossibility. Both are legitimate, but the written argument should make its structure clear. Testing a few cases is neither method. An arbitrary integer represented as $2k$ is general for even integers because the representation follows a definition, not because $k$ is a chosen sample.","diagram":null,"html":"A contrapositive proof establishes <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg Q\\Rightarrow\\neg P</annotation></semantics></math></span> for an original implication. A contradiction proof for that implication can assume both <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then derive an impossibility. Both are legitimate, but the written argument should make its structure clear. Testing a few cases is neither method. An arbitrary integer represented as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2k</annotation></semantics></math></span> is general for even integers because the representation follows a definition, not because <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a chosen sample."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Finish with the requested conclusion","text":"Once the negated assumption is impossible, return to the original statement and explain its status on the stated domain. A proof that no largest positive integer exists does not establish a largest integer elsewhere. In geometry, a sketch can organise information but cannot supply an unproved angle or length. The contradiction must come from givens, definitions or previously justified theorems. Keep exact expressions through the proof; rounded decimal approximations rarely establish exact impossibility.","diagram":null,"html":"Once the negated assumption is impossible, return to the original statement and explain its status on the stated domain. A proof that no largest positive integer exists does not establish a largest integer elsewhere. In geometry, a sketch can organise information but cannot supply an unproved angle or length. The contradiction must come from givens, definitions or previously justified theorems. Keep exact expressions through the proof; rounded decimal approximations rarely establish exact impossibility."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-contradiction-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Prove that there is no largest positive integer. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Suppose a largest positive integer $N$ exists.","[1] Then $N+1$ is also a positive integer.","[1] But $N+1>N$, contradicting the claimed maximality of $N$.","[1] Therefore no largest positive integer exists."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove that there is no largest positive integer. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Suppose a largest positive integer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></span> exists.","[1] Then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N+1</annotation></semantics></math></span> is also a positive integer.","[1] But <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N+1&gt;N</annotation></semantics></math></span>, contradicting the claimed maximality of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Therefore no largest positive integer exists."]},{"id":"mas11-contradiction-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Prove that an integer cannot be both even and odd. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Suppose $n=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ for integers $a,b$.","[1] Equating gives $2(a-b)=1$.","[1] Thus $a-b=1/2$, contradicting that a difference of integers is an integer.","[1] Therefore no integer is simultaneously even and odd."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove that an integer cannot be both even and odd. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Suppose <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2a</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2b+1</annotation></semantics></math></span> for integers <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Equating gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(a-b)=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a-b=1/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, contradicting that a difference of integers is an integer.","[1] Therefore no integer is simultaneously even and odd."]},{"id":"mas11-contradiction-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Prove that the sum of a rational number $r$ and an irrational number $u$ is irrational. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Suppose $r+u$ is rational.","[1] Rational numbers are closed under subtraction.","[1] Then $u=(r+u)-r$ would be rational, contradicting the given irrationality of $u$.","[1] Hence $r+u$ is irrational."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove that the sum of a rational number <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math></span> and an irrational number <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u</annotation></semantics></math></span> is irrational. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Suppose <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r+u</annotation></semantics></math></span> is rational.","[1] Rational numbers are closed under subtraction.","[1] Then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=(r+u)-r</annotation></semantics></math></span> would be rational, contradicting the given irrationality of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r+u</annotation></semantics></math></span> is irrational."]},{"id":"mas11-contradiction-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Let $x>0$. Prove $x+1/x\\ge2$ by assuming the opposite and using a square. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Suppose $x+1/x<2$ while $x>0$.","[1] Multiplication by positive $x$ preserves the inequality: $x^2+1<2x$.","[1] This gives $(x-1)^2<0$, impossible for a real square.","[1] Therefore $x+1/x\\ge2$; equality occurs at $x=1$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Let <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+1/x\\ge2</annotation></semantics></math></span> by assuming the opposite and using a square. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Suppose <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+1/x&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math></span> while <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Multiplication by positive <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span> preserves the inequality: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+1&lt;2x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-1)^2&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, impossible for a real square.","[1] Therefore <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+1/x\\ge2</annotation></semantics></math></span>; equality occurs at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-contradiction-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Prove that if an integer $n$ is odd, $n^2$ cannot be even. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Write $n=2k+1$ for an integer $k$.","[1] Then $n^2=4k^2+4k+1=2(2k^2+2k)+1$ is odd.","[1] Assuming $n^2$ even would make it both even and odd, which is impossible.","[1] Hence an odd integer has a square that is not even."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove that if an integer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> is odd, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> cannot be even. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2k+1</annotation></semantics></math></span> for an integer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2=4k^2+4k+1=2(2k^2+2k)+1</annotation></semantics></math></span> is odd.","[1] Assuming <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> even would make it both even and odd, which is impossible.","[1] Hence an odd integer has a square that is not even."]},{"id":"mas11-contradiction-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A proposed proof assumes $x\\ne0$ then divides by $x-y$ despite only knowing $x=y$. Explain the exact failure and why a surprising contradiction cannot repair it. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] From $x=y$, the divisor $x-y$ is zero.","[1] Division by zero is undefined, even though $x$ itself is nonzero.","[1] The resulting algebraic statements do not follow by valid implication.","[1] A contradiction reached after an invalid step cannot establish the desired theorem."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A proposed proof assumes <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span> then divides by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-y</annotation></semantics></math></span> despite only knowing <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=y</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Explain the exact failure and why a surprising contradiction cannot repair it. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] From <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=y</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the divisor <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-y</annotation></semantics></math></span> is zero.","[1] Division by zero is undefined, even though <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span> itself is nonzero.","[1] The resulting algebraic statements do not follow by valid implication.","[1] A contradiction reached after an invalid step cannot establish the desired theorem."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What must a contradiction proof show?","o":["The negation of the conclusion leads to an impossibility using valid steps.","One supportive numerical example.","An impossible result after division by zero.","That a stronger unrelated assertion is false."],"a":0,"why":"The negated conclusion must be the assumption ruled out, and the chain must remain valid.","qHtml":"What must a contradiction proof show?","oHtml":["The negation of the conclusion leads to an impossibility using valid steps.","One supportive numerical example.","An impossible result after division by zero.","That a stronger unrelated assertion is false."],"whyHtml":"The negated conclusion must be the assumption ruled out, and the chain must remain valid."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-proof-chains","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Geometry","title":"Building geometric proofs from stated results","outcomes":["1.1.3","1.1.5","1.1.15"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Plan backwards and write forwards · A diagram displays relationships but does not prove them · Use definitions and theorem hypotheses precisely · Write a complete chain with a conclusion","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Building geometric proofs from stated results</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Plan backwards and write forwards</li><li>A diagram displays relationships but does not prove them</li><li>Use definitions and theorem hypotheses precisely</li><li>Write a complete chain with a conclusion</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Plan backwards and write forwards","text":"A useful plan asks what would be sufficient for the requested conclusion, then searches the givens for a way to establish it. The final proof should run forwards from stated information to justified intermediate results and the conclusion. Planning backwards is not permission to assume the conclusion. For equal triangles, identify a valid congruence condition; for parallel lines, identify a suitable angle converse. Keep a short list of accepted results available so each line has an explicit reason.","diagram":null,"html":"A useful plan asks what would be sufficient for the requested conclusion, then searches the givens for a way to establish it. The final proof should run forwards from stated information to justified intermediate results and the conclusion. Planning backwards is not permission to assume the conclusion. For equal triangles, identify a valid congruence condition; for parallel lines, identify a suitable angle converse. Keep a short list of accepted results available so each line has an explicit reason."},{"kind":"learn","title":"A diagram displays relationships but does not prove them","text":"Sketches can exaggerate lengths or make angles appear equal. Mark only supplied or established facts. In a proof about a general quadrilateral, do not silently draw a rectangle and use right angles. Labels should distinguish points, lines and angles: $\\angle ABC$ has vertex B. If a theorem requires a circle or a parallelogram, establish that condition before applying it. The same-looking diagram can represent objects with different hypotheses.","diagram":"logic","html":"Sketches can exaggerate lengths or make angles appear equal. Mark only supplied or established facts. In a proof about a general quadrilateral, do not silently draw a rectangle and use right angles. Labels should distinguish points, lines and angles: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∠</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\angle ABC</annotation></semantics></math></span> has vertex B. If a theorem requires a circle or a parallelogram, establish that condition before applying it. The same-looking diagram can represent objects with different hypotheses."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use definitions and theorem hypotheses precisely","text":"A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel; a rhombus adds equal side lengths. Perpendicular diagonals alone do not establish a rhombus unless suitable additional conditions hold. Equal base angles in an isosceles triangle follow its equal sides, and converses need their own justification. A proof can combine algebra with geometry, provided the quantities genuinely represent the geometric objects and all lengths or angles obey the given conditions.","diagram":null,"html":"A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel; a rhombus adds equal side lengths. Perpendicular diagonals alone do not establish a rhombus unless suitable additional conditions hold. Equal base angles in an isosceles triangle follow its equal sides, and converses need their own justification. A proof can combine algebra with geometry, provided the quantities genuinely represent the geometric objects and all lengths or angles obey the given conditions."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Write a complete chain with a conclusion","text":"An exam proof typically earns credit for connected reasoning, not a disconnected list of true theorems. State each derived equality or angle relation and its reason. If proving equivalence, organise the two directions separately. If a construction is used, explain what it adds and why it is valid. End with the precise claim requested rather than a bare therefore. When checking, ask whether every diagram fact used was given, defined, constructed or previously proved.","diagram":null,"html":"An exam proof typically earns credit for connected reasoning, not a disconnected list of true theorems. State each derived equality or angle relation and its reason. If proving equivalence, organise the two directions separately. If a construction is used, explain what it adds and why it is valid. End with the precise claim requested rather than a bare therefore. When checking, ask whether every diagram fact used was given, defined, constructed or previously proved."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-proof-chains-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"In triangle ABC, AB = AC and angle A is $40^\\circ$. Find angles B and C with reasons. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Equal sides AB and AC make the opposite angles B and C equal.","[1] Triangle angles sum to $180^\\circ$.","[1] Therefore $2B=180^\\circ-40^\\circ=140^\\circ$.","[1] $B=C=70^\\circ$, with equality justified by the isosceles theorem."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"In triangle ABC, AB = AC and angle A is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>40</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find angles B and C with reasons. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Equal sides AB and AC make the opposite angles B and C equal.","[1] Triangle angles sum to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">180^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Therefore <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>40</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>140</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2B=180^\\circ-40^\\circ=140^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>70</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=C=70^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with equality justified by the isosceles theorem."]},{"id":"mas11-proof-chains-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Prove that a quadrilateral with all four interior angles equal has four right angles. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let each interior angle be $\\theta$.","[1] Interior angles of a quadrilateral sum to $360^\\circ$.","[1] Thus $4\\theta=360^\\circ$ and $\\theta=90^\\circ$.","[1] Therefore each of its four angles is a right angle; no scale measurement was needed."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove that a quadrilateral with all four interior angles equal has four right angles. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let each interior angle be <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Interior angles of a quadrilateral sum to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">360^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\theta=360^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Therefore each of its four angles is a right angle; no scale measurement was needed."]},{"id":"mas11-proof-chains-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"A parallelogram has one interior angle $65^\\circ$. Find the other three and give the angle relationships used. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal.","[1] Adjacent angles are supplementary because opposite sides are parallel.","[1] Adjacent angle $=180^\\circ-65^\\circ=115^\\circ$.","[1] The remaining angles are $115^\\circ,65^\\circ,115^\\circ$ in cyclic order after the given angle."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A parallelogram has one interior angle <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>65</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">65^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find the other three and give the angle relationships used. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal.","[1] Adjacent angles are supplementary because opposite sides are parallel.","[1] Adjacent angle <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>65</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>115</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=180^\\circ-65^\\circ=115^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The remaining angles are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>115</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mn>65</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mn>115</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">115^\\circ,65^\\circ,115^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> in cyclic order after the given angle."]},{"id":"mas11-proof-chains-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Explain why equal diagonals alone do not prove that a general quadrilateral is a rectangle. Give a coordinate counterexample. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use vertices $(-2,0),(2,0),(1,2),(-1,2)$ in order.","[1] Both diagonals have length $\\sqrt{3^2+2^2}=\\sqrt{13}$.","[1] Adjacent side vectors at $(2,0)$ are $(-4,0)$ and $(-1,2)$ with nonzero dot product 4, so that angle is not right.","[1] Equal diagonals do not imply rectangle for an unrestricted quadrilateral; extra hypotheses are required."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why equal diagonals alone do not prove that a general quadrilateral is a rectangle. Give a coordinate counterexample. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use vertices <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-2,0),(2,0),(1,2),(-1,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> in order.","[1] Both diagonals have length <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{3^2+2^2}=\\sqrt{13}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Adjacent side vectors at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-4,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with nonzero dot product 4, so that angle is not right.","[1] Equal diagonals do not imply rectangle for an unrestricted quadrilateral; extra hypotheses are required."]},{"id":"mas11-proof-chains-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Given AB = DE, BC = EF and angle ABC equals angle DEF, justify triangle congruence and identify one further corresponding equality. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The given equal angle lies between the two given sides in each triangle.","[1] Therefore SAS congruence applies to triangles ABC and DEF with A corresponding to D, B to E and C to F.","[1] Corresponding remaining sides are equal, so AC = DF.","[1] The correspondence and included-angle condition are necessary; the conclusion is not inferred from appearance."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Given AB = DE, BC = EF and angle ABC equals angle DEF, justify triangle congruence and identify one further corresponding equality. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The given equal angle lies between the two given sides in each triangle.","[1] Therefore SAS congruence applies to triangles ABC and DEF with A corresponding to D, B to E and C to F.","[1] Corresponding remaining sides are equal, so AC = DF.","[1] The correspondence and included-angle condition are necessary; the conclusion is not inferred from appearance."]},{"id":"mas11-proof-chains-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"For a convex quadrilateral, prove its interior-angle sum is $360^\\circ$ using a diagonal. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Draw a diagonal joining two nonadjacent vertices inside the convex quadrilateral.","[1] It divides the quadrilateral into two triangles.","[1] Each triangle has angle sum $180^\\circ$, and their angles together partition the quadrilateral angles.","[1] Hence the quadrilateral angle sum is $2\\times180^\\circ=360^\\circ$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For a convex quadrilateral, prove its interior-angle sum is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">360^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> using a diagonal. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Draw a diagonal joining two nonadjacent vertices inside the convex quadrilateral.","[1] It divides the quadrilateral into two triangles.","[1] Each triangle has angle sum <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">180^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and their angles together partition the quadrilateral angles.","[1] Hence the quadrilateral angle sum is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times180^\\circ=360^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which fact may be used in a geometric proof?","o":["A given, definition or previously justified result whose conditions hold.","A length inferred only from the drawing scale.","A right angle silently added to a general quadrilateral.","The desired conclusion assumed as an initial fact."],"a":0,"why":"Every geometric fact in the argument needs a valid origin and applicable hypotheses.","qHtml":"Which fact may be used in a geometric proof?","oHtml":["A given, definition or previously justified result whose conditions hold.","A length inferred only from the drawing scale.","A right angle silently added to a general quadrilateral.","The desired conclusion assumed as an initial fact."],"whyHtml":"Every geometric fact in the argument needs a valid origin and applicable hypotheses."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-circle-angles","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Geometry","title":"Semicircles, central angles and equal-arc angles","outcomes":["1.1.6","1.1.7","1.1.8"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Identify the arc and the vertex · The semicircle theorem is a special case · Equal-arc angles require the appropriate segment · Understand the proof behind the angle relationship","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Semicircles, central angles and equal-arc angles</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Identify the arc and the vertex</li><li>The semicircle theorem is a special case</li><li>Equal-arc angles require the appropriate segment</li><li>Understand the proof behind the angle relationship</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Identify the arc and the vertex","text":"An angle at the centre subtended by a particular arc is twice an angle at the circumference subtended by that same arc. The vertices must occupy the positions required by the theorem, and the intercepted arc matters. A reflex central angle can correspond to the major arc, so blindly halving the smaller central angle can give the wrong result. Label the two endpoints and the vertex before applying the relationship. Circle diagrams may not be drawn to scale.","diagram":null,"html":"An angle at the centre subtended by a particular arc is twice an angle at the circumference subtended by that same arc. The vertices must occupy the positions required by the theorem, and the intercepted arc matters. A reflex central angle can correspond to the major arc, so blindly halving the smaller central angle can give the wrong result. Label the two endpoints and the vertex before applying the relationship. Circle diagrams may not be drawn to scale."},{"kind":"learn","title":"The semicircle theorem is a special case","text":"If AB is a diameter and C lies on the circumference away from A and B, $\\angle ACB=90^\\circ$. The intercepted semicircle has central angle $180^\\circ$, and the circumference angle is half of it. The converse can establish that a point lies on the circle with AB as diameter when the angle is right under the relevant configuration. A chord that merely looks horizontal is not automatically a diameter: it must pass through the centre or be established as one.","diagram":"circle-angle","html":"If AB is a diameter and C lies on the circumference away from A and B, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∠</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\angle ACB=90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The intercepted semicircle has central angle <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">180^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and the circumference angle is half of it. The converse can establish that a point lies on the circle with AB as diameter when the angle is right under the relevant configuration. A chord that merely looks horizontal is not automatically a diameter: it must pass through the centre or be established as one."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Equal-arc angles require the appropriate segment","text":"Angles at the circumference subtending the same arc are equal. Points on opposite sides of a chord can subtend different complementary arcs, giving supplementary rather than equal angles. Draw the intercepted arc or identify which segment contains the vertices. When radii appear, their equal lengths can create isosceles triangles that connect the central theorem with ordinary triangle-angle sums. Use these facts in a chain rather than announcing unrelated equal angles.","diagram":null,"html":"Angles at the circumference subtending the same arc are equal. Points on opposite sides of a chord can subtend different complementary arcs, giving supplementary rather than equal angles. Draw the intercepted arc or identify which segment contains the vertices. When radii appear, their equal lengths can create isosceles triangles that connect the central theorem with ordinary triangle-angle sums. Use these facts in a chain rather than announcing unrelated equal angles."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Understand the proof behind the angle relationship","text":"Join the centre to the three circumference points and extend a radius where useful. The equal radii create isosceles triangles, whose base angles can be expressed in terms of the circumference angle. Exterior-angle reasoning then gives twice that angle at the centre. Depending on the centre’s position, the relevant pieces are added or subtracted; the same intercepted-arc relation results. A proof for one drawn arrangement should identify that arrangement rather than silently assuming it covers every possible centre location.","diagram":null,"html":"Join the centre to the three circumference points and extend a radius where useful. The equal radii create isosceles triangles, whose base angles can be expressed in terms of the circumference angle. Exterior-angle reasoning then gives twice that angle at the centre. Depending on the centre’s position, the relevant pieces are added or subtracted; the same intercepted-arc relation results. A proof for one drawn arrangement should identify that arrangement rather than silently assuming it covers every possible centre location."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-circle-angles-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"An arc AB subtends $124^\\circ$ at the centre. Point C lies on the remaining circumference so angle ACB intercepts that arc. Find angle ACB and explain the theorem. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Identify the same intercepted arc AB at both vertices.","[1] The circumference angle is half the central angle for that arc.","[1] $\\angle ACB=124^\\circ/2=62^\\circ$.","[1] The conclusion uses vertex and arc conditions, not a measurement of the sketch."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"An arc AB subtends <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>124</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">124^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> at the centre. Point C lies on the remaining circumference so angle ACB intercepts that arc. Find angle ACB and explain the theorem. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Identify the same intercepted arc AB at both vertices.","[1] The circumference angle is half the central angle for that arc.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∠</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>124</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>62</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\angle ACB=124^\\circ/2=62^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The conclusion uses vertex and arc conditions, not a measurement of the sketch."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-angles-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"AB is a diameter, C lies on the circle and $\\angle CAB=28^\\circ$. Find angles ACB and ABC. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] A diameter subtends a right angle at the circumference, so $\\angle ACB=90^\\circ$.","[1] Triangle ABC has angle sum $180^\\circ$.","[1] $\\angle ABC=180^\\circ-90^\\circ-28^\\circ=62^\\circ$.","[1] Both requested angles follow from the diameter and triangle theorems."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"AB is a diameter, C lies on the circle and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∠</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>28</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\angle CAB=28^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find angles ACB and ABC. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] A diameter subtends a right angle at the circumference, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∠</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\angle ACB=90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Triangle ABC has angle sum <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">180^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∠</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>28</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>62</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\angle ABC=180^\\circ-90^\\circ-28^\\circ=62^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both requested angles follow from the diameter and triangle theorems."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-angles-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Angles ACB and ADB intercept the same arc AB and C,D are in the same segment. If angle ACB is $47^\\circ$, find angle ADB and the corresponding central angle. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Same-arc circumference angles in this configuration are equal.","[1] $\\angle ADB=47^\\circ$.","[1] The corresponding central angle is twice the circumference angle.","[1] Central angle $=94^\\circ$ for the stated intercepted arc."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Angles ACB and ADB intercept the same arc AB and C,D are in the same segment. If angle ACB is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>47</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">47^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find angle ADB and the corresponding central angle. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Same-arc circumference angles in this configuration are equal.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∠</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>47</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\angle ADB=47^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The corresponding central angle is twice the circumference angle.","[1] Central angle <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>94</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=94^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> for the stated intercepted arc."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-angles-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"The minor central angle AOB is $100^\\circ$. A circumference angle intercepts the major arc AB. Find that angle. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The major-arc reflex central angle is $360^\\circ-100^\\circ=260^\\circ$.","[1] The circumference angle is half the central angle for the arc it actually intercepts.","[1] Required angle $=260^\\circ/2=130^\\circ$.","[1] Halving $100^\\circ$ would use the wrong arc."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"The minor central angle AOB is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>100</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A circumference angle intercepts the major arc AB. Find that angle. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The major-arc reflex central angle is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>100</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>260</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">360^\\circ-100^\\circ=260^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The circumference angle is half the central angle for the arc it actually intercepts.","[1] Required angle <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>260</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>130</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=260^\\circ/2=130^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Halving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>100</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> would use the wrong arc."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-angles-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"AB is a diameter of length 10 and C is on the circle with AC = 6. Find BC, justifying the use of Pythagoras. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\angle ACB=90^\\circ$ by the semicircle theorem.","[1] AB is the hypotenuse, so $AC^2+BC^2=AB^2$.","[1] $BC^2=100-36=64$.","[1] $BC=8$, choosing the positive length."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"AB is a diameter of length 10 and C is on the circle with AC = 6. Find BC, justifying the use of Pythagoras. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∠</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\angle ACB=90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> by the semicircle theorem.","[1] AB is the hypotenuse, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AC^2+BC^2=AB^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>36</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">BC^2=100-36=64</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">BC=8</annotation></semantics></math></span>, choosing the positive length."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-angles-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Prove the semicircle theorem directly by joining centre O to C. Let angles ACO and OCB be $\\alpha,\\beta$ with O between A and B. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] OA = OC and OB = OC are radii, so angles OAC and OBC equal $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ respectively.","[1] In triangle ABC, the three angles are $\\alpha,\\beta,\\alpha+\\beta$.","[1] Hence $2(\\alpha+\\beta)=180^\\circ$.","[1] $\\angle ACB=\\alpha+\\beta=90^\\circ$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove the semicircle theorem directly by joining centre O to C. Let angles ACO and OCB be <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha,\\beta</annotation></semantics></math></span> with O between A and B. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] OA = OC and OB = OC are radii, so angles OAC and OBC equal <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta</annotation></semantics></math></span> respectively.","[1] In triangle ABC, the three angles are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha,\\beta,\\alpha+\\beta</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(\\alpha+\\beta)=180^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∠</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\angle ACB=\\alpha+\\beta=90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What central angle must be used when a circumference angle intercepts the major arc?","o":["The reflex central angle for that major arc.","Always the smaller central angle.","The tangent angle regardless of endpoints.","Any angle measured from the drawing."],"a":0,"why":"The central and circumference angles must subtend the same identified arc.","qHtml":"What central angle must be used when a circumference angle intercepts the major arc?","oHtml":["The reflex central angle for that major arc.","Always the smaller central angle.","The tangent angle regardless of endpoints.","Any angle measured from the drawing."],"whyHtml":"The central and circumference angles must subtend the same identified arc."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-circle-chords","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Geometry","title":"Cyclic quadrilaterals, equal chords and converses","outcomes":["1.1.9","1.1.10","1.1.14"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Cyclic quadrilaterals have supplementary opposite angles · A converse can establish cyclicity · Equal chords connect with radii and central angles · Build multi-step deductions without guessing","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Cyclic quadrilaterals, equal chords and converses</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Cyclic quadrilaterals have supplementary opposite angles</li><li>A converse can establish cyclicity</li><li>Equal chords connect with radii and central angles</li><li>Build multi-step deductions without guessing</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Cyclic quadrilaterals have supplementary opposite angles","text":"A cyclic quadrilateral has all four vertices on one circle. Its opposite circumference angles intercept complementary arcs whose total central measure is $360^\\circ$, so those opposite angles sum to $180^\\circ$. This does not apply to every quadrilateral. When an exterior angle is used, first relate it to the adjacent interior angle by a straight-line sum; it can then equal the opposite interior angle in the cyclic configuration.","diagram":null,"html":"A cyclic quadrilateral has all four vertices on one circle. Its opposite circumference angles intercept complementary arcs whose total central measure is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">360^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so those opposite angles sum to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">180^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This does not apply to every quadrilateral. When an exterior angle is used, first relate it to the adjacent interior angle by a straight-line sum; it can then equal the opposite interior angle in the cyclic configuration."},{"kind":"learn","title":"A converse can establish cyclicity","text":"For a suitable simple quadrilateral, a pair of supplementary opposite angles can establish that the vertices are concyclic. State the configuration and the angles used. Establishing cyclicity can unlock other circle theorems, but do not begin by using a cyclic result to prove cyclicity without a valid converse argument. If the figure has crossing edges or reflex angles, interpret the theorem carefully rather than applying an unqualified interior-angle template.","diagram":"circle-chord","html":"For a suitable simple quadrilateral, a pair of supplementary opposite angles can establish that the vertices are concyclic. State the configuration and the angles used. Establishing cyclicity can unlock other circle theorems, but do not begin by using a cyclic result to prove cyclicity without a valid converse argument. If the figure has crossing edges or reflex angles, interpret the theorem carefully rather than applying an unqualified interior-angle template."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Equal chords connect with radii and central angles","text":"Within one circle, equal chords subtend equal central angles, and the converse holds for the corresponding angles. Joining the centre to chord endpoints creates triangles with two radii, so equal chords give SSS congruence and hence equal included central angles. Conversely, equal included angles with equal radii give SAS and equal chords. The comparison requires the same circle or equal-radius circles; a common central angle in circles of different radii need not give equal chord lengths.","diagram":null,"html":"Within one circle, equal chords subtend equal central angles, and the converse holds for the corresponding angles. Joining the centre to chord endpoints creates triangles with two radii, so equal chords give SSS congruence and hence equal included central angles. Conversely, equal included angles with equal radii give SAS and equal chords. The comparison requires the same circle or equal-radius circles; a common central angle in circles of different radii need not give equal chord lengths."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Build multi-step deductions without guessing","text":"Equal chords can imply equal corresponding arcs and suitable circumference angles. Combine those deductions with isosceles-triangle and cyclic-quadrilateral facts. State whether a selected angle intercepts a minor or major arc and ensure vertex correspondence is correct. A useful proof lists chord equality, radii equality, triangle congruence and the derived angle before the final result. The theorem is exact, while a drawing may only suggest the relationship.","diagram":null,"html":"Equal chords can imply equal corresponding arcs and suitable circumference angles. Combine those deductions with isosceles-triangle and cyclic-quadrilateral facts. State whether a selected angle intercepts a minor or major arc and ensure vertex correspondence is correct. A useful proof lists chord equality, radii equality, triangle congruence and the derived angle before the final result. The theorem is exact, while a drawing may only suggest the relationship."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-circle-chords-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"ABCD is cyclic in order and angle A is $108^\\circ$. Find angle C. If angle B is $76^\\circ$, find angle D. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Opposite interior angles in a cyclic quadrilateral sum to $180^\\circ$.","[1] $C=180^\\circ-108^\\circ=72^\\circ$.","[1] B and D are the other opposite pair.","[1] $D=180^\\circ-76^\\circ=104^\\circ$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"ABCD is cyclic in order and angle A is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>108</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">108^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find angle C. If angle B is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>76</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">76^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find angle D. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Opposite interior angles in a cyclic quadrilateral sum to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">180^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>108</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>72</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=180^\\circ-108^\\circ=72^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] B and D are the other opposite pair.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>76</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>104</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=180^\\circ-76^\\circ=104^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-chords-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Prove equal chords AB and CD of the same circle subtend equal minor central angles, using centre O. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] OA = OB = OC = OD because all are radii.","[1] AB = CD is given.","[1] Triangles OAB and OCD are congruent by SSS.","[1] Their corresponding included central angles AOB and COD are equal."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove equal chords AB and CD of the same circle subtend equal minor central angles, using centre O. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] OA = OB = OC = OD because all are radii.","[1] AB = CD is given.","[1] Triangles OAB and OCD are congruent by SSS.","[1] Their corresponding included central angles AOB and COD are equal."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-chords-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"A simple convex quadrilateral has opposite angles $83^\\circ$ and $97^\\circ$. Explain how a converse establishes cyclicity. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The specified angles are opposite interior angles.","[1] Their sum is $83^\\circ+97^\\circ=180^\\circ$.","[1] The converse of the cyclic-quadrilateral angle theorem applies to this suitable configuration.","[1] Therefore its four vertices lie on a circle."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A simple convex quadrilateral has opposite angles <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>83</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">83^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>97</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">97^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Explain how a converse establishes cyclicity. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The specified angles are opposite interior angles.","[1] Their sum is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>83</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>97</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">83^\\circ+97^\\circ=180^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The converse of the cyclic-quadrilateral angle theorem applies to this suitable configuration.","[1] Therefore its four vertices lie on a circle."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-chords-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"In cyclic ABCD, side AB is extended through B to E and exterior angle CBE is $112^\\circ$. Find interior angle B and opposite angle D. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Angles ABC and CBE lie on a straight line.","[1] Interior angle B is $180^\\circ-112^\\circ=68^\\circ$.","[1] Opposite cyclic angles B and D are supplementary.","[1] $D=112^\\circ$, equal to the stated exterior angle."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"In cyclic ABCD, side AB is extended through B to E and exterior angle CBE is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>112</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">112^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find interior angle B and opposite angle D. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Angles ABC and CBE lie on a straight line.","[1] Interior angle B is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>112</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>68</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">180^\\circ-112^\\circ=68^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Opposite cyclic angles B and D are supplementary.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>112</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=112^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>, equal to the stated exterior angle."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-chords-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Two chords in one circle subtend equal minor central angles of $70^\\circ$. Prove their lengths equal and explain why different-radius circles would need qualification. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Each triangle from centre to endpoints has two sides equal to that circle radius.","[1] Equal included central angles and equal pairs of radii establish SAS congruence.","[1] Corresponding chord lengths are equal.","[1] With different radii, the equal-side hypothesis across triangles fails, so equal angle alone does not force equal chord lengths."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Two chords in one circle subtend equal minor central angles of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>70</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">70^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Prove their lengths equal and explain why different-radius circles would need qualification. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Each triangle from centre to endpoints has two sides equal to that circle radius.","[1] Equal included central angles and equal pairs of radii establish SAS congruence.","[1] Corresponding chord lengths are equal.","[1] With different radii, the equal-side hypothesis across triangles fails, so equal angle alone does not force equal chord lengths."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-chords-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A student claims every quadrilateral has supplementary opposite angles because its angle sum is $360^\\circ$. Refute the reasoning with an angle set. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] A $360^\\circ$ total only constrains all four angles together.","[1] Consider consecutive interior angles $70^\\circ,100^\\circ,100^\\circ,90^\\circ$ of a suitable convex quadrilateral; their sum is 360 degrees.","[1] Opposite sums are $70+100=170$ and $100+90=190$, not 180.","[1] Thus the total-angle theorem does not establish supplementary opposite pairs; cyclicity is an additional condition."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A student claims every quadrilateral has supplementary opposite angles because its angle sum is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">360^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Refute the reasoning with an angle set. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] A <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">360^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> total only constrains all four angles together.","[1] Consider consecutive interior angles <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>70</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mn>100</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mn>100</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">70^\\circ,100^\\circ,100^\\circ,90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> of a suitable convex quadrilateral; their sum is 360 degrees.","[1] Opposite sums are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>170</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">70+100=170</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>190</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100+90=190</annotation></semantics></math></span>, not 180.","[1] Thus the total-angle theorem does not establish supplementary opposite pairs; cyclicity is an additional condition."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What additional condition permits the supplementary-opposite-angle theorem?","o":["The quadrilateral is cyclic in the appropriate configuration.","Only that the total angle sum is 360 degrees.","That two sides look horizontal.","That every chord is a diameter."],"a":0,"why":"All four vertices must lie on one circle for the forward theorem.","qHtml":"What additional condition permits the supplementary-opposite-angle theorem?","oHtml":["The quadrilateral is cyclic in the appropriate configuration.","Only that the total angle sum is 360 degrees.","That two sides look horizontal.","That every chord is a diameter."],"whyHtml":"All four vertices must lie on one circle for the forward theorem."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-circle-tangents","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Geometry","title":"Alternate segments and tangent–secant lengths","outcomes":["1.1.11","1.1.13","1.1.14"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Separate tangent facts from secant facts · Use the alternate-segment theorem with a labelled chord · Tangent–secant products use distances from the external point · Justify lengths and converses within their configurations","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Alternate segments and tangent–secant lengths</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Separate tangent facts from secant facts</li><li>Use the alternate-segment theorem with a labelled chord</li><li>Tangent–secant products use distances from the external point</li><li>Justify lengths and converses within their configurations</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate tangent facts from secant facts","text":"A tangent touches the circle at a point and is perpendicular to the radius at that point. A secant passes through two circumference points. In a tangent–chord angle problem, identify the angle between tangent and chord and the circumference angle in the alternate segment. Those angles are equal for the appropriate configuration. The phrase alternate segment refers to which arc and vertex are used, not to a generic parallel-line alternate-angle theorem.","diagram":null,"html":"A tangent touches the circle at a point and is perpendicular to the radius at that point. A secant passes through two circumference points. In a tangent–chord angle problem, identify the angle between tangent and chord and the circumference angle in the alternate segment. Those angles are equal for the appropriate configuration. The phrase alternate segment refers to which arc and vertex are used, not to a generic parallel-line alternate-angle theorem."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use the alternate-segment theorem with a labelled chord","text":"If AT is a chord and a tangent touches at T, the angle between tangent and chord equals a circumference angle subtending chord AT in the relevant opposite segment. Choosing the supplementary tangent direction changes which visible angle is being measured. Use a diagram with the tangent ray labelled and state the circumference vertex. A proof can link the radius–tangent right angle with isosceles radius triangles and the central/circumference angle relationship.","diagram":"circle-tangent","html":"If AT is a chord and a tangent touches at T, the angle between tangent and chord equals a circumference angle subtending chord AT in the relevant opposite segment. Choosing the supplementary tangent direction changes which visible angle is being measured. Use a diagram with the tangent ray labelled and state the circumference vertex. A proof can link the radius–tangent right angle with isosceles radius triangles and the central/circumference angle relationship."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Tangent–secant products use distances from the external point","text":"From an external point M, let a secant meet the circle first at A and then B, with tangent touching at T. Then $MT^2=MA\\times MB$. The factors are the near distance and the full distance from M, not the internal chord AB alone. Since $MB=MA+AB$ in this order, translate the given data before substitution. Squaring a tangent length is necessary; an equation equating one length to a product of two lengths is dimensionally inconsistent.","diagram":null,"html":"From an external point M, let a secant meet the circle first at A and then B, with tangent touching at T. Then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MT^2=MA\\times MB</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The factors are the near distance and the full distance from M, not the internal chord AB alone. Since <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MB=MA+AB</annotation></semantics></math></span> in this order, translate the given data before substitution. Squaring a tangent length is necessary; an equation equating one length to a product of two lengths is dimensionally inconsistent."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Justify lengths and converses within their configurations","text":"Lengths are positive, so a quadratic solution may require discarding a negative root. Similar triangles provide a proof of the tangent–secant product through angle relations and side ratios. Suitable converse statements can identify tangency, but the point order and circle conditions must be checked. Do not assume an arbitrary line is tangent because a picture suggests contact; use a perpendicular radius or another justified condition.","diagram":null,"html":"Lengths are positive, so a quadratic solution may require discarding a negative root. Similar triangles provide a proof of the tangent–secant product through angle relations and side ratios. Suitable converse statements can identify tangency, but the point order and circle conditions must be checked. Do not assume an arbitrary line is tangent because a picture suggests contact; use a perpendicular radius or another justified condition."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-circle-tangents-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"From external M, a tangent has length MT and a secant meets A then B with MA = 4 and AB = 5. Find MT. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Full secant distance is $MB=MA+AB=9$.","[1] Tangent–secant theorem gives $MT^2=MA\\times MB=4\\times9=36$.","[1] Taking the positive root gives $MT=6$.","[1] The internal length 5 is not the second factor; both factors start at M."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"From external M, a tangent has length MT and a secant meets A then B with MA = 4 and AB = 5. Find MT. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Full secant distance is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MB=MA+AB=9</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Tangent–secant theorem gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MT^2=MA\\times MB=4\\times9=36</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Taking the positive root gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MT=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The internal length 5 is not the second factor; both factors start at M."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-tangents-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"A tangent at T and chord TA form a specified angle of $58^\\circ$. B lies in the corresponding alternate segment. Find angle TBA and explain why a parallel-line theorem is unnecessary. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Identify chord TA and the circumference angle TBA subtending it.","[1] Apply the alternate-segment tangent–chord theorem in the stated configuration.","[1] $\\angle TBA=58^\\circ$.","[1] The equality comes from circle geometry, not an assumed pair of parallel lines."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A tangent at T and chord TA form a specified angle of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>58</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">58^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. B lies in the corresponding alternate segment. Find angle TBA and explain why a parallel-line theorem is unnecessary. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Identify chord TA and the circumference angle TBA subtending it.","[1] Apply the alternate-segment tangent–chord theorem in the stated configuration.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∠</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>58</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\angle TBA=58^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The equality comes from circle geometry, not an assumed pair of parallel lines."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-tangents-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"An external tangent has length 12 and the near secant distance is 8. Find the full secant distance and the internal chord length. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $MT^2=MA\\times MB$ gives $144=8MB$.","[1] $MB=18$.","[1] In the order M,A,B, internal chord $AB=MB-MA$.","[1] $AB=18-8=10$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"An external tangent has length 12 and the near secant distance is 8. Find the full secant distance and the internal chord length. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MT^2=MA\\times MB</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>144</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>M</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">144=8MB</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MB=18</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] In the order M,A,B, internal chord <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AB=MB-MA</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AB=18-8=10</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-tangents-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"A circle has radius 5 and external point M is 13 units from its centre O. T is a tangent point. Find MT. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Radius OT is perpendicular to tangent MT.","[1] Triangle OTM is right-angled at T with hypotenuse OM.","[1] $MT^2=OM^2-OT^2=169-25=144$.","[1] $MT=12$ as a positive length."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A circle has radius 5 and external point M is 13 units from its centre O. T is a tangent point. Find MT. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Radius OT is perpendicular to tangent MT.","[1] Triangle OTM is right-angled at T with hypotenuse OM.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>O</mi><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>O</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>169</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>144</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MT^2=OM^2-OT^2=169-25=144</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MT=12</annotation></semantics></math></span> as a positive length."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-tangents-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"From M, a tangent has length 10 and a secant has near distance x and internal chord 15. Find x. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Full distance is $x+15$, so $100=x(x+15)$.","[1] Rearrange as $x^2+15x-100=0=(x+20)(x-5)$.","[1] Roots are $x=-20$ and $x=5$.","[1] A distance must be positive, so $x=5$; the full secant is 20."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"From M, a tangent has length 10 and a secant has near distance x and internal chord 15. Find x. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Full distance is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+15</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100=x(x+15)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Rearrange as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+15x-100=0=(x+20)(x-5)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Roots are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-20</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] A distance must be positive, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=5</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the full secant is 20."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-tangents-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Outline a proof of $MT^2=MA\\times MB$ using triangles MAT and MTB for M,A,B collinear in that order. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Both triangles share the angle at M between the tangent and secant rays.","[1] The tangent–chord theorem gives the corresponding second angle equality, accounting for the chosen configuration.","[1] Thus triangles MAT and MTB are similar with $MA/MT=MT/MB$.","[1] Cross-multiplication gives $MT^2=MA\\times MB$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Outline a proof of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MT^2=MA\\times MB</annotation></semantics></math></span> using triangles MAT and MTB for M,A,B collinear in that order. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Both triangles share the angle at M between the tangent and secant rays.","[1] The tangent–chord theorem gives the corresponding second angle equality, accounting for the chosen configuration.","[1] Thus triangles MAT and MTB are similar with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MA/MT=MT/MB</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Cross-multiplication gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MT^2=MA\\times MB</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which two secant lengths occur in $MT^2=MA\\times MB$?","o":["The near and full distances measured from external point M.","Only the internal chord and the circle radius.","The tangent and the near distance with no square.","Any two visible segments."],"a":0,"why":"Both secant factors start at the external point, so the full length includes the internal chord.","qHtml":"Which two secant lengths occur in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MT^2=MA\\times MB</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","oHtml":["The near and full distances measured from external point M.","Only the internal chord and the circle radius.","The tangent and the near distance with no square.","Any two visible segments."],"whyHtml":"Both secant factors start at the external point, so the full length includes the internal chord."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-circle-intersections","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Geometry","title":"Intersecting chords and multi-step circle proofs","outcomes":["1.1.12","1.1.15"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Identify the actual intersecting segments · Similarity explains the product · Translate totals and solve with positive lengths · Combine circle results in a reasoned order","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Intersecting chords and multi-step circle proofs</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Identify the actual intersecting segments</li><li>Similarity explains the product</li><li>Translate totals and solve with positive lengths</li><li>Combine circle results in a reasoned order</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Identify the actual intersecting segments","text":"If chords AB and CD intersect inside a circle at P, $PA\\times PB=PC\\times PD$. Each product uses the two parts of one chord. They need not be diameters or intersect perpendicularly. Label the four endpoints and the interior intersection; a guessed multiplication of adjacent pieces can pair the wrong lengths. The equality is an exact circle theorem rather than a statement that the two complete chord lengths must be equal.","diagram":null,"html":"If chords AB and CD intersect inside a circle at P, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">PA\\times PB=PC\\times PD</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Each product uses the two parts of one chord. They need not be diameters or intersect perpendicularly. Label the four endpoints and the interior intersection; a guessed multiplication of adjacent pieces can pair the wrong lengths. The equality is an exact circle theorem rather than a statement that the two complete chord lengths must be equal."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Similarity explains the product","text":"Use triangles APC and DPB. The angles at P are vertically opposite, and appropriate circumference angles intercept the same arcs. Similarity gives a ratio such as $PA/PD=PC/PB$, and cross-multiplication produces the intersecting-chord product. Establishing the angle correspondence is part of the proof; a triangle sketch with similar-looking shapes is insufficient. Keep vertex order consistent so the side ratio has the intended matching pairs.","diagram":"circle-products","html":"Use triangles APC and DPB. The angles at P are vertically opposite, and appropriate circumference angles intercept the same arcs. Similarity gives a ratio such as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">PA/PD=PC/PB</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and cross-multiplication produces the intersecting-chord product. Establishing the angle correspondence is part of the proof; a triangle sketch with similar-looking shapes is insufficient. Keep vertex order consistent so the side ratio has the intended matching pairs."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Translate totals and solve with positive lengths","text":"If a complete chord is given, a segment can be total minus the other segment. For example, if AB = 13 and AP = x then PB = 13-x, and both must be positive, imposing $0<x<13$. A quadratic can give two valid positions related by exchanging endpoints. State what the question requests: a named segment, both possible positions or an unordered pair. An algebraic root outside the geometric bounds must be excluded.","diagram":null,"html":"If a complete chord is given, a segment can be total minus the other segment. For example, if AB = 13 and AP = x then PB = 13-x, and both must be positive, imposing <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;x&lt;13</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A quadratic can give two valid positions related by exchanging endpoints. State what the question requests: a named segment, both possible positions or an unordered pair. An algebraic root outside the geometric bounds must be excluded."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Combine circle results in a reasoned order","text":"A multi-step task may require angle facts to establish similarity, then a length product, then Pythagoras in a separately justified right triangle. List the conditions before each theorem. Suitable converses can establish concyclicity, but require the stated configuration. The product has units of length squared, which offers a quick consistency check. Do not interpret equal chord products as proving equal individual segments unless further symmetry is given.","diagram":null,"html":"A multi-step task may require angle facts to establish similarity, then a length product, then Pythagoras in a separately justified right triangle. List the conditions before each theorem. Suitable converses can establish concyclicity, but require the stated configuration. The product has units of length squared, which offers a quick consistency check. Do not interpret equal chord products as proving equal individual segments unless further symmetry is given."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-circle-intersections-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Chords AB and CD meet at P with PA = 3, PB = 8 and PC = 4. Find PD. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Intersecting-chord theorem gives $PA\\times PB=PC\\times PD$.","[1] Substitute $3\\times8=4PD$.","[1] $PD=24/4=6$.","[1] The result pairs pieces of the same chord, and is a positive length."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Chords AB and CD meet at P with PA = 3, PB = 8 and PC = 4. Find PD. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Intersecting-chord theorem gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">PA\\times PB=PC\\times PD</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Substitute <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\times8=4PD</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">PD=24/4=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The result pairs pieces of the same chord, and is a positive length."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-intersections-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"A chord of total length 13 is cut into x and 13-x by another chord whose segments are 4 and 9. Find the possible lengths x. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $x(13-x)=4\\times9=36$ with $0<x<13$.","[1] Rearrange as $x^2-13x+36=0$.","[1] Factor $(x-4)(x-9)=0$.","[1] Both $x=4$ and $x=9$ satisfy the geometric bounds; they exchange the named endpoint distances."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A chord of total length 13 is cut into x and 13-x by another chord whose segments are 4 and 9. Find the possible lengths x. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(13-x)=4\\times9=36</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;x&lt;13</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Rearrange as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>36</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2-13x+36=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Factor <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-4)(x-9)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=4</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=9</annotation></semantics></math></span> satisfy the geometric bounds; they exchange the named endpoint distances."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-intersections-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Two chords intersect with segment pairs 2 and 12, and 6 and y. Find y and decide whether the complete chords have equal length. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Products satisfy $2\\times12=6y$.","[1] $y=4$.","[1] Complete lengths are $2+12=14$ and $6+4=10$.","[1] They are not equal; equal products do not imply equal sums."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Two chords intersect with segment pairs 2 and 12, and 6 and y. Find y and decide whether the complete chords have equal length. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Products satisfy <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times12=6y</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Complete lengths are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+12=14</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6+4=10</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] They are not equal; equal products do not imply equal sums."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-intersections-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"An intersection P bisects both chords, whose half-lengths are a and b. Prove the chords have equal length. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The segment pairs are a,a and b,b with positive lengths.","[1] The chord theorem gives $a^2=b^2$.","[1] Since $a,b>0$, $a=b$.","[1] Complete chord lengths are $2a$ and $2b$, hence equal."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"An intersection P bisects both chords, whose half-lengths are a and b. Prove the chords have equal length. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The segment pairs are a,a and b,b with positive lengths.","[1] The chord theorem gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^2=b^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Since <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=b</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Complete chord lengths are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2a</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2b</annotation></semantics></math></span>, hence equal."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-intersections-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"For chords AB and CD intersecting at P, justify the similarity of triangles APC and DPB and derive the chord product. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Angles APC and DPB are vertically opposite.","[1] Suitable angles at C and B subtend the same arc AD, giving a second equality.","[1] AA similarity gives $PA/PD=PC/PB$ with corresponding sides.","[1] Cross-multiplication yields $PA\\times PB=PC\\times PD$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For chords AB and CD intersecting at P, justify the similarity of triangles APC and DPB and derive the chord product. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Angles APC and DPB are vertically opposite.","[1] Suitable angles at C and B subtend the same arc AD, giving a second equality.","[1] AA similarity gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">PA/PD=PC/PB</annotation></semantics></math></span> with corresponding sides.","[1] Cross-multiplication yields <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">PA\\times PB=PC\\times PD</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-circle-intersections-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A student uses $PA+PB=PC+PD$ instead of the chord product. Give a numerical circle-compatible counterexample and state the correct relationship. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Segment pairs 3,8 and 4,6 have equal products 24.","[1] Their sums are 11 and 10, so the proposed equality fails.","[1] Such product-compatible interior chord lengths satisfy the intersecting-chord relationship.","[1] The required theorem is $PA\\times PB=PC\\times PD$, not equality of chord sums."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"A student uses <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">PA+PB=PC+PD</annotation></semantics></math></span> instead of the chord product. Give a numerical circle-compatible counterexample and state the correct relationship. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Segment pairs 3,8 and 4,6 have equal products 24.","[1] Their sums are 11 and 10, so the proposed equality fails.","[1] Such product-compatible interior chord lengths satisfy the intersecting-chord relationship.","[1] The required theorem is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">PA\\times PB=PC\\times PD</annotation></semantics></math></span>, not equality of chord sums."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What geometric bound applies when a chord of length 13 is divided into x and 13-x?","o":["$0<x<13$.","$x$ can be any real number.","Only $x>13$ is allowed.","The two lengths must always be equal."],"a":0,"why":"Both segments must be positive, so the total imposes upper and lower bounds.","qHtml":"What geometric bound applies when a chord of length 13 is divided into x and 13-x?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;x&lt;13</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span> can be any real number.","Only <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;13</annotation></semantics></math></span> is allowed.","The two lengths must always be equal."],"whyHtml":"Both segments must be positive, so the total imposes upper and lower bounds."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-counting","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Combinatorics","title":"Addition, multiplication and factorial counting","outcomes":["1.2.1","1.2.2","1.2.3"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Choose whether cases add or stages multiply · Order determines the counting object · Factorials encode full arrangements · Check by a small case and then use the general model","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Addition, multiplication and factorial counting</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Choose whether cases add or stages multiply</li><li>Order determines the counting object</li><li>Factorials encode full arrangements</li><li>Check by a small case and then use the general model</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Choose whether cases add or stages multiply","text":"For mutually exclusive alternatives, add the number of possibilities in each case. For a sequence of choices, multiply the number available at each stage when the stage counts correctly reflect previous choices. Independence in a probability sense is not required for every multiplication count: without-replacement choices can have stage counts 5,4,3. Explain the structure rather than applying a memorised operation to every list of numbers. Overlapping alternatives need correction, treated explicitly in inclusion–exclusion.","diagram":null,"html":"For mutually exclusive alternatives, add the number of possibilities in each case. For a sequence of choices, multiply the number available at each stage when the stage counts correctly reflect previous choices. Independence in a probability sense is not required for every multiplication count: without-replacement choices can have stage counts 5,4,3. Explain the structure rather than applying a memorised operation to every list of numbers. Overlapping alternatives need correction, treated explicitly in inclusion–exclusion."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Order determines the counting object","text":"A permutation is an ordered arrangement or assignment. Selecting a president and secretary differs from selecting an unordered two-person committee because swapping roles produces a different outcome. Start by describing one complete outcome and whether swapping entries changes it. A count can be wrong before any arithmetic if the objects, positions, replacement policy or leading-zero restriction are not specified. Include those assumptions in the mathematical model.","diagram":"counting","html":"A permutation is an ordered arrangement or assignment. Selecting a president and secretary differs from selecting an unordered two-person committee because swapping roles produces a different outcome. Start by describing one complete outcome and whether swapping entries changes it. A count can be wrong before any arithmetic if the objects, positions, replacement policy or leading-zero restriction are not specified. Include those assumptions in the mathematical model."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Factorials encode full arrangements","text":"$n!=n(n-1)\\cdots2\\cdot1$ counts arrangements of n distinct objects into n distinct positions. Define $0!=1$, consistent with one empty arrangement and the factorial identities. Ordered selection of r distinct objects without replacement from n has $^nP_r=n!/(n-r)!$. The factorial ratio cancels unused trailing factors. This formula assumes the selected objects are distinct; repeated indistinguishable objects require a different denominator.","diagram":null,"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n!=n(n-1)\\cdots2\\cdot1</annotation></semantics></math></span> counts arrangements of n distinct objects into n distinct positions. Define <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0!=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, consistent with one empty arrangement and the factorial identities. Ordered selection of r distinct objects without replacement from n has <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^nP_r=n!/(n-r)!</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The factorial ratio cancels unused trailing factors. This formula assumes the selected objects are distinct; repeated indistinguishable objects require a different denominator."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Check by a small case and then use the general model","text":"For three distinct letters, list the six orders to see why $3!=6$. A small list is a useful diagnostic but not a proof of every more complicated formula. Show the stage counts in a restricted problem before evaluating. If repetition is allowed, each stage may retain the same number of choices; if it is forbidden, the counts decrease. Large factorial expressions can be simplified exactly before calculator evaluation so intermediate overflow or rounding does not obscure the count.","diagram":null,"html":"For three distinct letters, list the six orders to see why <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3!=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A small list is a useful diagnostic but not a proof of every more complicated formula. Show the stage counts in a restricted problem before evaluating. If repetition is allowed, each stage may retain the same number of choices; if it is forbidden, the counts decrease. Large factorial expressions can be simplified exactly before calculator evaluation so intermediate overflow or rounding does not obscure the count."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-counting-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"A code consists of one of 3 letters followed by one of 4 digits. Repetition rules do not affect these different positions. Count the codes. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] One complete outcome contains a letter choice and a digit choice.","[1] There are 3 possibilities for the first stage and 4 for the second.","[1] Multiplication principle gives $3\\times4=12$.","[1] Each letter–digit pair is counted once."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A code consists of one of 3 letters followed by one of 4 digits. Repetition rules do not affect these different positions. Count the codes. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] One complete outcome contains a letter choice and a digit choice.","[1] There are 3 possibilities for the first stage and 4 for the second.","[1] Multiplication principle gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\times4=12</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Each letter–digit pair is counted once."]},{"id":"mas11-counting-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Assign president, secretary and treasurer from 7 distinct people, with no person holding two roles. Count assignments. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Roles are distinct, so order/assignment matters.","[1] Available choices are 7, then 6, then 5.","[1] $^7P_3=7\\times6\\times5=210$.","[1] An unordered combination would not distinguish the roles and would undercount."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Assign president, secretary and treasurer from 7 distinct people, with no person holding two roles. Count assignments. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Roles are distinct, so order/assignment matters.","[1] Available choices are 7, then 6, then 5.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>7</mn></msup><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>210</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^7P_3=7\\times6\\times5=210</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] An unordered combination would not distinguish the roles and would undercount."]},{"id":"mas11-counting-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Count three-digit numbers formed from digits 0,1,2,3,4 without repeating a digit. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The first digit cannot be zero, so it has 4 choices.","[1] After that, 4 digits remain for the second position, including zero when unused.","[1] There are 3 choices for the last position.","[1] Count $=4\\times4\\times3=48$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Count three-digit numbers formed from digits 0,1,2,3,4 without repeating a digit. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The first digit cannot be zero, so it has 4 choices.","[1] After that, 4 digits remain for the second position, including zero when unused.","[1] There are 3 choices for the last position.","[1] Count <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=4\\times4\\times3=48</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-counting-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Count four-character digit strings using 0–9 with repetition allowed and leading zero permitted. Compare with four-digit numbers under the same repetition rule. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] A string permits 10 choices at each of four positions.","[1] Strings: $10^4=10000$.","[1] A four-digit number has 9 nonzero choices first and 10 thereafter.","[1] Numbers: $9\\times10^3=9000$; the object definition changes the count."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Count four-character digit strings using 0–9 with repetition allowed and leading zero permitted. Compare with four-digit numbers under the same repetition rule. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] A string permits 10 choices at each of four positions.","[1] Strings: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>10000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^4=10000</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] A four-digit number has 9 nonzero choices first and 10 thereafter.","[1] Numbers: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9\\times10^3=9000</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the object definition changes the count."]},{"id":"mas11-counting-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"A student chooses either one of 6 science projects or one of 5 unrelated mathematics projects, with no overlap. Count choices and explain why multiplication is wrong. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The alternatives are mutually exclusive and exactly one project is selected.","[1] Addition gives $6+5=11$ choices.","[1] Multiplication $6\\times5$ would describe choosing one from each category.","[1] That is a different outcome and does not model the stated task."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A student chooses either one of 6 science projects or one of 5 unrelated mathematics projects, with no overlap. Count choices and explain why multiplication is wrong. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The alternatives are mutually exclusive and exactly one project is selected.","[1] Addition gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6+5=11</annotation></semantics></math></span> choices.","[1] Multiplication <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\times5</annotation></semantics></math></span> would describe choosing one from each category.","[1] That is a different outcome and does not model the stated task."]},{"id":"mas11-counting-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Simplify $8!/5!$ and interpret it as a count of ordered selections. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Cancel $5!$ from numerator and denominator.","[1] $8!/5!=8\\times7\\times6=336$.","[1] This is $^8P_3$, ordered selection of 3 distinct objects from 8 without replacement.","[1] State distinct objects and ordered positions so the interpretation matches the formula."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Simplify <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8!/5!</annotation></semantics></math></span> and interpret it as a count of ordered selections. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Cancel <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5!</annotation></semantics></math></span> from numerator and denominator.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>336</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8!/5!=8\\times7\\times6=336</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>8</mn></msup><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^8P_3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, ordered selection of 3 distinct objects from 8 without replacement.","[1] State distinct objects and ordered positions so the interpretation matches the formula."]}],"bank":[{"q":"When do mutually exclusive alternatives use addition?","o":["When exactly one of the nonoverlapping alternatives is chosen.","Whenever all alternatives are selected in sequence.","Whenever alternatives overlap with no correction.","Only when every category has equal size."],"a":0,"why":"Nonoverlapping cases partition the outcomes, so their counts add.","qHtml":"When do mutually exclusive alternatives use addition?","oHtml":["When exactly one of the nonoverlapping alternatives is chosen.","Whenever all alternatives are selected in sequence.","Whenever alternatives overlap with no correction.","Only when every category has equal size."],"whyHtml":"Nonoverlapping cases partition the outcomes, so their counts add."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-restricted-orders","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Combinatorics","title":"Restricted permutations and block methods","outcomes":["1.2.1","1.2.4"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Treat adjacent objects as a block when required · Use the complement for a forbidden pattern · Use gaps for separated groups · Keep position labels and object identities clear","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Restricted permutations and block methods</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Treat adjacent objects as a block when required</li><li>Use the complement for a forbidden pattern</li><li>Use gaps for separated groups</li><li>Keep position labels and object identities clear</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Treat adjacent objects as a block when required","text":"If two specified distinct objects must remain adjacent in a linear arrangement, treat them as one block for the outer arrangement and multiply by their internal orders. With n distinct objects, the block reduces the outer count to n-1 units and the two objects can exchange positions. If their order is fixed, that internal factor changes. A block method is justified only when the required adjacency is genuinely preserved and each valid arrangement corresponds uniquely to the block description.","diagram":null,"html":"If two specified distinct objects must remain adjacent in a linear arrangement, treat them as one block for the outer arrangement and multiply by their internal orders. With n distinct objects, the block reduces the outer count to n-1 units and the two objects can exchange positions. If their order is fixed, that internal factor changes. A block method is justified only when the required adjacency is genuinely preserved and each valid arrangement corresponds uniquely to the block description."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use the complement for a forbidden pattern","text":"If two specified objects must not be adjacent, count all arrangements and subtract adjacent arrangements. This often avoids splitting many positions into overlapping cases. Verify that the subtracted set is exactly the forbidden set. When several restrictions occur together, subtracting each forbidden count independently can remove intersections twice, so inclusion–exclusion or a more careful construction may be needed.","diagram":"counting","html":"If two specified objects must not be adjacent, count all arrangements and subtract adjacent arrangements. This often avoids splitting many positions into overlapping cases. Verify that the subtracted set is exactly the forbidden set. When several restrictions occur together, subtracting each forbidden count independently can remove intersections twice, so inclusion–exclusion or a more careful construction may be needed."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use gaps for separated groups","text":"Arrange a base group first, then choose gaps before, between or after its members to place another group so its members cannot touch. The number of available gaps and whether multiple placements in a gap are allowed must match the restriction. Count which gaps are used and the order of distinct objects within them. Do not assume that keeping every other position empty models all valid arrangements; end gaps can matter.","diagram":null,"html":"Arrange a base group first, then choose gaps before, between or after its members to place another group so its members cannot touch. The number of available gaps and whether multiple placements in a gap are allowed must match the restriction. Count which gaps are used and the order of distinct objects within them. Do not assume that keeping every other position empty models all valid arrangements; end gaps can matter."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Keep position labels and object identities clear","text":"A fixed first object leaves the remaining objects to permute; several fixed positions remove corresponding objects and positions. Restrictions such as vowels together, exactly one person between two others or a particular object at an end require different models. Draw labelled positions or a block/gap sketch, write the factor interpretation and evaluate last. For line arrangements, reversing the order usually gives a different arrangement; circular symmetry needs a separately stated convention.","diagram":null,"html":"A fixed first object leaves the remaining objects to permute; several fixed positions remove corresponding objects and positions. Restrictions such as vowels together, exactly one person between two others or a particular object at an end require different models. Draw labelled positions or a block/gap sketch, write the factor interpretation and evaluate last. For line arrangements, reversing the order usually gives a different arrangement; circular symmetry needs a separately stated convention."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-restricted-orders-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Six distinct books are arranged in a line. Books A and B must be adjacent, in either order. Count arrangements. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Treat A and B as one block alongside the other four books.","[1] There are five distinct outer units, giving $5!$ orders.","[1] The block has AB or BA, giving 2 internal orders.","[1] Count $=2\\times5!=240$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Six distinct books are arranged in a line. Books A and B must be adjacent, in either order. Count arrangements. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Treat A and B as one block alongside the other four books.","[1] There are five distinct outer units, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5!</annotation></semantics></math></span> orders.","[1] The block has AB or BA, giving 2 internal orders.","[1] Count <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>240</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=2\\times5!=240</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-restricted-orders-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Using six distinct books, count arrangements in which A and B are not adjacent. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] All line arrangements number $6!=720$.","[1] Adjacent arrangements number $2\\times5!=240$.","[1] The complement subtracts exactly those forbidden arrangements.","[1] Valid count $=720-240=480$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Using six distinct books, count arrangements in which A and B are not adjacent. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] All line arrangements number <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>720</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6!=720</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Adjacent arrangements number <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>240</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times5!=240</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The complement subtracts exactly those forbidden arrangements.","[1] Valid count <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>720</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>240</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>480</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=720-240=480</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-restricted-orders-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Five distinct people stand in a line, with P fixed at the left end and Q fixed at the right end. Count arrangements. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Two specified objects occupy two fixed positions.","[1] Three distinct people remain for three middle positions.","[1] Their orders number $3!$.","[1] Count $=6$; the fixed people supply no additional permutation factor."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Five distinct people stand in a line, with P fixed at the left end and Q fixed at the right end. Count arrangements. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Two specified objects occupy two fixed positions.","[1] Three distinct people remain for three middle positions.","[1] Their orders number <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3!</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Count <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the fixed people supply no additional permutation factor."]},{"id":"mas11-restricted-orders-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Four distinct consonant cards are arranged with three distinct vowel cards so no two vowels are adjacent. Count linear arrangements. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Arrange the consonants in $4!$ orders.","[1] They create five gaps, including both ends; choose 3 different gaps in $\\binom53$ ways.","[1] Assign the distinct vowels to selected gaps in $3!$ orders.","[1] Count $=4!\\binom53 3!=24\\times10\\times6=1440$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Four distinct consonant cards are arranged with three distinct vowel cards so no two vowels are adjacent. Count linear arrangements. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Arrange the consonants in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4!</annotation></semantics></math></span> orders.","[1] They create five gaps, including both ends; choose 3 different gaps in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>5</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom53</annotation></semantics></math></span> ways.","[1] Assign the distinct vowels to selected gaps in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3!</annotation></semantics></math></span> orders.","[1] Count <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>5</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1440</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=4!\\binom53 3!=24\\times10\\times6=1440</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-restricted-orders-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Seven distinct runners line up. A,B,C must be consecutive in that fixed internal order. Count arrangements. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Treat ABC as one block, with four other runners.","[1] Five outer units have $5!$ arrangements.","[1] The internal order is fixed, so its factor is 1 rather than $3!$.","[1] Count $=120$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Seven distinct runners line up. A,B,C must be consecutive in that fixed internal order. Count arrangements. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Treat ABC as one block, with four other runners.","[1] Five outer units have <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5!</annotation></semantics></math></span> arrangements.","[1] The internal order is fixed, so its factor is 1 rather than <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3!</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Count <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>120</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=120</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-restricted-orders-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Six distinct people line up. A and B must occupy opposite ends, in either end assignment. Count arrangements. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Choose A-left/B-right or B-left/A-right: 2 assignments.","[1] Four other distinct people fill the four middle positions.","[1] Their orders number $4!$.","[1] Count $=2\\times24=48$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Six distinct people line up. A and B must occupy opposite ends, in either end assignment. Count arrangements. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Choose A-left/B-right or B-left/A-right: 2 assignments.","[1] Four other distinct people fill the four middle positions.","[1] Their orders number <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4!</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Count <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=2\\times24=48</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Why is the internal factor 2 used for an adjacent block of two distinct objects?","o":["They can occur in either order inside the block.","The entire line must be divided by two.","Each object is indistinguishable.","The block always occupies two fixed positions."],"a":0,"why":"The outer arrangement treats the block as one unit; its two internal orders remain distinct.","qHtml":"Why is the internal factor 2 used for an adjacent block of two distinct objects?","oHtml":["They can occur in either order inside the block.","The entire line must be divided by two.","Each object is indistinguishable.","The block always occupies two fixed positions."],"whyHtml":"The outer arrangement treats the block as one unit; its two internal orders remain distinct."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-repeated-orders","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Combinatorics","title":"Repeated objects and avoiding duplicate counts","outcomes":["1.2.4"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Indistinguishable objects change what counts as new · Count repeated groups separately · Restrictions must preserve the identical-object treatment · Do not apply the full-arrangement denominator blindly to a partial choice","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Repeated objects and avoiding duplicate counts</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Indistinguishable objects change what counts as new</li><li>Count repeated groups separately</li><li>Restrictions must preserve the identical-object treatment</li><li>Do not apply the full-arrangement denominator blindly to a partial choice</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Indistinguishable objects change what counts as new","text":"If identical letters exchange places, the visible arrangement does not change. Counting them as distinct first gives too many labelled arrangements. For n objects with repeated-group sizes $r_1,r_2,\\ldots$, divide $n!$ by $r_1!r_2!\\cdots$ because each visible arrangement has that many internal relabellings. This reasoning assumes all n positions are used and the repeated groups are genuinely indistinguishable. Different people with the same initial are still distinct unless the outcome definition says otherwise.","diagram":null,"html":"If identical letters exchange places, the visible arrangement does not change. Counting them as distinct first gives too many labelled arrangements. For n objects with repeated-group sizes <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_1,r_2,\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span>, divide <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n!</annotation></semantics></math></span> by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo>⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_1!r_2!\\cdots</annotation></semantics></math></span> because each visible arrangement has that many internal relabellings. This reasoning assumes all n positions are used and the repeated groups are genuinely indistinguishable. Different people with the same initial are still distinct unless the outcome definition says otherwise."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Count repeated groups separately","text":"In BANANA there are three As, two Ns and one B, so the count is $6!/(3!2!)$. Do not divide by the total number of repeated letters or by one factorial combining unrelated groups. An equivalent construction chooses positions for one repeated group, then positions for the next, leaving the final group automatic. Both methods describe the same visible arrangements and can provide an independent check.","diagram":"counting","html":"In BANANA there are three As, two Ns and one B, so the count is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6!/(3!2!)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Do not divide by the total number of repeated letters or by one factorial combining unrelated groups. An equivalent construction chooses positions for one repeated group, then positions for the next, leaving the final group automatic. Both methods describe the same visible arrangements and can provide an independent check."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Restrictions must preserve the identical-object treatment","text":"If repeated As must be together, they form one block with only one internal visible order, unlike three distinct letters with $3!$ internal orders. If two distinct objects must be adjacent within a repeated-letter word, treat their block as one unit while still dividing for the other identical groups. Carefully decide whether the restriction distinguishes an occurrence; labelling one of several identical As changes the counting object.","diagram":null,"html":"If repeated As must be together, they form one block with only one internal visible order, unlike three distinct letters with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3!</annotation></semantics></math></span> internal orders. If two distinct objects must be adjacent within a repeated-letter word, treat their block as one unit while still dividing for the other identical groups. Carefully decide whether the restriction distinguishes an occurrence; labelling one of several identical As changes the counting object."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Do not apply the full-arrangement denominator blindly to a partial choice","text":"For selecting only some letters from a repeated inventory, the multiplicities included can vary. A single $n!/(r_1!r_2!)$ for the entire inventory may not model a shorter word. Split into feasible composition cases, count each case and add if disjoint. Small examples help expose duplicate counting. The solution should state the word length, available counts and whether all objects are used before its formula.","diagram":null,"html":"For selecting only some letters from a repeated inventory, the multiplicities included can vary. A single <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n!/(r_1!r_2!)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for the entire inventory may not model a shorter word. Split into feasible composition cases, count each case and add if disjoint. Small examples help expose duplicate counting. The solution should state the word length, available counts and whether all objects are used before its formula."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-repeated-orders-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Count distinct arrangements of the letters BANANA. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] There are 6 letters with A repeated 3 times and N repeated 2 times.","[1] Distinct visible arrangements number $6!/(3!2!)$.","[1] Evaluate $720/(6\\times2)=60$.","[1] Permuting identical As or Ns does not create new words."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Count distinct arrangements of the letters BANANA. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] There are 6 letters with A repeated 3 times and N repeated 2 times.","[1] Distinct visible arrangements number <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6!/(3!2!)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>720</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">720/(6\\times2)=60</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Permuting identical As or Ns does not create new words."]},{"id":"mas11-repeated-orders-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Count distinct arrangements of MISSISSIPPI. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] There are 11 letters: M once, I four times, S four times and P twice.","[1] The count is $11!/(4!4!2!)$.","[1] $39916800/(24\\times24\\times2)=34650$.","[1] Each repeated group contributes its own factorial denominator."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Count distinct arrangements of MISSISSIPPI. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] There are 11 letters: M once, I four times, S four times and P twice.","[1] The count is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">11!/(4!4!2!)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>39916800</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>34650</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">39916800/(24\\times24\\times2)=34650</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Each repeated group contributes its own factorial denominator."]},{"id":"mas11-repeated-orders-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Count arrangements of AABBC, using all letters. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Five positions contain two identical As, two identical Bs and one C.","[1] Count $=5!/(2!2!)$.","[1] Evaluate $120/4=30$.","[1] A denominator $4!$ would incorrectly combine the distinct repeated groups."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Count arrangements of AABBC, using all letters. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Five positions contain two identical As, two identical Bs and one C.","[1] Count <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=5!/(2!2!)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>120</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">120/4=30</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] A denominator <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4!</annotation></semantics></math></span> would incorrectly combine the distinct repeated groups."]},{"id":"mas11-repeated-orders-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"In BANANA, require all three As to be consecutive. Count distinct arrangements. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Treat AAA as one block with one visible internal order.","[1] Outer units are AAA, B, N, N: four units with two identical Ns.","[1] Count $=4!/2!$.","[1] The answer is 12; no extra $3!$ multiplies the identical As."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"In BANANA, require all three As to be consecutive. Count distinct arrangements. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Treat AAA as one block with one visible internal order.","[1] Outer units are AAA, B, N, N: four units with two identical Ns.","[1] Count <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=4!/2!</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The answer is 12; no extra <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3!</annotation></semantics></math></span> multiplies the identical As."]},{"id":"mas11-repeated-orders-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Count arrangements of AABC in which B and C are adjacent in either order. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Treat BC or CB as one block.","[1] Outer units are the block, A, A, giving $3!/2!=3$ arrangements.","[1] The distinct B and C have 2 internal orders.","[1] Total $=3\\times2=6$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Count arrangements of AABC in which B and C are adjacent in either order. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Treat BC or CB as one block.","[1] Outer units are the block, A, A, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3!/2!=3</annotation></semantics></math></span> arrangements.","[1] The distinct B and C have 2 internal orders.","[1] Total <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=3\\times2=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-repeated-orders-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Form a two-letter string from the inventory AAB without using a letter more often than available. Count distinct strings by cases. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Two As gives only AA.","[1] One A and one B gives AB or BA.","[1] Two Bs is impossible because only one B is available.","[1] The disjoint possibilities total 3; a full three-letter factorial formula does not answer the shorter-string question."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Form a two-letter string from the inventory AAB without using a letter more often than available. Count distinct strings by cases. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Two As gives only AA.","[1] One A and one B gives AB or BA.","[1] Two Bs is impossible because only one B is available.","[1] The disjoint possibilities total 3; a full three-letter factorial formula does not answer the shorter-string question."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Why divide by $3!$ for three indistinguishable As in a complete arrangement?","o":["Their internal relabellings leave the visible arrangement unchanged.","Every word must have length three.","The three As are three different colours.","All positions are unordered committees."],"a":0,"why":"Each visible arrangement would be counted 3! times if the identical As were artificially labelled.","qHtml":"Why divide by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3!</annotation></semantics></math></span> for three indistinguishable As in a complete arrangement?","oHtml":["Their internal relabellings leave the visible arrangement unchanged.","Every word must have length three.","The three As are three different colours.","All positions are unordered committees."],"whyHtml":"Each visible arrangement would be counted 3! times if the identical As were artificially labelled."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-inclusion-exclusion","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Combinatorics","title":"Two-set and three-set inclusion–exclusion","outcomes":["1.2.5"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Overlapping alternatives are not disjoint · Three sets need the triple intersection restored · Exactly one and exactly two require region accounting · Check the universe and feasibility","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Two-set and three-set inclusion–exclusion</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Overlapping alternatives are not disjoint</li><li>Three sets need the triple intersection restored</li><li>Exactly one and exactly two require region accounting</li><li>Check the universe and feasibility</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Overlapping alternatives are not disjoint","text":"For two finite sets, $|A\\cup B|=|A|+|B|-|A\\cap B|$. Elements in both sets enter each individual count, so subtraction removes the duplicate. The intersection is an inclusive count: it can contain objects also belonging to another set when more sets are present. Translate exactly what the question asks: at least one, both, neither and exactly one are different regions. Draw and label a Venn diagram rather than assuming ordinary and means only two categories.","diagram":null,"html":"For two finite sets, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|A\\cup B|=|A|+|B|-|A\\cap B|</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Elements in both sets enter each individual count, so subtraction removes the duplicate. The intersection is an inclusive count: it can contain objects also belonging to another set when more sets are present. Translate exactly what the question asks: at least one, both, neither and exactly one are different regions. Draw and label a Venn diagram rather than assuming ordinary and means only two categories."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Three sets need the triple intersection restored","text":"For three sets, add single counts, subtract the three pairwise intersections and add the triple intersection. A triple member is initially counted three times and then removed three times, so it must be restored once. Pairwise totals normally include triple members unless explicitly described as exactly those two. Using inclusive and exclusive intersection values interchangeably is a frequent error. Fill the central region first when reconstructing individual regions.","diagram":"venn","html":"For three sets, add single counts, subtract the three pairwise intersections and add the triple intersection. A triple member is initially counted three times and then removed three times, so it must be restored once. Pairwise totals normally include triple members unless explicitly described as exactly those two. Using inclusive and exclusive intersection values interchangeably is a frequent error. Fill the central region first when reconstructing individual regions."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Exactly one and exactly two require region accounting","text":"For A-only, subtract its pairwise overlaps and restore the triple once: $|A\\text{ only}|=|A|-|A\\cap B|-|A\\cap C|+|A\\cap B\\cap C|$. Exactly two equals the sum of pairwise intersections minus three times the triple count. At least two equals that pairwise sum minus twice the triple count. These formulae follow from how many times a triple member is counted, not from interchangeable memorised expressions.","diagram":null,"html":"For A-only, subtract its pairwise overlaps and restore the triple once: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mtext> only</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|A\\text{ only}|=|A|-|A\\cap B|-|A\\cap C|+|A\\cap B\\cap C|</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Exactly two equals the sum of pairwise intersections minus three times the triple count. At least two equals that pairwise sum minus twice the triple count. These formulae follow from how many times a triple member is counted, not from interchangeable memorised expressions."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Check the universe and feasibility","text":"Neither is universe size minus the union, so the stated universe must include every counted category. Negative regions or a union larger than the universe can indicate inconsistent data or a translation error. Counts are integers and intersections cannot exceed either parent set. A reliable solution labels whether intersections are inclusive, shows substitutions and checks the total of all disjoint regions against the universe.","diagram":null,"html":"Neither is universe size minus the union, so the stated universe must include every counted category. Negative regions or a union larger than the universe can indicate inconsistent data or a translation error. Counts are integers and intersections cannot exceed either parent set. A reliable solution labels whether intersections are inclusive, shows substitutions and checks the total of all disjoint regions against the universe."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-inclusion-exclusion-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Among 40 students, 22 study A, 18 study B and 9 study both. Find the number studying at least one and neither. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The intersection is counted in both single totals.","[1] $|A\\cup B|=22+18-9=31$.","[1] Neither $=40-31$.","[1] Thus 31 study at least one and 9 neither."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Among 40 students, 22 study A, 18 study B and 9 study both. Find the number studying at least one and neither. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The intersection is counted in both single totals.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>31</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|A\\cup B|=22+18-9=31</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Neither <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>31</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=40-31</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus 31 study at least one and 9 neither."]},{"id":"mas11-inclusion-exclusion-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"In a universe of 100, three sets have sizes 40,35,30; pairwise inclusive intersections are 12,10,8 and triple intersection is 4. Find the union and neither. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Apply $|A\\cup B\\cup C|=40+35+30-12-10-8+4$.","[1] The union is 79.","[1] The triple is restored because subtracting all pairwise overlaps removed it too often.","[1] Neither $=100-79=21$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"In a universe of 100, three sets have sizes 40,35,30; pairwise inclusive intersections are 12,10,8 and triple intersection is 4. Find the union and neither. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Apply <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>35</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|A\\cup B\\cup C|=40+35+30-12-10-8+4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The union is 79.","[1] The triple is restored because subtracting all pairwise overlaps removed it too often.","[1] Neither <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>79</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=100-79=21</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-inclusion-exclusion-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Using the three-set data 40,35,30; pair intersections 12,10,8; triple 4, find exactly two and at least two. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Sum of pairwise intersections is $12+10+8=30$.","[1] Exactly two excludes all three counts of triple members: $30-3(4)=18$.","[1] At least two retains each triple member once: $30-2(4)=22$.","[1] The different requested regions explain the different subtraction factors."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Using the three-set data 40,35,30; pair intersections 12,10,8; triple 4, find exactly two and at least two. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Sum of pairwise intersections is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12+10+8=30</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Exactly two excludes all three counts of triple members: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30-3(4)=18</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] At least two retains each triple member once: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30-2(4)=22</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The different requested regions explain the different subtraction factors."]},{"id":"mas11-inclusion-exclusion-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"For the same three-set data, with AB = 12, AC = 10 and ABC = 4, find A only. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Start with $|A|=40$.","[1] Remove inclusive AB and AC, then restore their common triple region once.","[1] A only $=40-12-10+4=22$.","[1] The +4 corrects double subtraction of triple members."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For the same three-set data, with AB = 12, AC = 10 and ABC = 4, find A only. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Start with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|A|=40</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Remove inclusive AB and AC, then restore their common triple region once.","[1] A only <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=40-12-10+4=22</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The +4 corrects double subtraction of triple members."]},{"id":"mas11-inclusion-exclusion-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Two sets have sizes 15 and 12 in a universe of 20. Determine the minimum possible intersection size. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The union cannot exceed 20.","[1] $15+12-|A\\cap B|\\le20$.","[1] Hence $|A\\cap B|\\ge7$.","[1] Seven is feasible and is the minimum, not an assumed zero overlap."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Two sets have sizes 15 and 12 in a universe of 20. Determine the minimum possible intersection size. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The union cannot exceed 20.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15+12-|A\\cap B|\\le20</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|A\\cap B|\\ge7</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Seven is feasible and is the minimum, not an assumed zero overlap."]},{"id":"mas11-inclusion-exclusion-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"In a universe of 50, sets A and B have sizes 28 and 24 with 10 in both. Find exactly one and verify all four disjoint regions sum to 50. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] A only $=28-10=18$ and B only $=24-10=14$.","[1] Exactly one $=18+14=32$.","[1] Union $=28+24-10=42$, so neither is 8.","[1] Regions sum $18+14+10+8=50$, confirming the accounting."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"In a universe of 50, sets A and B have sizes 28 and 24 with 10 in both. Find exactly one and verify all four disjoint regions sum to 50. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] A only <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>28</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=28-10=18</annotation></semantics></math></span> and B only <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=24-10=14</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Exactly one <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=18+14=32</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Union <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>28</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>42</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=28+24-10=42</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so neither is 8.","[1] Regions sum <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">18+14+10+8=50</annotation></semantics></math></span>, confirming the accounting."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Why add the triple intersection in the three-set union formula?","o":["Triple members would otherwise have net count zero.","The triple is not part of any pairwise intersection.","Every member of the universe belongs to the triple.","All categories are disjoint."],"a":0,"why":"Three initial inclusions and three pairwise subtractions leave zero, so adding once restores the required count.","qHtml":"Why add the triple intersection in the three-set union formula?","oHtml":["Triple members would otherwise have net count zero.","The triple is not part of any pairwise intersection.","Every member of the universe belongs to the triple.","All categories are disjoint."],"whyHtml":"Three initial inclusions and three pairwise subtractions leave zero, so adding once restores the required count."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-pigeonhole","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Combinatorics","title":"Pigeonhole reasoning and guaranteed outcomes","outcomes":["1.2.6"],"icon":"📐","scope":"A guarantee is not a probability claim · Generalise through maximum capacity · Remainder classes create useful boxes · Construct boxes that encode the desired conclusion","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Pigeonhole reasoning and guaranteed outcomes</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>A guarantee is not a probability claim</li><li>Generalise through maximum capacity</li><li>Remainder classes create useful boxes</li><li>Construct boxes that encode the desired conclusion</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"A guarantee is not a probability claim","text":"If more than k objects are assigned to k boxes, at least one box contains at least two. This is a logical guarantee regardless of how the assignments occur. The boxes may represent birth months, remainders, colours or numerical intervals. Identify both objects and categories before applying the principle. A highly likely event is not the same as a guaranteed one; a probability model is unnecessary for this counting proof.","diagram":null,"html":"If more than k objects are assigned to k boxes, at least one box contains at least two. This is a logical guarantee regardless of how the assignments occur. The boxes may represent birth months, remainders, colours or numerical intervals. Identify both objects and categories before applying the principle. A highly likely event is not the same as a guaranteed one; a probability model is unnecessary for this counting proof."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Generalise through maximum capacity","text":"If each of k boxes held at most r objects, at most kr objects could be placed. Therefore kr+1 objects force at least r+1 in some box. Equivalently, at least one box holds at least $\\lceil N/k\\rceil$ among N objects. The ceiling matters for indivisible objects. A sufficiency count can be exact if a distribution just below the threshold shows failure is still possible; this establishes the minimum number for the guarantee.","diagram":"pigeonhole","html":"If each of k boxes held at most r objects, at most kr objects could be placed. Therefore kr+1 objects force at least r+1 in some box. Equivalently, at least one box holds at least <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⌈</mo><mi>N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lceil N/k\\rceil</annotation></semantics></math></span> among N objects. The ceiling matters for indivisible objects. A sufficiency count can be exact if a distribution just below the threshold shows failure is still possible; this establishes the minimum number for the guarantee."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Remainder classes create useful boxes","text":"Integers have one of m remainder classes modulo m. Among m+1 integers, two share a remainder, so their difference is divisible by m. The proof does not require the integers themselves to be consecutive or positive unless stated. Repeated numerical values must be interpreted according to the object definition. A divisibility conclusion about a difference needs the shared-remainder subtraction step, not merely the phrase pigeonhole principle.","diagram":null,"html":"Integers have one of m remainder classes modulo m. Among m+1 integers, two share a remainder, so their difference is divisible by m. The proof does not require the integers themselves to be consecutive or positive unless stated. Repeated numerical values must be interpreted according to the object definition. A divisibility conclusion about a difference needs the shared-remainder subtraction step, not merely the phrase pigeonhole principle."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Construct boxes that encode the desired conclusion","text":"A clever partition can pair numbers summing to a target or divide a length interval into subintervals. Choose the boxes so two objects in one box actually imply the requested property. Boundary conventions are important: overlapping intervals or a missing endpoint can break a partition. To prove a minimum, provide both the forced-outcome argument and an arrangement at the previous count that avoids it. State why all objects fall in exactly one relevant category.","diagram":null,"html":"A clever partition can pair numbers summing to a target or divide a length interval into subintervals. Choose the boxes so two objects in one box actually imply the requested property. Boundary conventions are important: overlapping intervals or a missing endpoint can break a partition. To prove a minimum, provide both the forced-outcome argument and an arrangement at the previous count that avoids it. State why all objects fall in exactly one relevant category."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-pigeonhole-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"What is the minimum number of people needed to guarantee at least three share a birth month? Use 12 possible months. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] If each month held at most two people, the total could be $12\\times2=24$.","[1] One more person forces some month to contain at least three.","[1] Thus 25 is sufficient.","[1] A distribution of 24 with two in each month avoids three, proving 25 is minimal."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"What is the minimum number of people needed to guarantee at least three share a birth month? Use 12 possible months. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] If each month held at most two people, the total could be <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\times2=24</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] One more person forces some month to contain at least three.","[1] Thus 25 is sufficient.","[1] A distribution of 24 with two in each month avoids three, proving 25 is minimal."]},{"id":"mas11-pigeonhole-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Prove that among any six integers, two have a difference divisible by 5. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The possible remainder classes modulo 5 are 0,1,2,3,4.","[1] Six integers in five classes force two with the same remainder.","[1] Write them $5a+r$ and $5b+r$.","[1] Their difference is $5(a-b)$, divisible by 5."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove that among any six integers, two have a difference divisible by 5. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The possible remainder classes modulo 5 are 0,1,2,3,4.","[1] Six integers in five classes force two with the same remainder.","[1] Write them <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5a+r</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5b+r</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Their difference is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5(a-b)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, divisible by 5."]},{"id":"mas11-pigeonhole-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Seventeen objects are distributed among five boxes. What occupancy is guaranteed in at least one box, and why? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] If all boxes held at most three, the total would be at most 15.","[1] Since 17 exceeds 15, some box holds at least four.","[1] Equivalently $\\lceil17/5\\rceil=4$.","[1] Four is the guaranteed lower bound; no particular named box is identified."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Seventeen objects are distributed among five boxes. What occupancy is guaranteed in at least one box, and why? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] If all boxes held at most three, the total would be at most 15.","[1] Since 17 exceeds 15, some box holds at least four.","[1] Equivalently <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⌈</mo><mn>17</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">⌉</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lceil17/5\\rceil=4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Four is the guaranteed lower bound; no particular named box is identified."]},{"id":"mas11-pigeonhole-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Choose six distinct numbers from 1 through 10. Prove two chosen numbers sum to 11. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Partition into pairs $\\{1,10\\},\\{2,9\\},\\{3,8\\},\\{4,7\\},\\{5,6\\}$.","[1] There are five boxes and six chosen distinct numbers.","[1] Some pair-box contains two chosen numbers.","[1] Those are the two complementary members and sum to 11."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Choose six distinct numbers from 1 through 10. Prove two chosen numbers sum to 11. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Partition into pairs <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{1,10\\},\\{2,9\\},\\{3,8\\},\\{4,7\\},\\{5,6\\}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] There are five boxes and six chosen distinct numbers.","[1] Some pair-box contains two chosen numbers.","[1] Those are the two complementary members and sum to 11."]},{"id":"mas11-pigeonhole-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"A drawer contains socks of four colours, with enough of each. How many draws guarantee at least two of one colour, and why is that minimum? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] With four draws, one of each colour is possible.","[1] A fifth sock occupies one of the same four colour categories.","[1] The pigeonhole principle forces at least two of a colour.","[1] Five is sufficient and the four-colour example shows fewer cannot guarantee it."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A drawer contains socks of four colours, with enough of each. How many draws guarantee at least two of one colour, and why is that minimum? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] With four draws, one of each colour is possible.","[1] A fifth sock occupies one of the same four colour categories.","[1] The pigeonhole principle forces at least two of a colour.","[1] Five is sufficient and the four-colour example shows fewer cannot guarantee it."]},{"id":"mas11-pigeonhole-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Nine students each receive one of three possible grades. Find the minimum number guaranteeing at least four of one grade, and explain why nine itself is insufficient. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] At most three per grade allows $3\\times3=9$ students.","[1] Nine can be distributed 3,3,3, so four is not guaranteed.","[1] Ten exceeds that capacity and forces at least four in one grade.","[1] The minimum count is 10, under the stated three-grade model."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Nine students each receive one of three possible grades. Find the minimum number guaranteeing at least four of one grade, and explain why nine itself is insufficient. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] At most three per grade allows <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\times3=9</annotation></semantics></math></span> students.","[1] Nine can be distributed 3,3,3, so four is not guaranteed.","[1] Ten exceeds that capacity and forces at least four in one grade.","[1] The minimum count is 10, under the stated three-grade model."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What count forces at least r+1 objects in one of k boxes?","o":["$kr+1$.","$kr$ always.","$k+r$ in every situation.","One object regardless of r."],"a":0,"why":"If every box had at most r objects, total capacity would be kr.","qHtml":"What count forces at least r+1 objects in one of k boxes?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">kr+1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">kr</annotation></semantics></math></span> always.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k+r</annotation></semantics></math></span> in every situation.","One object regardless of r."],"whyHtml":"If every box had at most r objects, total capacity would be kr."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-combinations","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Combinatorics","title":"Combinations, restrictions and Pascal identities","outcomes":["1.2.7","1.2.8","1.2.9"],"icon":"📐","scope":"A selection ignores internal order · Restrictions can split into disjoint composition cases · Symmetry follows from selecting the complement · Pascal’s identity partitions by a distinguished object","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Combinations, restrictions and Pascal identities</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>A selection ignores internal order</li><li>Restrictions can split into disjoint composition cases</li><li>Symmetry follows from selecting the complement</li><li>Pascal’s identity partitions by a distinguished object</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"A selection ignores internal order","text":"An r-person committee from n distinct people has $\\binom nr=n!/(r!(n-r)!)$ possibilities. An ordered selection counts each committee r! times, one for every internal ordering, so dividing removes those duplicates. If the selected people receive distinct roles, order matters and this combination alone is insufficient. Define the requested outcome before using the notation, and keep $0\\le r\\le n$ for the basic finite selection interpretation.","diagram":null,"html":"An r-person committee from n distinct people has <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom nr=n!/(r!(n-r)!)</annotation></semantics></math></span> possibilities. An ordered selection counts each committee r! times, one for every internal ordering, so dividing removes those duplicates. If the selected people receive distinct roles, order matters and this combination alone is insufficient. Define the requested outcome before using the notation, and keep <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le r\\le n</annotation></semantics></math></span> for the basic finite selection interpretation."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Restrictions can split into disjoint composition cases","text":"To choose a committee with exactly two members from group A and one from B, multiply the two independent selection counts. At least two from A may require adding the exactly-two and exactly-three cases. A complement can be simpler for at least one, but only after defining the forbidden set correctly. If groups overlap, independent group selection can count the same person twice; additional information or case handling is required.","diagram":"counting","html":"To choose a committee with exactly two members from group A and one from B, multiply the two independent selection counts. At least two from A may require adding the exactly-two and exactly-three cases. A complement can be simpler for at least one, but only after defining the forbidden set correctly. If groups overlap, independent group selection can count the same person twice; additional information or case handling is required."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Symmetry follows from selecting the complement","text":"Choosing r members determines exactly which n-r are excluded, giving $\\binom nr=\\binom n{n-r}$. This is a counting argument as well as a factorial identity. In particular, choosing none has one possibility: the empty set. Counts are whole numbers even when expressed as factorial ratios. Simplify or use a reliable combination command, and distinguish the command’s r,n ordering rather than blindly entering numbers.","diagram":null,"html":"Choosing r members determines exactly which n-r are excluded, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom nr=\\binom n{n-r}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This is a counting argument as well as a factorial identity. In particular, choosing none has one possibility: the empty set. Counts are whole numbers even when expressed as factorial ratios. Simplify or use a reliable combination command, and distinguish the command’s r,n ordering rather than blindly entering numbers."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Pascal’s identity partitions by a distinguished object","text":"For an r-person selection from n+1 people, separate selections that exclude one distinguished person from those that include them. Exclusion gives $\\binom nr$ and inclusion gives $\\binom n{r-1}$, so their sum is $\\binom{n+1}r$. These cases are mutually exclusive and exhaustive. This reasoning explains adjacent entries in Pascal’s triangle and supports a general proof, unlike verifying one row numerically.","diagram":null,"html":"For an r-person selection from n+1 people, separate selections that exclude one distinguished person from those that include them. Exclusion gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom nr</annotation></semantics></math></span> and inclusion gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom n{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so their sum is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom{n+1}r</annotation></semantics></math></span>. These cases are mutually exclusive and exhaustive. This reasoning explains adjacent entries in Pascal’s triangle and supports a general proof, unlike verifying one row numerically."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-combinations-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Choose an unordered committee of 3 from 8 distinct people. Count committees and compare with assigning three distinct roles. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Unordered selection gives $\\binom83=8!/(3!5!)$.","[1] Evaluate $56$.","[1] Three distinct roles give $^8P_3=8\\times7\\times6=336$.","[1] The role assignments are $3!=6$ times as numerous because order now matters."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Choose an unordered committee of 3 from 8 distinct people. Count committees and compare with assigning three distinct roles. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Unordered selection gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>8</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom83=8!/(3!5!)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>56</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">56</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Three distinct roles give <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>8</mn></msup><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>336</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^8P_3=8\\times7\\times6=336</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The role assignments are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">!</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3!=6</annotation></semantics></math></span> times as numerous because order now matters."]},{"id":"mas11-combinations-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Choose a three-person committee with exactly two from a group of five and one from a separate group of four. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Choose the two from the first group in $\\binom52=10$ ways.","[1] Choose one from the second in $\\binom41=4$ ways.","[1] Multiply the group selections: $10\\times4=40$.","[1] The groups are disjoint and the committee has no assigned roles."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Choose a three-person committee with exactly two from a group of five and one from a separate group of four. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Choose the two from the first group in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom52=10</annotation></semantics></math></span> ways.","[1] Choose one from the second in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>4</mn><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom41=4</annotation></semantics></math></span> ways.","[1] Multiply the group selections: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\times4=40</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The groups are disjoint and the committee has no assigned roles."]},{"id":"mas11-combinations-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Choose three from 5 group-A and 4 group-B members, requiring at least two from A. Count committees. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Exactly two A and one B gives $\\binom52\\binom41=40$.","[1] Exactly three A gives $\\binom53=10$.","[1] These disjoint cases exhaust at least two A in a committee of three.","[1] Total $=40+10=50$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Choose three from 5 group-A and 4 group-B members, requiring at least two from A. Count committees. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Exactly two A and one B gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>4</mn><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom52\\binom41=40</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Exactly three A gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>5</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom53=10</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] These disjoint cases exhaust at least two A in a committee of three.","[1] Total <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=40+10=50</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-combinations-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"From 8 people, two specified people must both belong to a four-person committee. Count the committees. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Fix the two required members.","[1] Choose the remaining two from the other six people.","[1] Count $=\\binom62=15$.","[1] Do not multiply by orders because the committee is unordered."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"From 8 people, two specified people must both belong to a four-person committee. Count the committees. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Fix the two required members.","[1] Choose the remaining two from the other six people.","[1] Count <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>6</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=\\binom62=15</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Do not multiply by orders because the committee is unordered."]},{"id":"mas11-combinations-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Prove $\\binom nr=\\binom n{n-r}$ by a counting argument. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Every r-element subset of an n-element set has a unique complement.","[1] The complement has n-r elements.","[1] Taking the complement reverses this association, giving a bijection.","[1] Therefore the two selection counts are equal for $0\\le r\\le n$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom nr=\\binom n{n-r}</annotation></semantics></math></span> by a counting argument. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Every r-element subset of an n-element set has a unique complement.","[1] The complement has n-r elements.","[1] Taking the complement reverses this association, giving a bijection.","[1] Therefore the two selection counts are equal for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le r\\le n</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-combinations-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Prove Pascal’s identity $\\binom n{r-1}+\\binom nr=\\binom{n+1}r$ by distinguishing one person, then verify for n = 6, r = 3. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] An r-person committee either includes or excludes the distinguished person.","[1] Inclusion requires r-1 from the other n; exclusion requires r from those n.","[1] Add the disjoint counts to obtain the identity.","[1] For n=6,r=3, $\\binom62+\\binom63=15+20=35=\\binom73$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove Pascal’s identity <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom n{r-1}+\\binom nr=\\binom{n+1}r</annotation></semantics></math></span> by distinguishing one person, then verify for n = 6, r = 3. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] An r-person committee either includes or excludes the distinguished person.","[1] Inclusion requires r-1 from the other n; exclusion requires r from those n.","[1] Add the disjoint counts to obtain the identity.","[1] For n=6,r=3, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>6</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>6</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>35</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>7</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\binom62+\\binom63=15+20=35=\\binom73</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which change turns a committee-selection problem into an ordered-assignment problem?","o":["Giving selected members distinct roles.","Using distinct people but no roles.","Writing the committee in a different list order without changing its membership.","Choosing the complement of a committee."],"a":0,"why":"Distinct roles make swapping members produce a different outcome.","qHtml":"Which change turns a committee-selection problem into an ordered-assignment problem?","oHtml":["Giving selected members distinct roles.","Using distinct people but no roles.","Writing the committee in a different list order without changing its membership.","Choosing the complement of a committee."],"whyHtml":"Distinct roles make swapping members produce a different outcome."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-vectors-components","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Vectors in the plane","title":"Directed vectors, components and unit vectors","outcomes":["1.3.1","1.3.2","1.3.5","1.3.6","1.3.7"],"icon":"📐","scope":"A vector has magnitude and direction · Components encode signed direction · Unit vectors require a nonzero starting vector · Construct displacements from endpoint minus start","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Directed vectors, components and unit vectors</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>A vector has magnitude and direction</li><li>Components encode signed direction</li><li>Unit vectors require a nonzero starting vector</li><li>Construct displacements from endpoint minus start</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"A vector has magnitude and direction","text":"A vector models a directed quantity such as displacement, velocity or force. A scalar specifies magnitude without a spatial direction. A free vector can be translated without changing it when its magnitude and direction remain the same; its drawn starting position is not part of that free-vector identity. A position vector, by contrast, is measured from a chosen origin. Distinguish displacement between two points from the position of one point, and state the coordinate convention in applied tasks.","diagram":null,"html":"A vector models a directed quantity such as displacement, velocity or force. A scalar specifies magnitude without a spatial direction. A free vector can be translated without changing it when its magnitude and direction remain the same; its drawn starting position is not part of that free-vector identity. A position vector, by contrast, is measured from a chosen origin. Distinguish displacement between two points from the position of one point, and state the coordinate convention in applied tasks."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Components encode signed direction","text":"In the plane, $\\mathbf a=(a_x,a_y)=a_x\\mathbf i+a_y\\mathbf j$ using perpendicular unit vectors. The magnitude is $|\\mathbf a|=\\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}$. Negative components indicate direction along negative axes, not a negative vector magnitude. The zero vector has zero magnitude and no defined direction. Ordered-pair and column forms represent the same vector information; use a consistent form and distinguish a point’s coordinates from a directed displacement.","diagram":"vector-add","html":"In the plane, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">i</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a=(a_x,a_y)=a_x\\mathbf i+a_y\\mathbf j</annotation></semantics></math></span> using perpendicular unit vectors. The magnitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf a|=\\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Negative components indicate direction along negative axes, not a negative vector magnitude. The zero vector has zero magnitude and no defined direction. Ordered-pair and column forms represent the same vector information; use a consistent form and distinguish a point’s coordinates from a directed displacement."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Unit vectors require a nonzero starting vector","text":"For $\\mathbf a\\ne\\mathbf0$, the unit vector in its direction is $\\hat{\\mathbf a}=\\mathbf a/|\\mathbf a|$. Dividing components by the magnitude gives norm one without changing direction. The opposite unit direction is its negative. Normalising the zero vector is undefined because its magnitude is zero. A direction angle should account for the component signs and quadrant; an inverse tangent of a ratio alone can select the wrong direction.","diagram":null,"html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a\\ne\\mathbf0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the unit vector in its direction is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{\\mathbf a}=\\mathbf a/|\\mathbf a|</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Dividing components by the magnitude gives norm one without changing direction. The opposite unit direction is its negative. Normalising the zero vector is undefined because its magnitude is zero. A direction angle should account for the component signs and quadrant; an inverse tangent of a ratio alone can select the wrong direction."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Construct displacements from endpoint minus start","text":"For points A and B with position vectors $\\mathbf a,\\mathbf b$, displacement $\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a$. Reversing the order gives the opposite vector. A distance is the magnitude of this displacement between the two endpoints, whereas distance travelled along a curved or multistage path can be larger. Include units for physical vectors and check that the square-root result is nonnegative. A coordinate sketch helps interpret signs but does not replace the calculation.","diagram":null,"html":"For points A and B with position vectors <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a,\\mathbf b</annotation></semantics></math></span>, displacement <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Reversing the order gives the opposite vector. A distance is the magnitude of this displacement between the two endpoints, whereas distance travelled along a curved or multistage path can be larger. Include units for physical vectors and check that the square-root result is nonnegative. A coordinate sketch helps interpret signs but does not replace the calculation."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-vectors-components-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find the magnitude and unit direction of $\\mathbf a=(3,4)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $|\\mathbf a|=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5$.","[1] Divide both components by the magnitude: $\\hat{\\mathbf a}=(3/5,4/5)$.","[1] Its magnitude is $\\sqrt{9/25+16/25}=1$.","[1] Positive scaling preserves the direction of the nonzero vector."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the magnitude and unit direction of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a=(3,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf a|=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Divide both components by the magnitude: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{\\mathbf a}=(3/5,4/5)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its magnitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>9</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>25</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>25</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{9/25+16/25}=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Positive scaling preserves the direction of the nonzero vector."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-components-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Points A and B have coordinates $(-2,1)$ and $(4,-7)$. Find AB displacement and endpoint distance. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Endpoint minus start gives $\\overrightarrow{AB}=(4-(-2),-7-1)=(6,-8)$.","[1] Distance is $\\sqrt{6^2+(-8)^2}$.","[1] The distance is 10 units.","[1] The displacement retains signed components; the distance is a nonnegative scalar."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Points A and B have coordinates <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-2,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,-7)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find AB displacement and endpoint distance. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Endpoint minus start gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overrightarrow{AB}=(4-(-2),-7-1)=(6,-8)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Distance is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>6</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{6^2+(-8)^2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The distance is 10 units.","[1] The displacement retains signed components; the distance is a nonnegative scalar."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-components-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Express $(-5,12)$ using $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j$ and find its magnitude. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The component form is $-5\\mathbf i+12\\mathbf j$.","[1] Magnitude uses the sum of squared components.","[1] $\\sqrt{25+144}=13$.","[1] The negative x component does not make the magnitude negative."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Express <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-5,12)</annotation></semantics></math></span> using <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j</annotation></semantics></math></span> and find its magnitude. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The component form is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-5\\mathbf i+12\\mathbf j</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Magnitude uses the sum of squared components.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>144</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{25+144}=13</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The negative x component does not make the magnitude negative."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-components-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find a vector of magnitude 10 in the direction of $(-3,4)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The starting vector has magnitude 5.","[1] Its unit direction is $(-3/5,4/5)$.","[1] Multiply by 10 to get $(-6,8)$.","[1] Magnitude is 10 and the positive scaling preserves direction."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find a vector of magnitude 10 in the direction of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-3,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The starting vector has magnitude 5.","[1] Its unit direction is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-3/5,4/5)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Multiply by 10 to get <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-6,8)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Magnitude is 10 and the positive scaling preserves direction."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-components-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"A particle moves from $(1,2)$ to $(1,7)$ then to $(5,7)$. Find net displacement, its magnitude and total path distance. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Net displacement is $(5-1,7-2)=(4,5)$.","[1] Its magnitude is $\\sqrt{41}$.","[1] The two perpendicular path stages have lengths 5 and 4.","[1] Total distance travelled is 9, distinct from endpoint displacement magnitude."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A particle moves from <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,7)</annotation></semantics></math></span> then to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(5,7)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find net displacement, its magnitude and total path distance. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Net displacement is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(5-1,7-2)=(4,5)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its magnitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>41</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{41}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The two perpendicular path stages have lengths 5 and 4.","[1] Total distance travelled is 9, distinct from endpoint displacement magnitude."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-components-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Explain why a unit vector cannot be formed by dividing the zero vector by its magnitude, and contrast with $(0,-2)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The zero vector magnitude is 0.","[1] Normalisation would divide by zero, and no unique direction is defined.","[1] For $(0,-2)$, magnitude is 2.","[1] Its valid unit direction is $(0,-1)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why a unit vector cannot be formed by dividing the zero vector by its magnitude, and contrast with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The zero vector magnitude is 0.","[1] Normalisation would divide by zero, and no unique direction is defined.","[1] For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, magnitude is 2.","[1] Its valid unit direction is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"How is $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ obtained from position vectors?","o":["Position of B minus position of A.","Position of A minus position of B.","The sum of both magnitudes only.","Always a positive pair of components."],"a":0,"why":"Displacement is endpoint minus start and retains signed direction.","qHtml":"How is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">→</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overrightarrow{AB}</annotation></semantics></math></span> obtained from position vectors?","oHtml":["Position of B minus position of A.","Position of A minus position of B.","The sum of both magnitudes only.","Always a positive pair of components."],"whyHtml":"Displacement is endpoint minus start and retains signed direction."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-vectors-operations","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Vectors in the plane","title":"Vector addition, subtraction and scalar multiples","outcomes":["1.3.3","1.3.4","1.3.8","1.3.9"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Add displacements head to tail · Calculate component by component · Parallel vectors are scalar multiples · Use position-vector combinations geometrically","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Vector addition, subtraction and scalar multiples</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Add displacements head to tail</li><li>Calculate component by component</li><li>Parallel vectors are scalar multiples</li><li>Use position-vector combinations geometrically</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Add displacements head to tail","text":"The triangle rule places the second vector’s tail at the first vector’s head; the resultant goes from the original tail to the final head. The parallelogram rule gives the same sum when both vectors start together. Translation preserves a free vector, so its placement can change while its components remain fixed. Vector addition is commutative, but a subtraction represents addition of the opposite vector and its order matters.","diagram":null,"html":"The triangle rule places the second vector’s tail at the first vector’s head; the resultant goes from the original tail to the final head. The parallelogram rule gives the same sum when both vectors start together. Translation preserves a free vector, so its placement can change while its components remain fixed. Vector addition is commutative, but a subtraction represents addition of the opposite vector and its order matters."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate component by component","text":"For $\\mathbf a=(a_x,a_y)$ and $\\mathbf b=(b_x,b_y)$, their sum is $(a_x+b_x,a_y+b_y)$. A scalar multiple k gives $(ka_x,ka_y)$. If k is negative, direction reverses as magnitude scales by $|k|$. The magnitude of a sum is not generally the sum of magnitudes because direction affects the resultant. Algebraic cancellation of components can reduce the resultant even when both input magnitudes are large.","diagram":"vector-add","html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a=(a_x,a_y)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf b=(b_x,b_y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, their sum is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(a_x+b_x,a_y+b_y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A scalar multiple k gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(ka_x,ka_y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. If k is negative, direction reverses as magnitude scales by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|k|</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The magnitude of a sum is not generally the sum of magnitudes because direction affects the resultant. Algebraic cancellation of components can reduce the resultant even when both input magnitudes are large."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Parallel vectors are scalar multiples","text":"Two nonzero vectors are parallel if one is a real scalar multiple of the other. A positive multiple has the same direction; a negative multiple has opposite direction. Component ratios can test this where denominators are nonzero, but zero components require direct handling. The zero vector is a special case without a direction, so do not infer an ordinary angle or unit direction from it. A consistent scalar must work in every component.","diagram":null,"html":"Two nonzero vectors are parallel if one is a real scalar multiple of the other. A positive multiple has the same direction; a negative multiple has opposite direction. Component ratios can test this where denominators are nonzero, but zero components require direct handling. The zero vector is a special case without a direction, so do not infer an ordinary angle or unit direction from it. A consistent scalar must work in every component."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use position-vector combinations geometrically","text":"The midpoint of AB has position $(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b)/2$. A point dividing the segment internally in a specified ratio can be represented as $\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)$ with $0\\le t\\le1$. The parameter measures fraction of travel from A towards B. Check whether the named ratio refers to AP:PB or the reversed order before assigning t. Vector equalities connect geometry with components, supporting exact proofs rather than measurements.","diagram":null,"html":"The midpoint of AB has position <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A point dividing the segment internally in a specified ratio can be represented as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le t\\le1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The parameter measures fraction of travel from A towards B. Check whether the named ratio refers to AP:PB or the reversed order before assigning t. Vector equalities connect geometry with components, supporting exact proofs rather than measurements."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-vectors-operations-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"For $\\mathbf a=(2,-1)$ and $\\mathbf b=(-3,4)$, calculate $2\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b$ and its magnitude. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $2\\mathbf a=(4,-2)$.","[1] Subtract componentwise: $(4,-2)-(-3,4)=(7,-6)$.","[1] Magnitude is $\\sqrt{7^2+(-6)^2}=\\sqrt{85}$.","[1] Subtraction adds the opposite vector and does not subtract magnitudes."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a=(2,-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf b=(-3,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, calculate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b</annotation></semantics></math></span> and its magnitude. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\mathbf a=(4,-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Subtract componentwise: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,-2)-(-3,4)=(7,-6)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Magnitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>85</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{7^2+(-6)^2}=\\sqrt{85}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Subtraction adds the opposite vector and does not subtract magnitudes."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-operations-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"A = $(1,-2)$ and B = $(7,4)$. Find the midpoint M and the point P with AP:PB = 1:2. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Midpoint is $M=((1+7)/2,(-2+4)/2)=(4,1)$.","[1] The ratio AP:PB = 1:2 places P one third along AB.","[1] $P=A+(B-A)/3=(1,-2)+(6,6)/3$.","[1] Hence $P=(3,0)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A = <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and B = <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(7,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find the midpoint M and the point P with AP:PB = 1:2. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Midpoint is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=((1+7)/2,(-2+4)/2)=(4,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The ratio AP:PB = 1:2 places P one third along AB.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=A+(B-A)/3=(1,-2)+(6,6)/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=(3,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-operations-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Determine whether $(3,-6)$ and $(-2,4)$ are parallel and state whether their directions agree. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Multiplying $(3,-6)$ by $-2/3$ gives $(-2,4)$.","[1] The same scalar works in both components.","[1] The vectors are parallel.","[1] The scalar is negative, so the directions are opposite."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Determine whether <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,-6)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-2,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span> are parallel and state whether their directions agree. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Multiplying <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,-6)</annotation></semantics></math></span> by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2/3</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-2,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The same scalar works in both components.","[1] The vectors are parallel.","[1] The scalar is negative, so the directions are opposite."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-operations-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find k so $(k,6)$ is parallel to $(2,3)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Write $(k,6)=\\lambda(2,3)$.","[1] The y component gives $6=3\\lambda$, so $\\lambda=2$.","[1] Then $k=2\\lambda=4$.","[1] Check $(4,6)=2(2,3)$, confirming both components."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find k so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k,6)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is parallel to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k,6)=\\lambda(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The y component gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6=3\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=2\\lambda=4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Check <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,6)=2(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, confirming both components."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-operations-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"For perpendicular unit vectors $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j$, compare $|\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j|$ with $|\\mathbf i|+|\\mathbf j|$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The resultant has components $(1,1)$.","[1] Its magnitude is $\\sqrt2$.","[1] The separate magnitudes sum to $1+1=2$.","[1] They differ, showing vector-sum magnitude is not generally the sum of magnitudes."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For perpendicular unit vectors <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j</annotation></semantics></math></span>, compare <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">j</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j|</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">j</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf i|+|\\mathbf j|</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The resultant has components <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its magnitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The separate magnitudes sum to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+1=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] They differ, showing vector-sum magnitude is not generally the sum of magnitudes."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-operations-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Given A = $(0,1)$ and B = $(8,5)$, find the point two thirds from A to B and decide whether it lies within the segment. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $B-A=(8,4)$.","[1] Position is $A+\\frac23(B-A)$.","[1] This gives $(16/3,11/3)$.","[1] Since the parameter $2/3$ lies between 0 and 1, the point lies within AB."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Given A = <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and B = <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(8,5)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find the point two thirds from A to B and decide whether it lies within the segment. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B-A=(8,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Position is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A+\\frac23(B-A)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>16</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>11</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(16/3,11/3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Since the parameter <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/3</annotation></semantics></math></span> lies between 0 and 1, the point lies within AB."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What does multiplying a nonzero vector by a negative scalar do?","o":["Reverses direction and scales magnitude by the scalar’s absolute value.","Makes the magnitude negative.","Always gives a perpendicular vector.","Changes only one component."],"a":0,"why":"All components scale together; magnitude remains nonnegative.","qHtml":"What does multiplying a nonzero vector by a negative scalar do?","oHtml":["Reverses direction and scales magnitude by the scalar’s absolute value.","Makes the magnitude negative.","Always gives a perpendicular vector.","Changes only one component."],"whyHtml":"All components scale together; magnitude remains nonnegative."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-vectors-dot","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Vectors in the plane","title":"Dot product, angles and perpendicularity","outcomes":["1.3.10","1.3.11","1.3.12"],"icon":"📐","scope":"The dot product produces a scalar · Angles require nonzero magnitudes · Perpendicular and parallel are different tests · Expand dot products to prove identities","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Dot product, angles and perpendicularity</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>The dot product produces a scalar</li><li>Angles require nonzero magnitudes</li><li>Perpendicular and parallel are different tests</li><li>Expand dot products to prove identities</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"The dot product produces a scalar","text":"For plane vectors, $\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b=a_xb_x+a_yb_y=|\\mathbf a||\\mathbf b|\\cos\\theta$ for nonzero vectors and their angle $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$. The result is a scalar, not another directed vector. It can be positive, zero or negative, reflecting an acute, right or obtuse angle. Dotting a vector with itself gives its squared magnitude. The component and geometric forms provide complementary ways to calculate and interpret the same quantity.","diagram":null,"html":"For plane vectors, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b=a_xb_x+a_yb_y=|\\mathbf a||\\mathbf b|\\cos\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> for nonzero vectors and their angle <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The result is a scalar, not another directed vector. It can be positive, zero or negative, reflecting an acute, right or obtuse angle. Dotting a vector with itself gives its squared magnitude. The component and geometric forms provide complementary ways to calculate and interpret the same quantity."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Angles require nonzero magnitudes","text":"Rearrange to $\\cos\\theta=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)/(|\\mathbf a||\\mathbf b|)$ and then use the appropriate angle mode. The zero vector has no direction, so this angle formula cannot be used when a denominator magnitude is zero. Exact cosine values can establish an angle without rounded intermediate approximations. A value outside [-1,1] usually signals a calculation or rounding error rather than an exotic real angle.","diagram":"projection","html":"Rearrange to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos\\theta=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)/(|\\mathbf a||\\mathbf b|)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and then use the appropriate angle mode. The zero vector has no direction, so this angle formula cannot be used when a denominator magnitude is zero. Exact cosine values can establish an angle without rounded intermediate approximations. A value outside [-1,1] usually signals a calculation or rounding error rather than an exotic real angle."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Perpendicular and parallel are different tests","text":"Two nonzero vectors are perpendicular when their dot product is zero. Parallelism is tested by a common scalar multiple, and parallel vectors can have a positive or negative dot product. A vector whose dot product with another is negative is not automatically opposite: it can form any obtuse angle. In geometric proofs, state nonzero assumptions when angle conclusions require them and distinguish algebraic zero-dot relations from a meaningful angle involving a zero vector.","diagram":null,"html":"Two nonzero vectors are perpendicular when their dot product is zero. Parallelism is tested by a common scalar multiple, and parallel vectors can have a positive or negative dot product. A vector whose dot product with another is negative is not automatically opposite: it can form any obtuse angle. In geometric proofs, state nonzero assumptions when angle conclusions require them and distinguish algebraic zero-dot relations from a meaningful angle involving a zero vector."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Expand dot products to prove identities","text":"Distributivity and symmetry give $(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b)\\cdot(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b)=|\\mathbf a|^2+2\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+|\\mathbf b|^2$. This connects vector algebra to length relationships and can prove perpendicularity or geometric results. Use the vector equality first, then dot or take squared magnitude; do not treat a vector as an ordinary scalar by squaring it without defining the operation. A rigorous proof states which scalar expression is being compared.","diagram":null,"html":"Distributivity and symmetry give <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b)\\cdot(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b)=|\\mathbf a|^2+2\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+|\\mathbf b|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This connects vector algebra to length relationships and can prove perpendicularity or geometric results. Use the vector equality first, then dot or take squared magnitude; do not treat a vector as an ordinary scalar by squaring it without defining the operation. A rigorous proof states which scalar expression is being compared."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-vectors-dot-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find the angle between $\\mathbf a=(1,0)$ and $\\mathbf b=(1,\\sqrt3)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Dot product is 1.","[1] Magnitudes are 1 and 2.","[1] $\\cos\\theta=1/(1\\times2)=1/2$.","[1] Hence $\\theta=60^\\circ=\\pi/3$ for the angle between the nonzero vectors."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the angle between <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a=(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf b=(1,\\sqrt3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Dot product is 1.","[1] Magnitudes are 1 and 2.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos\\theta=1/(1\\times2)=1/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=60^\\circ=\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span> for the angle between the nonzero vectors."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-dot-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find k so $(2,k)$ is perpendicular to $(3,-4)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Perpendicularity requires zero dot product for these nonzero vectors.","[1] $2(3)+k(-4)=0$.","[1] $6-4k=0$, so $k=3/2$.","[1] Substitution gives $6-6=0$, confirming the condition."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find k so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,k)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is perpendicular to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,-4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Perpendicularity requires zero dot product for these nonzero vectors.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(3)+k(-4)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6-4k=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=3/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Substitution gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6-6=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, confirming the condition."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-dot-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Calculate the dot product of $(2,3)$ and $(-1,4)$ and classify their angle as acute, right or obtuse. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Dot product is $2(-1)+3(4)=10$.","[1] Both vector magnitudes are positive.","[1] Therefore cosine has the same positive sign as the dot product.","[1] The angle is acute, without requiring its exact value."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Calculate the dot product of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and classify their angle as acute, right or obtuse. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Dot product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(-1)+3(4)=10</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both vector magnitudes are positive.","[1] Therefore cosine has the same positive sign as the dot product.","[1] The angle is acute, without requiring its exact value."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-dot-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"For $(1,2)$ and $(-3,0)$, find the exact cosine of their angle and classify it. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Dot product is -3.","[1] Magnitudes are $\\sqrt5$ and 3.","[1] $\\cos\\theta=-3/(3\\sqrt5)=-1/\\sqrt5$.","[1] The angle is obtuse; a negative dot product does not require exactly opposite vectors."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-3,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find the exact cosine of their angle and classify it. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Dot product is -3.","[1] Magnitudes are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt5</annotation></semantics></math></span> and 3.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos\\theta=-3/(3\\sqrt5)=-1/\\sqrt5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The angle is obtuse; a negative dot product does not require exactly opposite vectors."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-dot-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Prove $|\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b|^2=|\\mathbf a|^2+2\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+|\\mathbf b|^2$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Write squared magnitude as $(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b)\\cdot(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b)$.","[1] Expand by distributivity into four dot products.","[1] Symmetry combines $\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b$ and $\\mathbf b\\cdot\\mathbf a$ as twice the same scalar.","[1] Self dot products equal squared magnitudes, yielding the identity."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b|^2=|\\mathbf a|^2+2\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+|\\mathbf b|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Write squared magnitude as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b)\\cdot(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Expand by distributivity into four dot products.","[1] Symmetry combines <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf b\\cdot\\mathbf a</annotation></semantics></math></span> as twice the same scalar.","[1] Self dot products equal squared magnitudes, yielding the identity."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-dot-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Explain why $\\mathbf0\\cdot\\mathbf a=0$ does not establish a right angle between the zero vector and a nonzero vector. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The component dot product is indeed zero.","[1] The zero vector has no defined direction.","[1] The angle formula would divide by $|\\mathbf0|=0$.","[1] Thus ordinary angle-based perpendicularity must be stated for nonzero vectors; the algebraic zero dot product alone is insufficient here."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf0\\cdot\\mathbf a=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> does not establish a right angle between the zero vector and a nonzero vector. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The component dot product is indeed zero.","[1] The zero vector has no defined direction.","[1] The angle formula would divide by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf0|=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus ordinary angle-based perpendicularity must be stated for nonzero vectors; the algebraic zero dot product alone is insufficient here."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What type of object is a dot product?","o":["A scalar.","Always a unit vector.","A two-component displacement.","An angle with no additional calculation."],"a":0,"why":"The dot product is a number that relates magnitudes and the cosine of an angle.","qHtml":"What type of object is a dot product?","oHtml":["A scalar.","Always a unit vector.","A two-component displacement.","An angle with no additional calculation."],"whyHtml":"The dot product is a number that relates magnitudes and the cosine of an angle."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-vectors-projection","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Vectors in the plane","title":"Scalar and vector projections","outcomes":["1.3.13"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Projection resolves a vector along a specified direction · Use the direction vector consistently · The residual is perpendicular to the chosen line · Apply the decomposition to a physical or geometric problem","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Scalar and vector projections</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Projection resolves a vector along a specified direction</li><li>Use the direction vector consistently</li><li>The residual is perpendicular to the chosen line</li><li>Apply the decomposition to a physical or geometric problem</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Projection resolves a vector along a specified direction","text":"The scalar projection of $\\mathbf a$ onto nonzero $\\mathbf b$ is $(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)/|\\mathbf b|$. It is signed relative to the chosen direction of b. The vector projection is $\\operatorname{proj}_{\\mathbf b}\\mathbf a=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)\\mathbf b/|\\mathbf b|^2$. It has the units and direction type of the original vector. The denominator differs because one result is a signed component and the other a vector multiplied by b.","diagram":null,"html":"The scalar projection of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a</annotation></semantics></math></span> onto nonzero <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf b</annotation></semantics></math></span> is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)/|\\mathbf b|</annotation></semantics></math></span>. It is signed relative to the chosen direction of b. The vector projection is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">proj</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{proj}_{\\mathbf b}\\mathbf a=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)\\mathbf b/|\\mathbf b|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. It has the units and direction type of the original vector. The denominator differs because one result is a signed component and the other a vector multiplied by b."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use the direction vector consistently","text":"Projection onto b and onto -b produces the same vector projection along the line, while the scalar projection changes sign because its positive reference direction reverses. Scaling b by a nonzero factor does not change the projected vector onto that line. A zero direction vector is invalid. When using a unit direction u, the formula simplifies to $(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf u)\\mathbf u$, but this simplification requires $|\\mathbf u|=1$.","diagram":"projection","html":"Projection onto b and onto -b produces the same vector projection along the line, while the scalar projection changes sign because its positive reference direction reverses. Scaling b by a nonzero factor does not change the projected vector onto that line. A zero direction vector is invalid. When using a unit direction u, the formula simplifies to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf u)\\mathbf u</annotation></semantics></math></span>, but this simplification requires <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf u|=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"learn","title":"The residual is perpendicular to the chosen line","text":"Write $\\mathbf a=\\mathbf p+\\mathbf r$ where p is the projection and r = a-p. Dotting r with b gives zero by the projection formula. This verifies that the decomposition has a parallel part and perpendicular residual. The picture is a right-triangle decomposition, with signed direction information retained. Magnitudes alone can lose the sign or confuse which part was projected, so show components when requested.","diagram":null,"html":"Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a=\\mathbf p+\\mathbf r</annotation></semantics></math></span> where p is the projection and r = a-p. Dotting r with b gives zero by the projection formula. This verifies that the decomposition has a parallel part and perpendicular residual. The picture is a right-triangle decomposition, with signed direction information retained. Magnitudes alone can lose the sign or confuse which part was projected, so show components when requested."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply the decomposition to a physical or geometric problem","text":"A force component along a direction, a displacement resolved along a road or a point’s perpendicular distance from a line can use projection. For a line through a point A rather than the origin, first form the displacement from A to the point of interest. Units matter: force components remain force, not distance. Distinguish a scalar component from the magnitude of the projected vector, especially when the component is negative.","diagram":null,"html":"A force component along a direction, a displacement resolved along a road or a point’s perpendicular distance from a line can use projection. For a line through a point A rather than the origin, first form the displacement from A to the point of interest. Units matter: force components remain force, not distance. Distinguish a scalar component from the magnitude of the projected vector, especially when the component is negative."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-vectors-projection-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find scalar and vector projections of $\\mathbf a=(2,3)$ onto $\\mathbf b=(1,2)$, then the perpendicular residual. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Dot product is $2+6=8$ and $|\\mathbf b|^2=5$.","[1] Scalar projection is $8/\\sqrt5$.","[1] Vector projection is $(8/5)(1,2)=(8/5,16/5)$.","[1] Residual is $(2,3)-(8/5,16/5)=(2/5,-1/5)$, whose dot product with b is zero."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find scalar and vector projections of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a=(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> onto <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf b=(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then the perpendicular residual. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Dot product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+6=8</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf b|^2=5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Scalar projection is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8/\\sqrt5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Vector projection is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>16</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(8/5)(1,2)=(8/5,16/5)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Residual is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>16</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,3)-(8/5,16/5)=(2/5,-1/5)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, whose dot product with b is zero."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-projection-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Project $\\mathbf a=(-3,4)$ onto the positive x direction and explain the sign of the scalar component. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use unit direction $(1,0)$.","[1] Scalar projection is the dot product -3.","[1] Vector projection is $-3(1,0)=(-3,0)$.","[1] Negative scalar projection means the component points opposite the chosen positive x direction; its magnitude is 3."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Project <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a=(-3,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span> onto the positive x direction and explain the sign of the scalar component. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use unit direction <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Scalar projection is the dot product -3.","[1] Vector projection is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3(1,0)=(-3,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Negative scalar projection means the component points opposite the chosen positive x direction; its magnitude is 3."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-projection-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Project $(4,2)$ onto $(1,1)$ and calculate the perpendicular residual magnitude. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Dot product is 6 and direction squared magnitude is 2.","[1] Projection is $3(1,1)=(3,3)$.","[1] Residual is $(1,-1)$.","[1] Residual magnitude is $\\sqrt2$, with dot product zero against $(1,1)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Project <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> onto <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and calculate the perpendicular residual magnitude. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Dot product is 6 and direction squared magnitude is 2.","[1] Projection is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3(1,1)=(3,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Residual is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Residual magnitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with dot product zero against <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-projection-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Show that projecting a onto b and onto 2b gives the same vector for nonzero b. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\operatorname{proj}_{2\\mathbf b}\\mathbf a=((\\mathbf a\\cdot2\\mathbf b)/|2\\mathbf b|^2)(2\\mathbf b)$.","[1] Numerator scales by 2 and squared magnitude by 4.","[1] The final direction vector contributes another factor 2, so the factors cancel.","[1] The expression equals $(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)\\mathbf b/|\\mathbf b|^2$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Show that projecting a onto b and onto 2b gives the same vector for nonzero b. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">proj</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{proj}_{2\\mathbf b}\\mathbf a=((\\mathbf a\\cdot2\\mathbf b)/|2\\mathbf b|^2)(2\\mathbf b)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Numerator scales by 2 and squared magnitude by 4.","[1] The final direction vector contributes another factor 2, so the factors cancel.","[1] The expression equals <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)\\mathbf b/|\\mathbf b|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-projection-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"A force $(6,8)$ N is resolved along unit direction $(3/5,4/5)$. Find its scalar component and vector component. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The direction magnitude is 1.","[1] Scalar component is $6(3/5)+8(4/5)=10$ N.","[1] Vector component is $10(3/5,4/5)=(6,8)$ N.","[1] The entire force is already along that direction, so the perpendicular residual is zero."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A force <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(6,8)</annotation></semantics></math></span> N is resolved along unit direction <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3/5,4/5)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find its scalar component and vector component. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The direction magnitude is 1.","[1] Scalar component is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6(3/5)+8(4/5)=10</annotation></semantics></math></span> N.","[1] Vector component is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10(3/5,4/5)=(6,8)</annotation></semantics></math></span> N.","[1] The entire force is already along that direction, so the perpendicular residual is zero."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-projection-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Find the distance from P = $(3,4)$ to the line through the origin with direction $(1,0)$ using projection. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Position displacement is $(3,4)$.","[1] Projection onto the x-axis is $(3,0)$.","[1] Perpendicular residual is $(0,4)$.","[1] Its magnitude is 4 units, the perpendicular distance to the line."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the distance from P = <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span> to the line through the origin with direction <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> using projection. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Position displacement is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Projection onto the x-axis is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Perpendicular residual is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its magnitude is 4 units, the perpendicular distance to the line."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which denominator occurs in vector projection onto nonzero b?","o":["$|\\mathbf b|^2$.","$|\\mathbf b|$ while still multiplying by b.","Zero regardless of direction.","$|\\mathbf a|^2$ in every case."],"a":0,"why":"The vector projection coefficient multiplies b, so its denominator is b’s squared magnitude.","qHtml":"Which denominator occurs in vector projection onto nonzero b?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf b|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf b|</annotation></semantics></math></span> while still multiplying by b.","Zero regardless of direction.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf a|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> in every case."],"whyHtml":"The vector projection coefficient multiplies b, so its denominator is b’s squared magnitude."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-vectors-applications","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Vectors in the plane","title":"Displacement, velocity and force models","outcomes":["1.3.14"],"icon":"📐","scope":"State the axes, units and reference frame · Average velocity uses net displacement · Relative velocity is a subtraction with named order · Force equilibrium is a vector condition","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Displacement, velocity and force models</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>State the axes, units and reference frame</li><li>Average velocity uses net displacement</li><li>Relative velocity is a subtraction with named order</li><li>Force equilibrium is a vector condition</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"State the axes, units and reference frame","text":"A physical vector model needs a coordinate convention such as east-positive x and north-positive y. Components of displacement, velocity and force then have consistent signs and units. Ground velocity can differ from velocity relative to air or water; vector addition connects them in the stated reference frames. Do not add vectors of unrelated units simply because their components have the same numerical size. A sketch should show what each arrow represents and what is held constant.","diagram":null,"html":"A physical vector model needs a coordinate convention such as east-positive x and north-positive y. Components of displacement, velocity and force then have consistent signs and units. Ground velocity can differ from velocity relative to air or water; vector addition connects them in the stated reference frames. Do not add vectors of unrelated units simply because their components have the same numerical size. A sketch should show what each arrow represents and what is held constant."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Average velocity uses net displacement","text":"Average velocity is total displacement divided by elapsed time. Average speed is total distance travelled divided by time. A journey returning to its start can have zero average velocity and positive average speed. If velocities vary between stages, calculate each displacement using its duration, sum the displacements and divide by total time. Averaging velocity values without time weights is valid only under the appropriate equal-duration condition.","diagram":"vector-add","html":"Average velocity is total displacement divided by elapsed time. Average speed is total distance travelled divided by time. A journey returning to its start can have zero average velocity and positive average speed. If velocities vary between stages, calculate each displacement using its duration, sum the displacements and divide by total time. Averaging velocity values without time weights is valid only under the appropriate equal-duration condition."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Relative velocity is a subtraction with named order","text":"Velocity of A relative to B is $\\mathbf v_A-\\mathbf v_B$ when both are expressed in the same frame. Reversing the order reverses the vector. The magnitude of relative velocity need not equal the difference of speed magnitudes. For a river or wind model, establish whether the known direction is the vehicle’s heading relative to the medium or its track relative to the ground. Those can require different unknown components.","diagram":null,"html":"Velocity of A relative to B is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf v_A-\\mathbf v_B</annotation></semantics></math></span> when both are expressed in the same frame. Reversing the order reverses the vector. The magnitude of relative velocity need not equal the difference of speed magnitudes. For a river or wind model, establish whether the known direction is the vehicle’s heading relative to the medium or its track relative to the ground. Those can require different unknown components."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Force equilibrium is a vector condition","text":"A stationary idealised object in translational equilibrium has zero resultant force. Two or more forces can cancel components while each remains nonzero. An equilibrant is the negative of the resultant of the other forces. This is not the same as requiring every separate force magnitude to be equal. A mechanics model may need other physical information beyond vector algebra; state the assumptions supplied and interpret the calculated direction and units in the context.","diagram":null,"html":"A stationary idealised object in translational equilibrium has zero resultant force. Two or more forces can cancel components while each remains nonzero. An equilibrant is the negative of the resultant of the other forces. This is not the same as requiring every separate force magnitude to be equal. A mechanics model may need other physical information beyond vector algebra; state the assumptions supplied and interpret the calculated direction and units in the context."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-vectors-applications-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"A particle has velocity $(3,0)$ m s$^{-1}$ for 4 s, then $(0,2)$ m s$^{-1}$ for 3 s. Find average velocity and average speed. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Stage displacements are $(12,0)$ m and $(0,6)$ m.","[1] Net displacement is $(12,6)$ m over 7 s, so average velocity is $(12/7,6/7)$ m s$^{-1}$.","[1] Path distance is $12+6=18$ m.","[1] Average speed is $18/7$ m s$^{-1}$, distinct from the magnitude of average velocity."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A particle has velocity <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> m s<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> for 4 s, then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> m s<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> for 3 s. Find average velocity and average speed. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Stage displacements are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(12,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> m and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,6)</annotation></semantics></math></span> m.","[1] Net displacement is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(12,6)</annotation></semantics></math></span> m over 7 s, so average velocity is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>7</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(12/7,6/7)</annotation></semantics></math></span> m s<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Path distance is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12+6=18</annotation></semantics></math></span> m.","[1] Average speed is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>18</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">18/7</annotation></semantics></math></span> m s<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, distinct from the magnitude of average velocity."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-applications-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"A boat travels at $(4,0)$ m s$^{-1}$ relative to water while the current is $(0,3)$ m s$^{-1}$ relative to ground. Find its ground velocity and speed. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Ground velocity is boat-relative-water plus water-relative-ground.","[1] Sum is $(4,3)$ m s$^{-1}$.","[1] Speed is $\\sqrt{4^2+3^2}=5$ m s$^{-1}$.","[1] Its ground track differs from its eastward heading relative to water."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A boat travels at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> m s<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> relative to water while the current is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> m s<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> relative to ground. Find its ground velocity and speed. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Ground velocity is boat-relative-water plus water-relative-ground.","[1] Sum is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> m s<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Speed is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{4^2+3^2}=5</annotation></semantics></math></span> m s<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its ground track differs from its eastward heading relative to water."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-applications-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Two forces $(5,-2)$ N and $(-1,7)$ N act on an idealised point object. Find a third force making the resultant zero. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The first two sum to $(4,5)$ N.","[1] Equilibrium requires their sum plus the third force to be zero.","[1] The third force is $(-4,-5)$ N.","[1] Check component totals $5-1-4=0$ and $-2+7-5=0$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Two forces <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(5,-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> N and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1,7)</annotation></semantics></math></span> N act on an idealised point object. Find a third force making the resultant zero. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The first two sum to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,5)</annotation></semantics></math></span> N.","[1] Equilibrium requires their sum plus the third force to be zero.","[1] The third force is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-4,-5)</annotation></semantics></math></span> N.","[1] Check component totals <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5-1-4=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2+7-5=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-applications-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"A has velocity $(6,2)$ and B has velocity $(1,5)$ m s$^{-1}$. Find A’s velocity relative to B and its magnitude. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Relative velocity in the named order is $\\mathbf v_A-\\mathbf v_B$.","[1] This gives $(5,-3)$ m s$^{-1}$.","[1] Magnitude is $\\sqrt{25+9}=\\sqrt{34}$ m s$^{-1}$.","[1] Reversing the named order would give $(-5,3)$, not the same directed vector."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A has velocity <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(6,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and B has velocity <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,5)</annotation></semantics></math></span> m s<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find A’s velocity relative to B and its magnitude. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Relative velocity in the named order is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf v_A-\\mathbf v_B</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(5,-3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> m s<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Magnitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>34</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{25+9}=\\sqrt{34}</annotation></semantics></math></span> m s<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Reversing the named order would give <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-5,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, not the same directed vector."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-applications-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"A walker travels 30 m east and 30 m west in a total of 40 s. Find average velocity and average speed. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Net displacement is zero because the endpoint equals the start.","[1] Average velocity is the zero vector divided by 40 s, hence zero.","[1] Total path distance is 60 m.","[1] Average speed is $60/40=1.5$ m s$^{-1}$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A walker travels 30 m east and 30 m west in a total of 40 s. Find average velocity and average speed. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Net displacement is zero because the endpoint equals the start.","[1] Average velocity is the zero vector divided by 40 s, hence zero.","[1] Total path distance is 60 m.","[1] Average speed is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>60</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>40</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60/40=1.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> m s<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-applications-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"An aircraft has air-relative velocity $(120,0)$ km h$^{-1}$ and wind velocity $(-20,30)$ km h$^{-1}$. Find ground velocity, speed and the track angle north of east. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Ground velocity is $(100,30)$ km h$^{-1}$.","[1] Speed is $\\sqrt{100^2+30^2}=10\\sqrt{109}\\approx104.4$ km h$^{-1}$.","[1] The components place the track in the northeast quadrant.","[1] Angle is $\\tan^{-1}(30/100)\\approx16.7^\\circ$ north of east, with rounding only at the end."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"An aircraft has air-relative velocity <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>120</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(120,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> km h<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and wind velocity <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>30</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-20,30)</annotation></semantics></math></span> km h<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find ground velocity, speed and the track angle north of east. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Ground velocity is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>100</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>30</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(100,30)</annotation></semantics></math></span> km h<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Speed is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>100</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>30</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><msqrt><mn>109</mn></msqrt><mo>≈</mo><mn>104.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{100^2+30^2}=10\\sqrt{109}\\approx104.4</annotation></semantics></math></span> km h<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The components place the track in the northeast quadrant.","[1] Angle is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>30</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>100</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><msup><mn>16.7</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tan^{-1}(30/100)\\approx16.7^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> north of east, with rounding only at the end."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which quantity defines average velocity?","o":["Net displacement divided by total elapsed time.","Total distance divided by time.","The unweighted average of any two speed readings.","The sum of force magnitudes."],"a":0,"why":"Velocity is directed; distance/time instead defines average speed.","qHtml":"Which quantity defines average velocity?","oHtml":["Net displacement divided by total elapsed time.","Total distance divided by time.","The unweighted average of any two speed readings.","The sum of force magnitudes."],"whyHtml":"Velocity is directed; distance/time instead defines average speed."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-vectors-quadrilaterals","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Vectors in the plane","title":"Rhombus diagonals and midpoint-parallelogram proofs","outcomes":["1.3.15","1.3.16"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Represent a parallelogram by adjacent sides · Prove the perpendicular-diagonal equivalence · Midpoints of quadrilateral sides form a parallelogram · Keep the theorem’s conditions visible","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Rhombus diagonals and midpoint-parallelogram proofs</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Represent a parallelogram by adjacent sides</li><li>Prove the perpendicular-diagonal equivalence</li><li>Midpoints of quadrilateral sides form a parallelogram</li><li>Keep the theorem’s conditions visible</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Represent a parallelogram by adjacent sides","text":"Place one vertex at the origin with nonparallel nonzero adjacent side vectors a and b. The vertices are $\\mathbf0,\\mathbf a,\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b,\\mathbf b$. Diagonals have vectors a+b and b-a in suitable directions. This representation encodes parallel opposite sides rather than merely assuming their appearance. Equal adjacent side magnitudes characterise a rhombus within this nondegenerate parallelogram setting.","diagram":null,"html":"Place one vertex at the origin with nonparallel nonzero adjacent side vectors a and b. The vertices are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf0,\\mathbf a,\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b,\\mathbf b</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Diagonals have vectors a+b and b-a in suitable directions. This representation encodes parallel opposite sides rather than merely assuming their appearance. Equal adjacent side magnitudes characterise a rhombus within this nondegenerate parallelogram setting."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Prove the perpendicular-diagonal equivalence","text":"The diagonal dot product is $(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b)\\cdot(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)=|\\mathbf b|^2-|\\mathbf a|^2$ because cross terms cancel. It is zero exactly when adjacent side magnitudes are equal. Nonzero diagonals and the parallelogram hypothesis make the zero dot product a perpendicularity statement and equal adjacent sides make the shape a rhombus. This proves both directions, rather than only showing one drawn rhombus has perpendicular diagonals.","diagram":"parallelogram","html":"The diagonal dot product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b)\\cdot(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)=|\\mathbf b|^2-|\\mathbf a|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> because cross terms cancel. It is zero exactly when adjacent side magnitudes are equal. Nonzero diagonals and the parallelogram hypothesis make the zero dot product a perpendicularity statement and equal adjacent sides make the shape a rhombus. This proves both directions, rather than only showing one drawn rhombus has perpendicular diagonals."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Midpoints of quadrilateral sides form a parallelogram","text":"Let original vertex position vectors be a,b,c,d in order, with side midpoints E,F,G,H. Then $\\overrightarrow{EF}=(\\mathbf c-\\mathbf a)/2$ and $\\overrightarrow{HG}=(\\mathbf c-\\mathbf a)/2$; likewise the other opposite directed sides match. Equal and parallel opposite sides establish the midpoint parallelogram in the suitable nondegenerate configuration. The result uses the original diagonals as midpoint-side directions and does not require the original quadrilateral to be a parallelogram.","diagram":null,"html":"Let original vertex position vectors be a,b,c,d in order, with side midpoints E,F,G,H. Then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overrightarrow{EF}=(\\mathbf c-\\mathbf a)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overrightarrow{HG}=(\\mathbf c-\\mathbf a)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>; likewise the other opposite directed sides match. Equal and parallel opposite sides establish the midpoint parallelogram in the suitable nondegenerate configuration. The result uses the original diagonals as midpoint-side directions and does not require the original quadrilateral to be a parallelogram."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Keep the theorem’s conditions visible","text":"Perpendicular diagonals in a general quadrilateral do not alone prove a rhombus: the parallelogram condition matters. A midpoint theorem can be established with position-vector subtraction without measuring slopes, which also avoids special handling of vertical lines. Write directed sides in matching orientation to avoid false sign conflicts. A proof should state the arbitrary vectors, compute the relevant differences and conclude the geometric result with nondegeneracy understood.","diagram":null,"html":"Perpendicular diagonals in a general quadrilateral do not alone prove a rhombus: the parallelogram condition matters. A midpoint theorem can be established with position-vector subtraction without measuring slopes, which also avoids special handling of vertical lines. Write directed sides in matching orientation to avoid false sign conflicts. A proof should state the arbitrary vectors, compute the relevant differences and conclude the geometric result with nondegeneracy understood."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-vectors-quadrilaterals-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Prove a nondegenerate parallelogram has perpendicular diagonals if and only if it is a rhombus, using adjacent side vectors a,b. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The diagonals are a+b and b-a.","[1] Their dot product expands to $|\\mathbf b|^2-|\\mathbf a|^2$.","[1] It vanishes exactly when $|\\mathbf a|=|\\mathbf b|$, since lengths are nonnegative.","[1] Equal adjacent sides in a parallelogram give a rhombus; conversely a rhombus has equal adjacent sides and therefore perpendicular diagonals."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove a nondegenerate parallelogram has perpendicular diagonals if and only if it is a rhombus, using adjacent side vectors a,b. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The diagonals are a+b and b-a.","[1] Their dot product expands to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf b|^2-|\\mathbf a|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] It vanishes exactly when <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf a|=|\\mathbf b|</annotation></semantics></math></span>, since lengths are nonnegative.","[1] Equal adjacent sides in a parallelogram give a rhombus; conversely a rhombus has equal adjacent sides and therefore perpendicular diagonals."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-quadrilaterals-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"A quadrilateral has vertices A,B,C,D with position vectors a,b,c,d. Prove side midpoints E,F,G,H form a parallelogram. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Midpoint positions are $(a+b)/2,(b+c)/2,(c+d)/2,(d+a)/2$.","[1] Subtract to get $\\overrightarrow{EF}=(c-a)/2=\\overrightarrow{HG}$.","[1] Similarly $\\overrightarrow{FG}=(d-b)/2=\\overrightarrow{EH}$.","[1] Both pairs of opposite directed sides are equal and parallel, proving the midpoint parallelogram in the suitable configuration."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A quadrilateral has vertices A,B,C,D with position vectors a,b,c,d. Prove side midpoints E,F,G,H form a parallelogram. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Midpoint positions are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(a+b)/2,(b+c)/2,(c+d)/2,(d+a)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Subtract to get <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">→</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overrightarrow{EF}=(c-a)/2=\\overrightarrow{HG}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Similarly <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">→</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overrightarrow{FG}=(d-b)/2=\\overrightarrow{EH}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both pairs of opposite directed sides are equal and parallel, proving the midpoint parallelogram in the suitable configuration."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-quadrilaterals-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"A parallelogram has adjacent side vectors $(2,1)$ and $(-1,2)$. Verify that it is a rhombus and that its diagonals are perpendicular. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Both side magnitudes are $\\sqrt5$, so the parallelogram is a rhombus.","[1] Diagonals can be $(1,3)$ and $(-3,1)$.","[1] Their dot product is $1(-3)+3(1)=0$.","[1] Both diagonals are nonzero, confirming perpendicularity."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A parallelogram has adjacent side vectors <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Verify that it is a rhombus and that its diagonals are perpendicular. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Both side magnitudes are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so the parallelogram is a rhombus.","[1] Diagonals can be <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-3,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Their dot product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1(-3)+3(1)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both diagonals are nonzero, confirming perpendicularity."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-quadrilaterals-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"A parallelogram has adjacent vectors $(3,0)$ and $(1,2)$. Decide whether its diagonals are perpendicular. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Side squared magnitudes are 9 and 5.","[1] Diagonal dot product in the b-a convention is $5-9=-4$.","[1] It is not zero, so diagonals are not perpendicular.","[1] The unequal adjacent lengths also show it is not a rhombus."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A parallelogram has adjacent vectors <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Decide whether its diagonals are perpendicular. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Side squared magnitudes are 9 and 5.","[1] Diagonal dot product in the b-a convention is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5-9=-4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] It is not zero, so diagonals are not perpendicular.","[1] The unequal adjacent lengths also show it is not a rhombus."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-quadrilaterals-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"A quadrilateral’s diagonals are perpendicular. What can be concluded about its midpoint parallelogram’s adjacent sides? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The midpoint parallelogram sides are half of the original diagonal vectors in matching directions.","[1] Scaling does not change perpendicularity.","[1] Its adjacent sides are therefore perpendicular.","[1] A nondegenerate parallelogram with perpendicular adjacent sides is a rectangle; it need not be a square without equal diagonal lengths."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A quadrilateral’s diagonals are perpendicular. What can be concluded about its midpoint parallelogram’s adjacent sides? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The midpoint parallelogram sides are half of the original diagonal vectors in matching directions.","[1] Scaling does not change perpendicularity.","[1] Its adjacent sides are therefore perpendicular.","[1] A nondegenerate parallelogram with perpendicular adjacent sides is a rectangle; it need not be a square without equal diagonal lengths."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-quadrilaterals-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Explain why a proof using a+b and b-a cannot automatically prove the perpendicular-diagonal converse for every quadrilateral. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Those diagonal expressions came from the special vertices $0,a,a+b,b$.","[1] This representation already encodes the parallelogram property.","[1] A general quadrilateral need not have its opposite vertex at a+b.","[1] The proof’s hypothesis must remain stated; removing it changes the claim and allows counterexamples such as suitable kites."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why a proof using a+b and b-a cannot automatically prove the perpendicular-diagonal converse for every quadrilateral. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Those diagonal expressions came from the special vertices <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,a,a+b,b</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This representation already encodes the parallelogram property.","[1] A general quadrilateral need not have its opposite vertex at a+b.","[1] The proof’s hypothesis must remain stated; removing it changes the claim and allows counterexamples such as suitable kites."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which hypothesis is essential for perpendicular diagonals to characterise a rhombus in this theorem?","o":["The figure is a nondegenerate parallelogram.","It is any quadrilateral whatsoever.","One diagonal happens to be horizontal.","The drawing is symmetric on the page."],"a":0,"why":"The vector identity uses parallelogram adjacent sides and their resulting diagonal expressions.","qHtml":"Which hypothesis is essential for perpendicular diagonals to characterise a rhombus in this theorem?","oHtml":["The figure is a nondegenerate parallelogram.","It is any quadrilateral whatsoever.","One diagonal happens to be horizontal.","The drawing is symmetric on the page."],"whyHtml":"The vector identity uses parallelogram adjacent sides and their resulting diagonal expressions."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-vectors-diagonals","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Geometry, combinatorics and vectors","area":"Vectors in the plane","title":"Parallelogram law and geometric vector proof","outcomes":["1.3.17"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Square a magnitude using a dot product · Derive the parallelogram law · Use the law with sufficient information · Combine exact identities with a clear geometric conclusion","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Parallelogram law and geometric vector proof</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Square a magnitude using a dot product</li><li>Derive the parallelogram law</li><li>Use the law with sufficient information</li><li>Combine exact identities with a clear geometric conclusion</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Square a magnitude using a dot product","text":"The expression $|\\mathbf a|^2$ means $\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf a$, a scalar. Expanding the squared magnitude of a sum gives self terms and a cross term. Expanding a difference gives the same self terms with the opposite cross-term sign. Adding these expressions cancels direction-dependent cross terms, which is why the parallelogram law holds even when adjacent sides are not perpendicular.","diagram":null,"html":"The expression <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf a|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> means <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf a</annotation></semantics></math></span>, a scalar. Expanding the squared magnitude of a sum gives self terms and a cross term. Expanding a difference gives the same self terms with the opposite cross-term sign. Adding these expressions cancels direction-dependent cross terms, which is why the parallelogram law holds even when adjacent sides are not perpendicular."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Derive the parallelogram law","text":"For adjacent side vectors a,b, diagonal vectors can be a+b and a-b. Their squared lengths sum to $2|\\mathbf a|^2+2|\\mathbf b|^2$. The four side squared lengths also sum to that expression because each adjacent magnitude occurs twice. Thus the sum of squared diagonal lengths equals the sum of squared lengths of all four sides. Do not replace squared lengths by ordinary lengths: the square is essential.","diagram":"parallelogram","html":"For adjacent side vectors a,b, diagonal vectors can be a+b and a-b. Their squared lengths sum to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2|\\mathbf a|^2+2|\\mathbf b|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The four side squared lengths also sum to that expression because each adjacent magnitude occurs twice. Thus the sum of squared diagonal lengths equals the sum of squared lengths of all four sides. Do not replace squared lengths by ordinary lengths: the square is essential."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use the law with sufficient information","text":"Knowing both diagonals gives the sum of adjacent side squares, not each side individually without further information. In a rhombus, equal adjacent side lengths allow one side to be determined. In a rectangle, adjacent sides are perpendicular and diagonal lengths agree, consistent with Pythagoras. The law can verify data or support a proof, but it is not a licence to classify a completely general quadrilateral as a parallelogram from one numerical equality alone.","diagram":null,"html":"Knowing both diagonals gives the sum of adjacent side squares, not each side individually without further information. In a rhombus, equal adjacent side lengths allow one side to be determined. In a rectangle, adjacent sides are perpendicular and diagonal lengths agree, consistent with Pythagoras. The law can verify data or support a proof, but it is not a licence to classify a completely general quadrilateral as a parallelogram from one numerical equality alone."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Combine exact identities with a clear geometric conclusion","text":"An algebraic result should be translated back to the labelled sides or diagonals. Check the number of times each side appears and the units: every term has length squared. If angles or a dot product are supplied, individual diagonal squares can also be calculated from the cross terms. Keep signs attached to the chosen diagonal vector; reversing a diagonal leaves its squared magnitude unchanged. An exact identity is stronger than an approximate diagram measurement.","diagram":null,"html":"An algebraic result should be translated back to the labelled sides or diagonals. Check the number of times each side appears and the units: every term has length squared. If angles or a dot product are supplied, individual diagonal squares can also be calculated from the cross terms. Keep signs attached to the chosen diagonal vector; reversing a diagonal leaves its squared magnitude unchanged. An exact identity is stronger than an approximate diagram measurement."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-vectors-diagonals-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Prove $|a+b|^2+|a-b|^2=2|a|^2+2|b|^2$ using dot products. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Expand $|a+b|^2=|a|^2+2a\\cdot b+|b|^2$.","[1] Expand $|a-b|^2=|a|^2-2a\\cdot b+|b|^2$.","[1] Add to cancel the cross terms.","[1] The result is $2|a|^2+2|b|^2$, establishing the identity."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|a+b|^2+|a-b|^2=2|a|^2+2|b|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> using dot products. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Expand <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|a+b|^2=|a|^2+2a\\cdot b+|b|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Expand <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|a-b|^2=|a|^2-2a\\cdot b+|b|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Add to cancel the cross terms.","[1] The result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2|a|^2+2|b|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, establishing the identity."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-diagonals-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"A parallelogram has adjacent side lengths 3 and 4, with side-vector dot product 6. Find both diagonal squared lengths and verify the law. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Sum-diagonal square is $9+16+2(6)=37$.","[1] Difference-diagonal square is $9+16-2(6)=13$.","[1] Their sum is 50.","[1] Four side squares sum to $2(9)+2(16)=50$, verifying the law."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A parallelogram has adjacent side lengths 3 and 4, with side-vector dot product 6. Find both diagonal squared lengths and verify the law. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Sum-diagonal square is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>37</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9+16+2(6)=37</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Difference-diagonal square is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9+16-2(6)=13</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Their sum is 50.","[1] Four side squares sum to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(9)+2(16)=50</annotation></semantics></math></span>, verifying the law."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-diagonals-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"A rhombus has diagonal lengths 6 and 8. Find its side length using the parallelogram law. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] If each side length is s, four side squares sum to $4s^2$.","[1] The diagonal-square sum is $6^2+8^2=100$.","[1] Hence $4s^2=100$ and $s^2=25$.","[1] Positive side length is 5."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A rhombus has diagonal lengths 6 and 8. Find its side length using the parallelogram law. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] If each side length is s, four side squares sum to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4s^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The diagonal-square sum is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>6</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>8</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6^2+8^2=100</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4s^2=100</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2=25</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Positive side length is 5."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-diagonals-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"A parallelogram has one side length 5 and diagonal lengths 8 and 10. Find the other adjacent side length. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let the unknown adjacent side length be b.","[1] $8^2+10^2=2(5^2+b^2)$.","[1] $164=50+2b^2$, so $b^2=57$.","[1] $b=\\sqrt{57}$ as a positive length."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A parallelogram has one side length 5 and diagonal lengths 8 and 10. Find the other adjacent side length. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let the unknown adjacent side length be b.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>8</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8^2+10^2=2(5^2+b^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>164</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">164=50+2b^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>57</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b^2=57</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>57</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=\\sqrt{57}</annotation></semantics></math></span> as a positive length."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-diagonals-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"For a = $(1,2)$ and b = $(3,-1)$, verify the parallelogram law numerically. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Sum vector is $(4,1)$ with squared magnitude 17.","[1] Difference vector is $(-2,3)$ with squared magnitude 13.","[1] Their sum is 30.","[1] $2|a|^2+2|b|^2=2(5)+2(10)=30$, matching the diagonal result."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"For a = <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and b = <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, verify the parallelogram law numerically. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Sum vector is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with squared magnitude 17.","[1] Difference vector is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-2,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with squared magnitude 13.","[1] Their sum is 30.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>b</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2|a|^2+2|b|^2=2(5)+2(10)=30</annotation></semantics></math></span>, matching the diagonal result."]},{"id":"mas11-vectors-diagonals-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A student replaces squared lengths by ordinary lengths in the parallelogram law. Refute this using a unit square. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Four unit sides have length sum 4.","[1] The two diagonals each have length $\\sqrt2$, giving ordinary sum $2\\sqrt2$, not 4.","[1] Their squared lengths instead sum to $2+2=4$.","[1] This equals the sum of four side squares, confirming that the true law requires squares."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A student replaces squared lengths by ordinary lengths in the parallelogram law. Refute this using a unit square. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Four unit sides have length sum 4.","[1] The two diagonals each have length <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving ordinary sum <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, not 4.","[1] Their squared lengths instead sum to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+2=4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This equals the sum of four side squares, confirming that the true law requires squares."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What does the parallelogram law equate?","o":["The sum of diagonal squared lengths and the sum of all four side squared lengths.","The sum of ordinary diagonal and side lengths.","Each diagonal to each side.","Every quadrilateral’s area to its perimeter."],"a":0,"why":"Cross terms cancel in squared-magnitude expansions, yielding the stated length-squared identity.","qHtml":"What does the parallelogram law equate?","oHtml":["The sum of diagonal squared lengths and the sum of all four side squared lengths.","The sum of ordinary diagonal and side lengths.","Each diagonal to each side.","Every quadrilateral’s area to its perimeter."],"whyHtml":"Cross terms cancel in squared-magnitude expansions, yielding the stated length-squared identity."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-trig-equations","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Trigonometry","title":"All trigonometric solutions and transformed intervals","outcomes":["2.1.1"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Solve in the transformed angle first · General solutions depend on the function · Preserve units, boundaries and exclusions · Verify every candidate in the original equation","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>All trigonometric solutions and transformed intervals</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Solve in the transformed angle first</li><li>General solutions depend on the function</li><li>Preserve units, boundaries and exclusions</li><li>Verify every candidate in the original equation</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Solve in the transformed angle first","text":"For $\\sin(a(x-b))=c$, introduce $u=a(x-b)$ and translate the required x interval into its u interval before listing solutions. A transformation can sweep through several cycles even if x occupies one familiar cycle. A calculator’s inverse sine supplies one principal value, not the full solution set. Use the unit circle or symmetry to find the second sine branch and add periods, then convert each valid transformed angle back to x.","diagram":null,"html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(a(x-b))=c</annotation></semantics></math></span>, introduce <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=a(x-b)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and translate the required x interval into its u interval before listing solutions. A transformation can sweep through several cycles even if x occupies one familiar cycle. A calculator’s inverse sine supplies one principal value, not the full solution set. Use the unit circle or symmetry to find the second sine branch and add periods, then convert each valid transformed angle back to x."},{"kind":"learn","title":"General solutions depend on the function","text":"If $\\sin u=\\sin\\alpha$, solutions are $u=\\alpha+2k\\pi$ or $u=\\pi-\\alpha+2k\\pi$. For cosine, $u=\\pm\\alpha+2k\\pi$ when $\\cos\\alpha=c$. For tangent, $u=\\alpha+k\\pi$. Here k is an integer and special endpoint cases can produce duplicate descriptions, which should be merged. The sine and cosine output must lie in [-1,1]; otherwise there are no real solutions under those equations.","diagram":"trig","html":"If <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin u=\\sin\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span>, solutions are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=\\alpha+2k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=\\pi-\\alpha+2k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For cosine, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=\\pm\\alpha+2k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> when <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos\\alpha=c</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For tangent, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=\\alpha+k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Here k is an integer and special endpoint cases can produce duplicate descriptions, which should be merged. The sine and cosine output must lie in [-1,1]; otherwise there are no real solutions under those equations."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Preserve units, boundaries and exclusions","text":"Radian and degree forms have different period constants. Keep one system through an equation and check calculator mode when evaluating. A closed endpoint is included, an open endpoint excluded; an interval crossing several periods needs every valid branch. Tangent is undefined at odd multiples of $\\pi/2$, so a rearrangement introducing those values requires checking. If a multiplier a is negative, interval endpoints reverse in the transformed angle and must be ordered correctly.","diagram":null,"html":"Radian and degree forms have different period constants. Keep one system through an equation and check calculator mode when evaluating. A closed endpoint is included, an open endpoint excluded; an interval crossing several periods needs every valid branch. Tangent is undefined at odd multiples of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so a rearrangement introducing those values requires checking. If a multiplier a is negative, interval endpoints reverse in the transformed angle and must be ordered correctly."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Verify every candidate in the original equation","text":"Algebraic operations such as squaring or multiplying by a potentially zero expression can introduce or remove candidates. Substitute exact solutions where practical and test all original restrictions. A full solution identifies no missing cycles and no duplicates, then reports the requested interval or general integer-family form. Do not round exact familiar angles unnecessarily; use exact multiples of pi when the data permit them.","diagram":null,"html":"Algebraic operations such as squaring or multiplying by a potentially zero expression can introduce or remove candidates. Substitute exact solutions where practical and test all original restrictions. A full solution identifies no missing cycles and no duplicates, then reports the requested interval or general integer-family form. Do not round exact familiar angles unnecessarily; use exact multiples of pi when the data permit them."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-trig-equations-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Solve $\\sin(2x)=1/2$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let $u=2x$, so $0\\le u<4\\pi$.","[1] In this range $u=\\pi/6,5\\pi/6,13\\pi/6,17\\pi/6$.","[1] Divide each by 2: $x=\\pi/12,5\\pi/12,13\\pi/12,17\\pi/12$.","[1] All four satisfy the original interval; taking only one inverse-sine result would omit branches."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(2x)=1/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le x&lt;2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=2x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le u&lt;4\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] In this range <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>13</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>17</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=\\pi/6,5\\pi/6,13\\pi/6,17\\pi/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Divide each by 2: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>12</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>12</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>13</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>12</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>17</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pi/12,5\\pi/12,13\\pi/12,17\\pi/12</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] All four satisfy the original interval; taking only one inverse-sine result would omit branches."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-equations-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Solve $\\cos(x-\\pi/3)=1/2$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] General transformed angles are $x-\\pi/3=\\pm\\pi/3+2k\\pi$.","[1] Hence $x=0+2k\\pi$ or $x=2\\pi/3+2k\\pi$.","[1] The stated interval retains $x=0$ and $x=2\\pi/3$.","[1] The endpoint $2\\pi$ is excluded, while 0 is included."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(x-\\pi/3)=1/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le x&lt;2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] General transformed angles are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-\\pi/3=\\pm\\pi/3+2k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0+2k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2\\pi/3+2k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The stated interval retains <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The endpoint <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> is excluded, while 0 is included."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-equations-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Solve $\\tan(3x)=1$ for $0\\le x<\\pi$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] General transformed solutions are $3x=\\pi/4+k\\pi$.","[1] Thus $x=\\pi/12+k\\pi/3$.","[1] The interval permits $k=0,1,2$.","[1] Solutions are $\\pi/12,5\\pi/12,3\\pi/4$, none at a tangent singularity."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tan(3x)=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le x&lt;\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] General transformed solutions are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3x=\\pi/4+k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>12</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pi/12+k\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The interval permits <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0,1,2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Solutions are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>12</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>12</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/12,5\\pi/12,3\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>, none at a tangent singularity."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-equations-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Give all real solutions of $\\sin x=0$ in radians and explain why the two usual sine branches combine. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] One branch is $x=0+2k\\pi$.","[1] The second is $x=\\pi+2k\\pi$.","[1] Together they comprise every integer multiple of pi.","[1] Write $x=k\\pi$, $k\\in\\mathbb Z$, without duplicate counting."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Give all real solutions of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> in radians and explain why the two usual sine branches combine. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] One branch is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0+2k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The second is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pi+2k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Together they comprise every integer multiple of pi.","[1] Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\in\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math></span>, without duplicate counting."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-equations-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Solve $2\\cos x=3$ over the real numbers. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Rearrange to $\\cos x=3/2$.","[1] Real cosine lies in [-1,1].","[1] $3/2$ is outside that range.","[1] There are no real solutions; an inverse-cosine command cannot supply a real angle."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cos x=3</annotation></semantics></math></span> over the real numbers. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Rearrange to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos x=3/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Real cosine lies in [-1,1].","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is outside that range.","[1] There are no real solutions; an inverse-cosine command cannot supply a real angle."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-equations-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Solve $\\sin(x-30^\\circ)=-1/2$ for $0^\\circ\\le x<360^\\circ$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let $u=x-30^\\circ$, so $-30^\\circ\\le u<330^\\circ$.","[1] Sine equals -1/2 at $u=-30^\\circ,210^\\circ$ within that interval; $330^\\circ$ is excluded.","[1] Add 30 degrees to obtain $x=0^\\circ,240^\\circ$.","[1] Both satisfy the original equation and its endpoint convention."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>30</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(x-30^\\circ)=-1/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0^\\circ\\le x&lt;360^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>30</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=x-30^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>30</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>330</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-30^\\circ\\le u&lt;330^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Sine equals -1/2 at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>30</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mn>210</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=-30^\\circ,210^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> within that interval; <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>330</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">330^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> is excluded.","[1] Add 30 degrees to obtain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mn>240</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0^\\circ,240^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both satisfy the original equation and its endpoint convention."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Why translate the x interval when solving $\\sin(2x)=c$?","o":["The transformed angle can span more cycles than x.","Inverse sine automatically lists every branch.","The factor 2 never affects periodicity.","Only the first quadrant can contain solutions."],"a":0,"why":"Complete solutions require every transformed-angle branch inside the transformed interval.","qHtml":"Why translate the x interval when solving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(2x)=c</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","oHtml":["The transformed angle can span more cycles than x.","Inverse sine automatically lists every branch.","The factor 2 never affects periodicity.","Only the first quadrant can contain solutions."],"whyHtml":"Complete solutions require every transformed-angle branch inside the transformed interval."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-trig-graphs","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Trigonometry","title":"Transformed sine, cosine and tangent graphs","outcomes":["2.1.2","2.1.9"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Separate amplitude, period and vertical shift · Use transformed key points for a sketch · Tangent graphs have asymptotes and no amplitude · Use a periodic model within its interpretation","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Transformed sine, cosine and tangent graphs</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Separate amplitude, period and vertical shift</li><li>Use transformed key points for a sketch</li><li>Tangent graphs have asymptotes and no amplitude</li><li>Use a periodic model within its interpretation</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate amplitude, period and vertical shift","text":"For $y=A\\sin(B(x-C))+D$ or its cosine version, amplitude is $|A|$ and period is $2\\pi/|B|$ for nonzero B. The midline is y=D and range is $[D-|A|,D+|A|]$. A negative A reflects the graph across its midline, but does not make amplitude negative. The shift C appears inside the factored argument; if the expression is Bx+E, solve $B(x-C)=Bx+E$ to find $C=-E/B$.","diagram":null,"html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=A\\sin(B(x-C))+D</annotation></semantics></math></span> or its cosine version, amplitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|A|</annotation></semantics></math></span> and period is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/|B|</annotation></semantics></math></span> for nonzero B. The midline is y=D and range is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[D-|A|,D+|A|]</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A negative A reflects the graph across its midline, but does not make amplitude negative. The shift C appears inside the factored argument; if the expression is Bx+E, solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(x-C)=Bx+E</annotation></semantics></math></span> to find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=-E/B</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use transformed key points for a sketch","text":"Begin with a known cycle and map quarter-period points into the transformed x coordinates. For cosine, a positive amplitude starts at a maximum when the inner angle is zero; sine crosses the midline there. Label axes, midline, extrema and one period. A sketch should reflect exact important points rather than connect a few calculator samples blindly. If B is negative, note the equivalent identities or handle the parameter direction consistently.","diagram":"trig","html":"Begin with a known cycle and map quarter-period points into the transformed x coordinates. For cosine, a positive amplitude starts at a maximum when the inner angle is zero; sine crosses the midline there. Label axes, midline, extrema and one period. A sketch should reflect exact important points rather than connect a few calculator samples blindly. If B is negative, note the equivalent identities or handle the parameter direction consistently."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Tangent graphs have asymptotes and no amplitude","text":"For $A\\tan(B(x-C))+D$, period is $\\pi/|B|$ and vertical asymptotes occur when the inner angle is $\\pi/2+k\\pi$. There is no finite amplitude or bounded range. A vertical shift moves centres of branches but does not remove singularities. Sketch separate branches rather than drawing a continuous line through an asymptote. Intercepts and asymptotes must be obtained from the actual transformed argument.","diagram":null,"html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\tan(B(x-C))+D</annotation></semantics></math></span>, period is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/|B|</annotation></semantics></math></span> and vertical asymptotes occur when the inner angle is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/2+k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. There is no finite amplitude or bounded range. A vertical shift moves centres of branches but does not remove singularities. Sketch separate branches rather than drawing a continuous line through an asymptote. Intercepts and asymptotes must be obtained from the actual transformed argument."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use a periodic model within its interpretation","text":"A physical or statistical model’s period has units of the independent variable, while its amplitude and midline have dependent-variable units. Infer parameters from maxima, minima and cycle duration and choose a phase consistent with a stated event. A model can approximate repeating behaviour without predicting every future measurement exactly. State a reasonable domain and distinguish the first requested time from all times in one cycle.","diagram":null,"html":"A physical or statistical model’s period has units of the independent variable, while its amplitude and midline have dependent-variable units. Infer parameters from maxima, minima and cycle duration and choose a phase consistent with a stated event. A model can approximate repeating behaviour without predicting every future measurement exactly. State a reasonable domain and distinguish the first requested time from all times in one cycle."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-trig-graphs-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"For $y=3-2\\cos(2(x-\\pi/4))$, find amplitude, period, midline and range. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Amplitude is $|-2|=2$.","[1] Period is $2\\pi/2=\\pi$ and phase shift is $\\pi/4$ to the right.","[1] Midline is y=3.","[1] Range is [1,5]; the negative coefficient reverses maxima and minima, not amplitude sign."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=3-2\\cos(2(x-\\pi/4))</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find amplitude, period, midline and range. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Amplitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|-2|=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Period is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/2=\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> and phase shift is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span> to the right.","[1] Midline is y=3.","[1] Range is [1,5]; the negative coefficient reverses maxima and minima, not amplitude sign."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-graphs-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"A periodic quantity has maximum 9, minimum 3 and period 8 hours. It is at a maximum at t = 0. Give a simple cosine model. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Midline is $(9+3)/2=6$.","[1] Amplitude is $(9-3)/2=3$.","[1] Angular multiplier is $2\\pi/8=\\pi/4$ per hour.","[1] A suitable model is $y=6+3\\cos(\\pi t/4)$, with t in hours."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A periodic quantity has maximum 9, minimum 3 and period 8 hours. It is at a maximum at t = 0. Give a simple cosine model. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Midline is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(9+3)/2=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Amplitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(9-3)/2=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Angular multiplier is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/8=\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span> per hour.","[1] A suitable model is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=6+3\\cos(\\pi t/4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with t in hours."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-graphs-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find the period and all vertical asymptotes of $y=\\tan(2x)$ in radians. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Tangent period is $\\pi/|2|=\\pi/2$.","[1] Singularities occur when $2x=\\pi/2+k\\pi$.","[1] Therefore $x=\\pi/4+k\\pi/2$.","[1] The branches are separate; tangent has no finite amplitude."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the period and all vertical asymptotes of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=\\tan(2x)</annotation></semantics></math></span> in radians. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Tangent period is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/|2|=\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Singularities occur when <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x=\\pi/2+k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Therefore <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pi/4+k\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The branches are separate; tangent has no finite amplitude."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-graphs-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"For $y=2\\sin(x+\\pi/6)-1$, identify shift and find the first maximum x at or after 0. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Midline is -1, amplitude 2 and shift is $\\pi/6$ left.","[1] A maximum occurs when $x+\\pi/6=\\pi/2+2k\\pi$.","[1] Thus $x=\\pi/3+2k\\pi$.","[1] The first nonnegative maximum is at $\\pi/3$, with y=1."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=2\\sin(x+\\pi/6)-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, identify shift and find the first maximum x at or after 0. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Midline is -1, amplitude 2 and shift is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/6</annotation></semantics></math></span> left.","[1] A maximum occurs when <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+\\pi/6=\\pi/2+2k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pi/3+2k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The first nonnegative maximum is at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with y=1."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-graphs-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"The model $h=4+3\\cos(\\pi t/6)$ uses hours. Find period and all t in $[0,12)$ at which h=4. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Period is $2\\pi/(\\pi/6)=12$ hours.","[1] h=4 requires $\\cos(\\pi t/6)=0$.","[1] Within one cycle the inner angle is $\\pi/2$ or $3\\pi/2$.","[1] Times are t=3 and t=9 hours."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"The model <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=4+3\\cos(\\pi t/6)</annotation></semantics></math></span> uses hours. Find period and all t in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,12)</annotation></semantics></math></span> at which h=4. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Period is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/(\\pi/6)=12</annotation></semantics></math></span> hours.","[1] h=4 requires <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(\\pi t/6)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Within one cycle the inner angle is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Times are t=3 and t=9 hours."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-graphs-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Rewrite the argument $3x-\\pi$ in factored shift form and give the period and shift of $\\sin(3x-\\pi)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $3x-\\pi=3(x-\\pi/3)$.","[1] Therefore the shift is $\\pi/3$ right, not pi right.","[1] The period is $2\\pi/3$.","[1] Factoring the multiplier before reading the shift avoids confusing an angle constant with an x displacement."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Rewrite the argument <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3x-\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> in factored shift form and give the period and shift of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(3x-\\pi)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3x-\\pi=3(x-\\pi/3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Therefore the shift is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span> right, not pi right.","[1] The period is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Factoring the multiplier before reading the shift avoids confusing an angle constant with an x displacement."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What is the amplitude of $-4\\sin(3x)+2$?","o":["4.","-4.","3.","2."],"a":0,"why":"Amplitude is the absolute value of the sine coefficient; the sign controls reflection.","qHtml":"What is the amplitude of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4\\sin(3x)+2</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","oHtml":["4.","-4.","3.","2."],"whyHtml":"Amplitude is the absolute value of the sine coefficient; the sign controls reflection."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-trig-compound","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Trigonometry","title":"Proving and applying compound and double angles","outcomes":["2.1.3"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Compound identities relate different angles · Derive double-angle forms rather than memorising fragments · Use signs and quadrants when recovering a ratio · Prove an identity through justified equalities · A derivation from unit vectors","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Proving and applying compound and double angles</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Compound identities relate different angles</li><li>Derive double-angle forms rather than memorising fragments</li><li>Use signs and quadrants when recovering a ratio</li><li>Prove an identity through justified equalities</li><li>A derivation from unit vectors</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compound identities relate different angles","text":"The sum and difference formulae are $\\sin(A\\pm B)=\\sin A\\cos B\\pm\\cos A\\sin B$ and $\\cos(A\\pm B)=\\cos A\\cos B\\mp\\sin A\\sin B$. The cosine sign changes opposite to the angle sign. These identities are not obtained by simply distributing a sine over addition. They follow from geometric or rotation arguments and hold for real angles. Choose known exact angles to calculate unfamiliar exact values.","diagram":null,"html":"The sum and difference formulae are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(A\\pm B)=\\sin A\\cos B\\pm\\cos A\\sin B</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>∓</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(A\\pm B)=\\cos A\\cos B\\mp\\sin A\\sin B</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The cosine sign changes opposite to the angle sign. These identities are not obtained by simply distributing a sine over addition. They follow from geometric or rotation arguments and hold for real angles. Choose known exact angles to calculate unfamiliar exact values."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Derive double-angle forms rather than memorising fragments","text":"Set B=A in the compound formulae to obtain $\\sin2A=2\\sin A\\cos A$ and $\\cos2A=\\cos^2A-\\sin^2A$. Using $\\sin^2A+\\cos^2A=1$ gives $\\cos2A=2\\cos^2A-1=1-2\\sin^2A$. Different forms are convenient when the question supplies only sine or cosine. The tangent double-angle formula follows by division where defined, and its denominator restrictions must remain visible.","diagram":"trig-identities","html":"Set B=A in the compound formulae to obtain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin2A=2\\sin A\\cos A</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos2A=\\cos^2A-\\sin^2A</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Using <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2A+\\cos^2A=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos2A=2\\cos^2A-1=1-2\\sin^2A</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Different forms are convenient when the question supplies only sine or cosine. The tangent double-angle formula follows by division where defined, and its denominator restrictions must remain visible."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use signs and quadrants when recovering a ratio","text":"If $\\sin A=3/5$, cosine can be positive or negative depending on the quadrant. A compound-angle calculation can change sign if a quadrant is omitted. Use the stated domain before taking a square root from a Pythagorean identity. Keep surds exact for calculator-free work and simplify the final expression. A decimal approximation can check magnitude but does not replace an exact answer when one is requested.","diagram":null,"html":"If <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin A=3/5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, cosine can be positive or negative depending on the quadrant. A compound-angle calculation can change sign if a quadrant is omitted. Use the stated domain before taking a square root from a Pythagorean identity. Keep surds exact for calculator-free work and simplify the final expression. A decimal approximation can check magnitude but does not replace an exact answer when one is requested."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Prove an identity through justified equalities","text":"A proof may begin from a compound-angle theorem already established in the course and transform one side to the requested expression. State identities used and preserve the domain when dividing. Numerical verification at one angle is a useful check, not a universal proof. Different valid derivations can earn the same reasoning credit if they demonstrate the equality under the appropriate conditions and do not assume it at the start.","diagram":null,"html":"A proof may begin from a compound-angle theorem already established in the course and transform one side to the requested expression. State identities used and preserve the domain when dividing. Numerical verification at one angle is a useful check, not a universal proof. Different valid derivations can earn the same reasoning credit if they demonstrate the equality under the appropriate conditions and do not assume it at the start."},{"kind":"learn","title":"A derivation from unit vectors","text":"Take unit vectors $u=(\\cos A,\\sin A)$ and $v=(\\cos B,\\sin B)$. Their dot product is both $\\cos(A-B)$ and $\\cos A\\cos B+\\sin A\\sin B$, proving the cosine difference formula. Replace B by -B and use cosine evenness and sine oddness to obtain the cosine sum formula. For sine, write $\\sin(A+B)=\\cos((\\pi/2-A)-B)$ and apply the difference formula to obtain $\\sin A\\cos B+\\cos A\\sin B$. Replacing B by -B gives the sine difference formula. Setting B=A derives the double-angle formulas, so this chain establishes the identities rather than relying on numerical examples.","diagram":null,"html":"Take unit vectors <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=(\\cos A,\\sin A)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=(\\cos B,\\sin B)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Their dot product is both <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(A-B)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos A\\cos B+\\sin A\\sin B</annotation></semantics></math></span>, proving the cosine difference formula. Replace B by -B and use cosine evenness and sine oddness to obtain the cosine sum formula. For sine, write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(A+B)=\\cos((\\pi/2-A)-B)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and apply the difference formula to obtain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin A\\cos B+\\cos A\\sin B</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Replacing B by -B gives the sine difference formula. Setting B=A derives the double-angle formulas, so this chain establishes the identities rather than relying on numerical examples."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-trig-compound-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find the exact value of $\\sin75^\\circ$ using a compound-angle formula. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Write $75^\\circ=45^\\circ+30^\\circ$.","[1] $\\sin75=\\sin45\\cos30+\\cos45\\sin30$.","[1] Substitute $\\sqrt2/2,\\sqrt3/2,1/2$ in the appropriate places.","[1] The exact result is $(\\sqrt6+\\sqrt2)/4$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the exact value of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mn>75</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin75^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> using a compound-angle formula. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>75</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>45</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>30</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">75^\\circ=45^\\circ+30^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>75</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>45</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>45</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin75=\\sin45\\cos30+\\cos45\\sin30</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Substitute <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2/2,\\sqrt3/2,1/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> in the appropriate places.","[1] The exact result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msqrt><mn>6</mn></msqrt><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\sqrt6+\\sqrt2)/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-compound-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Given $\\sin A=3/5$ and A is in quadrant II, find $\\sin2A$ and $\\cos2A$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Quadrant II requires $\\cos A=-4/5$.","[1] $\\sin2A=2(3/5)(-4/5)=-24/25$.","[1] $\\cos2A=1-2(3/5)^2=1-18/25$.","[1] Thus $\\cos2A=7/25$, with signs derived from the specified quadrant."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Given <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin A=3/5</annotation></semantics></math></span> and A is in quadrant II, find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin2A</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos2A</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Quadrant II requires <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos A=-4/5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>24</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin2A=2(3/5)(-4/5)=-24/25</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>18</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos2A=1-2(3/5)^2=1-18/25</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos2A=7/25</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with signs derived from the specified quadrant."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-compound-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find the exact value of $\\cos15^\\circ$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Write $15^\\circ=45^\\circ-30^\\circ$.","[1] Use $\\cos(A-B)=\\cos A\\cos B+\\sin A\\sin B$.","[1] Substitute exact standard-angle values.","[1] Result is $(\\sqrt6+\\sqrt2)/4$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the exact value of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mn>15</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos15^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>15</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>45</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>30</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15^\\circ=45^\\circ-30^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(A-B)=\\cos A\\cos B+\\sin A\\sin B</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Substitute exact standard-angle values.","[1] Result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msqrt><mn>6</mn></msqrt><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\sqrt6+\\sqrt2)/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-compound-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Derive $\\cos2x=1-2\\sin^2x$ from the compound cosine identity. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Set both compound angles equal to x: $\\cos2x=\\cos^2x-\\sin^2x$.","[1] Use $\\cos^2x=1-\\sin^2x$.","[1] Substitute and combine terms.","[1] The result is $1-2\\sin^2x$, valid for all real x."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Derive <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos2x=1-2\\sin^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span> from the compound cosine identity. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Set both compound angles equal to x: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos2x=\\cos^2x-\\sin^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos^2x=1-\\sin^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Substitute and combine terms.","[1] The result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-2\\sin^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, valid for all real x."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-compound-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"If $\\tan A=1/3$ and A is acute, find $\\tan2A$ and check the denominator condition. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\tan2A=2\\tan A/(1-\\tan^2A)$ where the expressions are defined.","[1] Numerator is $2/3$ and denominator $1-1/9=8/9$.","[1] The quotient is $(2/3)(9/8)=3/4$.","[1] The denominator is nonzero, so this application is valid."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"If <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tan A=1/3</annotation></semantics></math></span> and A is acute, find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tan2A</annotation></semantics></math></span> and check the denominator condition. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tan2A=2\\tan A/(1-\\tan^2A)</annotation></semantics></math></span> where the expressions are defined.","[1] Numerator is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/3</annotation></semantics></math></span> and denominator <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-1/9=8/9</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The quotient is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>9</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2/3)(9/8)=3/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The denominator is nonzero, so this application is valid."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-compound-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Explain why $\\sin(A+B)=\\sin A+\\sin B$ is false in general, using a standard-angle counterexample and stating the correct formula. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Take $A=B=30^\\circ$.","[1] Left side is $\\sin60^\\circ=\\sqrt3/2$ while the proposed right side is 1.","[1] These are unequal, disproving the general assertion.","[1] The correct formula is $\\sin A\\cos B+\\cos A\\sin B$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(A+B)=\\sin A+\\sin B</annotation></semantics></math></span> is false in general, using a standard-angle counterexample and stating the correct formula. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Take <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>30</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=B=30^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Left side is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin60^\\circ=\\sqrt3/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> while the proposed right side is 1.","[1] These are unequal, disproving the general assertion.","[1] The correct formula is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin A\\cos B+\\cos A\\sin B</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What sign occurs in $\\cos(A-B)$ between the two products?","o":["A plus sign.","Always a minus sign.","No second product.","A sign determined by calculator rounding."],"a":0,"why":"Cosine’s compound identity uses the opposite sign to the angle combination.","qHtml":"What sign occurs in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(A-B)</annotation></semantics></math></span> between the two products?","oHtml":["A plus sign.","Always a minus sign.","No second product.","A sign determined by calculator rounding."],"whyHtml":"Cosine’s compound identity uses the opposite sign to the angle combination."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-trig-reciprocal","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Trigonometry","title":"Reciprocal functions, domains and asymptotes","outcomes":["2.1.4"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Reciprocal functions preserve exclusions · Sketch secant and cosecant from the base graph · Cotangent has its own period and branch direction · Apply transformations to range and singularities","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Reciprocal functions, domains and asymptotes</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Reciprocal functions preserve exclusions</li><li>Sketch secant and cosecant from the base graph</li><li>Cotangent has its own period and branch direction</li><li>Apply transformations to range and singularities</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reciprocal functions preserve exclusions","text":"$\\sec x=1/\\cos x$, $\\csc x=1/\\sin x$ and $\\cot x=\\cos x/\\sin x$ wherever the denominators are nonzero. The functions are not inverse trigonometric functions: reciprocal secant differs from arccosine. A cotangent definition by cosine over sine also clarifies its values where tangent is zero or undefined. State domains from the defining denominators rather than assuming all real angles are allowed.","diagram":null,"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sec x=1/\\cos x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>csc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\csc x=1/\\sin x</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cot</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cot x=\\cos x/\\sin x</annotation></semantics></math></span> wherever the denominators are nonzero. The functions are not inverse trigonometric functions: reciprocal secant differs from arccosine. A cotangent definition by cosine over sine also clarifies its values where tangent is zero or undefined. State domains from the defining denominators rather than assuming all real angles are allowed."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Sketch secant and cosecant from the base graph","text":"Zeros of cosine create secant vertical asymptotes, and zeros of sine create cosecant asymptotes. Where the base function equals 1 or -1, its reciprocal has the same value. Because sine and cosine magnitudes do not exceed 1, secant and cosecant have values at least 1 in magnitude. Their branches lie outside the strip -1<y<1, with signs inherited from the denominator. Do not draw a continuous connecting segment across an asymptote.","diagram":"secant","html":"Zeros of cosine create secant vertical asymptotes, and zeros of sine create cosecant asymptotes. Where the base function equals 1 or -1, its reciprocal has the same value. Because sine and cosine magnitudes do not exceed 1, secant and cosecant have values at least 1 in magnitude. Their branches lie outside the strip -1&lt;y&lt;1, with signs inherited from the denominator. Do not draw a continuous connecting segment across an asymptote."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Cotangent has its own period and branch direction","text":"Cotangent has period pi, vertical asymptotes at $x=k\\pi$ and zeros at $x=\\pi/2+k\\pi$. On a standard interval between consecutive asymptotes it decreases, unlike the standard increasing tangent branch. The graph’s period is not automatically $2\\pi$ just because sine and cosine appear in the definition. A transformed argument changes all key x locations and must be solved explicitly.","diagram":null,"html":"Cotangent has period pi, vertical asymptotes at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> and zeros at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pi/2+k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. On a standard interval between consecutive asymptotes it decreases, unlike the standard increasing tangent branch. The graph’s period is not automatically <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> just because sine and cosine appear in the definition. A transformed argument changes all key x locations and must be solved explicitly."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply transformations to range and singularities","text":"For a vertical transformation $a\\sec x+d$, map the two original range branches carefully, especially when a is negative. For a horizontal transformation, solve the inner cosine-zero condition. Reflections and shifts do not make a singularity into an ordinary point. Use exact asymptote families and labelled key points, and explain why a requested angle is excluded before simplifying a formula that may hide the exclusion.","diagram":null,"html":"For a vertical transformation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\sec x+d</annotation></semantics></math></span>, map the two original range branches carefully, especially when a is negative. For a horizontal transformation, solve the inner cosine-zero condition. Reflections and shifts do not make a singularity into an ordinary point. Use exact asymptote families and labelled key points, and explain why a requested angle is excluded before simplifying a formula that may hide the exclusion."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-trig-reciprocal-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Give domain exclusions and range for $y=\\sec x$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Secant is $1/\\cos x$.","[1] Exclude cosine zeros $x=\\pi/2+k\\pi$, $k\\in\\mathbb Z$.","[1] Nonzero cosine lies between -1 and 1, so its reciprocal has magnitude at least 1.","[1] Range is $(-\\infty,-1]\\cup[1,\\infty)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Give domain exclusions and range for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=\\sec x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Secant is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\cos x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Exclude cosine zeros <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pi/2+k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\in\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Nonzero cosine lies between -1 and 1, so its reciprocal has magnitude at least 1.","[1] Range is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>∪</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-\\infty,-1]\\cup[1,\\infty)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-reciprocal-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find vertical asymptotes of $y=\\sec(2x)$ and the period. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Singularities require $\\cos2x=0$.","[1] $2x=\\pi/2+k\\pi$, hence $x=\\pi/4+k\\pi/2$.","[1] Secant’s base period is $2\\pi$.","[1] The transformed period is pi."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find vertical asymptotes of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=\\sec(2x)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the period. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Singularities require <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos2x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x=\\pi/2+k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>, hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pi/4+k\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Secant’s base period is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The transformed period is pi."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-reciprocal-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find exact $\\csc(\\pi/6)$, $\\sec(\\pi/3)$ and $\\cot(\\pi/4)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\csc(\\pi/6)=1/(1/2)=2$.","[1] $\\sec(\\pi/3)=1/(1/2)=2$.","[1] $\\cot(\\pi/4)=\\cos(\\pi/4)/\\sin(\\pi/4)=1$.","[1] All defining denominators are nonzero at the stated angles."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find exact <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>csc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\csc(\\pi/6)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sec(\\pi/3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cot</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cot(\\pi/4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>csc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\csc(\\pi/6)=1/(1/2)=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sec(\\pi/3)=1/(1/2)=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cot</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cot(\\pi/4)=\\cos(\\pi/4)/\\sin(\\pi/4)=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] All defining denominators are nonzero at the stated angles."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-reciprocal-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Give all zeros and vertical asymptotes of $\\cot x$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Cotangent is cosine divided by sine.","[1] Zeros occur where cosine is zero and sine nonzero: $x=\\pi/2+k\\pi$.","[1] Asymptotes occur at sine zeros $x=k\\pi$.","[1] The families repeat every pi."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Give all zeros and vertical asymptotes of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cot</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cot x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Cotangent is cosine divided by sine.","[1] Zeros occur where cosine is zero and sine nonzero: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pi/2+k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Asymptotes occur at sine zeros <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The families repeat every pi."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-reciprocal-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find the range of $y=2\\sec x+1$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Base secant ranges are $\\sec x\\le-1$ or $\\sec x\\ge1$.","[1] Multiplication by positive 2 gives $2\\sec x\\le-2$ or $\\ge2$.","[1] Adding 1 gives $y\\le-1$ or $y\\ge3$.","[1] Range is $(-\\infty,-1]\\cup[3,\\infty)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the range of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=2\\sec x+1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Base secant ranges are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sec x\\le-1</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sec x\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Multiplication by positive 2 gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sec x\\le-2</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ge2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Adding 1 gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\le-1</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\ge3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Range is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>∪</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-\\infty,-1]\\cup[3,\\infty)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-reciprocal-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Explain why $\\sec(\\pi/2)$ is undefined but $\\cot(\\pi/2)$ is defined, using the direct definitions. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\cos(\\pi/2)=0$, so secant would divide by zero.","[1] $\\sin(\\pi/2)=1$.","[1] Cotangent is cosine/sine, giving $0/1=0$.","[1] Treating cotangent only as a reciprocal of a possibly undefined tangent can obscure the direct-domain reasoning."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sec(\\pi/2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is undefined but <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cot</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cot(\\pi/2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is defined, using the direct definitions. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(\\pi/2)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so secant would divide by zero.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(\\pi/2)=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Cotangent is cosine/sine, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0/1=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Treating cotangent only as a reciprocal of a possibly undefined tangent can obscure the direct-domain reasoning."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Where does $\\csc x$ have vertical asymptotes?","o":["At $x=k\\pi$.","Only where sine is 1.","At every angle.","Only at cosine’s zeros."],"a":0,"why":"Cosecant is the reciprocal of sine, so sine zeros determine its singularities.","qHtml":"Where does <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>csc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\csc x</annotation></semantics></math></span> have vertical asymptotes?","oHtml":["At <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Only where sine is 1.","At every angle.","Only at cosine’s zeros."],"whyHtml":"Cosecant is the reciprocal of sine, so sine zeros determine its singularities."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-trig-identities","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Trigonometry","title":"Pythagorean, product-to-sum and derived identities","outcomes":["2.1.5","2.1.6","2.1.8"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Identity domains remain part of the result · Prove by transforming a justified expression · Product-to-sum follows from compound identities · Build higher-angle identities step by step","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Pythagorean, product-to-sum and derived identities</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Identity domains remain part of the result</li><li>Prove by transforming a justified expression</li><li>Product-to-sum follows from compound identities</li><li>Build higher-angle identities step by step</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Identity domains remain part of the result","text":"The identity $\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1$ holds for all real x. Dividing by cosine squared yields $1+\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x$ only where cosine is nonzero; dividing by sine squared yields $1+\\cot^2x=\\csc^2x$ where sine is nonzero. A simplified expression can look defined at extra points, but the original denominator still constrains an equivalence. State restrictions explicitly rather than losing them during cancellation.","diagram":null,"html":"The identity <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> holds for all real x. Dividing by cosine squared yields <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span> only where cosine is nonzero; dividing by sine squared yields <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cot</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>csc</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+\\cot^2x=\\csc^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span> where sine is nonzero. A simplified expression can look defined at extra points, but the original denominator still constrains an equivalence. State restrictions explicitly rather than losing them during cancellation."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Prove by transforming a justified expression","text":"Choose the more complicated side and apply accepted identities to reach the other. Avoid an argument that starts by assuming the target equality and performs irreversible operations without explanation. Multiplying both sides can be a valid equivalent transformation when its conditions are stated, but a direct chain is often clearer. A proof must apply to the whole stated domain, not only a chosen numerical angle.","diagram":"trig-identities","html":"Choose the more complicated side and apply accepted identities to reach the other. Avoid an argument that starts by assuming the target equality and performs irreversible operations without explanation. Multiplying both sides can be a valid equivalent transformation when its conditions are stated, but a direct chain is often clearer. A proof must apply to the whole stated domain, not only a chosen numerical angle."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Product-to-sum follows from compound identities","text":"Adding or subtracting compound formulae gives $\\cos A\\cos B=\\tfrac12[\\cos(A-B)+\\cos(A+B)]$, $\\sin A\\sin B=\\tfrac12[\\cos(A-B)-\\cos(A+B)]$ and $\\sin A\\cos B=\\tfrac12[\\sin(A+B)+\\sin(A-B)]$. These convert products to sums useful in equations and later integration. Carefully distinguish which expression carries a minus sign and which angle is subtracted.","diagram":null,"html":"Adding or subtracting compound formulae gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos A\\cos B=\\tfrac12[\\cos(A-B)+\\cos(A+B)]</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin A\\sin B=\\tfrac12[\\cos(A-B)-\\cos(A+B)]</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin A\\cos B=\\tfrac12[\\sin(A+B)+\\sin(A-B)]</annotation></semantics></math></span>. These convert products to sums useful in equations and later integration. Carefully distinguish which expression carries a minus sign and which angle is subtracted."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Build higher-angle identities step by step","text":"For a triple angle, express 3x as 2x+x, use a compound identity and then double-angle and Pythagorean relations. This gives $\\cos3x=4\\cos^3x-3\\cos x$. The power belongs to cosine, not to the input angle. Derived formulae should be checked at familiar values as a diagnostic, but the general algebra supplies the proof. Keep the original notation clear so $\\cos^3x$ is not confused with $\\cos(3x)$.","diagram":null,"html":"For a triple angle, express 3x as 2x+x, use a compound identity and then double-angle and Pythagorean relations. This gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos3x=4\\cos^3x-3\\cos x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The power belongs to cosine, not to the input angle. Derived formulae should be checked at familiar values as a diagnostic, but the general algebra supplies the proof. Keep the original notation clear so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos^3x</annotation></semantics></math></span> is not confused with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(3x)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-trig-identities-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Prove $(1-\\cos x)(1+\\cos x)=\\sin^2x$ and state its domain. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Expand the product as $1-\\cos^2x$.","[1] Apply $\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1$.","[1] The expression equals $\\sin^2x$.","[1] No division occurs, so the identity holds for all real x."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-\\cos x)(1+\\cos x)=\\sin^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span> and state its domain. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Expand the product as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\cos^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Apply <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The expression equals <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] No division occurs, so the identity holds for all real x."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-identities-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Prove $\\sin^2x/(1-\\cos x)=1+\\cos x$ on the original domain. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Original denominator requires $\\cos x\\ne1$.","[1] Factor $\\sin^2x=1-\\cos^2x=(1-\\cos x)(1+\\cos x)$.","[1] Cancel the nonzero factor $1-\\cos x$.","[1] The result is $1+\\cos x$ with $x\\ne2k\\pi$ retained; the simplified expression alone has a larger domain."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x/(1-\\cos x)=1+\\cos x</annotation></semantics></math></span> on the original domain. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Original denominator requires <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos x\\ne1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Factor <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x=1-\\cos^2x=(1-\\cos x)(1+\\cos x)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Cancel the nonzero factor <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\cos x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+\\cos x</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ne2k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> retained; the simplified expression alone has a larger domain."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-identities-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Express $\\sin(3x)\\cos x$ as a sum of sine terms. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use $\\sin A\\cos B=\\tfrac12[\\sin(A+B)+\\sin(A-B)]$.","[1] Set A=3x and B=x.","[1] Add/subtract the angles to get 4x and 2x.","[1] Result is $\\tfrac12(\\sin4x+\\sin2x)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Express <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(3x)\\cos x</annotation></semantics></math></span> as a sum of sine terms. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin A\\cos B=\\tfrac12[\\sin(A+B)+\\sin(A-B)]</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Set A=3x and B=x.","[1] Add/subtract the angles to get 4x and 2x.","[1] Result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12(\\sin4x+\\sin2x)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-identities-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Prove $\\cos3x=4\\cos^3x-3\\cos x$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\cos3x=\\cos(2x+x)=\\cos2x\\cos x-\\sin2x\\sin x$.","[1] Substitute $\\cos2x=2\\cos^2x-1$ and $\\sin2x=2\\sin x\\cos x$.","[1] Replace $\\sin^2x$ by $1-\\cos^2x$ and collect terms.","[1] This gives $4\\cos^3x-3\\cos x$, valid for all real x."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos3x=4\\cos^3x-3\\cos x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos3x=\\cos(2x+x)=\\cos2x\\cos x-\\sin2x\\sin x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Substitute <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos2x=2\\cos^2x-1</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin2x=2\\sin x\\cos x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Replace <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span> by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\cos^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span> and collect terms.","[1] This gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\cos^3x-3\\cos x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, valid for all real x."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-identities-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Derive $1+\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x$ and state the excluded angles. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Start with $\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1$.","[1] Divide by $\\cos^2x$ only when cosine is nonzero.","[1] The quotients give $\\tan^2x+1=\\sec^2x$.","[1] Exclude $x=\\pi/2+k\\pi$ from the identity’s domain."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Derive <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span> and state the excluded angles. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Start with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Divide by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span> only when cosine is nonzero.","[1] The quotients give <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tan^2x+1=\\sec^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Exclude <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pi/2+k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> from the identity’s domain."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-identities-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Express $\\sin(5x)\\sin(2x)$ as a cosine difference and explain its sign. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use $\\sin A\\sin B=\\tfrac12[\\cos(A-B)-\\cos(A+B)]$.","[1] Here A-B=3x and A+B=7x.","[1] The expression is $\\tfrac12(\\cos3x-\\cos7x)$.","[1] The minus belongs to the sum-angle cosine because the formula follows by subtracting compound cosines."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Express <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(5x)\\sin(2x)</annotation></semantics></math></span> as a cosine difference and explain its sign. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin A\\sin B=\\tfrac12[\\cos(A-B)-\\cos(A+B)]</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Here A-B=3x and A+B=7x.","[1] The expression is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12(\\cos3x-\\cos7x)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The minus belongs to the sum-angle cosine because the formula follows by subtracting compound cosines."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What restriction survives simplifying $\\sin^2x/(1-\\cos x)$?","o":["$\\cos x\\ne1$.","No restriction because cancellation always extends equality everywhere.","Only $\\sin x=1$.","All x are excluded."],"a":0,"why":"Cancellation preserves equivalence only where the original denominator is nonzero.","qHtml":"What restriction survives simplifying <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x/(1-\\cos x)</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos x\\ne1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","No restriction because cancellation always extends equality everywhere.","Only <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin x=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","All x are excluded."],"whyHtml":"Cancellation preserves equivalence only where the original denominator is nonzero."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-trig-auxiliary","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Trigonometry","title":"Auxiliary-angle forms and periodic modelling","outcomes":["2.1.7","2.1.9"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Combine sine and cosine using an auxiliary angle · The sine version uses a different coefficient match · Solve the reduced equation completely · Interpret amplitude and phase in a periodic model","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Auxiliary-angle forms and periodic modelling</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Combine sine and cosine using an auxiliary angle</li><li>The sine version uses a different coefficient match</li><li>Solve the reduced equation completely</li><li>Interpret amplitude and phase in a periodic model</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Combine sine and cosine using an auxiliary angle","text":"Write $a\\cos x+b\\sin x=R\\cos(x-\\alpha)$ with $R=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\\ge0$. Matching coefficients gives $R\\cos\\alpha=a$ and $R\\sin\\alpha=b$. Determine alpha from both signs, not just an inverse tangent ratio that may select the wrong quadrant. The representation explains the combined amplitude and phase and provides a compact way to solve equations. The zero-coefficient pair is a separate constant-zero case.","diagram":null,"html":"Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\cos x+b\\sin x=R\\cos(x-\\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Matching coefficients gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\cos\\alpha=a</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\sin\\alpha=b</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Determine alpha from both signs, not just an inverse tangent ratio that may select the wrong quadrant. The representation explains the combined amplitude and phase and provides a compact way to solve equations. The zero-coefficient pair is a separate constant-zero case."},{"kind":"learn","title":"The sine version uses a different coefficient match","text":"An alternative $R\\sin(x+\\beta)$ has cosine coefficient $R\\sin\\beta$ and sine coefficient $R\\cos\\beta$. Both forms can be correct with suitable phases. State which form you use before calculating the angle. A sign in the requested template affects coefficient equations. The amplitude remains nonnegative, while phase can be given modulo $2\\pi$ unless a principal convention is requested.","diagram":"trig-identities","html":"An alternative <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\sin(x+\\beta)</annotation></semantics></math></span> has cosine coefficient <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\sin\\beta</annotation></semantics></math></span> and sine coefficient <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\cos\\beta</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Both forms can be correct with suitable phases. State which form you use before calculating the angle. A sign in the requested template affects coefficient equations. The amplitude remains nonnegative, while phase can be given modulo <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> unless a principal convention is requested."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Solve the reduced equation completely","text":"After rewriting, divide by R for nonzero amplitude and check whether the resulting right-hand value lies in [-1,1]. Use both trigonometric branches and the original interval. For a maximum value R, the reduced cosine is 1 and its full solutions are the phase plus integer periods. A reduced value outside the range proves no real solutions. Numeric phases should retain sufficient precision until final angles are reported.","diagram":null,"html":"After rewriting, divide by R for nonzero amplitude and check whether the resulting right-hand value lies in [-1,1]. Use both trigonometric branches and the original interval. For a maximum value R, the reduced cosine is 1 and its full solutions are the phase plus integer periods. A reduced value outside the range proves no real solutions. Numeric phases should retain sufficient precision until final angles are reported."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret amplitude and phase in a periodic model","text":"A sum of same-frequency sine and cosine terms can be represented as one shifted wave. If the argument is omega t, phase angle divided by omega gives a time shift, so angle and time are not interchangeable. The model’s vertical shift sets a baseline and R its excursion. Use the stated context to choose an appropriate time domain and identify the first occurrence or every occurrence as requested. Validate a model against given extrema and starting conditions.","diagram":null,"html":"A sum of same-frequency sine and cosine terms can be represented as one shifted wave. If the argument is omega t, phase angle divided by omega gives a time shift, so angle and time are not interchangeable. The model’s vertical shift sets a baseline and R its excursion. Use the stated context to choose an appropriate time domain and identify the first occurrence or every occurrence as requested. Validate a model against given extrema and starting conditions."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-trig-auxiliary-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Write $3\\cos x+4\\sin x$ as $R\\cos(x-\\alpha)$ with $0<\\alpha<\\pi/2$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $R=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5$.","[1] Coefficient matching gives $\\cos\\alpha=3/5$ and $\\sin\\alpha=4/5$.","[1] Both are positive, so alpha is in quadrant I with $\\alpha=\\tan^{-1}(4/3)$.","[1] The required form is $5\\cos(x-\\alpha)$, with $\\alpha\\approx0.9273$ radians."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\cos x+4\\sin x</annotation></semantics></math></span> as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\cos(x-\\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\alpha&lt;\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Coefficient matching gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos\\alpha=3/5</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\alpha=4/5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both are positive, so alpha is in quadrant I with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=\\tan^{-1}(4/3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The required form is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\cos(x-\\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.9273</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha\\approx0.9273</annotation></semantics></math></span> radians."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-auxiliary-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Solve $3\\cos x+4\\sin x=5$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use $5\\cos(x-\\alpha)$ where $\\alpha=\\tan^{-1}(4/3)$ in quadrant I.","[1] Divide by 5 to get $\\cos(x-\\alpha)=1$.","[1] General solutions are $x=\\alpha+2k\\pi$.","[1] The interval retains $x=\\alpha\\approx0.9273$ radians only."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\cos x+4\\sin x=5</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le x&lt;2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\cos(x-\\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></span> where <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=\\tan^{-1}(4/3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> in quadrant I.","[1] Divide by 5 to get <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(x-\\alpha)=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] General solutions are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\alpha+2k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The interval retains <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.9273</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\alpha\\approx0.9273</annotation></semantics></math></span> radians only."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-auxiliary-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find the maximum and minimum of $6\\cos x-8\\sin x+3$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The combined-wave amplitude is $\\sqrt{36+64}=10$.","[1] The vertical baseline is 3.","[1] Maximum is $3+10=13$.","[1] Minimum is $3-10=-7$, independently of the exact phase."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the maximum and minimum of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\cos x-8\\sin x+3</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The combined-wave amplitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>36</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{36+64}=10</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The vertical baseline is 3.","[1] Maximum is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3+10=13</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Minimum is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3-10=-7</annotation></semantics></math></span>, independently of the exact phase."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-auxiliary-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Write $-\\sqrt3\\cos x+\\sin x$ as $R\\cos(x-\\alpha)$ with $0\\le\\alpha<2\\pi$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $R=\\sqrt{3+1}=2$.","[1] $\\cos\\alpha=-\\sqrt3/2$ and $\\sin\\alpha=1/2$.","[1] These signs require quadrant II, giving $\\alpha=5\\pi/6$.","[1] The form is $2\\cos(x-5\\pi/6)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\sqrt3\\cos x+\\sin x</annotation></semantics></math></span> as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\cos(x-\\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le\\alpha&lt;2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\sqrt{3+1}=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos\\alpha=-\\sqrt3/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\alpha=1/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] These signs require quadrant II, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=5\\pi/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The form is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cos(x-5\\pi/6)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-auxiliary-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Solve $3\\cos x+4\\sin x=0$ on $0\\le x<2\\pi$, using alpha from the worked example. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The equation reduces to $\\cos(x-\\alpha)=0$.","[1] $x-\\alpha=\\pi/2+k\\pi$.","[1] Since $0<\\alpha<\\pi/2$, the two interval solutions are $\\alpha+\\pi/2$ and $\\alpha+3\\pi/2$.","[1] Approximately 2.4981 and 5.6397 radians; retain the exact phase forms when requested."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\cos x+4\\sin x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> on <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le x&lt;2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>, using alpha from the worked example. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The equation reduces to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(x-\\alpha)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-\\alpha=\\pi/2+k\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Since <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\alpha&lt;\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the two interval solutions are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha+\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha+3\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Approximately 2.4981 and 5.6397 radians; retain the exact phase forms when requested."]},{"id":"mas11-trig-auxiliary-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A model is $y=7+3\\cos(2t)+4\\sin(2t)$. Find its range, period and first maximum at t at or after 0. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Combine the terms as $5\\cos(2t-\\alpha)$, with $\\alpha=\\tan^{-1}(4/3)$ in quadrant I.","[1] Range is [2,12].","[1] Period is $2\\pi/2=\\pi$ time units.","[1] First maximum occurs at $2t=\\alpha$, so $t=\\alpha/2\\approx0.4636$; phase angle and time shift differ."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A model is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=7+3\\cos(2t)+4\\sin(2t)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find its range, period and first maximum at t at or after 0. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Combine the terms as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\cos(2t-\\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=\\tan^{-1}(4/3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> in quadrant I.","[1] Range is [2,12].","[1] Period is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/2=\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> time units.","[1] First maximum occurs at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2t=\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.4636</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\alpha/2\\approx0.4636</annotation></semantics></math></span>; phase angle and time shift differ."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Why must alpha’s quadrant be checked in coefficient matching?","o":["An inverse tangent ratio alone can lose the signs of sine and cosine.","Every phase must be acute.","Amplitude can always be chosen negative without a phase convention.","The period determines the signs of every coefficient."],"a":0,"why":"Both matched coefficient signs identify the correct phase quadrant.","qHtml":"Why must alpha’s quadrant be checked in coefficient matching?","oHtml":["An inverse tangent ratio alone can lose the signs of sine and cosine.","Every phase must be acute.","Amplitude can always be chosen negative without a phase convention.","The period determines the signs of every coefficient."],"whyHtml":"Both matched coefficient signs identify the correct phase quadrant."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-matrix-arithmetic","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Matrices","title":"Matrix notation, arithmetic and compatible dimensions","outcomes":["2.2.1","2.2.2"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Read dimensions before calculating · Equality compares corresponding entries · Addition and scalar multiplication · Interpret arithmetic and check signs","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Matrix notation, arithmetic and compatible dimensions</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Read dimensions before calculating</li><li>Equality compares corresponding entries</li><li>Addition and scalar multiplication</li><li>Interpret arithmetic and check signs</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Read dimensions before calculating","text":"A matrix is a rectangular array of entries. Its order is rows by columns, so a $2\\times3$ matrix has two rows and three columns. The entry $a_{ij}$ lies in row $i$ and column $j$, counted from 1. A row vector has one row and a column vector has one column. Order matters because rows and columns can represent different quantities. Label the quantities represented by an applied matrix rather than treating it as an unlabelled collection of numbers. An entry can be negative or zero; neither changes the dimensions.","diagram":null,"html":"A matrix is a rectangular array of entries. Its order is rows by columns, so a <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times3</annotation></semantics></math></span> matrix has two rows and three columns. The entry <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></span> lies in row <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and column <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math></span>, counted from 1. A row vector has one row and a column vector has one column. Order matters because rows and columns can represent different quantities. Label the quantities represented by an applied matrix rather than treating it as an unlabelled collection of numbers. An entry can be negative or zero; neither changes the dimensions."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Equality compares corresponding entries","text":"Two matrices are equal precisely when their dimensions agree and every corresponding entry is equal. Matrix equality can therefore generate simultaneous scalar equations for unknown parameters. Check all entries: solving one entry equation does not establish equality of the whole matrices. A transpose interchanges rows and columns, with $(A^{\\mathsf T})_{ij}=a_{ji}$. It changes an $m\\times n$ matrix into an $n\\times m$ matrix and need not equal the original even when both are square. Symmetry means $A^{\\mathsf T}=A$.","diagram":"matrix-rules","html":"Two matrices are equal precisely when their dimensions agree and every corresponding entry is equal. Matrix equality can therefore generate simultaneous scalar equations for unknown parameters. Check all entries: solving one entry equation does not establish equality of the whole matrices. A transpose interchanges rows and columns, with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(A^{\\mathsf T})_{ij}=a_{ji}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. It changes an <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\times n</annotation></semantics></math></span> matrix into an <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\times m</annotation></semantics></math></span> matrix and need not equal the original even when both are square. Symmetry means <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{\\mathsf T}=A</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Addition and scalar multiplication","text":"Addition and subtraction require identical dimensions and operate entry by entry. Scalar multiplication multiplies every entry, including zeros and negative values. Thus $2A-B$ means double all entries of A and subtract the corresponding entries of B. The zero matrix has every entry zero and must have the appropriate dimensions. For compatible matrices, addition is commutative and associative, while scalar multiplication distributes over addition. Matrix multiplication has a different dimension rule; do not infer that any two matrices that can be added can also be multiplied.","diagram":null,"html":"Addition and subtraction require identical dimensions and operate entry by entry. Scalar multiplication multiplies every entry, including zeros and negative values. Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2A-B</annotation></semantics></math></span> means double all entries of A and subtract the corresponding entries of B. The zero matrix has every entry zero and must have the appropriate dimensions. For compatible matrices, addition is commutative and associative, while scalar multiplication distributes over addition. Matrix multiplication has a different dimension rule; do not infer that any two matrices that can be added can also be multiplied."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret arithmetic and check signs","text":"When rows represent stores and columns represent products, a sum can combine compatible monthly sales tables. Subtracting can represent a change, with a negative entry indicating a decrease rather than an impossible sales quantity. Keep the same row and column ordering in both arrays. A scalar can convert common units or represent an explicitly justified scaling assumption; doubling last month's sales is a model, not an automatic prediction. In an exam, state compatibility, show the intermediate array when signs are difficult and check the resulting dimensions and contextual units.","diagram":null,"html":"When rows represent stores and columns represent products, a sum can combine compatible monthly sales tables. Subtracting can represent a change, with a negative entry indicating a decrease rather than an impossible sales quantity. Keep the same row and column ordering in both arrays. A scalar can convert common units or represent an explicitly justified scaling assumption; doubling last month&#39;s sales is a model, not an automatic prediction. In an exam, state compatibility, show the intermediate array when signs are difficult and check the resulting dimensions and contextual units."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-matrix-arithmetic-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Let $A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&-1\\\\0&3\\end{pmatrix}$ and $B=\\begin{pmatrix}1&4\\\\-2&5\\end{pmatrix}$. Find $2A-B$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Both matrices have order $2\\times2$, so subtraction is defined.","[1] $2A=\\begin{pmatrix}4&-2\\\\0&6\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Subtract corresponding entries: $2A-B=\\begin{pmatrix}4-1&-2-4\\\\0-(-2)&6-5\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Therefore $2A-B=\\begin{pmatrix}3&-6\\\\2&1\\end{pmatrix}$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Let <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;-1\\\\0&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;4\\\\-2&amp;5\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2A-B</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Both matrices have order <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so subtraction is defined.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>6</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2A=\\begin{pmatrix}4&amp;-2\\\\0&amp;6\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Subtract corresponding entries: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2A-B=\\begin{pmatrix}4-1&amp;-2-4\\\\0-(-2)&amp;6-5\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Therefore <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2A-B=\\begin{pmatrix}3&amp;-6\\\\2&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-arithmetic-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find $x,y$ if $\\begin{pmatrix}x+1&2y\\\\3&x-y\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}5&6\\\\3&1\\end{pmatrix}$. Check all entries. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Matching top-left entries gives $x+1=5$, so $x=4$.","[1] Matching top-right entries gives $2y=6$, so $y=3$.","[1] The bottom-right condition is $x-y=1$, satisfied by $4-3=1$.","[1] Bottom-left entries already match; all four conditions hold, giving $(x,y)=(4,3)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x,y</annotation></semantics></math></span> if <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>6</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}x+1&amp;2y\\\\3&amp;x-y\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}5&amp;6\\\\3&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Check all entries. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Matching top-left entries gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+1=5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Matching top-right entries gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2y=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The bottom-right condition is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-y=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, satisfied by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-3=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Bottom-left entries already match; all four conditions hold, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x,y)=(4,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-arithmetic-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"For $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3\\\\4&5&6\\end{pmatrix}$, state its order, find $a_{23}$ and write $A^{\\mathsf T}$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] A has two rows and three columns, so order $2\\times3$.","[1] Row 2, column 3 contains $a_{23}=6$.","[1] Each original row becomes a transpose column: $A^{\\mathsf T}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&4\\\\2&5\\\\3&6\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] The transpose order is $3\\times2$, so $A+A^{\\mathsf T}$ is undefined."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>6</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2&amp;3\\\\4&amp;5&amp;6\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, state its order, find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>23</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{23}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{\\mathsf T}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] A has two rows and three columns, so order <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Row 2, column 3 contains <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>23</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{23}=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Each original row becomes a transpose column: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>6</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{\\mathsf T}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;4\\\\2&amp;5\\\\3&amp;6\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The transpose order is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\times2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A+A^{\\mathsf T}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is undefined."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-arithmetic-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Let $C=\\begin{pmatrix}-2&5\\\\4&0\\end{pmatrix}$ and $D=\\begin{pmatrix}3&-1\\\\-2&6\\end{pmatrix}$. Find $C+3D$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The two $2\\times2$ arrays can be added.","[1] $3D=\\begin{pmatrix}9&-3\\\\-6&18\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] $C+3D=\\begin{pmatrix}-2+9&5-3\\\\4-6&0+18\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Result $=\\begin{pmatrix}7&2\\\\-2&18\\end{pmatrix}$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Let <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=\\begin{pmatrix}-2&amp;5\\\\4&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>6</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\begin{pmatrix}3&amp;-1\\\\-2&amp;6\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C+3D</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The two <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times2</annotation></semantics></math></span> arrays can be added.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>9</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>18</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3D=\\begin{pmatrix}9&amp;-3\\\\-6&amp;18\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C+3D=\\begin{pmatrix}-2+9&amp;5-3\\\\4-6&amp;0+18\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>7</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>18</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=\\begin{pmatrix}7&amp;2\\\\-2&amp;18\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-arithmetic-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Two stores sell products P and Q. Rows denote stores and columns P,Q. January sales are $J=\\begin{pmatrix}30&20\\\\25&40\\end{pmatrix}$ and February sales $F=\\begin{pmatrix}35&18\\\\20&44\\end{pmatrix}$. Find and interpret $F-J$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The matrices have the same dimensions and category ordering.","[1] $F-J=\\begin{pmatrix}35-30&18-20\\\\20-25&44-40\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}5&-2\\\\-5&4\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Store 1 sold 5 more P and 2 fewer Q; store 2 sold 5 fewer P and 4 more Q.","[1] Entries are changes in units sold, so negative values represent decreases."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Two stores sell products P and Q. Rows denote stores and columns P,Q. January sales are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>30</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>20</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>25</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>40</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=\\begin{pmatrix}30&amp;20\\\\25&amp;40\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and February sales <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>35</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>18</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>20</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>44</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=\\begin{pmatrix}35&amp;18\\\\20&amp;44\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find and interpret <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F-J</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The matrices have the same dimensions and category ordering.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>35</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>44</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F-J=\\begin{pmatrix}35-30&amp;18-20\\\\20-25&amp;44-40\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}5&amp;-2\\\\-5&amp;4\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Store 1 sold 5 more P and 2 fewer Q; store 2 sold 5 fewer P and 4 more Q.","[1] Entries are changes in units sold, so negative values represent decreases."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-arithmetic-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Find $k$ for which $M=\\begin{pmatrix}2&k+1\\\\3k-5&4\\end{pmatrix}$ is symmetric. Explain why its diagonal entries need not be equal. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Symmetry requires $M^{\\mathsf T}=M$.","[1] The two off-diagonal entries must match: $k+1=3k-5$.","[1] Thus $2k=6$ and $k=3$, giving both off-diagonal entries 4.","[1] Transposing leaves each diagonal entry in its original position; it does not interchange the 2 and 4, so unequal diagonal entries are permitted."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span> for which <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;k+1\\\\3k-5&amp;4\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is symmetric. Explain why its diagonal entries need not be equal. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Symmetry requires <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M^{\\mathsf T}=M</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The two off-diagonal entries must match: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k+1=3k-5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2k=6</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving both off-diagonal entries 4.","[1] Transposing leaves each diagonal entry in its original position; it does not interchange the 2 and 4, so unequal diagonal entries are permitted."]}],"bank":[{"q":"When is matrix addition defined?","o":["When the matrices have identical row and column counts.","Whenever they have the same total number of entries.","Whenever the first column count equals the second row count.","Only when every entry is positive."],"a":0,"why":"Addition compares corresponding positions; equal total size alone does not guarantee matching shapes.","qHtml":"When is matrix addition defined?","oHtml":["When the matrices have identical row and column counts.","Whenever they have the same total number of entries.","Whenever the first column count equals the second row count.","Only when every entry is positive."],"whyHtml":"Addition compares corresponding positions; equal total size alone does not guarantee matching shapes."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-matrix-products","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Matrices","title":"Matrix products, order and identity","outcomes":["2.2.2"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Compatibility is a row-column rule · Compute systematically · Order changes the answer · Identity and powers","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Matrix products, order and identity</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Compatibility is a row-column rule</li><li>Compute systematically</li><li>Order changes the answer</li><li>Identity and powers</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compatibility is a row-column rule","text":"If A has order $m\\times n$ and B has order $n\\times p$, AB exists and has order $m\\times p$. The inner dimensions must agree; the outer dimensions describe the result. The entry in row i, column j is the dot product of row i of A with column j of B. This combines products and sums, rather than multiplying entries in matching positions.","diagram":null,"html":"If A has order <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\times n</annotation></semantics></math></span> and B has order <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\times p</annotation></semantics></math></span>, AB exists and has order <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\times p</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The inner dimensions must agree; the outer dimensions describe the result. The entry in row i, column j is the dot product of row i of A with column j of B. This combines products and sums, rather than multiplying entries in matching positions."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compute systematically","text":"Write the rows of A and columns of B clearly. For a $2\\times2$ example, $(AB)_{12}=a_{11}b_{12}+a_{12}b_{22}$. Complete all entries and check the resulting dimensions. A common error uses a row of B rather than a column, or omits a negative sign. In a context, explain the units of each product and why the sum represents the requested quantity.","diagram":"matrix-rules","html":"Write the rows of A and columns of B clearly. For a <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times2</annotation></semantics></math></span> example, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(AB)_{12}=a_{11}b_{12}+a_{12}b_{22}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Complete all entries and check the resulting dimensions. A common error uses a row of B rather than a column, or omits a negative sign. In a context, explain the units of each product and why the sum represents the requested quantity."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Order changes the answer","text":"AB and BA may have different dimensions, one may be undefined, or both may exist but have different entries. Matrix multiplication is associative and distributes over compatible sums, but is generally not commutative. Cancellation is also unsafe without an invertible factor: AB = AC need not imply B = C. A zero product does not necessarily mean either factor is the zero matrix.","diagram":null,"html":"AB and BA may have different dimensions, one may be undefined, or both may exist but have different entries. Matrix multiplication is associative and distributes over compatible sums, but is generally not commutative. Cancellation is also unsafe without an invertible factor: AB = AC need not imply B = C. A zero product does not necessarily mean either factor is the zero matrix."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Identity and powers","text":"The identity $I_n$ has ones on its diagonal and zeros elsewhere. For an $m\\times n$ matrix A, $I_mA=A=AI_n$. A power $A^2$ means AA, so powers require a square matrix. It does not mean squaring each entry. Use explicit small products to test proposed matrix laws before treating them as general rules.","diagram":null,"html":"The identity <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_n</annotation></semantics></math></span> has ones on its diagonal and zeros elsewhere. For an <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\times n</annotation></semantics></math></span> matrix A, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_mA=A=AI_n</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A power <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> means AA, so powers require a square matrix. It does not mean squaring each entry. Use explicit small products to test proposed matrix laws before treating them as general rules."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-matrix-products-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find AB for $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\-1&3\\end{pmatrix}$ and $B=\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\4&1\\end{pmatrix}$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Both are $2\\times2$, giving a $2\\times2$ product.","[1] First row is $(1(2)+2(4),1(0)+2(1))=(10,2)$.","[1] Second row is $(-1(2)+3(4),-1(0)+3(1))=(10,3)$.","[1] $AB=\\begin{pmatrix}10&2\\\\10&3\\end{pmatrix}$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find AB for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2\\\\-1&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;0\\\\4&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Both are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving a <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times2</annotation></semantics></math></span> product.","[1] First row is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1(2)+2(4),1(0)+2(1))=(10,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Second row is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1(2)+3(4),-1(0)+3(1))=(10,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>10</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>10</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AB=\\begin{pmatrix}10&amp;2\\\\10&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-products-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Using the same A,B, find BA and decide whether AB = BA. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] First row of BA is $(2,4)$.","[1] Second row is $(4-1,8+3)=(3,11)$.","[1] $BA=\\begin{pmatrix}2&4\\\\3&11\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Comparison with AB gives unequal entries, so the products differ."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Using the same A,B, find BA and decide whether AB = BA. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] First row of BA is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Second row is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>11</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4-1,8+3)=(3,11)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>11</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">BA=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;4\\\\3&amp;11\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Comparison with AB gives unequal entries, so the products differ."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-products-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"A has order $3\\times2$, B order $2\\times4$. State the order of AB and whether BA exists. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] AB has matching inner dimensions 2.","[1] Its outer dimensions give order $3\\times4$.","[1] BA would require inner dimensions 4 and 3 to match.","[1] They do not, so BA is undefined."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A has order <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\times2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, B order <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times4</annotation></semantics></math></span>. State the order of AB and whether BA exists. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] AB has matching inner dimensions 2.","[1] Its outer dimensions give order <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\times4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] BA would require inner dimensions 4 and 3 to match.","[1] They do not, so BA is undefined."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-products-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find $A^2$ for $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Multiply A by itself, rather than squaring entries.","[1] First row gives $(1,2)$.","[1] Second row gives $(0,1)$.","[1] $A^2=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Multiply A by itself, rather than squaring entries.","[1] First row gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Second row gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^2=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-products-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Quantities of two products sold by two stores form $Q=\\begin{pmatrix}3&5\\\\4&2\\end{pmatrix}$. Prices are $p=\\begin{pmatrix}10\\\\6\\end{pmatrix}$ dollars. Find and interpret Qp. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Orders $2\\times2$ and $2\\times1$ give a $2\\times1$ result.","[1] First revenue is $3(10)+5(6)=60$.","[1] Second revenue is $4(10)+2(6)=52$.","[1] $Qp=\\begin{pmatrix}60\\\\52\\end{pmatrix}$ dollars lists the stores in row order."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Quantities of two products sold by two stores form <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=\\begin{pmatrix}3&amp;5\\\\4&amp;2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Prices are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>10</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>6</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=\\begin{pmatrix}10\\\\6\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> dollars. Find and interpret Qp. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Orders <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times1</annotation></semantics></math></span> give a <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times1</annotation></semantics></math></span> result.","[1] First revenue is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3(10)+5(6)=60</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Second revenue is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>52</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4(10)+2(6)=52</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>60</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>52</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Qp=\\begin{pmatrix}60\\\\52\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> dollars lists the stores in row order."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-products-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Give two nonzero $2\\times2$ matrices with zero product, disproving the scalar zero-product rule for matrices. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Choose $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$ and $B=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Each matrix contains a nonzero entry.","[1] Every row-column dot product in AB is zero.","[1] Thus $AB=0$ although neither factor is zero."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Give two nonzero <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times2</annotation></semantics></math></span> matrices with zero product, disproving the scalar zero-product rule for matrices. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Choose <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;0\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Each matrix contains a nonzero entry.","[1] Every row-column dot product in AB is zero.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AB=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> although neither factor is zero."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What determines entry $(AB)_{ij}$?","o":["Row i of A dotted with column j of B.","Entry $a_{ij}$ times entry $b_{ij}$.","Column i of A dotted with row j of B.","The sum of all entries."],"a":0,"why":"Multiplication combines a row of the left factor with a column of the right.","qHtml":"What determines entry <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(AB)_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","oHtml":["Row i of A dotted with column j of B.","Entry <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></span> times entry <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Column i of A dotted with row j of B.","The sum of all entries."],"whyHtml":"Multiplication combines a row of the left factor with a column of the right."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-matrix-inverse","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Matrices","title":"Determinants, inverses and singular matrices","outcomes":["2.2.3"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Determinant of a two-by-two matrix · Derive and use the inverse · Singular matrices cannot be undone · Inverses preserve order carefully","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Determinants, inverses and singular matrices</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Determinant of a two-by-two matrix</li><li>Derive and use the inverse</li><li>Singular matrices cannot be undone</li><li>Inverses preserve order carefully</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Determinant of a two-by-two matrix","text":"For $A=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\det A=ad-bc$. The diagonal products are subtracted in that order. A nonzero determinant means the associated linear transformation preserves two independent directions, although it may rotate, stretch, shear or reflect them. A zero determinant indicates collapse into a line or point, with information lost.","diagram":null,"html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>a</mi></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>b</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>c</mi></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>d</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;b\\\\c&amp;d\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det A=ad-bc</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The diagonal products are subtracted in that order. A nonzero determinant means the associated linear transformation preserves two independent directions, although it may rotate, stretch, shear or reflect them. A zero determinant indicates collapse into a line or point, with information lost."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Derive and use the inverse","text":"When $ad-bc\\ne0$, $A^{-1}=\\frac1{ad-bc}\\begin{pmatrix}d&-b\\\\-c&a\\end{pmatrix}$. Swap the diagonal entries, negate the off-diagonal entries and divide every entry by the determinant. Direct multiplication proves $AA^{-1}=A^{-1}A=I$. Keeping a fraction outside the array can make exact arithmetic clearer and avoid premature rounding.","diagram":"matrix-inverse","html":"When <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ad-bc\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>d</mi></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>a</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{-1}=\\frac1{ad-bc}\\begin{pmatrix}d&amp;-b\\\\-c&amp;a\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Swap the diagonal entries, negate the off-diagonal entries and divide every entry by the determinant. Direct multiplication proves <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AA^{-1}=A^{-1}A=I</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Keeping a fraction outside the array can make exact arithmetic clearer and avoid premature rounding."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Singular matrices cannot be undone","text":"A matrix with zero determinant is singular and has no inverse. For example, proportional rows produce dependent equations or a collapsed transformation. Singularity does not mean the matrix itself is zero, nor that an associated system always has no solutions: a system can have infinitely many solutions or be inconsistent. Division by a zero determinant is never a valid workaround.","diagram":null,"html":"A matrix with zero determinant is singular and has no inverse. For example, proportional rows produce dependent equations or a collapsed transformation. Singularity does not mean the matrix itself is zero, nor that an associated system always has no solutions: a system can have infinitely many solutions or be inconsistent. Division by a zero determinant is never a valid workaround."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Inverses preserve order carefully","text":"If both factors are invertible, $(AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}$, reversing the order. The last action must be undone first. To solve $AX=B$, left-multiply by $A^{-1}$; to solve $XA=B$, right-multiply. Matrix division is not a general operation. Check a proposed inverse through multiplication and check parameter exclusions before reporting a formula.","diagram":null,"html":"If both factors are invertible, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, reversing the order. The last action must be undone first. To solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AX=B</annotation></semantics></math></span>, left-multiply by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>; to solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">XA=B</annotation></semantics></math></span>, right-multiply. Matrix division is not a general operation. Check a proposed inverse through multiplication and check parameter exclusions before reporting a formula."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-matrix-inverse-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find the inverse of $A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\5&3\\end{pmatrix}$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\det A=6-5=1\\ne0$.","[1] Swap diagonal entries and negate off-diagonal entries.","[1] $A^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}3&-1\\\\-5&2\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Multiplication gives $AA^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the inverse of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\5&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det A=6-5=1\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Swap diagonal entries and negate off-diagonal entries.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}3&amp;-1\\\\-5&amp;2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Multiplication gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AA^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-inverse-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"For $A=\\begin{pmatrix}k&2\\\\3&1\\end{pmatrix}$, find the singular value of k and its inverse otherwise. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\det A=k-6$.","[1] A is singular exactly when $k=6$.","[1] For $k\\ne6$, $A^{-1}=\\frac1{k-6}\\begin{pmatrix}1&-2\\\\-3&k\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] The exclusion is part of the answer; the formula is undefined at 6."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>k</mi></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}k&amp;2\\\\3&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find the singular value of k and its inverse otherwise. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det A=k-6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] A is singular exactly when <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\ne6</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>k</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{-1}=\\frac1{k-6}\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;-2\\\\-3&amp;k\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The exclusion is part of the answer; the formula is undefined at 6."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-inverse-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find the inverse of $B=\\begin{pmatrix}4&0\\\\0&-2\\end{pmatrix}$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\det B=-8\\ne0$.","[1] Apply the inverse formula: $B^{-1}=\\frac1{-8}\\begin{pmatrix}-2&0\\\\0&4\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Hence $B^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}1/4&0\\\\0&-1/2\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] The diagonal products in $BB^{-1}$ are both 1."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the inverse of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\begin{pmatrix}4&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det B=-8\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Apply the inverse formula: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B^{-1}=\\frac1{-8}\\begin{pmatrix}-2&amp;0\\\\0&amp;4\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}1/4&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-1/2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The diagonal products in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">BB^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> are both 1."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-inverse-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Explain why $C=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\2&4\\end{pmatrix}$ has no inverse, and give a nonzero vector mapped to zero. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\det C=4-4=0$, so C is singular.","[1] Choose $v=\\begin{pmatrix}-2\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] $Cv=\\begin{pmatrix}-2+2\\\\-4+4\\end{pmatrix}=0$.","[1] Since a nonzero vector and zero share the same image, no transformation can recover every input."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2\\\\2&amp;4\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> has no inverse, and give a nonzero vector mapped to zero. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det C=4-4=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so C is singular.","[1] Choose <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\begin{pmatrix}-2\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Cv=\\begin{pmatrix}-2+2\\\\-4+4\\end{pmatrix}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Since a nonzero vector and zero share the same image, no transformation can recover every input."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-inverse-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Prove $(AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}$ for invertible A,B. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $AB(B^{-1}A^{-1})=A(BB^{-1})A^{-1}$.","[1] This equals $AIA^{-1}=I$.","[1] $(B^{-1}A^{-1})AB=B^{-1}(A^{-1}A)B=I$.","[1] Both inverse conditions hold, proving the formula."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> for invertible A,B. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AB(B^{-1}A^{-1})=A(BB^{-1})A^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This equals <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AIA^{-1}=I</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(B^{-1}A^{-1})AB=B^{-1}(A^{-1}A)B=I</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both inverse conditions hold, proving the formula."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-inverse-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A student claims that $\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&2\\end{pmatrix}$. Correct the answer. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Determinant is $2-1=1$.","[1] The diagonal entries were correctly exchanged.","[1] The off-diagonal entries must be negated: inverse $=\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\-1&2\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Multiplication yields I; the proposed positive off-diagonal entries do not."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A student claims that <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\1&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\1&amp;2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Correct the answer. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Determinant is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2-1=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The diagonal entries were correctly exchanged.","[1] The off-diagonal entries must be negated: inverse <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;-1\\\\-1&amp;2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Multiplication yields I; the proposed positive off-diagonal entries do not."]}],"bank":[{"q":"When does a $2\\times2$ real matrix have an inverse?","o":["When its determinant is nonzero.","Whenever all four entries are nonzero.","Only when its determinant is positive.","Whenever it is square, without another condition."],"a":0,"why":"Both positive and negative nonzero determinants allow inversion; zero does not.","qHtml":"When does a <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times2</annotation></semantics></math></span> real matrix have an inverse?","oHtml":["When its determinant is nonzero.","Whenever all four entries are nonzero.","Only when its determinant is positive.","Whenever it is square, without another condition."],"whyHtml":"Both positive and negative nonzero determinants allow inversion; zero does not."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-matrix-systems","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Matrices","title":"Matrix equations and two-variable systems","outcomes":["2.2.3","2.2.11"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Translate equations into a matrix equation · Solve by a valid inverse · Classify singular systems · Interpret and assess the result","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Matrix equations and two-variable systems</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Translate equations into a matrix equation</li><li>Solve by a valid inverse</li><li>Classify singular systems</li><li>Interpret and assess the result</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Translate equations into a matrix equation","text":"The system $ax+by=e$, $cx+dy=f$ becomes $AX=b$, with coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}$, unknown column $X=\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{pmatrix}$ and right-hand column $b=\\begin{pmatrix}e\\\\f\\end{pmatrix}$. Preserve the same variable order in each row. Reordering an equation is allowed, but the coefficient and right-hand sides must move together.","diagram":null,"html":"The system <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ax+by=e</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">cx+dy=f</annotation></semantics></math></span> becomes <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AX=b</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with coefficient matrix <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>a</mi></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>b</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>c</mi></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>d</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;b\\\\c&amp;d\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, unknown column <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>x</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>y</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and right-hand column <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>e</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>f</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=\\begin{pmatrix}e\\\\f\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Preserve the same variable order in each row. Reordering an equation is allowed, but the coefficient and right-hand sides must move together."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Solve by a valid inverse","text":"If the determinant is nonzero, left multiplication gives $X=A^{-1}b$. Show the determinant and intermediate multiplication, then state the scalar values. Check by substitution into both original equations. Calculator output can check arithmetic, but an unexplained decimal pair may miss method marks and conceal a transcription error. Exact solutions remain preferable when values are simple fractions.","diagram":"matrix-inverse","html":"If the determinant is nonzero, left multiplication gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=A^{-1}b</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Show the determinant and intermediate multiplication, then state the scalar values. Check by substitution into both original equations. Calculator output can check arithmetic, but an unexplained decimal pair may miss method marks and conceal a transcription error. Exact solutions remain preferable when values are simple fractions."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Classify singular systems","text":"A zero determinant means no unique solution. Compare the full equations, including constants. If one complete equation is a multiple of the other, there are infinitely many solutions along a line; parameterise them. If coefficient rows are proportional but constants are not in the same proportion, the lines are parallel and distinct, so no solution exists. Failure of the inverse method is a signal to analyse consistency, not an answer by itself.","diagram":null,"html":"A zero determinant means no unique solution. Compare the full equations, including constants. If one complete equation is a multiple of the other, there are infinitely many solutions along a line; parameterise them. If coefficient rows are proportional but constants are not in the same proportion, the lines are parallel and distinct, so no solution exists. Failure of the inverse method is a signal to analyse consistency, not an answer by itself."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret and assess the result","text":"For applied systems, define the unknowns and their units before forming equations. A mathematical solution can violate contextual restrictions such as nonnegative quantities or whole-number counts. Explain whether the solution is feasible. In equations involving a matrix unknown, retain multiplication order: $AX=B$ gives $X=A^{-1}B$, whereas $XA=B$ gives $X=BA^{-1}$.","diagram":null,"html":"For applied systems, define the unknowns and their units before forming equations. A mathematical solution can violate contextual restrictions such as nonnegative quantities or whole-number counts. Explain whether the solution is feasible. In equations involving a matrix unknown, retain multiplication order: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AX=B</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=A^{-1}B</annotation></semantics></math></span>, whereas <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">XA=B</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=BA^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-matrix-systems-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Solve $2x+y=7$, $x-y=2$ using matrices. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}$, $b=\\begin{pmatrix}7\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}$, determinant $-3$.","[1] $A^{-1}=\\frac1{-3}\\begin{pmatrix}-1&-1\\\\-1&2\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] $X=A^{-1}b=\\frac1{-3}\\begin{pmatrix}-9\\\\-3\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Both substitutions hold, so $x=3,y=1$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x+y=7</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-y=2</annotation></semantics></math></span> using matrices. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\1&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>7</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=\\begin{pmatrix}7\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, determinant <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{-1}=\\frac1{-3}\\begin{pmatrix}-1&amp;-1\\\\-1&amp;2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=A^{-1}b=\\frac1{-3}\\begin{pmatrix}-9\\\\-3\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both substitutions hold, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3,y=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-systems-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Classify $x+2y=3$, $2x+4y=8$ and explain why an inverse cannot solve it. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Coefficient determinant is $4-4=0$.","[1] Twice the first left-hand side equals the second left-hand side.","[1] But twice the first constant is 6, not 8.","[1] The equations are inconsistent, so there is no solution."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Classify <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+2y=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x+4y=8</annotation></semantics></math></span> and explain why an inverse cannot solve it. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Coefficient determinant is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-4=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Twice the first left-hand side equals the second left-hand side.","[1] But twice the first constant is 6, not 8.","[1] The equations are inconsistent, so there is no solution."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-systems-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Solve $3x+2y=12$, $x-y=1$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $A=\\begin{pmatrix}3&2\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}$ has determinant $-5$.","[1] $A^{-1}=\\frac1{-5}\\begin{pmatrix}-1&-2\\\\-1&3\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] $X=\\frac1{-5}\\begin{pmatrix}-14\\\\-9\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Thus $x=14/5,y=9/5$, satisfying both equations."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3x+2y=12</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-y=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}3&amp;2\\\\1&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> has determinant <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{-1}=\\frac1{-5}\\begin{pmatrix}-1&amp;-2\\\\-1&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=\\frac1{-5}\\begin{pmatrix}-14\\\\-9\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=14/5,y=9/5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, satisfying both equations."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-systems-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Parameterise all solutions of $x+2y=3$, $2x+4y=6$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The second equation is twice the entire first equation.","[1] There is one independent constraint and infinitely many solutions.","[1] Set $y=t$, where $t\\in\\mathbb R$.","[1] Then $x=3-2t$, so $(x,y)=(3-2t,t)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Parameterise all solutions of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+2y=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x+4y=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The second equation is twice the entire first equation.","[1] There is one independent constraint and infinitely many solutions.","[1] Set <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=t</annotation></semantics></math></span>, where <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\in\\mathbb R</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3-2t</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x,y)=(3-2t,t)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-systems-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Adult tickets cost 12 dollars and student tickets 7 dollars. A group buys 20 tickets for 190 dollars. Find the numbers of each using simultaneous equations. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Define adult count a and student count s: $a+s=20$, $12a+7s=190$.","[1] The coefficient determinant is $7-12=-5\\ne0$, so there is a unique solution.","[1] Subtracting seven times the first equation gives $5a=50$, hence $a=10$, $s=10$.","[1] Both are nonnegative integers and $120+70=190$, so the contextual solution is feasible."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Adult tickets cost 12 dollars and student tickets 7 dollars. A group buys 20 tickets for 190 dollars. Find the numbers of each using simultaneous equations. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Define adult count a and student count s: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a+s=20</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>190</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12a+7s=190</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The coefficient determinant is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7-12=-5\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so there is a unique solution.","[1] Subtracting seven times the first equation gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5a=50</annotation></semantics></math></span>, hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=10</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=10</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both are nonnegative integers and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>120</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>70</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>190</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">120+70=190</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so the contextual solution is feasible."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-systems-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"If $XA=B$ and $A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\0&3\\end{pmatrix}$, $B=\\begin{pmatrix}4&6\\\\8&9\\end{pmatrix}$, find X. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Right-multiply by $A^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}1/2&0\\\\0&1/3\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] $X=BA^{-1}$, not $A^{-1}B$.","[1] Multiplication scales the first column by $1/2$ and the second by $1/3$.","[1] $X=\\begin{pmatrix}2&2\\\\4&3\\end{pmatrix}$; checking XA recovers B."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"If <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">XA=B</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;0\\\\0&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>6</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>8</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>9</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\begin{pmatrix}4&amp;6\\\\8&amp;9\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find X. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Right-multiply by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}1/2&amp;0\\\\0&amp;1/3\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=BA^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, not <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{-1}B</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Multiplication scales the first column by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the second by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;2\\\\4&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>; checking XA recovers B."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What does a zero coefficient determinant tell you about a two-variable linear system?","o":["It has no unique solution; consistency must still be checked.","It always has no solutions.","It always has infinitely many solutions.","It has exactly one solution at the origin."],"a":0,"why":"Proportional coefficient rows may describe either coincident or distinct parallel lines.","qHtml":"What does a zero coefficient determinant tell you about a two-variable linear system?","oHtml":["It has no unique solution; consistency must still be checked.","It always has no solutions.","It always has infinitely many solutions.","It has exactly one solution at the origin."],"whyHtml":"Proportional coefficient rows may describe either coincident or distinct parallel lines."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-matrix-transformations","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Matrices","title":"Dilations, rotations, reflections and translations","outcomes":["2.2.4","2.2.5","2.2.6"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Use column vectors and fixed axes · Rotation and reflection matrices · Dilations and projections · Translations require an added vector","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Dilations, rotations, reflections and translations</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Use column vectors and fixed axes</li><li>Rotation and reflection matrices</li><li>Dilations and projections</li><li>Translations require an added vector</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use column vectors and fixed axes","text":"A linear plane transformation sends $\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{pmatrix}$ to $A\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{pmatrix}$. The columns of A are the images of the basis vectors $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$. This provides a reliable way to construct a matrix from a geometric description. State column-vector convention because row-vector conventions alter the written order. A linear transformation always fixes the origin and preserves addition and scalar multiplication of vectors.","diagram":null,"html":"A linear plane transformation sends <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>x</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>y</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>x</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>y</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The columns of A are the images of the basis vectors <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This provides a reliable way to construct a matrix from a geometric description. State column-vector convention because row-vector conventions alter the written order. A linear transformation always fixes the origin and preserves addition and scalar multiplication of vectors."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Rotation and reflection matrices","text":"Anticlockwise rotation through $\\theta$ has matrix $R_\\theta=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\theta&-\\sin\\theta\\\\\\sin\\theta&\\cos\\theta\\end{pmatrix}$. Reflection in the x-axis is $\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$; reflection in the y-axis is $\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1)$. Reflection in $y=x$ interchanges coordinates. Determine images of basis vectors or multiply a test point to check direction and signs. A rotation about a centre other than the origin also needs translations.","diagram":"matrix-area","html":"Anticlockwise rotation through <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> has matrix <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\theta=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\theta&amp;-\\sin\\theta\\\\\\sin\\theta&amp;\\cos\\theta\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Reflection in the x-axis is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; reflection in the y-axis is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Reflection in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x</annotation></semantics></math></span> interchanges coordinates. Determine images of basis vectors or multiply a test point to check direction and signs. A rotation about a centre other than the origin also needs translations."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Dilations and projections","text":"A dilation with scale factor k about the origin has matrix $kI$. Separate horizontal and vertical stretches use $\\operatorname{diag}(a,b)$. Negative factors include a reversal of direction; a zero factor collapses one coordinate. Projection onto the x-axis uses $\\operatorname{diag}(1,0)$ and is singular. A projection discards information rather than reflecting it. Compare transformed basis vectors and determinant before describing what the transformation does.","diagram":null,"html":"A dilation with scale factor k about the origin has matrix <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">kI</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Separate horizontal and vertical stretches use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{diag}(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Negative factors include a reversal of direction; a zero factor collapses one coordinate. Projection onto the x-axis uses <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{diag}(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and is singular. A projection discards information rather than reflecting it. Compare transformed basis vectors and determinant before describing what the transformation does."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Translations require an added vector","text":"Translation through vector b has rule $X^{\\prime}=X+b$. A nonzero translation cannot be represented by multiplication with a $2\\times2$ matrix alone, since every such matrix sends zero to zero. An affine transformation combines a linear part and translation, $X'=AX+b$. To rotate about C, translate C to the origin, rotate and translate back: $X'=R(X-C)+C$. State the centre and direction, rather than naming rotation alone.","diagram":null,"html":"Translation through vector b has rule <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^{\\prime}=X+b</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A nonzero translation cannot be represented by multiplication with a <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times2</annotation></semantics></math></span> matrix alone, since every such matrix sends zero to zero. An affine transformation combines a linear part and translation, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X&#x27;=AX+b</annotation></semantics></math></span>. To rotate about C, translate C to the origin, rotate and translate back: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X&#x27;=R(X-C)+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>. State the centre and direction, rather than naming rotation alone."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-matrix-transformations-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find the matrix for an anticlockwise $90^\\circ$ rotation and the image of $(3,-2)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\cos90^\\circ=0$, $\\sin90^\\circ=1$.","[1] $R=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] $R\\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\-2\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\3\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] The image is $(2,3)$, preserving distance $\\sqrt{13}$ from the origin."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the matrix for an anticlockwise <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> rotation and the image of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos90^\\circ=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin90^\\circ=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\-2\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\3\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The image is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, preserving distance <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{13}</annotation></semantics></math></span> from the origin."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-transformations-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Rotate $(4,1)$ anticlockwise $90^\\circ$ about $(1,1)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Relative vector is $(4,1)-(1,1)=(3,0)$.","[1] Rotation sends $(3,0)$ to $(0,3)$.","[1] Translate back: $(1,1)+(0,3)=(1,4)$.","[1] The image remains distance 3 from the specified centre."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Rotate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> anticlockwise <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> about <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Relative vector is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4,1)-(1,1)=(3,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Rotation sends <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Translate back: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)+(0,3)=(1,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The image remains distance 3 from the specified centre."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-transformations-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find the matrix for reflection in $y=x$ and apply it to $(-2,5)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The first basis vector maps to $(0,1)$.","[1] The second maps to $(1,0)$.","[1] Columns give $A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Image is $(5,-2)$, obtained by interchanging coordinates."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the matrix for reflection in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x</annotation></semantics></math></span> and apply it to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-2,5)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The first basis vector maps to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The second maps to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Columns give <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Image is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(5,-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, obtained by interchanging coordinates."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-transformations-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Describe $A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\0&3\\end{pmatrix}$ and find the image of $(1,-2)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The first coordinate is multiplied by 2.","[1] The second is multiplied by 3.","[1] This is a horizontal stretch factor 2 and vertical stretch factor 3, both relative to the axes.","[1] The image is $(2,-6)$; it is not a uniform dilation."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Describe <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;0\\\\0&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and find the image of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The first coordinate is multiplied by 2.","[1] The second is multiplied by 3.","[1] This is a horizontal stretch factor 2 and vertical stretch factor 3, both relative to the axes.","[1] The image is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,-6)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; it is not a uniform dilation."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-transformations-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Prove no $2\\times2$ multiplication matrix alone represents translation by $(2,-1)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Every matrix A satisfies $A\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}=0$.","[1] The required translation sends the origin to $(2,-1)$.","[1] These origin images differ, contradicting the proposed representation.","[1] Use $X^{\\prime}=X+\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}$ instead."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove no <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times2</annotation></semantics></math></span> multiplication matrix alone represents translation by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Every matrix A satisfies <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The required translation sends the origin to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] These origin images differ, contradicting the proposed representation.","[1] Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^{\\prime}=X+\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> instead."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-transformations-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Compare reflection and projection onto the x-axis by giving both matrices and their images of $(2,3)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Reflection matrix is $\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$, image $(2,-3)$.","[1] Projection matrix is $\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$, image $(2,0)$.","[1] Reflection preserves distance and is invertible.","[1] Projection removes the vertical component and is singular."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Compare reflection and projection onto the x-axis by giving both matrices and their images of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Reflection matrix is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, image <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,-3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Projection matrix is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, image <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Reflection preserves distance and is invertible.","[1] Projection removes the vertical component and is singular."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What do the columns of a plane transformation matrix represent?","o":["The images of the two standard basis vectors.","The images of every point at once without multiplication.","The translation vector for any affine transformation.","The two original coordinate axes only."],"a":0,"why":"Linearity determines the image of any vector from its two basis components.","qHtml":"What do the columns of a plane transformation matrix represent?","oHtml":["The images of the two standard basis vectors.","The images of every point at once without multiplication.","The translation vector for any affine transformation.","The two original coordinate axes only."],"whyHtml":"Linearity determines the image of any vector from its two basis components."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-matrix-composition","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Matrices","title":"Composition, inverse transformations and reflection proofs","outcomes":["2.2.7","2.2.8","2.2.10"],"icon":"📐","scope":"The right-hand factor acts first · Undo compositions in reverse order · Reflection in a line through the origin · Prove identities through multiplication","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Composition, inverse transformations and reflection proofs</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>The right-hand factor acts first</li><li>Undo compositions in reverse order</li><li>Reflection in a line through the origin</li><li>Prove identities through multiplication</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"The right-hand factor acts first","text":"With column vectors, applying A first and B second gives $X'=BAX$. Read a product from right to left as a sequence of actions. This is a frequent source of incorrect transformation descriptions. Composition remains a linear transformation when both component maps are linear, but changing the order can alter the image. Translate a verbal instruction into an explicit chain of intermediate vectors before writing the final product.","diagram":null,"html":"With column vectors, applying A first and B second gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X&#x27;=BAX</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Read a product from right to left as a sequence of actions. This is a frequent source of incorrect transformation descriptions. Composition remains a linear transformation when both component maps are linear, but changing the order can alter the image. Translate a verbal instruction into an explicit chain of intermediate vectors before writing the final product."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Undo compositions in reverse order","text":"An inverse transformation maps every image back to its original point. Rotation through $\\theta$ is undone by rotation through $-\\theta$; nonzero dilation by k is undone by dilation by $1/k$. A reflection is its own inverse. If T = BA, then $T^{-1}=A^{-1}B^{-1}$. A singular projection cannot be undone for every point because different originals share an image.","diagram":"matrix-area","html":"An inverse transformation maps every image back to its original point. Rotation through <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> is undone by rotation through <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>; nonzero dilation by k is undone by dilation by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/k</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A reflection is its own inverse. If T = BA, then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^{-1}=A^{-1}B^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A singular projection cannot be undone for every point because different originals share an image."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reflection in a line through the origin","text":"For a line making angle $\\theta$ to the x-axis, rotate by $-\\theta$, reflect in the x-axis, then rotate back by $\\theta$. The product gives $F_\\theta=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos2\\theta&\\sin2\\theta\\\\\\sin2\\theta&-\\cos2\\theta\\end{pmatrix}$. This construction explains the doubled angles; memorising signs without the construction is fragile. Check a point on the mirror line remains fixed and a perpendicular vector reverses.","diagram":null,"html":"For a line making angle <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> to the x-axis, rotate by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>, reflect in the x-axis, then rotate back by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The product gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\theta=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos2\\theta&amp;\\sin2\\theta\\\\\\sin2\\theta&amp;-\\cos2\\theta\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This construction explains the doubled angles; memorising signs without the construction is fragile. Check a point on the mirror line remains fixed and a perpendicular vector reverses."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Prove identities through multiplication","text":"Two reflections in lines through the origin with angles $\\alpha$ then $\\beta$ compose to rotation through $2(\\beta-\\alpha)$. The directed order matters. Matrix multiplication and compound-angle identities provide a general proof, while a numerical example only illustrates it. State that both lines pass through the origin; parallel displaced mirror lines instead involve a translation and need an affine treatment.","diagram":null,"html":"Two reflections in lines through the origin with angles <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta</annotation></semantics></math></span> compose to rotation through <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(\\beta-\\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The directed order matters. Matrix multiplication and compound-angle identities provide a general proof, while a numerical example only illustrates it. State that both lines pass through the origin; parallel displaced mirror lines instead involve a translation and need an affine treatment."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Extended exam task","q":6},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-matrix-composition-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Apply reflection in the x-axis followed by anticlockwise $90^\\circ$ rotation. Find the composite matrix and image of $(2,1)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $F=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$, $R=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Composite is $RF=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] It maps $(2,1)$ to $(1,2)$.","[1] The composite is reflection in $y=x$, with F acting first."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Apply reflection in the x-axis followed by anticlockwise <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> rotation. Find the composite matrix and image of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Composite is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">RF=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] It maps <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The composite is reflection in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with F acting first."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-composition-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find the reflection matrix for the line making $30^\\circ$ with the x-axis. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use $F_\\theta=R_\\theta F_0R_{-\\theta}$.","[1] The resulting entries use $2\\theta=60^\\circ$.","[1] $F_{30}=\\begin{pmatrix}1/2&\\sqrt3/2\\\\\\sqrt3/2&-1/2\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Its square is I, confirming reflection is self-inverse."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the reflection matrix for the line making <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>30</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> with the x-axis. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\theta=R_\\theta F_0R_{-\\theta}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The resulting entries use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\theta=60^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>30</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{30}=\\begin{pmatrix}1/2&amp;\\sqrt3/2\\\\\\sqrt3/2&amp;-1/2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its square is I, confirming reflection is self-inverse."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-composition-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Reverse the two transformations in the first example and find the image of $(2,1)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The new product is FR.","[1] $FR=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Its image of $(2,1)$ is $(-1,-2)$.","[1] This differs from $(1,2)$, proving order matters."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Reverse the two transformations in the first example and find the image of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The new product is FR.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">FR=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-1\\\\-1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its image of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1,-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This differs from <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, proving order matters."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-composition-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"A point is dilated by factor 3 then rotated anticlockwise $60^\\circ$. State the inverse sequence. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The composite is $T=R_{60}(3I)$.","[1] Its inverse is $(1/3)I\\,R_{-60}$.","[1] Operationally, first rotate clockwise $60^\\circ$, then dilate by $1/3$.","[1] Multiplication of the inverse with T gives I; both actions are invertible."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A point is dilated by factor 3 then rotated anticlockwise <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. State the inverse sequence. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The composite is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>60</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=R_{60}(3I)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its inverse is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>I</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1/3)I\\,R_{-60}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Operationally, first rotate clockwise <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then dilate by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Multiplication of the inverse with T gives I; both actions are invertible."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-composition-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find the transformation produced by reflection in the x-axis followed by reflection in $y=x$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The mirror angles are $\\alpha=0$, $\\beta=\\pi/4$.","[1] Rotation angle is $2(\\beta-\\alpha)=\\pi/2$.","[1] Direct product is $\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] This is anticlockwise $90^\\circ$ rotation about the origin."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the transformation produced by reflection in the x-axis followed by reflection in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The mirror angles are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Rotation angle is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(\\beta-\\alpha)=\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Direct product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This is anticlockwise <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> rotation about the origin."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-composition-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Prove that reflection in a line through the origin performed twice is the identity, using $c=\\cos2\\theta$, $s=\\sin2\\theta$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $F=\\begin{pmatrix}c&s\\\\s&-c\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] $F^2=\\begin{pmatrix}c^2+s^2&cs-sc\\\\sc-cs&s^2+c^2\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Use $c^2+s^2=1$ and cancellation of off-diagonal entries.","[1] Thus $F^2=I$ for every real mirror angle."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove that reflection in a line through the origin performed twice is the identity, using <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=\\cos2\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=\\sin2\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>c</mi></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>s</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>s</mi></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=\\begin{pmatrix}c&amp;s\\\\s&amp;-c\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^2=\\begin{pmatrix}c^2+s^2&amp;cs-sc\\\\sc-cs&amp;s^2+c^2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c^2+s^2=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> and cancellation of off-diagonal entries.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^2=I</annotation></semantics></math></span> for every real mirror angle."]},{"prompt":"A point is first reflected in the line $y=x$, then rotated anticlockwise $60^\\circ$ about the origin. (a) Find the composite matrix. (b) Find the image of $(2,0)$. (c) Identify the composite transformation and its area factor. (d) Explain how the order affects the mirror line. (8 marks)","steps":["[2] With $F=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$ and $R=\\begin{pmatrix}1/2&-\\sqrt3/2\\\\\\sqrt3/2&1/2\\end{pmatrix}$, the composite is $RF=\\begin{pmatrix}-\\sqrt3/2&1/2\\\\1/2&\\sqrt3/2\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Multiplying $(2,0)$ gives $(-\\sqrt3,1)$.","[2] Compare with $F_\\theta$: $\\cos2\\theta=-\\sqrt3/2$, $\\sin2\\theta=1/2$, so $2\\theta=150^\\circ$ and the map is reflection in the line at $75^\\circ$.","[1] Determinant is -1; area factor is 1 and orientation reverses.","[2] Reversing the order gives $FR=\\begin{pmatrix}\\sqrt3/2&1/2\\\\1/2&-\\sqrt3/2\\end{pmatrix}$, reflection in the line at $15^\\circ$. The mirror lines differ by $60^\\circ$."],"id":"mas11-matrix-composition-extended","title":"Extended exam task","allocations":[2,1,2,1,2],"marks":8,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed or exact working","promptHtml":"A point is first reflected in the line <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then rotated anticlockwise <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> about the origin. (a) Find the composite matrix. (b) Find the image of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (c) Identify the composite transformation and its area factor. (d) Explain how the order affects the mirror line. (8 marks)","stepsHtml":["[2] With <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\begin{pmatrix}1/2&amp;-\\sqrt3/2\\\\\\sqrt3/2&amp;1/2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the composite is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">RF=\\begin{pmatrix}-\\sqrt3/2&amp;1/2\\\\1/2&amp;\\sqrt3/2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Multiplying <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-\\sqrt3,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[2] Compare with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos2\\theta=-\\sqrt3/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin2\\theta=1/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>150</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\theta=150^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the map is reflection in the line at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>75</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">75^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Determinant is -1; area factor is 1 and orientation reverses.","[2] Reversing the order gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">FR=\\begin{pmatrix}\\sqrt3/2&amp;1/2\\\\1/2&amp;-\\sqrt3/2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, reflection in the line at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>15</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The mirror lines differ by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"For column vectors, which product applies A then B?","o":["BA.","AB.","A+B.","Entrywise multiplication of A and B."],"a":0,"why":"The right-hand factor acts on the original vector first.","qHtml":"For column vectors, which product applies A then B?","oHtml":["BA.","AB.","A+B.","Entrywise multiplication of A and B."],"whyHtml":"The right-hand factor acts on the original vector first."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 5 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-matrix-area","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Matrices","title":"Determinants, area scaling and orientation","outcomes":["2.2.9"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Area scale is the absolute determinant · Orientation is a separate statement · Collapse and composition · Solve constraints carefully","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Determinants, area scaling and orientation</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Area scale is the absolute determinant</li><li>Orientation is a separate statement</li><li>Collapse and composition</li><li>Solve constraints carefully</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Area scale is the absolute determinant","text":"A plane transformation with matrix A multiplies area by $|\\det A|$. The images of the unit basis vectors form a parallelogram whose signed area is the determinant. Any polygon can be decomposed into triangles, so the same area factor applies throughout the plane. Use the absolute value for ordinary geometric area, which cannot be negative.","diagram":null,"html":"A plane transformation with matrix A multiplies area by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\det A|</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The images of the unit basis vectors form a parallelogram whose signed area is the determinant. Any polygon can be decomposed into triangles, so the same area factor applies throughout the plane. Use the absolute value for ordinary geometric area, which cannot be negative."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Orientation is a separate statement","text":"A positive determinant preserves orientation and a negative determinant reverses it. This describes the order of vertices or handedness, not merely whether a shape looks rotated. Rotations have determinant 1; reflections have determinant -1. Both preserve area magnitude. A dilation by k has determinant $k^2$ in two dimensions, so even a negative k preserves orientation while doubling lengths quadruples areas.","diagram":"matrix-area","html":"A positive determinant preserves orientation and a negative determinant reverses it. This describes the order of vertices or handedness, not merely whether a shape looks rotated. Rotations have determinant 1; reflections have determinant -1. Both preserve area magnitude. A dilation by k has determinant <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> in two dimensions, so even a negative k preserves orientation while doubling lengths quadruples areas."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Collapse and composition","text":"A zero determinant maps plane regions into a line or point, producing zero area. For compositions, $\\det(BA)=\\det B\\det A$, so area factors multiply. Two orientation reversals compose to an orientation-preserving transformation. Area preservation does not imply length or angle preservation: a shear can have determinant 1 while altering angles and lengths.","diagram":null,"html":"A zero determinant maps plane regions into a line or point, producing zero area. For compositions, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(BA)=\\det B\\det A</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so area factors multiply. Two orientation reversals compose to an orientation-preserving transformation. Area preservation does not imply length or angle preservation: a shear can have determinant 1 while altering angles and lengths."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Solve constraints carefully","text":"If transformed area is given, write $|ad-bc|$ equal to the required area ratio. An absolute-value equation can admit two parameter values, corresponding to different orientations. Report both unless a direction condition removes one. Inverse transformations use reciprocal area factor when the determinant is nonzero. Check units: area ratios are dimensionless, whereas original and image areas carry squared length units.","diagram":null,"html":"If transformed area is given, write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|ad-bc|</annotation></semantics></math></span> equal to the required area ratio. An absolute-value equation can admit two parameter values, corresponding to different orientations. Report both unless a direction condition removes one. Inverse transformations use reciprocal area factor when the determinant is nonzero. Check units: area ratios are dimensionless, whereas original and image areas carry squared length units."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-matrix-area-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"A triangle has area 6 square units. It is transformed by $A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\0&3\\end{pmatrix}$. Find image area and orientation. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\det A=2(3)-1(0)=6$.","[1] Area factor is $|6|=6$.","[1] Image area is $6\\times6=36$ square units.","[1] The positive determinant preserves orientation."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A triangle has area 6 square units. It is transformed by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\0&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find image area and orientation. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det A=2(3)-1(0)=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Area factor is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>6</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|6|=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Image area is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\times6=36</annotation></semantics></math></span> square units.","[1] The positive determinant preserves orientation."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-area-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"A transformation $A=\\begin{pmatrix}k&1\\\\2&3\\end{pmatrix}$ sends area 4 to area 20. Find k, then identify the orientation-preserving value. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Area ratio is $20/4=5$, so $|3k-2|=5$.","[1] $3k-2=5$ gives $k=7/3$.","[1] $3k-2=-5$ gives $k=-1$.","[1] Only $k=7/3$ gives a positive determinant and preserves orientation."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A transformation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>k</mi></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}k&amp;1\\\\2&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> sends area 4 to area 20. Find k, then identify the orientation-preserving value. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Area ratio is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20/4=5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|3k-2|=5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3k-2=5</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=7/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3k-2=-5</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Only <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=7/3</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives a positive determinant and preserves orientation."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-area-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"A dilation has scale factor -2. Find its matrix, determinant, area factor and orientation. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $A=-2I=\\begin{pmatrix}-2&0\\\\0&-2\\end{pmatrix}$.","[1] Determinant is $(-2)(-2)=4$.","[1] Areas are multiplied by 4 while lengths are multiplied by 2.","[1] Orientation is preserved; this is a half-turn combined with dilation factor 2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A dilation has scale factor -2. Find its matrix, determinant, area factor and orientation. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=-2I=\\begin{pmatrix}-2&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Determinant is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-2)(-2)=4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Areas are multiplied by 4 while lengths are multiplied by 2.","[1] Orientation is preserved; this is a half-turn combined with dilation factor 2."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-area-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Explain why $S=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$ preserves area but not all lengths. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\det S=1$, giving area factor 1.","[1] The unit vector $(0,1)$ maps to $(2,1)$.","[1] Its length changes from 1 to $\\sqrt5$.","[1] Thus area preservation does not imply an isometry."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span> preserves area but not all lengths. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det S=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving area factor 1.","[1] The unit vector <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> maps to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its length changes from 1 to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus area preservation does not imply an isometry."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-area-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Transformation A has determinant -3 and B determinant -2. Find the area factor and orientation of BA. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\det(BA)=\\det B\\det A$.","[1] The determinant is $(-2)(-3)=6$.","[1] Area factor is 6.","[1] The positive product preserves orientation, combining two reversals."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Transformation A has determinant -3 and B determinant -2. Find the area factor and orientation of BA. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(BA)=\\det B\\det A</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The determinant is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-2)(-3)=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Area factor is 6.","[1] The positive product preserves orientation, combining two reversals."]},{"id":"mas11-matrix-area-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A rectangle of area 12 is transformed by $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\2&4\\end{pmatrix}$. Find image area and explain the geometric collapse. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\det A=4-4=0$.","[1] Therefore the image area is $0\\times12=0$.","[1] Every image satisfies $y^{\\prime}=2x^{\\prime}$ because the second row is twice the first.","[1] The region collapses onto a line, so the map is singular."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A rectangle of area 12 is transformed by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;2\\\\2&amp;4\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find image area and explain the geometric collapse. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det A=4-4=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Therefore the image area is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\times12=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Every image satisfies <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^{\\prime}=2x^{\\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></span> because the second row is twice the first.","[1] The region collapses onto a line, so the map is singular."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What is the geometric area scale for determinant -4?","o":["4, with orientation reversed.","-4 square units.","2, with orientation preserved.","Zero because the determinant is negative."],"a":0,"why":"Absolute determinant determines area magnitude; its sign determines orientation.","qHtml":"What is the geometric area scale for determinant -4?","oHtml":["4, with orientation reversed.","-4 square units.","2, with orientation preserved.","Zero because the determinant is negative."],"whyHtml":"Absolute determinant determines area magnitude; its sign determines orientation."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-rational-numbers","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Real and complex numbers","title":"Rational numbers, recurring decimals and number proofs","outcomes":["2.3.1","2.3.2"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Define the set precisely · Recurring decimals become fractions · Closure under operations · Separate closure from roots","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Rational numbers, recurring decimals and number proofs</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Define the set precisely</li><li>Recurring decimals become fractions</li><li>Closure under operations</li><li>Separate closure from roots</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Define the set precisely","text":"A rational number can be written $p/q$ where p and q are integers and $q\\ne0$. An integer is rational because its denominator can be 1. Fractions need not be proper or positive. A noninteger is not automatically irrational. Distinguish an exact recurring decimal from a finite rounded display: $0.333\\ldots$ means a specified infinite pattern, whereas 0.333 is exactly $333/1000$.","diagram":null,"html":"A rational number can be written <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p/q</annotation></semantics></math></span> where p and q are integers and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. An integer is rational because its denominator can be 1. Fractions need not be proper or positive. A noninteger is not automatically irrational. Distinguish an exact recurring decimal from a finite rounded display: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.333</mn><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.333\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span> means a specified infinite pattern, whereas 0.333 is exactly <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>333</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">333/1000</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recurring decimals become fractions","text":"Let x denote the recurring decimal, multiply by appropriate powers of 10 to align the repeated tails, then subtract. For $x=0.272727\\ldots$, $100x-x=27$, giving $x=27/99$. For a nonrecurring initial part, choose two powers that align the repeating portions rather than subtracting an unaligned tail. Reduce the resulting fraction and verify it by division.","diagram":"fractions","html":"Let x denote the recurring decimal, multiply by appropriate powers of 10 to align the repeated tails, then subtract. For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.272727</mn><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0.272727\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100x-x=27</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>27</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=27/99</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For a nonrecurring initial part, choose two powers that align the repeating portions rather than subtracting an unaligned tail. Reduce the resulting fraction and verify it by division."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Closure under operations","text":"If $a=p/q$ and $b=r/s$ are rational with nonzero denominators, $a+b=(ps+rq)/(qs)$ and $ab=pr/(qs)$ are rational because their numerators and denominators are integers. Differences are similar. A quotient is rational only when the divisor is nonzero. A proof of closure uses arbitrary rational inputs, not a small collection of examples.","diagram":null,"html":"If <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=p/q</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=r/s</annotation></semantics></math></span> are rational with nonzero denominators, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a+b=(ps+rq)/(qs)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ab=pr/(qs)</annotation></semantics></math></span> are rational because their numerators and denominators are integers. Differences are similar. A quotient is rational only when the divisor is nonzero. A proof of closure uses arbitrary rational inputs, not a small collection of examples."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate closure from roots","text":"Rational numbers are closed under addition, subtraction, multiplication and permitted division, but not under taking square roots. $\\sqrt4=2$ is rational while $\\sqrt2$ is irrational. A rational squared value does not force a rational original value. Use definitions and reduced fractions in proofs, and avoid relying on calculator decimal appearances to classify an exact number.","diagram":null,"html":"Rational numbers are closed under addition, subtraction, multiplication and permitted division, but not under taking square roots. <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>4</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt4=2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is rational while <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is irrational. A rational squared value does not force a rational original value. Use definitions and reduced fractions in proofs, and avoid relying on calculator decimal appearances to classify an exact number."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-rational-numbers-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Express $0.272727\\ldots$ as a reduced fraction. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Set $x=0.272727\\ldots$.","[1] $100x=27.272727\\ldots$.","[1] Subtract to obtain $99x=27$.","[1] $x=27/99=3/11$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Express <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.272727</mn><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.272727\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span> as a reduced fraction. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Set <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.272727</mn><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0.272727\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>27.272727</mn><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100x=27.272727\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Subtract to obtain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>99</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">99x=27</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>27</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>99</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=27/99=3/11</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-rational-numbers-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Express $0.166666\\ldots$ as a reduced fraction. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let $x=0.166666\\ldots$.","[1] $10x=1.666666\\ldots$ and $100x=16.666666\\ldots$.","[1] Subtraction gives $90x=15$.","[1] $x=15/90=1/6$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Express <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.166666</mn><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.166666\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span> as a reduced fraction. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.166666</mn><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0.166666\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.666666</mn><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10x=1.666666\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16.666666</mn><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100x=16.666666\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Subtraction gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90x=15</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>90</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=15/90=1/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-rational-numbers-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Prove the sum of two rational numbers is rational. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let $a=p/q$, $b=r/s$ with integer p,q,r,s and $qs\\ne0$.","[1] $a+b=(ps+rq)/(qs)$.","[1] Both ps+rq and qs are integers, and qs is nonzero.","[1] Thus the sum meets the rational-number definition."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove the sum of two rational numbers is rational. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=p/q</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=r/s</annotation></semantics></math></span> with integer p,q,r,s and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>s</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">qs\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a+b=(ps+rq)/(qs)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both ps+rq and qs are integers, and qs is nonzero.","[1] Thus the sum meets the rational-number definition."]},{"id":"mas11-rational-numbers-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Convert $0.123123\\ldots$ to a fraction. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Set $x=0.123123\\ldots$ and align the three-digit period with $1000x$.","[1] $1000x-x=123$.","[1] $x=123/999$.","[1] Divide numerator and denominator by 3 to obtain $41/333$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Convert <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.123123</mn><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.123123\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span> to a fraction. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Set <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.123123</mn><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0.123123\\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span> and align the three-digit period with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>123</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000x-x=123</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>123</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=123/999</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Divide numerator and denominator by 3 to obtain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>41</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>333</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">41/333</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-rational-numbers-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Classify $-7$, $0.125$, $\\sqrt{49}$ and $\\sqrt2$ as rational or irrational, justifying each. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $-7=-7/1$ is rational.","[1] $0.125=1/8$ is rational.","[1] $\\sqrt{49}=7$ is rational.","[1] $\\sqrt2$ is irrational by the reduced-fraction parity contradiction, not its rounded decimal."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Classify <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-7</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.125</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.125</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>49</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{49}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> as rational or irrational, justifying each. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-7=-7/1</annotation></semantics></math></span> is rational.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.125</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.125=1/8</annotation></semantics></math></span> is rational.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>49</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{49}=7</annotation></semantics></math></span> is rational.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is irrational by the reduced-fraction parity contradiction, not its rounded decimal."]},{"id":"mas11-rational-numbers-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Prove that a nonzero rational times an irrational is irrational. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let a be nonzero rational and b irrational; assume ab is rational.","[1] Since $a\\ne0$, $b=(ab)/a$.","[1] Division of rationals by a nonzero rational is rational, contradicting b irrational.","[1] Therefore ab is irrational; the nonzero condition is essential because $0b=0$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove that a nonzero rational times an irrational is irrational. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let a be nonzero rational and b irrational; assume ab is rational.","[1] Since <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=(ab)/a</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Division of rationals by a nonzero rational is rational, contradicting b irrational.","[1] Therefore ab is irrational; the nonzero condition is essential because <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0b=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which exact decimal is certainly rational?","o":["An eventually recurring decimal with a specified repeating pattern.","Any number whose calculator display stops after ten digits.","Every noninteger.","Every square root."],"a":0,"why":"An eventual repeating pattern can be converted algebraically to an integer fraction.","qHtml":"Which exact decimal is certainly rational?","oHtml":["An eventually recurring decimal with a specified repeating pattern.","Any number whose calculator display stops after ten digits.","Every noninteger.","Every square root."],"whyHtml":"An eventual repeating pattern can be converted algebraically to an integer fraction."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-irrational-proof","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Real and complex numbers","title":"Irrationality and contradiction with reduced fractions","outcomes":["2.3.3"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Begin with a reduced fraction · Justify divisibility transitions · Use closure to extend results · Conclude the exact assertion","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Irrationality and contradiction with reduced fractions</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Begin with a reduced fraction</li><li>Justify divisibility transitions</li><li>Use closure to extend results</li><li>Conclude the exact assertion</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Begin with a reduced fraction","text":"To prove $\\sqrt2$ irrational, assume the opposite and write $\\sqrt2=p/q$ with coprime integers p,q and $q>0$. The reduced-fraction condition is essential because the contradiction concerns a common factor. Squaring gives $p^2=2q^2$, hence p is even. Write $p=2k$ and substitute to show q is even too, contradicting coprimality.","diagram":null,"html":"To prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> irrational, assume the opposite and write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2=p/q</annotation></semantics></math></span> with coprime integers p,q and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The reduced-fraction condition is essential because the contradiction concerns a common factor. Squaring gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p^2=2q^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, hence p is even. Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=2k</annotation></semantics></math></span> and substitute to show q is even too, contradicting coprimality."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Justify divisibility transitions","text":"When a prime divides a square, it divides the original integer. For parity, prove the contrapositive: the square of an odd integer $2k+1$ is odd. For a prime such as 3, integer residues can justify the corresponding conclusion. It is not valid to generalise from composite divisors: 4 divides $2^2$ but does not divide 2. Each divisibility inference needs its applicable hypothesis.","diagram":"contrapositive","html":"When a prime divides a square, it divides the original integer. For parity, prove the contrapositive: the square of an odd integer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2k+1</annotation></semantics></math></span> is odd. For a prime such as 3, integer residues can justify the corresponding conclusion. It is not valid to generalise from composite divisors: 4 divides <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> but does not divide 2. Each divisibility inference needs its applicable hypothesis."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use closure to extend results","text":"Once an irrationality result is known, rational closure often gives shorter proofs for related expressions. If $3+\\sqrt2$ were rational, subtracting 3 would make $\\sqrt2$ rational. A nonzero rational multiple also remains irrational. However, the sum or product of two irrationals can be rational: $\\sqrt2+(-\\sqrt2)=0$ and $\\sqrt2\\sqrt2=2$.","diagram":null,"html":"Once an irrationality result is known, rational closure often gives shorter proofs for related expressions. If <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3+\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> were rational, subtracting 3 would make <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> rational. A nonzero rational multiple also remains irrational. However, the sum or product of two irrationals can be rational: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2+(-\\sqrt2)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2\\sqrt2=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Conclude the exact assertion","text":"An irrationality proof establishes that no integer fraction can equal the number, rather than that a calculator has not found one. State the contradiction and reject the initial assumption. Keep domain restrictions visible when dividing or squaring. For square roots of positive integers, a nonsquare integer has irrational square root; prime-factor exponents provide the general explanation.","diagram":null,"html":"An irrationality proof establishes that no integer fraction can equal the number, rather than that a calculator has not found one. State the contradiction and reject the initial assumption. Keep domain restrictions visible when dividing or squaring. For square roots of positive integers, a nonsquare integer has irrational square root; prime-factor exponents provide the general explanation."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-irrational-proof-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Prove $\\sqrt2$ is irrational. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Assume $\\sqrt2=p/q$ in lowest terms, $q>0$.","[1] $p^2=2q^2$ makes p even, so $p=2k$.","[1] Substitution gives $q^2=2k^2$, so q is also even.","[1] This contradicts coprimality, so $\\sqrt2$ is irrational."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is irrational. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Assume <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2=p/q</annotation></semantics></math></span> in lowest terms, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p^2=2q^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> makes p even, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=2k</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Substitution gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q^2=2k^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so q is also even.","[1] This contradicts coprimality, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is irrational."]},{"id":"mas11-irrational-proof-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Prove $\\sqrt3$ is irrational using residues modulo 3. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Assume $\\sqrt3=p/q$ in lowest terms; $p^2=3q^2$.","[1] Squares modulo 3 are 0 or 1, so $3\\mid p^2$ implies $3\\mid p$; write $p=3k$.","[1] Substitution gives $q^2=3k^2$, so $3\\mid q$.","[1] Both share factor 3, contradicting lowest terms."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt3</annotation></semantics></math></span> is irrational using residues modulo 3. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Assume <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt3=p/q</annotation></semantics></math></span> in lowest terms; <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p^2=3q^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Squares modulo 3 are 0 or 1, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>∣</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\mid p^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> implies <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>∣</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\mid p</annotation></semantics></math></span>; write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=3k</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Substitution gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q^2=3k^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>∣</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\mid q</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both share factor 3, contradicting lowest terms."]},{"id":"mas11-irrational-proof-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Prove $5-2\\sqrt2$ is irrational. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Assume $c=5-2\\sqrt2$ is rational.","[1] Rearrangement gives $\\sqrt2=(5-c)/2$.","[1] The right side is rational by closure and division by nonzero 2.","[1] This contradicts known irrationality of $\\sqrt2$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5-2\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is irrational. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Assume <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=5-2\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is rational.","[1] Rearrangement gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2=(5-c)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The right side is rational by closure and division by nonzero 2.","[1] This contradicts known irrationality of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-irrational-proof-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Give counterexamples to: the sum of two irrational numbers is always irrational; their product is always irrational. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Both $\\sqrt2$ and $-\\sqrt2$ are irrational.","[1] Their sum is 0, which is rational.","[1] Both factors in $\\sqrt2\\times\\sqrt2$ are irrational.","[1] The product is 2, disproving the second universal assertion."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Give counterexamples to: the sum of two irrational numbers is always irrational; their product is always irrational. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Both <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> are irrational.","[1] Their sum is 0, which is rational.","[1] Both factors in <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>×</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2\\times\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> are irrational.","[1] The product is 2, disproving the second universal assertion."]},{"id":"mas11-irrational-proof-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Prove $\\sqrt8$ is irrational using a known result. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\sqrt8=2\\sqrt2$.","[1] Suppose $\\sqrt8$ is rational.","[1] Dividing by 2 would make $\\sqrt2$ rational.","[1] This is a contradiction, so $\\sqrt8$ is irrational."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>8</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt8</annotation></semantics></math></span> is irrational using a known result. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>8</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt8=2\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Suppose <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>8</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt8</annotation></semantics></math></span> is rational.","[1] Dividing by 2 would make <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> rational.","[1] This is a contradiction, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>8</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt8</annotation></semantics></math></span> is irrational."]},{"id":"mas11-irrational-proof-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A student writes: p and q are both even, therefore no fraction p/q exists. Correct the logical conclusion in the standard $\\sqrt2$ proof. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Fractions with even numerators and denominators certainly exist, such as 2/4.","[1] The initial fraction was chosen in lowest terms.","[1] Both even means a common factor 2, contradicting that specific condition.","[1] Therefore the assumed reduced representation of $\\sqrt2$ cannot exist; irrationality follows."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A student writes: p and q are both even, therefore no fraction p/q exists. Correct the logical conclusion in the standard <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> proof. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Fractions with even numerators and denominators certainly exist, such as 2/4.","[1] The initial fraction was chosen in lowest terms.","[1] Both even means a common factor 2, contradicting that specific condition.","[1] Therefore the assumed reduced representation of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> cannot exist; irrationality follows."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Why is lowest terms specified in the standard irrationality proof?","o":["To make a common factor in numerator and denominator a contradiction.","To require numerator and denominator both positive.","To make all fractions irrational.","To avoid squaring."],"a":0,"why":"Without coprimality, even numerator and denominator would not contradict the fraction representation.","qHtml":"Why is lowest terms specified in the standard irrationality proof?","oHtml":["To make a common factor in numerator and denominator a contradiction.","To require numerator and denominator both positive.","To make all fractions irrational.","To avoid squaring."],"whyHtml":"Without coprimality, even numerator and denominator would not contradict the fraction representation."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-induction-sums","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Real and complex numbers","title":"Mathematical induction for sums","outcomes":["2.3.4","2.3.5"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Induction links all positive integers · The hypothesis is a precise equation · Useful factorizations · Scope and limitations","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Mathematical induction for sums</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Induction links all positive integers</li><li>The hypothesis is a precise equation</li><li>Useful factorizations</li><li>Scope and limitations</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Induction links all positive integers","text":"To prove P(n) for every integer $n\\ge n_0$, prove the base case P($n_0$), assume P(k) for an arbitrary integer $k\\ge n_0$, and use that assumption to prove P(k+1). This creates an unbroken chain. Checking many separate cases is not induction; assuming the conclusion for k+1 is circular. State the integer domain and starting value explicitly.","diagram":null,"html":"To prove P(n) for every integer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge n_0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, prove the base case P(<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_0</annotation></semantics></math></span>), assume P(k) for an arbitrary integer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\ge n_0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and use that assumption to prove P(k+1). This creates an unbroken chain. Checking many separate cases is not induction; assuming the conclusion for k+1 is circular. State the integer domain and starting value explicitly."},{"kind":"learn","title":"The hypothesis is a precise equation","text":"For a sum identity, the induction hypothesis concerns the first k terms. To form the k+1 sum, add the next term to both the known sum and its formula, then simplify to the desired expression with k replaced by k+1. Do not change an upper limit without adding its new term. Each algebraic line should show where the hypothesis was used.","diagram":"induction","html":"For a sum identity, the induction hypothesis concerns the first k terms. To form the k+1 sum, add the next term to both the known sum and its formula, then simplify to the desired expression with k replaced by k+1. Do not change an upper limit without adding its new term. Each algebraic line should show where the hypothesis was used."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Useful factorizations","text":"For $1+\\cdots+n=n(n+1)/2$, the step adds k+1 and factors $(k+1)(k+2)/2$. For squares, the next term is $(k+1)^2$, leading to $(k+1)(k+2)(2k+3)/6$. Geometric sums use the next power and a fixed ratio, with ratio 1 handled separately if the denominator contains r-1. Exact factorisation connects the two consecutive statements.","diagram":null,"html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+\\cdots+n=n(n+1)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the step adds k+1 and factors <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k+1)(k+2)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For squares, the next term is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k+1)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, leading to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k+1)(k+2)(2k+3)/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Geometric sums use the next power and a fixed ratio, with ratio 1 handled separately if the denominator contains r-1. Exact factorisation connects the two consecutive statements."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Scope and limitations","text":"Induction proves a formula on the stated integer range; it does not automatically establish it for real n. A missing base case leaves the chain unanchored. A false formula can have a misleading-looking manipulation if the hypothesis is assumed without verification. In an exam, label base, assumption, step and conclusion, and show enough algebra to establish the new case rather than simply claiming it follows.","diagram":null,"html":"Induction proves a formula on the stated integer range; it does not automatically establish it for real n. A missing base case leaves the chain unanchored. A false formula can have a misleading-looking manipulation if the hypothesis is assumed without verification. In an exam, label base, assumption, step and conclusion, and show enough algebra to establish the new case rather than simply claiming it follows."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-induction-sums-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Prove $1+2+\\cdots+n=n(n+1)/2$ for integers $n\\ge1$. (5 marks)","steps":["[1] At $n=1$, both sides equal 1.","[1] Assume $S_k=k(k+1)/2$.","[2] $S_{k+1}=S_k+(k+1)=(k+1)(k/2+1)=(k+1)(k+2)/2$.","[1] The base and implication hold, so the formula holds for all integers $n\\ge1$."],"allocations":[1,1,2,1],"marks":5,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+2+\\cdots+n=n(n+1)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> for integers <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (5 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] At <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, both sides equal 1.","[1] Assume <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_k=k(k+1)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[2] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{k+1}=S_k+(k+1)=(k+1)(k/2+1)=(k+1)(k+2)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The base and implication hold, so the formula holds for all integers <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-induction-sums-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Prove $1+3+\\cdots+(2n-1)=n^2$ for $n\\ge1$. (5 marks)","steps":["[1] Base $n=1$: $1=1^2$.","[1] Assume the first k odd numbers sum to $k^2$.","[2] The next odd number is $2k+1$, giving $S_{k+1}=k^2+2k+1=(k+1)^2$.","[1] Induction proves the claim for all positive integers."],"allocations":[1,1,2,1],"marks":5,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+3+\\cdots+(2n-1)=n^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (5 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Base <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1=1^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Assume the first k odd numbers sum to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[2] The next odd number is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2k+1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{k+1}=k^2+2k+1=(k+1)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Induction proves the claim for all positive integers."]},{"id":"mas11-induction-sums-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Prove $\\sum_{j=1}^n j^2=n(n+1)(2n+1)/6$. (5 marks)","steps":["[1] Base $n=1$ gives 1 on both sides.","[1] Assume the formula at k.","[2] Add $(k+1)^2$: $S_{k+1}=(k+1)[k(2k+1)+6(k+1)]/6=(k+1)(2k^2+7k+6)/6$.","[1] Factor the bracket as $(k+2)(2k+3)$; this is the required k+1 formula, proving the claim."],"allocations":[1,1,2,1],"marks":5,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{j=1}^n j^2=n(n+1)(2n+1)/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (5 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Base <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives 1 on both sides.","[1] Assume the formula at k.","[2] Add <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k+1)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{k+1}=(k+1)[k(2k+1)+6(k+1)]/6=(k+1)(2k^2+7k+6)/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Factor the bracket as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k+2)(2k+3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; this is the required k+1 formula, proving the claim."]},{"id":"mas11-induction-sums-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Prove $1+2+4+\\cdots+2^{n-1}=2^n-1$ for $n\\ge1$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Base $n=1$: $1=2-1$.","[1] Assume the first k terms sum to $2^k-1$.","[1] Add next term $2^k$ to obtain $2^{k+1}-1$.","[1] Thus the induction step and base establish the identity."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+2+4+\\cdots+2^{n-1}=2^n-1</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Base <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1=2-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Assume the first k terms sum to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^k-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Add next term <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^k</annotation></semantics></math></span> to obtain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{k+1}-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus the induction step and base establish the identity."]},{"id":"mas11-induction-sums-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Prove $\\sum_{j=1}^n j(j+1)=n(n+1)(n+2)/3$. (5 marks)","steps":["[1] Base $n=1$: $2=1(2)(3)/3$.","[1] Assume the identity at k.","[2] Add $(k+1)(k+2)$: $S_{k+1}=(k+1)(k+2)(k/3+1)=(k+1)(k+2)(k+3)/3$.","[1] This proves P(k+1), so the result holds for all positive integers."],"allocations":[1,1,2,1],"marks":5,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{j=1}^n j(j+1)=n(n+1)(n+2)/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (5 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Base <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2=1(2)(3)/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Assume the identity at k.","[2] Add <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k+1)(k+2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_{k+1}=(k+1)(k+2)(k/3+1)=(k+1)(k+2)(k+3)/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This proves P(k+1), so the result holds for all positive integers."]},{"id":"mas11-induction-sums-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Explain the gap in: the identity is true for n=1,2,3, so it is true for all positive integers. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The checks establish only those three individual cases.","[1] Infinitely many later cases remain unproved.","[1] An induction proof must assume P(k) for arbitrary eligible k and establish P(k+1).","[1] Combined with a base case, that general implication supplies the missing chain."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain the gap in: the identity is true for n=1,2,3, so it is true for all positive integers. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The checks establish only those three individual cases.","[1] Infinitely many later cases remain unproved.","[1] An induction proof must assume P(k) for arbitrary eligible k and establish P(k+1).","[1] Combined with a base case, that general implication supplies the missing chain."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What is added when extending $\\sum_{j=1}^k j^2$ to k+1 terms?","o":["$(k+1)^2$.","$k^2$.","$k+1$.","Nothing; just change the notation."],"a":0,"why":"The new upper limit introduces the square of the next integer.","qHtml":"What is added when extending <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{j=1}^k j^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> to k+1 terms?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k+1)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k+1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Nothing; just change the notation."],"whyHtml":"The new upper limit introduces the square of the next integer."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-induction-divisibility","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Real and complex numbers","title":"Induction for divisibility and its logical limits","outcomes":["2.3.4","2.3.6"],"icon":"📐","scope":"State divisibility as an integer multiple · Rearrange powers strategically · Prove inequalities with a valid starting point · Avoid incomplete or circular proofs","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Induction for divisibility and its logical limits</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>State divisibility as an integer multiple</li><li>Rearrange powers strategically</li><li>Prove inequalities with a valid starting point</li><li>Avoid incomplete or circular proofs</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"State divisibility as an integer multiple","text":"The notation $d\\mid N$ means $N=dm$ for some integer m. To prove divisibility by induction, start with a base case, assume the expression at k is a multiple of d and rewrite the k+1 expression as a multiple of the old expression plus another visibly divisible term. The multiplier introduced by the hypothesis must remain an integer.","diagram":null,"html":"The notation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\mid N</annotation></semantics></math></span> means <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=dm</annotation></semantics></math></span> for some integer m. To prove divisibility by induction, start with a base case, assume the expression at k is a multiple of d and rewrite the k+1 expression as a multiple of the old expression plus another visibly divisible term. The multiplier introduced by the hypothesis must remain an integer."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Rearrange powers strategically","text":"For $3^n-1$, write $3^{k+1}-1=3(3^k-1)+2$. This makes divisibility by 2 immediate from the hypothesis. For a different divisor or expression, the extra term must be checked rather than presumed divisible. Aim to expose the hypothesis exactly; replacing it with a superficially similar expression can invalidate the step.","diagram":"induction","html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3^n-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3^{k+1}-1=3(3^k-1)+2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This makes divisibility by 2 immediate from the hypothesis. For a different divisor or expression, the extra term must be checked rather than presumed divisible. Aim to expose the hypothesis exactly; replacing it with a superficially similar expression can invalidate the step."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Prove inequalities with a valid starting point","text":"Induction also proves inequalities, but the starting integer matters. The claim $2^n>n^2$ fails for several small n and holds for $n\\ge5$. When multiplying an inequality, check the multiplier is positive. A step may need an auxiliary comparison such as $2k^2>(k+1)^2$ for the eligible k range. State strict versus non-strict inequalities precisely.","diagram":null,"html":"Induction also proves inequalities, but the starting integer matters. The claim <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^n&gt;n^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> fails for several small n and holds for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge5</annotation></semantics></math></span>. When multiplying an inequality, check the multiplier is positive. A step may need an auxiliary comparison such as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2k^2&gt;(k+1)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> for the eligible k range. State strict versus non-strict inequalities precisely."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Avoid incomplete or circular proofs","text":"The induction hypothesis is an assumption for one arbitrary eligible k, not permission to assume the entire theorem. Both the base and step are necessary. If a recurrence jumps by two, one base proves only one parity unless the other chain is anchored too. An induction result is limited to the established integer range; do not extrapolate to negative integers or nonintegers without a separate argument.","diagram":null,"html":"The induction hypothesis is an assumption for one arbitrary eligible k, not permission to assume the entire theorem. Both the base and step are necessary. If a recurrence jumps by two, one base proves only one parity unless the other chain is anchored too. An induction result is limited to the established integer range; do not extrapolate to negative integers or nonintegers without a separate argument."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Extended exam task","q":6},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-induction-divisibility-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Prove $2\\mid(3^n-1)$ for every integer $n\\ge1$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Base: $3^1-1=2$ is divisible by 2.","[1] Assume $3^k-1=2m$ for integer m.","[1] $3^{k+1}-1=3(3^k-1)+2=2(3m+1)$.","[1] The bracket is an integer; induction proves divisibility."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>∣</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\mid(3^n-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for every integer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Base: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3^1-1=2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is divisible by 2.","[1] Assume <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3^k-1=2m</annotation></semantics></math></span> for integer m.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3^{k+1}-1=3(3^k-1)+2=2(3m+1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The bracket is an integer; induction proves divisibility."]},{"id":"mas11-induction-divisibility-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Prove $6\\mid(7^n-1)$ for all $n\\ge1$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Base is $7-1=6$.","[1] Assume $7^k-1=6m$.","[1] $7^{k+1}-1=7(7^k-1)+6=6(7m+1)$.","[1] This is an integer multiple of 6, completing induction."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>∣</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>7</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\mid(7^n-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for all <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Base is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7-1=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Assume <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>7</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7^k-1=6m</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>7</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>7</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7^{k+1}-1=7(7^k-1)+6=6(7m+1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This is an integer multiple of 6, completing induction."]},{"id":"mas11-induction-divisibility-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Prove $3\\mid(n^3-n)$ for every positive integer n by induction. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Base $n=1$ gives 0, divisible by 3.","[1] Assume $k^3-k=3m$.","[1] $(k+1)^3-(k+1)=(k^3-k)+3k^2+3k=3(m+k^2+k)$.","[1] The expression is divisible by 3, so the theorem follows."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>∣</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\mid(n^3-n)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for every positive integer n by induction. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Base <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives 0, divisible by 3.","[1] Assume <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k^3-k=3m</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k+1)^3-(k+1)=(k^3-k)+3k^2+3k=3(m+k^2+k)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The expression is divisible by 3, so the theorem follows."]},{"id":"mas11-induction-divisibility-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Prove $2^n>n^2$ for integers $n\\ge5$. (5 marks)","steps":["[1] Base $n=5$: $32>25$.","[1] Assume $2^k>k^2$ for $k\\ge5$, so $2^{k+1}>2k^2$.","[2] $2k^2-(k+1)^2=k^2-2k-1=(k-1)^2-2>0$ for $k\\ge5$.","[1] Hence $2^{k+1}>(k+1)^2$, completing induction on the stated range."],"allocations":[1,1,2,1],"marks":5,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^n&gt;n^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> for integers <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge5</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (5 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Base <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=5</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">32&gt;25</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Assume <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^k&gt;k^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\ge5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{k+1}&gt;2k^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[2] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2k^2-(k+1)^2=k^2-2k-1=(k-1)^2-2&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\ge5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{k+1}&gt;(k+1)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, completing induction on the stated range."]},{"id":"mas11-induction-divisibility-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"A proof establishes P(1) and P(k) implies P(k+2). Does it prove P(n) for every positive integer? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Starting from 1, the implication reaches 3,5,7 and subsequent odd integers.","[1] It never reaches 2 or any even integer from that base.","[1] The even chain needs a separate base, such as P(2).","[1] Without it, the claimed all-integer conclusion is unsupported."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A proof establishes P(1) and P(k) implies P(k+2). Does it prove P(n) for every positive integer? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Starting from 1, the implication reaches 3,5,7 and subsequent odd integers.","[1] It never reaches 2 or any even integer from that base.","[1] The even chain needs a separate base, such as P(2).","[1] Without it, the claimed all-integer conclusion is unsupported."]},{"id":"mas11-induction-divisibility-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Prove $8\\mid(5^{2n}-1)$ for $n\\ge1$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Base $n=1$: $25-1=24$ is divisible by 8.","[1] Assume $5^{2k}-1=8m$.","[1] $5^{2(k+1)}-1=25(5^{2k}-1)+24=8(25m+3)$.","[1] The integer bracket proves the next case and completes induction."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>∣</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\mid(5^{2n}-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Base <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">25-1=24</annotation></semantics></math></span> is divisible by 8.","[1] Assume <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>5</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5^{2k}-1=8m</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>5</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>25</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5^{2(k+1)}-1=25(5^{2k}-1)+24=8(25m+3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The integer bracket proves the next case and completes induction."]},{"prompt":"For integers $n\\ge1$, prove $7\\mid(8^n-1)$ by induction. Then determine whether $7\\mid(8^n+1)$ for any eligible n, and explain why checking five examples cannot replace your proof. (8 marks)","steps":["[1] Base: $8^1-1=7$.","[1] Assume $8^k-1=7m$ for an integer m.","[2] $8^{k+1}-1=8(8^k-1)+7=7(8m+1)$, an integer multiple of 7.","[1] The base and step establish the result for all positive integers.","[2] $8^n+1=(8^n-1)+2=7m+2$, never divisible by 7; its remainder is 2.","[1] A finite example list proves only the selected cases; induction provides the arbitrary-k link."],"id":"mas11-induction-divisibility-extended","title":"Extended exam task","allocations":[1,1,2,1,2,1],"marks":8,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed or exact working","promptHtml":"For integers <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>∣</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>8</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7\\mid(8^n-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> by induction. Then determine whether <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>∣</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>8</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7\\mid(8^n+1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for any eligible n, and explain why checking five examples cannot replace your proof. (8 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Base: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>8</mn><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8^1-1=7</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Assume <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>8</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8^k-1=7m</annotation></semantics></math></span> for an integer m.","[2] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>8</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>8</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8^{k+1}-1=8(8^k-1)+7=7(8m+1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, an integer multiple of 7.","[1] The base and step establish the result for all positive integers.","[2] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>8</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>8</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8^n+1=(8^n-1)+2=7m+2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, never divisible by 7; its remainder is 2.","[1] A finite example list proves only the selected cases; induction provides the arbitrary-k link."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which completes a divisibility induction step?","o":["Writing the next expression as the divisor times an integer.","Checking only the next numerical example.","Assuming the desired next expression is divisible.","Giving a calculator approximation."],"a":0,"why":"Divisibility is an exact integer statement, established for arbitrary k.","qHtml":"Which completes a divisibility induction step?","oHtml":["Writing the next expression as the divisor times an integer.","Checking only the next numerical example.","Assuming the desired next expression is divisible.","Giving a calculator approximation."],"whyHtml":"Divisibility is an exact integer statement, established for arbitrary k."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 5 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-complex-arithmetic","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Real and complex numbers","title":"Imaginary unit and Cartesian complex arithmetic","outcomes":["2.3.7","2.3.8","2.3.10"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Extend the real number system · Add and subtract components · Multiply and simplify powers · Write exact results clearly","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Imaginary unit and Cartesian complex arithmetic</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Extend the real number system</li><li>Add and subtract components</li><li>Multiply and simplify powers</li><li>Write exact results clearly</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Extend the real number system","text":"The imaginary unit i satisfies $i^2=-1$. A complex number has Cartesian form $z=a+bi$ where a,b are real. Its real part is a and its imaginary part is b, not bi. Real numbers are included by taking b=0. Equality of complex numbers requires equality of both parts. Complex numbers are not ordered by the usual real-number inequality, so avoid writing one nonreal complex number as greater than another.","diagram":null,"html":"The imaginary unit i satisfies <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i^2=-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A complex number has Cartesian form <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=a+bi</annotation></semantics></math></span> where a,b are real. Its real part is a and its imaginary part is b, not bi. Real numbers are included by taking b=0. Equality of complex numbers requires equality of both parts. Complex numbers are not ordered by the usual real-number inequality, so avoid writing one nonreal complex number as greater than another."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Add and subtract components","text":"Combine real parts and imaginary parts separately. A minus sign before a bracket changes both components. If an equation contains unknown real parameters, equate real parts and imaginary parts to obtain scalar equations. Always state that the parameters are real: the component argument depends on the Cartesian representation with real coefficients.","diagram":"argand","html":"Combine real parts and imaginary parts separately. A minus sign before a bracket changes both components. If an equation contains unknown real parameters, equate real parts and imaginary parts to obtain scalar equations. Always state that the parameters are real: the component argument depends on the Cartesian representation with real coefficients."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Multiply and simplify powers","text":"Expand products distributively, then replace $i^2$ by -1. Thus $(a+bi)(c+di)=(ac-bd)+(ad+bc)i$. Powers of i repeat in a cycle of four: $1,i,-1,-i$. Reduce a nonnegative integer exponent modulo 4 to find the appropriate value. An imaginary product can be real, and squaring a complex number need not leave a nonnegative real result.","diagram":null,"html":"Expand products distributively, then replace <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> by -1. Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(a+bi)(c+di)=(ac-bd)+(ad+bc)i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Powers of i repeat in a cycle of four: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1,i,-1,-i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Reduce a nonnegative integer exponent modulo 4 to find the appropriate value. An imaginary product can be real, and squaring a complex number need not leave a nonnegative real result."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Write exact results clearly","text":"Finish in the requested form, usually a+bi at this stage. Keep real and imaginary coefficients exact when possible. Check expansion by collecting all four terms before simplifying. A square-root symbol for a negative real uses the principal complex square root when written alone, while an equation $z^2=-a$ with $a>0$ has both roots $\\pm i\\sqrt a$.","diagram":null,"html":"Finish in the requested form, usually a+bi at this stage. Keep real and imaginary coefficients exact when possible. Check expansion by collecting all four terms before simplifying. A square-root symbol for a negative real uses the principal complex square root when written alone, while an equation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^2=-a</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> has both roots <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm i\\sqrt a</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-complex-arithmetic-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Calculate $(3+2i)(1-4i)$ in Cartesian form. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Expand: $3-12i+2i-8i^2$.","[1] Replace $i^2=-1$, so $-8i^2=8$.","[1] Real part is $3+8=11$, imaginary coefficient is $-12+2=-10$.","[1] Result $11-10i$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Calculate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3+2i)(1-4i)</annotation></semantics></math></span> in Cartesian form. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Expand: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3-12i+2i-8i^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Replace <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i^2=-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-8i^2=8</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Real part is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3+8=11</annotation></semantics></math></span>, imaginary coefficient is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-12+2=-10</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">11-10i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-arithmetic-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find real x,y if $(x+2)+(3y-1)i=7+8i$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Equate real parts: $x+2=7$.","[1] Equate imaginary coefficients: $3y-1=8$.","[1] Hence $x=5$, $y=3$.","[1] Substitution gives exactly $7+8i$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find real x,y if <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x+2)+(3y-1)i=7+8i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Equate real parts: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+2=7</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Equate imaginary coefficients: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3y-1=8</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Substitution gives exactly <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7+8i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-arithmetic-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Calculate $(5-3i)-(2+4i)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Distribute the subtraction: $5-3i-2-4i$.","[1] Real terms combine to 3.","[1] Imaginary terms combine to $-7i$.","[1] Result $3-7i$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Calculate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(5-3i)-(2+4i)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Distribute the subtraction: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5-3i-2-4i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Real terms combine to 3.","[1] Imaginary terms combine to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-7i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3-7i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-arithmetic-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find $i^{37}+i^{22}$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Powers repeat every four.","[1] $37\\equiv1\\pmod4$, so $i^{37}=i$.","[1] $22\\equiv2\\pmod4$, so $i^{22}=-1$.","[1] Sum is $-1+i$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>37</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>22</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i^{37}+i^{22}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Powers repeat every four.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>37</mn><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace></mspace><mspace width=\"0.4444em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.3333em\"/><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">37\\equiv1\\pmod4</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>37</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i^{37}=i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>22</mn><mo>≡</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace></mspace><mspace width=\"0.4444em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.3333em\"/><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">22\\equiv2\\pmod4</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>22</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i^{22}=-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Sum is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1+i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-arithmetic-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Expand $(2-3i)^2$ and state its real and imaginary parts. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Expansion is $4-12i+9i^2$.","[1] Replace the last term by -9.","[1] The result is $-5-12i$.","[1] Real part is -5 and imaginary part is -12."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Expand <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2-3i)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and state its real and imaginary parts. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Expansion is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-12i+9i^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Replace the last term by -9.","[1] The result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-5-12i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Real part is -5 and imaginary part is -12."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-arithmetic-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Solve $z^2=-20$ over the complex numbers and check both roots. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $-20=20i^2$.","[1] Roots are $z=\\pm i\\sqrt{20}=\\pm2\\sqrt5\\,i$.","[1] Squaring either gives $4(5)i^2=-20$.","[1] Both signs are required for the equation; giving only the principal square root misses one root."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^2=-20</annotation></semantics></math></span> over the complex numbers and check both roots. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-20=20i^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Roots are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi><msqrt><mn>20</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=\\pm i\\sqrt{20}=\\pm2\\sqrt5\\,i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Squaring either gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4(5)i^2=-20</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both signs are required for the equation; giving only the principal square root misses one root."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What is the imaginary part of $4-7i$?","o":["-7.","$-7i$.","4.","7."],"a":0,"why":"The imaginary part is the real coefficient of i.","qHtml":"What is the imaginary part of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-7i</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","oHtml":["-7.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-7i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","4.","7."],"whyHtml":"The imaginary part is the real coefficient of i."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-complex-conjugates","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Real and complex numbers","title":"Conjugates, division and real denominators","outcomes":["2.3.9","2.3.10"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Conjugation reverses the imaginary part · A conjugate product is real · Division uses the denominator conjugate · Use conjugate identities","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Conjugates, division and real denominators</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Conjugation reverses the imaginary part</li><li>A conjugate product is real</li><li>Division uses the denominator conjugate</li><li>Use conjugate identities</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Conjugation reverses the imaginary part","text":"The conjugate of $z=a+bi$ is $\\bar z=a-bi$. It reflects the point in the real axis on an Argand diagram. Conjugating twice returns z. The sum $z+\\bar z=2a$ and difference $z-\\bar z=2bi$ isolate the two components. A complex number is real exactly when it equals its conjugate.","diagram":null,"html":"The conjugate of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=a+bi</annotation></semantics></math></span> is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar z=a-bi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. It reflects the point in the real axis on an Argand diagram. Conjugating twice returns z. The sum <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z+\\bar z=2a</annotation></semantics></math></span> and difference <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z-\\bar z=2bi</annotation></semantics></math></span> isolate the two components. A complex number is real exactly when it equals its conjugate."},{"kind":"learn","title":"A conjugate product is real","text":"$z\\bar z=(a+bi)(a-bi)=a^2+b^2$. The modulus is $|z|=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$, so $z\\bar z=|z|^2$. For $z\\ne0$ this is positive, allowing a real denominator. Modulus is a real distance, distinct from z itself; $|z|^2$ is not the same operation as $z^2$.","diagram":"conjugate","html":"<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\bar z=(a+bi)(a-bi)=a^2+b^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The modulus is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z|=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\bar z=|z|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span> this is positive, allowing a real denominator. Modulus is a real distance, distinct from z itself; <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is not the same operation as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Division uses the denominator conjugate","text":"For $w\\ne0$, multiply numerator and denominator of z/w by $\\bar w$. Expand the numerator carefully and replace the denominator by $|w|^2$. This changes the appearance without changing the value. Dividing only the real parts and imaginary parts separately is invalid. State the excluded zero denominator for parameterised expressions.","diagram":null,"html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, multiply numerator and denominator of z/w by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar w</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Expand the numerator carefully and replace the denominator by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>w</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|w|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This changes the appearance without changing the value. Dividing only the real parts and imaginary parts separately is invalid. State the excluded zero denominator for parameterised expressions."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use conjugate identities","text":"Conjugation distributes over sums and products: $\\overline{z+w}=\\bar z+\\bar w$ and $\\overline{zw}=\\bar z\\bar w$. It also respects a quotient when the denominator is nonzero. These identities can simplify expressions and help verify answers. An equation that z and its conjugate satisfy can impose real-coordinate constraints; derive those rather than guessing from a sketch.","diagram":null,"html":"Conjugation distributes over sums and products: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overline{z+w}=\\bar z+\\bar w</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overline{zw}=\\bar z\\bar w</annotation></semantics></math></span>. It also respects a quotient when the denominator is nonzero. These identities can simplify expressions and help verify answers. An equation that z and its conjugate satisfy can impose real-coordinate constraints; derive those rather than guessing from a sketch."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-complex-conjugates-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Write $(3+2i)/(1-i)$ in Cartesian form. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Multiply numerator and denominator by $1+i$.","[1] Numerator is $3+3i+2i+2i^2=1+5i$.","[1] Denominator is $(1-i)(1+i)=2$.","[1] Result $1/2+(5/2)i$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3+2i)/(1-i)</annotation></semantics></math></span> in Cartesian form. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Multiply numerator and denominator by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Numerator is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3+3i+2i+2i^2=1+5i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Denominator is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-i)(1+i)=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2+(5/2)i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-conjugates-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"For $z=2-5i$, find $\\bar z$, $z\\bar z$ and $|z|$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\bar z=2+5i$.","[1] $z\\bar z=2^2+(-5)^2=29$.","[1] $|z|=\\sqrt{29}$.","[1] The conjugate product equals the square of the modulus, not the modulus itself."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=2-5i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar z</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\bar z</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z|</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar z=2+5i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>29</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\bar z=2^2+(-5)^2=29</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>29</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z|=\\sqrt{29}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The conjugate product equals the square of the modulus, not the modulus itself."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-conjugates-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Simplify $1/(2+3i)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Multiply by $(2-3i)/(2-3i)$.","[1] Denominator becomes $4+9=13$.","[1] The result is $(2-3i)/13$.","[1] Cartesian form is $2/13-(3/13)i$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Simplify <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(2+3i)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Multiply by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2-3i)/(2-3i)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Denominator becomes <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4+9=13</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2-3i)/13</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Cartesian form is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>13</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/13-(3/13)i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-conjugates-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find real a,b if $z+\\bar z=6$ and $z-\\bar z=-8i$, with $z=a+bi$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $z+\\bar z=2a=6$.","[1] $z-\\bar z=2bi=-8i$.","[1] Thus $a=3$, $b=-4$.","[1] $z=3-4i$, and both conditions check."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find real a,b if <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z+\\bar z=6</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z-\\bar z=-8i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=a+bi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z+\\bar z=2a=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z-\\bar z=2bi=-8i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=-4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=3-4i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and both conditions check."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-conjugates-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Prove $\\overline{zw}=\\bar z\\bar w$ for $z=a+bi$, $w=c+di$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $zw=(ac-bd)+(ad+bc)i$.","[1] Its conjugate is $(ac-bd)-(ad+bc)i$.","[1] Expanding $(a-bi)(c-di)$ gives that same expression.","[1] Hence the identity holds for arbitrary real a,b,c,d."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overline{zw}=\\bar z\\bar w</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=a+bi</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=c+di</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">zw=(ac-bd)+(ad+bc)i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its conjugate is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(ac-bd)-(ad+bc)i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Expanding <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(a-bi)(c-di)</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives that same expression.","[1] Hence the identity holds for arbitrary real a,b,c,d."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-conjugates-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Solve $1/z=(1+2i)/5$ and state the restriction on z. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Division requires $z\\ne0$.","[1] Rearrange to $z=5/(1+2i)$.","[1] Multiply by the conjugate: $z=5(1-2i)/(1+4)=1-2i$.","[1] This is nonzero and its reciprocal equals $(1+2i)/5$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/z=(1+2i)/5</annotation></semantics></math></span> and state the restriction on z. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Division requires <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Rearrange to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=5/(1+2i)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Multiply by the conjugate: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=5(1-2i)/(1+4)=1-2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This is nonzero and its reciprocal equals <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+2i)/5</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Why multiply by a denominator conjugate?","o":["To replace a nonzero complex denominator by its positive real modulus squared.","To make both numerator and denominator zero.","To remove every imaginary term from the numerator.","To divide real and imaginary parts separately."],"a":0,"why":"The conjugate product is $a^2+b^2$, enabling ordinary real division of the numerator.","qHtml":"Why multiply by a denominator conjugate?","oHtml":["To replace a nonzero complex denominator by its positive real modulus squared.","To make both numerator and denominator zero.","To remove every imaginary term from the numerator.","To divide real and imaginary parts separately."],"whyHtml":"The conjugate product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^2+b^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, enabling ordinary real division of the numerator."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-complex-argand","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Real and complex numbers","title":"Argand diagrams and complex vector addition","outcomes":["2.3.11","2.3.12","2.3.13"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Plot complex numbers as points · Addition is vector addition · Conjugation and modulus are geometric · Explain the diagram rather than measuring it","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Argand diagrams and complex vector addition</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Plot complex numbers as points</li><li>Addition is vector addition</li><li>Conjugation and modulus are geometric</li><li>Explain the diagram rather than measuring it</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Plot complex numbers as points","text":"An Argand diagram uses real part horizontally and imaginary part vertically. The complex number $a+bi$ corresponds to the point (a,b) and its position vector from the origin. Label axes Re(z) and Im(z), use consistent scales and mark points exactly where possible. The imaginary coordinate is the coefficient b, not the expression bi.","diagram":null,"html":"An Argand diagram uses real part horizontally and imaginary part vertically. The complex number <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a+bi</annotation></semantics></math></span> corresponds to the point (a,b) and its position vector from the origin. Label axes Re(z) and Im(z), use consistent scales and mark points exactly where possible. The imaginary coordinate is the coefficient b, not the expression bi."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Addition is vector addition","text":"Complex addition combines Cartesian components, so the parallelogram or head-to-tail rule describes it. Subtraction z-w is the vector from the point w to the point z. Its modulus is the distance between them. A midpoint is $(z+w)/2$; an internal division can be expressed using weighted sums after checking which endpoint the ratio refers to.","diagram":"argand","html":"Complex addition combines Cartesian components, so the parallelogram or head-to-tail rule describes it. Subtraction z-w is the vector from the point w to the point z. Its modulus is the distance between them. A midpoint is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z+w)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>; an internal division can be expressed using weighted sums after checking which endpoint the ratio refers to."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Conjugation and modulus are geometric","text":"Conjugation reflects in the real axis, while multiplication by -1 sends a point to its opposite through the origin. The modulus $|z|$ is its distance from the origin. The locus $|z-a|=r$ for fixed complex a and positive real r is a circle with centre a and radius r. Translating the diagram into Cartesian coordinates connects complex notation with coordinate geometry.","diagram":null,"html":"Conjugation reflects in the real axis, while multiplication by -1 sends a point to its opposite through the origin. The modulus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z|</annotation></semantics></math></span> is its distance from the origin. The locus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z-a|=r</annotation></semantics></math></span> for fixed complex a and positive real r is a circle with centre a and radius r. Translating the diagram into Cartesian coordinates connects complex notation with coordinate geometry."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the diagram rather than measuring it","text":"A drawing supports reasoning, but exact distances and equalities need component calculations or a theorem. Distinguish points from displacement vectors in language. When plotting several related values, show how one arises from the others. Complex multiplication will later introduce rotation and scaling; addition alone is a translation by a fixed vector when one addend is held constant.","diagram":null,"html":"A drawing supports reasoning, but exact distances and equalities need component calculations or a theorem. Distinguish points from displacement vectors in language. When plotting several related values, show how one arises from the others. Complex multiplication will later introduce rotation and scaling; addition alone is a translation by a fixed vector when one addend is held constant."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-complex-argand-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Let $z=3+i$, $w=-1+2i$. Find z+w and describe the Argand construction. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Add real components: $3-1=2$.","[1] Add imaginary coefficients: $1+2=3$.","[1] Thus $z+w=2+3i$.","[1] The position vectors form adjacent sides of a parallelogram whose diagonal ends at (2,3)."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Let <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=3+i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=-1+2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find z+w and describe the Argand construction. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Add real components: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3-1=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Add imaginary coefficients: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+2=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z+w=2+3i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The position vectors form adjacent sides of a parallelogram whose diagonal ends at (2,3)."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-argand-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find the distance between points representing $z=1+2i$ and $w=4-2i$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Difference is $w-z=3-4i$.","[1] Distance is $|w-z|$.","[1] $|3-4i|=\\sqrt{9+16}=5$.","[1] The displacement from z to w is (3,-4), while its magnitude is 5."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the distance between points representing <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=1+2i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=4-2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Difference is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w-z=3-4i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Distance is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|w-z|</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|3-4i|=\\sqrt{9+16}=5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The displacement from z to w is (3,-4), while its magnitude is 5."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-argand-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find the midpoint of the complex points $-2+3i$ and $4-i$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Midpoint is half their sum.","[1] Sum is $2+2i$.","[1] Midpoint is $1+i$.","[1] Averaging Cartesian coordinates gives (1,1), confirming the result."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the midpoint of the complex points <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2+3i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Midpoint is half their sum.","[1] Sum is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Midpoint is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Averaging Cartesian coordinates gives (1,1), confirming the result."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-argand-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Describe and write the Cartesian equation of $|z-(2-i)|=3$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Write $z=x+yi$ so the displacement is $(x-2)+(y+1)i$.","[1] Modulus condition is $\\sqrt{(x-2)^2+(y+1)^2}=3$.","[1] Squaring gives $(x-2)^2+(y+1)^2=9$.","[1] It is the circle with centre (2,-1) and radius 3."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Describe and write the Cartesian equation of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z-(2-i)|=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=x+yi</annotation></semantics></math></span> so the displacement is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-2)+(y+1)i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Modulus condition is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{(x-2)^2+(y+1)^2}=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Squaring gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-2)^2+(y+1)^2=9</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] It is the circle with centre (2,-1) and radius 3."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-argand-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"If A represents $1+i$ and B represents $7+4i$, find the point P with AP:PB = 1:2 internally. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] P is one-third of the displacement from A to B.","[1] $B-A=6+3i$.","[1] $P=A+(B-A)/3=1+i+2+i=3+2i$.","[1] Displacements are $2+i$ and $4+2i$, giving the required ratio."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"If A represents <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and B represents <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7+4i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find the point P with AP:PB = 1:2 internally. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] P is one-third of the displacement from A to B.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B-A=6+3i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=A+(B-A)/3=1+i+2+i=3+2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Displacements are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4+2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving the required ratio."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-argand-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Plotting is requested for $z=-3+4i$, its conjugate and -z. Give coordinates and identify the two geometric relationships. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] z is at (-3,4).","[1] $\\bar z=-3-4i$ is at (-3,-4), reflection in the real axis.","[1] $-z=3-4i$ is at (3,-4), a half-turn about the origin.","[1] All three points have modulus 5, so lie on the radius-5 circle."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Plotting is requested for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=-3+4i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, its conjugate and -z. Give coordinates and identify the two geometric relationships. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] z is at (-3,4).","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>z</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar z=-3-4i</annotation></semantics></math></span> is at (-3,-4), reflection in the real axis.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-z=3-4i</annotation></semantics></math></span> is at (3,-4), a half-turn about the origin.","[1] All three points have modulus 5, so lie on the radius-5 circle."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What does $|z-w|$ represent on an Argand diagram?","o":["The distance between points z and w.","The sum of their distances from the origin in every case.","The imaginary part of z-w.","The angle between their vectors."],"a":0,"why":"Subtraction gives a displacement and its modulus gives distance.","qHtml":"What does <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z-w|</annotation></semantics></math></span> represent on an Argand diagram?","oHtml":["The distance between points z and w.","The sum of their distances from the origin in every case.","The imaginary part of z-w.","The angle between their vectors."],"whyHtml":"Subtraction gives a displacement and its modulus gives distance."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas11-complex-quadratics","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Trigonometry, matrices and complex numbers","area":"Real and complex numbers","title":"Complex quadratic roots and linear factors","outcomes":["2.3.14","2.3.15","2.3.16"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Use the discriminant over complex numbers · Roots and factors correspond · Compare sums and products · Check complex-coefficient cases separately","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Complex quadratic roots and linear factors</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Use the discriminant over complex numbers</li><li>Roots and factors correspond</li><li>Compare sums and products</li><li>Check complex-coefficient cases separately</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use the discriminant over complex numbers","text":"For a real-coefficient quadratic $az^2+bz+c=0$ with $a\\ne0$, the quadratic formula still applies. A negative discriminant gives two nonreal conjugate roots because $\\sqrt{-d}=i\\sqrt d$ for $d>0$ in the principal-root convention. The plus-or-minus in the formula supplies both roots. A zero discriminant gives a repeated root, counted twice when considering polynomial degree.","diagram":null,"html":"For a real-coefficient quadratic <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">az^2+bz+c=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the quadratic formula still applies. A negative discriminant gives two nonreal conjugate roots because <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{-d}=i\\sqrt d</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> in the principal-root convention. The plus-or-minus in the formula supplies both roots. A zero discriminant gives a repeated root, counted twice when considering polynomial degree."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Roots and factors correspond","text":"If r is a root, z-r is a factor. A quadratic with roots r,s and leading coefficient a is $a(z-r)(z-s)$. The leading coefficient must be retained. For a real polynomial, a nonreal root occurs with its conjugate; the product $(z-(u+vi))(z-(u-vi))=(z-u)^2+v^2$ has real coefficients. This explains the conjugate pairing.","diagram":"conjugate","html":"If r is a root, z-r is a factor. A quadratic with roots r,s and leading coefficient a is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a(z-r)(z-s)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The leading coefficient must be retained. For a real polynomial, a nonreal root occurs with its conjugate; the product <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z-(u+vi))(z-(u-vi))=(z-u)^2+v^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> has real coefficients. This explains the conjugate pairing."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare sums and products","text":"For a monic quadratic $z^2+bz+c$, roots satisfy $r+s=-b$, $rs=c$. These relationships help reconstruct a polynomial and check an answer, but do not replace solving if roots are requested. With nonmonic leading coefficient a, the sums and products become -b/a and c/a. An imaginary root pair can have a positive real product even though each root is nonreal.","diagram":null,"html":"For a monic quadratic <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^2+bz+c</annotation></semantics></math></span>, roots satisfy <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r+s=-b</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">rs=c</annotation></semantics></math></span>. These relationships help reconstruct a polynomial and check an answer, but do not replace solving if roots are requested. With nonmonic leading coefficient a, the sums and products become -b/a and c/a. An imaginary root pair can have a positive real product even though each root is nonreal."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Check complex-coefficient cases separately","text":"When coefficients are nonreal, conjugate pairing is not guaranteed. Direct substitution and factor expansion remain valid. A root of $z^2-2iz-5$ is not found by forcing a real-coefficient theorem onto it. State the number system and express final roots in Cartesian form unless polar form is requested later.","diagram":null,"html":"When coefficients are nonreal, conjugate pairing is not guaranteed. Direct substitution and factor expansion remain valid. A root of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^2-2iz-5</annotation></semantics></math></span> is not found by forcing a real-coefficient theorem onto it. State the number system and express final roots in Cartesian form unless polar form is requested later."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas11-complex-quadratics-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Solve $z^2-4z+13=0$ and write its linear factors. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Discriminant is $16-52=-36$.","[1] $z=(4\\pm6i)/2=2\\pm3i$.","[1] Factors are $(z-2-3i)(z-2+3i)$.","[1] Their product is $(z-2)^2+9=z^2-4z+13$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^2-4z+13=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and write its linear factors. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Discriminant is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>52</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">16-52=-36</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=(4\\pm6i)/2=2\\pm3i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Factors are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z-2-3i)(z-2+3i)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Their product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z-2)^2+9=z^2-4z+13</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-quadratics-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find the monic real quadratic with root $1+2i$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Real coefficients require the conjugate root $1-2i$.","[1] Product is $(z-1-2i)(z-1+2i)$.","[1] This equals $(z-1)^2+4$.","[1] The polynomial is $z^2-2z+5$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the monic real quadratic with root <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Real coefficients require the conjugate root <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z-1-2i)(z-1+2i)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This equals <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z-1)^2+4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The polynomial is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^2-2z+5</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-quadratics-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Solve $2z^2+4z+10=0$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Divide by 2 to obtain $z^2+2z+5=0$.","[1] Discriminant is $4-20=-16$.","[1] Roots are $(-2\\pm4i)/2=-1\\pm2i$.","[1] Sum is -2 and product 5, consistent with the reduced equation."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2z^2+4z+10=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Divide by 2 to obtain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^2+2z+5=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Discriminant is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-20=-16</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Roots are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-2\\pm4i)/2=-1\\pm2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Sum is -2 and product 5, consistent with the reduced equation."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-quadratics-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find a quadratic with leading coefficient 3 and roots $-2\\pm i$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use $3(z+2-i)(z+2+i)$.","[1] The conjugate product is $3[(z+2)^2+1]$.","[1] Expand to $3z^2+12z+15$.","[1] The coefficient 3 is retained; a monic polynomial would not meet the instruction."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find a quadratic with leading coefficient 3 and roots <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2\\pm i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3(z+2-i)(z+2+i)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The conjugate product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3[(z+2)^2+1]</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Expand to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3z^2+12z+15</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The coefficient 3 is retained; a monic polynomial would not meet the instruction."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-quadratics-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Solve $z^2-2iz-5=0$ and explain whether the roots must be conjugates. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Complete the square: $(z-i)^2=4$.","[1] Thus $z-i=\\pm2$.","[1] Roots are $2+i$ and $-2+i$.","[1] They are not conjugates; the coefficient -2i is nonreal, so the real-coefficient pairing theorem does not apply."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^2-2iz-5=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and explain whether the roots must be conjugates. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Complete the square: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z-i)^2=4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z-i=\\pm2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Roots are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2+i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] They are not conjugates; the coefficient -2i is nonreal, so the real-coefficient pairing theorem does not apply."]},{"id":"mas11-complex-quadratics-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"An equation has roots $3\\pm4i$. Find their sum, product and the monic quadratic equation. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Sum is $6$.","[1] Product is $3^2+4^2=25$.","[1] The monic equation is $z^2-6z+25=0$.","[1] Its discriminant is $36-100=-64$, giving the stated roots."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"An equation has roots <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\pm4i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find their sum, product and the monic quadratic equation. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Sum is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3^2+4^2=25</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The monic equation is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^2-6z+25=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its discriminant is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>36</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">36-100=-64</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving the stated roots."]}],"bank":[{"q":"When must a nonreal root occur with its conjugate?","o":["When the polynomial has real coefficients.","For every polynomial with any complex coefficients.","Only when the polynomial is monic.","Only when the root has modulus 1."],"a":0,"why":"Real coefficients make conjugation preserve the polynomial equation.","qHtml":"When must a nonreal root occur with its conjugate?","oHtml":["When the polynomial has real coefficients.","For every polynomial with any complex coefficients.","Only when the polynomial is monic.","Only when the root has modulus 1."],"whyHtml":"Real coefficients make conjugation preserve the polynomial equation."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-complex-review","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Complex numbers","title":"Cartesian complex numbers and geometric interpretation","outcomes":["3.1.1","3.1.2","3.1.3","3.1.8"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Re-establish Cartesian fluency · Distances and perpendicularity · Reconstruct shapes from algebra · Use reasoning across representations","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Cartesian complex numbers and geometric interpretation</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Re-establish Cartesian fluency</li><li>Distances and perpendicularity</li><li>Reconstruct shapes from algebra</li><li>Use reasoning across representations</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Re-establish Cartesian fluency","text":"Use $z=x+yi$ with real x,y. Equality compares both components; multiplication expands distributively using $i^2=-1$. Division by nonzero w is performed with its conjugate. Keep exact Cartesian values when interpreting geometric constraints. Year 12 questions often combine algebra, a diagram and a parameter condition rather than asking only a routine calculation.","diagram":null,"html":"Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=x+yi</annotation></semantics></math></span> with real x,y. Equality compares both components; multiplication expands distributively using <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i^2=-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Division by nonzero w is performed with its conjugate. Keep exact Cartesian values when interpreting geometric constraints. Year 12 questions often combine algebra, a diagram and a parameter condition rather than asking only a routine calculation."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distances and perpendicularity","text":"The vector between complex points a,b is b-a and its length is $|b-a|$. For displacements $u=u_1+u_2i$, $v=v_1+v_2i$, their real-plane dot product is $u_1v_1+u_2v_2=\\operatorname{Re}(u\\bar v)$. A zero dot product establishes perpendicularity for nonzero displacements. Distinguish this real dot product from the complex product uv.","diagram":"argand","html":"The vector between complex points a,b is b-a and its length is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|b-a|</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For displacements <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=u_1+u_2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=v_1+v_2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, their real-plane dot product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_1v_1+u_2v_2=\\operatorname{Re}(u\\bar v)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A zero dot product establishes perpendicularity for nonzero displacements. Distinguish this real dot product from the complex product uv."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconstruct shapes from algebra","text":"Midpoints and division points come from vector combinations. For consecutive parallelogram vertices a,b,c,d, $a+c=b+d$. Equal moduli express equal distances from the origin, whereas $|z-a|=|z-b|$ expresses the perpendicular bisector of AB. Translate constraints into x,y before simplifying. Check whether a requested shape is nondegenerate.","diagram":null,"html":"Midpoints and division points come from vector combinations. For consecutive parallelogram vertices a,b,c,d, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a+c=b+d</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Equal moduli express equal distances from the origin, whereas <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z-a|=|z-b|</annotation></semantics></math></span> expresses the perpendicular bisector of AB. Translate constraints into x,y before simplifying. Check whether a requested shape is nondegenerate."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use reasoning across representations","text":"A labelled Argand diagram should display the relevant points and displacement vectors. It can suggest a relationship but cannot establish an exact angle solely by appearance. Explain which algebraic statement proves each geometric conclusion. Calculator-assisted work still needs setup, restrictions and the interpretation of the result. A zero displacement cannot define a direction or a unique line.","diagram":null,"html":"A labelled Argand diagram should display the relevant points and displacement vectors. It can suggest a relationship but cannot establish an exact angle solely by appearance. Explain which algebraic statement proves each geometric conclusion. Calculator-assisted work still needs setup, restrictions and the interpretation of the result. A zero displacement cannot define a direction or a unique line."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-complex-review-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"For $a=1+i$, $b=4+2i$, $c=3+5i$, show angle ABC is right. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Vectors from B are $a-b=-3-i$, $c-b=-1+3i$.","[1] Their real-plane dot product is $(-3)(-1)+(-1)(3)=0$.","[1] Both vectors are nonzero.","[1] Thus the angle between BA and BC is $90^\\circ$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=1+i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=4+2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=3+5i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, show angle ABC is right. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Vectors from B are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a-b=-3-i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c-b=-1+3i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Their real-plane dot product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-3)(-1)+(-1)(3)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both vectors are nonzero.","[1] Thus the angle between BA and BC is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-review-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"A,B,C are consecutive vertices $1+i$, $4+2i$, $3+5i$. Find the fourth parallelogram vertex. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Opposite vertices satisfy $a+c=b+d$.","[1] Thus $d=a+c-b$.","[1] $d=(1+3-4)+(1+5-2)i=4i$.","[1] Opposite side displacements match, confirming the parallelogram."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A,B,C are consecutive vertices <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4+2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3+5i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find the fourth parallelogram vertex. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Opposite vertices satisfy <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a+c=b+d</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=a+c-b</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=(1+3-4)+(1+5-2)i=4i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Opposite side displacements match, confirming the parallelogram."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-review-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Simplify $(2-i)/(1+2i)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Multiply numerator and denominator by $1-2i$.","[1] Numerator is $2-5i+2i^2=-5i$.","[1] Denominator is 5.","[1] Result is $-i$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Simplify <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2-i)/(1+2i)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Multiply numerator and denominator by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Numerator is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2-5i+2i^2=-5i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Denominator is 5.","[1] Result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-review-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find the locus $|z-1|=|z+3|$ in Cartesian form. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Set $z=x+yi$ and square both nonnegative distances.","[1] $(x-1)^2+y^2=(x+3)^2+y^2$.","[1] Simplification gives $-2x+1=6x+9$, so $x=-1$.","[1] It is the perpendicular bisector of the real-axis points 1 and -3."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the locus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z-1|=|z+3|</annotation></semantics></math></span> in Cartesian form. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Set <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=x+yi</annotation></semantics></math></span> and square both nonnegative distances.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-1)^2+y^2=(x+3)^2+y^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Simplification gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2x+1=6x+9</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] It is the perpendicular bisector of the real-axis points 1 and -3."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-review-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find real k for which vectors represented by $2+ki$ and $3-2i$ are perpendicular. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The vectors are (2,k) and (3,-2).","[1] Their dot product is $6-2k$.","[1] Set it to zero, giving $k=3$.","[1] Both vectors are nonzero; perpendicularity is well-defined."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find real k for which vectors represented by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+ki</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3-2i</annotation></semantics></math></span> are perpendicular. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The vectors are (2,k) and (3,-2).","[1] Their dot product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6-2k</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Set it to zero, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both vectors are nonzero; perpendicularity is well-defined."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-review-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Find the midpoint and distance between $-1+4i$ and $5-4i$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Midpoint is $[(-1+4i)+(5-4i)]/2=2$.","[1] Displacement is $6-8i$.","[1] Its modulus is $\\sqrt{36+64}=10$.","[1] Midpoint is (2,0) and distance is 10 units."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the midpoint and distance between <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1+4i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5-4i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Midpoint is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[(-1+4i)+(5-4i)]/2=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Displacement is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6-8i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its modulus is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>36</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{36+64}=10</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Midpoint is (2,0) and distance is 10 units."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which expression gives the real-plane dot product of complex vectors u,v?","o":["$\\operatorname{Re}(u\\bar v)$.","$uv$ in all cases.","$|u|+|v|$.","$\\operatorname{Im}(u+v)$."],"a":0,"why":"Expanding u times the conjugate of v yields the Cartesian dot product in its real part.","qHtml":"Which expression gives the real-plane dot product of complex vectors u,v?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Re}(u\\bar v)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">uv</annotation></semantics></math></span> in all cases.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|u|+|v|</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Im}(u+v)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."],"whyHtml":"Expanding u times the conjugate of v yields the Cartesian dot product in its real part."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-complex-polar","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Complex numbers","title":"Modulus, principal argument and polar conversion","outcomes":["3.1.4","3.1.5"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Modulus and argument · Choose the correct quadrant · Convert in both directions · Understand equivalent representations · Proofs of modulus and argument identities","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Modulus, principal argument and polar conversion</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Modulus and argument</li><li>Choose the correct quadrant</li><li>Convert in both directions</li><li>Understand equivalent representations</li><li>Proofs of modulus and argument identities</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Modulus and argument","text":"For nonzero $z=x+yi$, polar form is $r(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)$ with $r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}>0$. The angle is measured anticlockwise from the positive real axis. All arguments differ by integer multiples of $2\\pi$. Under the SCSA convention, the principal argument satisfies $-\\pi<\\operatorname{Arg}z\\le\\pi$. The argument of zero is undefined.","diagram":null,"html":"For nonzero <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=x+yi</annotation></semantics></math></span>, polar form is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The angle is measured anticlockwise from the positive real axis. All arguments differ by integer multiples of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Under the SCSA convention, the principal argument satisfies <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\pi&lt;\\operatorname{Arg}z\\le\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The argument of zero is undefined."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Choose the correct quadrant","text":"The equation $\\tan\\theta=y/x$ alone does not distinguish opposite quadrants. Use signs of x,y and a reference angle, or an atan2 calculation followed by the required interval adjustment. Points on an axis are handled directly. In particular, a negative real number has principal argument $\\pi$, not $-\\pi$ under this convention.","diagram":"argand","html":"The equation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tan\\theta=y/x</annotation></semantics></math></span> alone does not distinguish opposite quadrants. Use signs of x,y and a reference angle, or an atan2 calculation followed by the required interval adjustment. Points on an axis are handled directly. In particular, a negative real number has principal argument <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>, not <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> under this convention."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Convert in both directions","text":"From polar form, $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Exact familiar angles give surds and fractions. From Cartesian form, calculate r and identify the angle. A negative radial coefficient is not a standard modulus; change its sign and add $\\pi$ to the angle, then normalise. State whether an angle is exact or rounded and use radians unless degrees are explicitly requested.","diagram":null,"html":"From polar form, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=r\\cos\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=r\\sin\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Exact familiar angles give surds and fractions. From Cartesian form, calculate r and identify the angle. A negative radial coefficient is not a standard modulus; change its sign and add <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> to the angle, then normalise. State whether an angle is exact or rounded and use radians unless degrees are explicitly requested."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Understand equivalent representations","text":"Polar notation $r\\operatorname{cis}\\theta$ abbreviates $r(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)$. Adding $2\\pi$ does not change the complex point, but a principal-argument answer must lie in the specified interval. Avoid rounding before later powers or roots. A clear Argand diagram connects the Cartesian coordinates, radius and directed angle.","diagram":null,"html":"Polar notation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r\\operatorname{cis}\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> abbreviates <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Adding <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> does not change the complex point, but a principal-argument answer must lie in the specified interval. Avoid rounding before later powers or roots. A clear Argand diagram connects the Cartesian coordinates, radius and directed angle."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Proofs of modulus and argument identities","text":"For $z=a+bi$ and $w=c+di$, expansion gives $|zw|^2=(ac-bd)^2+(ad+bc)^2=(a^2+b^2)(c^2+d^2)$. Taking nonnegative square roots proves $|zw|=|z||w|$; nonzero w then gives $|z/w|=|z|/|w|$. For the triangle inequality, $|z+w|^2=|z|^2+|w|^2+2\\operatorname{Re}(z\\bar w)\\le(|z|+|w|)^2$, since the real-plane dot product is at most the product of magnitudes. For nonzero factors, arguments add under multiplication modulo $2\\pi$ by the compound-angle identities. A principal argument requires reducing the sum to the specified interval; unqualified equality of principal arguments can fail at its boundary.","diagram":null,"html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=a+bi</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=c+di</annotation></semantics></math></span>, expansion gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>w</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|zw|^2=(ac-bd)^2+(ad+bc)^2=(a^2+b^2)(c^2+d^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Taking nonnegative square roots proves <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|zw|=|z||w|</annotation></semantics></math></span>; nonzero w then gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z/w|=|z|/|w|</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For the triangle inequality, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>w</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z+w|^2=|z|^2+|w|^2+2\\operatorname{Re}(z\\bar w)\\le(|z|+|w|)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, since the real-plane dot product is at most the product of magnitudes. For nonzero factors, arguments add under multiplication modulo <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> by the compound-angle identities. A principal argument requires reducing the sum to the specified interval; unqualified equality of principal arguments can fail at its boundary."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-complex-polar-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Write $-\\sqrt3+i$ in polar form using the principal argument. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Modulus is $\\sqrt{3+1}=2$.","[1] The point is in quadrant II, reference angle $\\pi/6$.","[1] Principal argument is $5\\pi/6$.","[1] $z=2\\operatorname{cis}(5\\pi/6)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\sqrt3+i</annotation></semantics></math></span> in polar form using the principal argument. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Modulus is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{3+1}=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The point is in quadrant II, reference angle <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Principal argument is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\pi/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=2\\operatorname{cis}(5\\pi/6)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-polar-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Convert $4\\operatorname{cis}(-\\pi/3)$ to Cartesian form. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $x=4\\cos(-\\pi/3)=2$.","[1] $y=4\\sin(-\\pi/3)=-2\\sqrt3$.","[1] Thus $z=2-2\\sqrt3i$.","[1] Its quadrant IV position agrees with the negative argument."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Convert <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\operatorname{cis}(-\\pi/3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> to Cartesian form. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=4\\cos(-\\pi/3)=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=4\\sin(-\\pi/3)=-2\\sqrt3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=2-2\\sqrt3i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its quadrant IV position agrees with the negative argument."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-polar-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find modulus and principal argument of $-3-3i$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Modulus is $\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt2$.","[1] The point is in quadrant III with reference angle $\\pi/4$.","[1] An argument is $5\\pi/4$; subtract $2\\pi$ to normalise.","[1] Principal argument is $-3\\pi/4$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find modulus and principal argument of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3-3i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Modulus is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>18</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The point is in quadrant III with reference angle <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] An argument is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>; subtract <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> to normalise.","[1] Principal argument is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-polar-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"State principal arguments of 5, -5, 5i and -5i. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Positive real 5 has argument 0.","[1] Negative real -5 has principal argument $\\pi$.","[1] Positive imaginary 5i has argument $\\pi/2$.","[1] Negative imaginary -5i has argument $-\\pi/2$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"State principal arguments of 5, -5, 5i and -5i. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Positive real 5 has argument 0.","[1] Negative real -5 has principal argument <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Positive imaginary 5i has argument <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Negative imaginary -5i has argument <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-polar-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Rewrite $-2\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/4)$ with positive modulus and principal argument. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Replace coefficient -2 by modulus 2 and add $\\pi$.","[1] Angle becomes $5\\pi/4$.","[1] Subtract $2\\pi$ to obtain $-3\\pi/4$.","[1] Equivalent standard form is $2\\operatorname{cis}(-3\\pi/4)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Rewrite <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/4)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with positive modulus and principal argument. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Replace coefficient -2 by modulus 2 and add <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Angle becomes <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Subtract <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> to obtain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Equivalent standard form is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\operatorname{cis}(-3\\pi/4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-polar-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A calculator gives $\\tan^{-1}(y/x)=\\pi/4$ for $z=-2-2i$. Explain and correct the argument. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The ratio y/x is positive in both quadrants I and III.","[1] The actual coordinates are both negative, placing z in quadrant III.","[1] A corresponding argument is $5\\pi/4$.","[1] The principal argument is $-3\\pi/4$, not the returned reference angle."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A calculator gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tan^{-1}(y/x)=\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=-2-2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Explain and correct the argument. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The ratio y/x is positive in both quadrants I and III.","[1] The actual coordinates are both negative, placing z in quadrant III.","[1] A corresponding argument is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The principal argument is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>, not the returned reference angle."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What is the principal argument of a negative real number under the course convention?","o":["$\\pi$.","$-\\pi$.","0.","Undefined for every real number."],"a":0,"why":"The interval includes positive pi and excludes negative pi; zero alone has undefined argument.","qHtml":"What is the principal argument of a negative real number under the course convention?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","0.","Undefined for every real number."],"whyHtml":"The interval includes positive pi and excludes negative pi; zero alone has undefined argument."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-complex-products","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Complex numbers","title":"Polar products, quotients and transformations","outcomes":["3.1.6","3.1.9"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Multiply radii and add angles · Divide radii and subtract angles · Complex affine transformations · Relate product to geometry","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Polar products, quotients and transformations</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Multiply radii and add angles</li><li>Divide radii and subtract angles</li><li>Complex affine transformations</li><li>Relate product to geometry</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Multiply radii and add angles","text":"If $z=r\\operatorname{cis}\\alpha$, $w=s\\operatorname{cis}\\beta$, then $zw=rs\\operatorname{cis}(\\alpha+\\beta)$. Expansion and compound-angle identities establish this rule. Normalize only when a principal argument is required. Polar multiplication is often simpler than repeated Cartesian expansion, particularly when moduli and angles are exact.","diagram":null,"html":"If <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=r\\operatorname{cis}\\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=s\\operatorname{cis}\\beta</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">zw=rs\\operatorname{cis}(\\alpha+\\beta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Expansion and compound-angle identities establish this rule. Normalize only when a principal argument is required. Polar multiplication is often simpler than repeated Cartesian expansion, particularly when moduli and angles are exact."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Divide radii and subtract angles","text":"For $w\\ne0$, $z/w=(r/s)\\operatorname{cis}(\\alpha-\\beta)$. A reciprocal has modulus 1/s and opposite argument. Multiplication by a fixed nonzero complex number w is a dilation by |w| and rotation by an argument of w about the origin. Modulus 1 gives pure rotation; a positive real multiplier gives pure dilation.","diagram":"argand","html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z/w=(r/s)\\operatorname{cis}(\\alpha-\\beta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A reciprocal has modulus 1/s and opposite argument. Multiplication by a fixed nonzero complex number w is a dilation by |w| and rotation by an argument of w about the origin. Modulus 1 gives pure rotation; a positive real multiplier gives pure dilation."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Complex affine transformations","text":"The rule $z^{\\prime}=az+b$ with $a\\ne0$ applies rotation and scaling from multiplication by a, then translation by b. The order is determined by the formula. If a is zero, all points collapse to b. When interpreting a centre of rotation, a form such as $z^{\\prime}=a(z-c)+c$ makes the fixed centre c visible.","diagram":null,"html":"The rule <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^{\\prime}=az+b</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span> applies rotation and scaling from multiplication by a, then translation by b. The order is determined by the formula. If a is zero, all points collapse to b. When interpreting a centre of rotation, a form such as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^{\\prime}=a(z-c)+c</annotation></semantics></math></span> makes the fixed centre c visible."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Relate product to geometry","text":"For nonzero displacement vectors u,v, the quotient v/u has modulus equal to their length ratio and argument equal to their directed angle difference. A positive real quotient means the same direction; a negative real quotient means opposite directions; a purely imaginary nonzero quotient means perpendicular vectors. Do not use these direction statements when a displacement is zero.","diagram":null,"html":"For nonzero displacement vectors u,v, the quotient v/u has modulus equal to their length ratio and argument equal to their directed angle difference. A positive real quotient means the same direction; a negative real quotient means opposite directions; a purely imaginary nonzero quotient means perpendicular vectors. Do not use these direction statements when a displacement is zero."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-complex-products-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find $[2\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/3)][3\\operatorname{cis}(5\\pi/6)]$ with principal argument. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Multiply moduli to obtain 6.","[1] Add angles to obtain $7\\pi/6$.","[1] Subtract $2\\pi$ to normalise to $-5\\pi/6$.","[1] Product is $6\\operatorname{cis}(-5\\pi/6)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[2\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/3)][3\\operatorname{cis}(5\\pi/6)]</annotation></semantics></math></span> with principal argument. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Multiply moduli to obtain 6.","[1] Add angles to obtain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7\\pi/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Subtract <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> to normalise to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-5\\pi/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\operatorname{cis}(-5\\pi/6)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-products-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Describe $z^{\\prime}=(1+i)z+2$ as a sequence of transformations. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $1+i=\\sqrt2\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/4)$.","[1] Multiplication rotates anticlockwise $\\pi/4$ about the origin and scales lengths by $\\sqrt2$.","[1] Adding 2 translates by (2,0).","[1] The translation occurs after the multiplication; area is scaled by 2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Describe <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^{\\prime}=(1+i)z+2</annotation></semantics></math></span> as a sequence of transformations. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+i=\\sqrt2\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Multiplication rotates anticlockwise <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span> about the origin and scales lengths by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Adding 2 translates by (2,0).","[1] The translation occurs after the multiplication; area is scaled by 2."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-products-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find $(8\\operatorname{cis}(3\\pi/4))/(2\\operatorname{cis}(-\\pi/4))$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Divide moduli: $8/2=4$.","[1] Subtract angles: $3\\pi/4-(-\\pi/4)=\\pi$.","[1] Quotient is $4\\operatorname{cis}\\pi$.","[1] Cartesian result is -4."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(8\\operatorname{cis}(3\\pi/4))/(2\\operatorname{cis}(-\\pi/4))</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Divide moduli: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8/2=4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Subtract angles: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\pi/4-(-\\pi/4)=\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Quotient is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\operatorname{cis}\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Cartesian result is -4."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-products-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find the Cartesian image of $z=2-i$ under multiplication by i. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $i(2-i)=2i-i^2$.","[1] Since $i^2=-1$, image is $1+2i$.","[1] Multiplication by i is anticlockwise $90^\\circ$ rotation.","[1] Modulus is unchanged at $\\sqrt5$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the Cartesian image of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=2-i</annotation></semantics></math></span> under multiplication by i. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i(2-i)=2i-i^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Since <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i^2=-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, image is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Multiplication by i is anticlockwise <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> rotation.","[1] Modulus is unchanged at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt5</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-products-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"If nonzero complex displacements u,v satisfy $v/u=-2i$, compare their lengths and directions. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Quotient modulus is 2.","[1] Therefore $|v|=2|u|$.","[1] Quotient principal argument is $-\\pi/2$.","[1] v points $90^\\circ$ clockwise from u, hence the vectors are perpendicular."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"If nonzero complex displacements u,v satisfy <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v/u=-2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, compare their lengths and directions. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Quotient modulus is 2.","[1] Therefore <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|v|=2|u|</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Quotient principal argument is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] v points <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> clockwise from u, hence the vectors are perpendicular."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-products-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Find the inverse of $z^{\\prime}=2iz+3-i$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Subtract the translation: $z^{\\prime}-(3-i)=2iz$.","[1] Divide by nonzero 2i.","[1] $z=[z^{\\prime}-(3-i)]/(2i)=-(i/2)[z^{\\prime}-(3-i)]$.","[1] Undo translation first, then rotate clockwise $90^\\circ$ and scale by $1/2$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the inverse of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^{\\prime}=2iz+3-i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Subtract the translation: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^{\\prime}-(3-i)=2iz</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Divide by nonzero 2i.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=[z^{\\prime}-(3-i)]/(2i)=-(i/2)[z^{\\prime}-(3-i)]</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Undo translation first, then rotate clockwise <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> and scale by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What geometric action is multiplication by $2\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/6)$?","o":["Dilation factor 2 and anticlockwise rotation pi/6 about the origin.","Translation by (2,pi/6).","Reflection in the real axis only.","Dilation factor pi/6 and rotation 2 radians."],"a":0,"why":"The modulus supplies length scale and the argument supplies directed rotation.","qHtml":"What geometric action is multiplication by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/6)</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","oHtml":["Dilation factor 2 and anticlockwise rotation pi/6 about the origin.","Translation by (2,pi/6).","Reflection in the real axis only.","Dilation factor pi/6 and rotation 2 radians."],"whyHtml":"The modulus supplies length scale and the argument supplies directed rotation."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-complex-demoivre","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Complex numbers","title":"De Moivre theorem, powers and proof","outcomes":["3.1.7"],"icon":"📐","scope":"De Moivre links powers to angles · Induction proves the theorem · Recover trigonometric identities · Keep principal arguments separate","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>De Moivre theorem, powers and proof</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>De Moivre links powers to angles</li><li>Induction proves the theorem</li><li>Recover trigonometric identities</li><li>Keep principal arguments separate</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"De Moivre links powers to angles","text":"For an integer n, $(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)^n=\\cos(n\\theta)+i\\sin(n\\theta)$. For $z=r\\operatorname{cis}\\theta$, $z^n=r^n\\operatorname{cis}(n\\theta)$. The usual positive-integer proof follows from polar multiplication. Zero powers require a nonzero base; negative powers use reciprocals and also require a nonzero base.","diagram":null,"html":"For an integer n, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)^n=\\cos(n\\theta)+i\\sin(n\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=r\\operatorname{cis}\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^n=r^n\\operatorname{cis}(n\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The usual positive-integer proof follows from polar multiplication. Zero powers require a nonzero base; negative powers use reciprocals and also require a nonzero base."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Induction proves the theorem","text":"For n=1 the formula is immediate. Assume it holds for k, then multiply the k expression by $\\operatorname{cis}\\theta$. The product rule produces $\\operatorname{cis}((k+1)\\theta)$. A complete proof names the base, assumption, angle-addition step and conclusion. Simply citing the theorem is not a proof when asked to establish it.","diagram":"complex-roots","html":"For n=1 the formula is immediate. Assume it holds for k, then multiply the k expression by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{cis}\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The product rule produces <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{cis}((k+1)\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A complete proof names the base, assumption, angle-addition step and conclusion. Simply citing the theorem is not a proof when asked to establish it."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recover trigonometric identities","text":"Expanding $(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)^n$ and comparing real and imaginary parts gives multiple-angle identities. For n=3, the real part is $\\cos^3\\theta-3\\cos\\theta\\sin^2\\theta$, giving $\\cos3\\theta=4\\cos^3\\theta-3\\cos\\theta$. Show the conversion using $\\sin^2\\theta=1-\\cos^2\\theta$ rather than skipping to the memorised answer.","diagram":null,"html":"Expanding <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)^n</annotation></semantics></math></span> and comparing real and imaginary parts gives multiple-angle identities. For n=3, the real part is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos^3\\theta-3\\cos\\theta\\sin^2\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos3\\theta=4\\cos^3\\theta-3\\cos\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Show the conversion using <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2\\theta=1-\\cos^2\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> rather than skipping to the memorised answer."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Keep principal arguments separate","text":"Multiplying an argument by n gives a valid argument of the power, but it may lie outside the principal interval. Reduce by full turns when requested. A complex power can be real or purely imaginary even when the original is neither. For a noninteger exponent, the same simple statement requires additional choices of branches and is not the integer theorem taught here.","diagram":null,"html":"Multiplying an argument by n gives a valid argument of the power, but it may lie outside the principal interval. Reduce by full turns when requested. A complex power can be real or purely imaginary even when the original is neither. For a noninteger exponent, the same simple statement requires additional choices of branches and is not the integer theorem taught here."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-complex-demoivre-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find $(1+i)^6$ exactly. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $1+i=\\sqrt2\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/4)$.","[1] Sixth power is $(\\sqrt2)^6\\operatorname{cis}(6\\pi/4)=8\\operatorname{cis}(3\\pi/2)$.","[1] Cosine is 0 and sine is -1.","[1] Therefore the result is $-8i$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+i)^6</annotation></semantics></math></span> exactly. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+i=\\sqrt2\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Sixth power is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>6</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\sqrt2)^6\\operatorname{cis}(6\\pi/4)=8\\operatorname{cis}(3\\pi/2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Cosine is 0 and sine is -1.","[1] Therefore the result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-8i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-demoivre-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Prove De Moivre for all positive integers using the polar product rule. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] For n=1, both sides equal $\\operatorname{cis}\\theta$.","[1] Assume $(\\operatorname{cis}\\theta)^k=\\operatorname{cis}(k\\theta)$.","[1] Multiply by $\\operatorname{cis}\\theta$ to obtain $\\operatorname{cis}((k+1)\\theta)$.","[1] Induction proves the formula for all positive integers."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove De Moivre for all positive integers using the polar product rule. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] For n=1, both sides equal <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{cis}\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Assume <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\operatorname{cis}\\theta)^k=\\operatorname{cis}(k\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Multiply by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{cis}\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> to obtain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{cis}((k+1)\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Induction proves the formula for all positive integers."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-demoivre-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find $(\\sqrt3+i)^3$ in Cartesian form. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Base has modulus 2 and argument $\\pi/6$.","[1] Cubing gives $8\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/2)$.","[1] $\\cos(\\pi/2)=0$, $\\sin(\\pi/2)=1$.","[1] Result is $8i$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\sqrt3+i)^3</annotation></semantics></math></span> in Cartesian form. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Base has modulus 2 and argument <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Cubing gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(\\pi/2)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(\\pi/2)=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-demoivre-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find $[2\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/3)]^{-2}$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Base is nonzero, so negative powers are permitted.","[1] Modulus is $2^{-2}=1/4$.","[1] Angle is $-2\\pi/3$.","[1] Cartesian result is $-1/8-(\\sqrt3/8)i$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[2\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/3)]^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Base is nonzero, so negative powers are permitted.","[1] Modulus is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{-2}=1/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Angle is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Cartesian result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1/8-(\\sqrt3/8)i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-demoivre-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Derive $\\sin3\\theta=3\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^3\\theta$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Expand $(c+is)^3$ with $c=\\cos\\theta$, $s=\\sin\\theta$.","[1] Its imaginary coefficient is $3c^2s-s^3$.","[1] De Moivre makes this coefficient $\\sin3\\theta$.","[1] Substitute $c^2=1-s^2$ to obtain $3s-4s^3$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Derive <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin3\\theta=3\\sin\\theta-4\\sin^3\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Expand <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(c+is)^3</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=\\cos\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=\\sin\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its imaginary coefficient is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3c^2s-s^3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] De Moivre makes this coefficient <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin3\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Substitute <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c^2=1-s^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> to obtain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3s-4s^3</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-demoivre-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"For $z=3\\operatorname{cis}(4\\pi/5)$, find $z^4$ with principal argument. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Modulus is $3^4=81$.","[1] Argument is $16\\pi/5$.","[1] Subtract $4\\pi$ to get $-4\\pi/5$ in the principal interval.","[1] $z^4=81\\operatorname{cis}(-4\\pi/5)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=3\\operatorname{cis}(4\\pi/5)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^4</annotation></semantics></math></span> with principal argument. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Modulus is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>81</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3^4=81</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Argument is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">16\\pi/5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Subtract <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> to get <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4\\pi/5</annotation></semantics></math></span> in the principal interval.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>81</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^4=81\\operatorname{cis}(-4\\pi/5)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What happens to a modulus r when raising a complex number to the positive integer power n?","o":["It becomes $r^n$.","It becomes nr.","It stays r for all n.","It becomes r+n."],"a":0,"why":"Repeated multiplication multiplies the moduli, while arguments add.","qHtml":"What happens to a modulus r when raising a complex number to the positive integer power n?","oHtml":["It becomes <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^n</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","It becomes nr.","It stays r for all n.","It becomes r+n."],"whyHtml":"Repeated multiplication multiplies the moduli, while arguments add."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-complex-loci","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Complex numbers","title":"Complex loci, intersections and argument restrictions","outcomes":["3.1.10"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Modulus equations describe distances · Argument conditions describe directions · Combine conditions by intersection · Build and interpret exact geometry · An angular-sector example","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Complex loci, intersections and argument restrictions</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Modulus equations describe distances</li><li>Argument conditions describe directions</li><li>Combine conditions by intersection</li><li>Build and interpret exact geometry</li><li>An angular-sector example</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Modulus equations describe distances","text":"The locus $|z-a|=r$ is a circle centred at a with radius r when r>0. A less-than inequality selects the interior and a greater-than inequality the exterior; strict inequalities exclude the boundary. The equation $|z-a|=|z-b|$ for distinct a,b is their perpendicular bisector. Weighted distance conditions can instead produce a circle, so derive the Cartesian equation rather than assuming a bisector.","diagram":null,"html":"The locus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z-a|=r</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a circle centred at a with radius r when r&gt;0. A less-than inequality selects the interior and a greater-than inequality the exterior; strict inequalities exclude the boundary. The equation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z-a|=|z-b|</annotation></semantics></math></span> for distinct a,b is their perpendicular bisector. Weighted distance conditions can instead produce a circle, so derive the Cartesian equation rather than assuming a bisector."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Argument conditions describe directions","text":"For fixed nonzero displacement, $\\operatorname{Arg}(z-a)=\\theta$ describes a ray from a in direction theta, excluding a because its displacement is zero. A full line generally contains two opposite directions and does not satisfy one fixed principal argument. Argument inequalities describe angular regions subject to the principal interval; check boundary rays and any undefined origin separately.","diagram":"locus","html":"For fixed nonzero displacement, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Arg}(z-a)=\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> describes a ray from a in direction theta, excluding a because its displacement is zero. A full line generally contains two opposite directions and does not satisfy one fixed principal argument. Argument inequalities describe angular regions subject to the principal interval; check boundary rays and any undefined origin separately."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Combine conditions by intersection","text":"A point satisfying two loci lies in their intersection. Translate modulus equations to x,y and solve simultaneously, then check argument restrictions to discard algebraic candidates on the wrong ray. Squaring positive distance equalities is valid, but an argument equation cannot be reduced to tangent alone without quadrant information. Sketch both loci and mark eligible intersections.","diagram":null,"html":"A point satisfying two loci lies in their intersection. Translate modulus equations to x,y and solve simultaneously, then check argument restrictions to discard algebraic candidates on the wrong ray. Squaring positive distance equalities is valid, but an argument equation cannot be reduced to tangent alone without quadrant information. Sketch both loci and mark eligible intersections."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Build and interpret exact geometry","text":"State centres, radii, boundary inclusion and exclusions. An equation alone can omit the information needed to identify a ray or sector. When expressing answers as complex numbers, convert solved coordinate pairs back to x+yi. Graphing technology helps locate intersections, while exact substitution and angle checks justify which points belong.","diagram":null,"html":"State centres, radii, boundary inclusion and exclusions. An equation alone can omit the information needed to identify a ray or sector. When expressing answers as complex numbers, convert solved coordinate pairs back to x+yi. Graphing technology helps locate intersections, while exact substitution and angle checks justify which points belong."},{"kind":"learn","title":"An angular-sector example","text":"The condition $\\pi/4\\le\\operatorname{Arg}z\\le3\\pi/4$ selects the closed boundary rays at $45^\\circ$ and $135^\\circ$ and the region between them above the origin. Cartesian form is $y\\ge|x|$ together with $z\\ne0$; the origin must still be removed because argument is undefined there. Intersecting this condition with $|z-3i|\\le4$ retains only the part of the closed radius-4 disk centred at (0,3) inside that sector. Label both boundary rays, the circular boundary and the excluded origin. This example shows why squaring a modulus and using tangent alone is insufficient to encode an argument restriction.","diagram":null,"html":"The condition <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/4\\le\\operatorname{Arg}z\\le3\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span> selects the closed boundary rays at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>45</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">45^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>135</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">135^\\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the region between them above the origin. Cartesian form is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\ge|x|</annotation></semantics></math></span> together with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the origin must still be removed because argument is undefined there. Intersecting this condition with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z-3i|\\le4</annotation></semantics></math></span> retains only the part of the closed radius-4 disk centred at (0,3) inside that sector. Label both boundary rays, the circular boundary and the excluded origin. This example shows why squaring a modulus and using tangent alone is insufficient to encode an argument restriction."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-complex-loci-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find the intersections of $|z|=5$ and $\\operatorname{Re}z=3$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Set $z=x+yi$, giving $x^2+y^2=25$ and $x=3$.","[1] Then $y^2=16$, so $y=\\pm4$.","[1] Candidates are $3+4i$ and $3-4i$.","[1] Both satisfy the circle and vertical-line conditions."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the intersections of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z|=5</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Re}z=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Set <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=x+yi</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2=25</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Then <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^2=16</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=\\pm4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Candidates are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3+4i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3-4i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both satisfy the circle and vertical-line conditions."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-loci-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find the point satisfying $|z|=4$ and $\\operatorname{Arg}z=\\pi/3$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The argument selects the ray at $\\pi/3$, excluding zero.","[1] Radius 4 gives $z=4\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/3)$.","[1] Cartesian coordinates are $(2,2\\sqrt3)$.","[1] Thus $z=2+2\\sqrt3i$; the opposite point has the wrong argument."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the point satisfying <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z|=4</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Arg}z=\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The argument selects the ray at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, excluding zero.","[1] Radius 4 gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=4\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Cartesian coordinates are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,2\\sqrt3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=2+2\\sqrt3i</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the opposite point has the wrong argument."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-loci-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Describe $|z-(1+2i)|<3$ including its boundary. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] It is a distance condition from (1,2).","[1] Cartesian inequality is $(x-1)^2+(y-2)^2<9$.","[1] It describes the open disk of radius 3.","[1] The circumference is excluded because the inequality is strict."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Describe <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z-(1+2i)|&lt;3</annotation></semantics></math></span> including its boundary. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] It is a distance condition from (1,2).","[1] Cartesian inequality is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-1)^2+(y-2)^2&lt;9</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] It describes the open disk of radius 3.","[1] The circumference is excluded because the inequality is strict."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-loci-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find the Cartesian equation of $|z-2|=2|z|$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Square nonnegative distances: $(x-2)^2+y^2=4(x^2+y^2)$.","[1] Rearrange to $3x^2+3y^2+4x-4=0$.","[1] Complete the square: $(x+2/3)^2+y^2=16/9$.","[1] It is a circle centred at (-2/3,0) with radius 4/3."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the Cartesian equation of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z-2|=2|z|</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Square nonnegative distances: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-2)^2+y^2=4(x^2+y^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Rearrange to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3x^2+3y^2+4x-4=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Complete the square: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x+2/3)^2+y^2=16/9</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] It is a circle centred at (-2/3,0) with radius 4/3."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-loci-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find $|z-1|=|z+1|$ and $|z|=2$ simultaneously. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Equal distances from 1 and -1 give $x=0$.","[1] The circle then gives $y^2=4$.","[1] Thus $z=2i$ or $z=-2i$.","[1] Both are on the perpendicular bisector and radius-2 circle."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z-1|=|z+1|</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z|=2</annotation></semantics></math></span> simultaneously. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Equal distances from 1 and -1 give <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The circle then gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^2=4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=2i</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=-2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both are on the perpendicular bisector and radius-2 circle."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-loci-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Explain why $y=x$ is insufficient to specify $\\operatorname{Arg}z=\\pi/4$. Give the complete Cartesian description. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The line includes both quadrant I and quadrant III points.","[1] Only the positive-direction ray has argument $\\pi/4$.","[1] Thus require $y=x$ and $x>0$.","[1] The origin is excluded since its argument is undefined."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x</annotation></semantics></math></span> is insufficient to specify <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Arg}z=\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Give the complete Cartesian description. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The line includes both quadrant I and quadrant III points.","[1] Only the positive-direction ray has argument <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus require <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The origin is excluded since its argument is undefined."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Why is the endpoint excluded from a fixed-argument ray?","o":["The zero displacement has no defined argument.","All locus endpoints are always excluded.","The modulus cannot equal zero in any complex equation.","A ray must contain negative radius values."],"a":0,"why":"The argument is a direction of a nonzero vector.","qHtml":"Why is the endpoint excluded from a fixed-argument ray?","oHtml":["The zero displacement has no defined argument.","All locus endpoints are always excluded.","The modulus cannot equal zero in any complex equation.","A ray must contain negative radius values."],"whyHtml":"The argument is a direction of a nonzero vector."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-complex-unity","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Complex numbers","title":"Roots of unity and regular polygons","outcomes":["3.1.11"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Solve unity by distributing full turns · Regular-polygon geometry · Use powers and primitive roots · Relate roots to factors","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Roots of unity and regular polygons</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Solve unity by distributing full turns</li><li>Regular-polygon geometry</li><li>Use powers and primitive roots</li><li>Relate roots to factors</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Solve unity by distributing full turns","text":"The nth roots of unity satisfy $z^n=1$. Write $1=\\operatorname{cis}(2\\pi k)$, so roots are $z_k=\\operatorname{cis}(2\\pi k/n)$ for $k=0,\\ldots,n-1$. All have modulus 1 and consecutive arguments differ by $2\\pi/n$. Values beyond this range repeat earlier roots.","diagram":null,"html":"The nth roots of unity satisfy <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^n=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1=\\operatorname{cis}(2\\pi k)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so roots are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_k=\\operatorname{cis}(2\\pi k/n)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0,\\ldots,n-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. All have modulus 1 and consecutive arguments differ by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/n</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Values beyond this range repeat earlier roots."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Regular-polygon geometry","text":"The roots are vertices of a regular n-gon on the unit circle, with one vertex at 1. For adjacent vertices, chord length is $2\\sin(\\pi/n)$. The arrangement supplies geometric checks for completeness and symmetry. For n>1, the sum of all roots is zero, corresponding to vectors balancing about the centre.","diagram":"complex-roots","html":"The roots are vertices of a regular n-gon on the unit circle, with one vertex at 1. For adjacent vertices, chord length is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sin(\\pi/n)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The arrangement supplies geometric checks for completeness and symmetry. For n&gt;1, the sum of all roots is zero, corresponding to vectors balancing about the centre."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use powers and primitive roots","text":"If $\\omega=\\operatorname{cis}(2\\pi/n)$, roots are $1,\\omega,\\ldots,\\omega^{n-1}$. The identity $\\omega^n=1$ reduces exponents modulo n. A primitive root generates all n roots through its powers; a root with a smaller period does not. For $\\omega\\ne1$, the geometric-sum identity gives $1+\\omega+\\cdots+\\omega^{n-1}=0$.","diagram":null,"html":"If <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=\\operatorname{cis}(2\\pi/n)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, roots are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1,\\omega,\\ldots,\\omega^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The identity <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega^n=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> reduces exponents modulo n. A primitive root generates all n roots through its powers; a root with a smaller period does not. For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega\\ne1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the geometric-sum identity gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+\\omega+\\cdots+\\omega^{n-1}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Relate roots to factors","text":"The factorisation $z^n-1=\\prod_{k=0}^{n-1}(z-z_k)$ contains all n distinct roots. Pair conjugate roots to obtain real quadratic factors. Do not confuse roots of unity with all roots of any other complex number: their common modulus is specifically 1. Exact angles and Cartesian special values help check the polygon.","diagram":null,"html":"The factorisation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^n-1=\\prod_{k=0}^{n-1}(z-z_k)</annotation></semantics></math></span> contains all n distinct roots. Pair conjugate roots to obtain real quadratic factors. Do not confuse roots of unity with all roots of any other complex number: their common modulus is specifically 1. Exact angles and Cartesian special values help check the polygon."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-complex-unity-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find all cube roots of unity in Cartesian form. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Angles are $0,2\\pi/3,4\\pi/3$.","[1] Root at angle 0 is 1.","[1] Other roots are $-1/2+(\\sqrt3/2)i$ and $-1/2-(\\sqrt3/2)i$.","[1] They are three equally spaced points on the unit circle."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find all cube roots of unity in Cartesian form. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Angles are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,2\\pi/3,4\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Root at angle 0 is 1.","[1] Other roots are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1/2+(\\sqrt3/2)i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1/2-(\\sqrt3/2)i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] They are three equally spaced points on the unit circle."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-unity-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Let $\\omega=\\operatorname{cis}(2\\pi/5)$. Prove $1+\\omega+\\omega^2+\\omega^3+\\omega^4=0$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let the sum be S and note $\\omega\\ne1$.","[1] $(\\omega-1)S=\\omega^5-1$.","[1] Since $\\omega^5=1$, the right side is zero.","[1] Divide by nonzero $\\omega-1$ to obtain S=0."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Let <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=\\operatorname{cis}(2\\pi/5)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Prove <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+\\omega+\\omega^2+\\omega^3+\\omega^4=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let the sum be S and note <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega\\ne1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\omega-1)S=\\omega^5-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Since <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega^5=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the right side is zero.","[1] Divide by nonzero <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega-1</annotation></semantics></math></span> to obtain S=0."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-unity-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"List all fourth roots of unity and their sum. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Angles are $0,\\pi/2,\\pi,3\\pi/2$.","[1] Roots are $1,i,-1,-i$.","[1] The real pair and imaginary pair each cancel.","[1] Their sum is zero."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"List all fourth roots of unity and their sum. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Angles are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,\\pi/2,\\pi,3\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Roots are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1,i,-1,-i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The real pair and imaginary pair each cancel.","[1] Their sum is zero."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-unity-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"For $\\omega=\\operatorname{cis}(2\\pi/7)$, simplify $\\omega^{23}$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\omega^7=1$.","[1] $23=3(7)+2$.","[1] $\\omega^{23}=(\\omega^7)^3\\omega^2=\\omega^2$.","[1] Equivalent polar form is $\\operatorname{cis}(4\\pi/7)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=\\operatorname{cis}(2\\pi/7)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, simplify <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>23</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega^{23}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega^7=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>23</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">23=3(7)+2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>23</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega^{23}=(\\omega^7)^3\\omega^2=\\omega^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Equivalent polar form is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{cis}(4\\pi/7)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-unity-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find the distance between adjacent sixth roots of unity. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Their central angle is $2\\pi/6=\\pi/3$.","[1] Unit-circle chord length is $2\\sin(\\pi/6)$.","[1] This equals 1.","[1] The hexagon therefore has side length equal to its radius."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the distance between adjacent sixth roots of unity. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Their central angle is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/6=\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Unit-circle chord length is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sin(\\pi/6)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This equals 1.","[1] The hexagon therefore has side length equal to its radius."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-unity-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Explain why -1 is a sixth root of unity but does not generate all sixth roots through its powers. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $(-1)^6=1$, so it is a sixth root.","[1] Its powers alternate between 1 and -1.","[1] Its order is 2 rather than 6.","[1] Thus it is not a primitive sixth root and cannot generate the other four roots."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why -1 is a sixth root of unity but does not generate all sixth roots through its powers. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1)^6=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so it is a sixth root.","[1] Its powers alternate between 1 and -1.","[1] Its order is 2 rather than 6.","[1] Thus it is not a primitive sixth root and cannot generate the other four roots."]}],"bank":[{"q":"How far apart are adjacent arguments of nth roots of unity?","o":["$2\\pi/n$.","$\\pi/n$ always.","2pi regardless of n.","n radians."],"a":0,"why":"The n equally spaced roots divide one full turn into n parts.","qHtml":"How far apart are adjacent arguments of nth roots of unity?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/n</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/n</annotation></semantics></math></span> always.","2pi regardless of n.","n radians."],"whyHtml":"The n equally spaced roots divide one full turn into n parts."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-complex-roots","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Complex numbers","title":"All roots of a complex number","outcomes":["3.1.12"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Include all arguments before dividing · Check completeness and distinctness · Use polygon geometry · Shifted equations and restrictions","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>All roots of a complex number</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Include all arguments before dividing</li><li>Check completeness and distinctness</li><li>Use polygon geometry</li><li>Shifted equations and restrictions</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Include all arguments before dividing","text":"For nonzero $w=r\\operatorname{cis}\\theta$, solutions of $z^n=w$ are $r^{1/n}\\operatorname{cis}((\\theta+2\\pi k)/n)$ for $k=0,\\ldots,n-1$. Writing only $\\theta/n$ produces just one root. The radius is positive and common to all roots, while the angles differ by $2\\pi/n$.","diagram":null,"html":"For nonzero <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=r\\operatorname{cis}\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>, solutions of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^n=w</annotation></semantics></math></span> are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^{1/n}\\operatorname{cis}((\\theta+2\\pi k)/n)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0,\\ldots,n-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Writing only <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta/n</annotation></semantics></math></span> produces just one root. The radius is positive and common to all roots, while the angles differ by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/n</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Check completeness and distinctness","text":"Exactly n distinct roots occur for nonzero w and positive integer n. Their angles differ by less than a full turn in the selected index range. Substitution raises each root to modulus r and angle theta plus full turns. If w=0, the only distinct root is zero, with multiplicity n in the polynomial $z^n$.","diagram":"complex-roots","html":"Exactly n distinct roots occur for nonzero w and positive integer n. Their angles differ by less than a full turn in the selected index range. Substitution raises each root to modulus r and angle theta plus full turns. If w=0, the only distinct root is zero, with multiplicity n in the polynomial <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^n</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use polygon geometry","text":"All roots form a regular n-gon centred at the origin, rotated according to theta/n and scaled to radius $r^{1/n}$. This helps identify missing roots and expected symmetries. Conjugate pairing is guaranteed when w is real, not for arbitrary nonreal w. State all roots exactly before using approximate Cartesian coordinates for a diagram.","diagram":null,"html":"All roots form a regular n-gon centred at the origin, rotated according to theta/n and scaled to radius <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^{1/n}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This helps identify missing roots and expected symmetries. Conjugate pairing is guaranteed when w is real, not for arbitrary nonreal w. State all roots exactly before using approximate Cartesian coordinates for a diagram."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Shifted equations and restrictions","text":"For $(z-a)^n=w$, solve for u=z-a and then translate every root by a. The resulting polygon is centred at a. Equations involving denominators must also preserve exclusions from the original expression. A principal root is one selected branch, whereas an instruction to solve the equation normally requires every solution.","diagram":null,"html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z-a)^n=w</annotation></semantics></math></span>, solve for u=z-a and then translate every root by a. The resulting polygon is centred at a. Equations involving denominators must also preserve exclusions from the original expression. A principal root is one selected branch, whereas an instruction to solve the equation normally requires every solution."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-complex-roots-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find all roots of $z^3=8$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Radius is $8^{1/3}=2$.","[1] Angles are $0,2\\pi/3,4\\pi/3$.","[1] Roots are $2,-1+\\sqrt3i,-1-\\sqrt3i$.","[1] Cubing each yields 8; the three roots are distinct."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find all roots of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^3=8</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Radius is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>8</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8^{1/3}=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Angles are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,2\\pi/3,4\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Roots are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2,-1+\\sqrt3i,-1-\\sqrt3i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Cubing each yields 8; the three roots are distinct."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-roots-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Solve $z^4=-16$ in polar form. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Write $-16=16\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi+2\\pi k)$.","[1] Root radius is 2.","[1] Angles are $\\pi/4,3\\pi/4,5\\pi/4,7\\pi/4$.","[1] The four solutions are 2 times cis of these angles."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^4=-16</annotation></semantics></math></span> in polar form. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-16=16\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi+2\\pi k)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Root radius is 2.","[1] Angles are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/4,3\\pi/4,5\\pi/4,7\\pi/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The four solutions are 2 times cis of these angles."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-roots-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find the two square roots of $3+4i$ in Cartesian form. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Check $(2+i)^2=3+4i$.","[1] Its negative $-2-i$ has the same square.","[1] A nonzero complex number has two distinct square roots.","[1] Therefore the full solution set is $\\{2+i,-2-i\\}$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the two square roots of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3+4i</annotation></semantics></math></span> in Cartesian form. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Check <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2+i)^2=3+4i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its negative <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2-i</annotation></semantics></math></span> has the same square.","[1] A nonzero complex number has two distinct square roots.","[1] Therefore the full solution set is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{2+i,-2-i\\}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-roots-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Solve $(z-1)^3=-8$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Put $u=z-1$, giving $u^3=8\\operatorname{cis}\\pi$.","[1] Radius 2 and angles $\\pi/3,\\pi,5\\pi/3$ give $u=1+\\sqrt3i,-2,1-\\sqrt3i$.","[1] Add 1 to every solution.","[1] $z=2+\\sqrt3i,-1,2-\\sqrt3i$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z-1)^3=-8</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Put <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=z-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u^3=8\\operatorname{cis}\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Radius 2 and angles <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/3,\\pi,5\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span> give <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=1+\\sqrt3i,-2,1-\\sqrt3i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Add 1 to every solution.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=2+\\sqrt3i,-1,2-\\sqrt3i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-roots-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find all fifth roots of $32i$ in polar form. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $32i=32\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/2)$.","[1] Radius is 2.","[1] Angles are $\\pi/10+2\\pi k/5$, for $k=0,1,2,3,4$.","[1] These five roots are distinct and raising them to the fifth power gives $32i$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find all fifth roots of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">32i</annotation></semantics></math></span> in polar form. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">32i=32\\operatorname{cis}(\\pi/2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Radius is 2.","[1] Angles are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>10</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/10+2\\pi k/5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0,1,2,3,4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] These five roots are distinct and raising them to the fifth power gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">32i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-roots-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A solution to $z^6=64$ lists only z=2 and z=-2. Explain the omissions and give all roots. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] These are the two real roots, but the equation is being solved over the complex numbers.","[1] Every root has radius 2.","[1] Complete roots are $2\\operatorname{cis}(k\\pi/3)$ for $k=0,\\ldots,5$.","[1] Four nonreal roots were omitted; six distinct roots are required."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A solution to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^6=64</annotation></semantics></math></span> lists only z=2 and z=-2. Explain the omissions and give all roots. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] These are the two real roots, but the equation is being solved over the complex numbers.","[1] Every root has radius 2.","[1] Complete roots are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\operatorname{cis}(k\\pi/3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0,\\ldots,5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Four nonreal roots were omitted; six distinct roots are required."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Why add $2\\pi k$ before dividing the argument by n?","o":["To generate all n distinct roots rather than one selected root.","To change the modulus.","To remove every nonreal solution.","To guarantee that all roots have principal argument zero."],"a":0,"why":"Full turns of the original argument become distinct angular positions after division.","qHtml":"Why add <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi k</annotation></semantics></math></span> before dividing the argument by n?","oHtml":["To generate all n distinct roots rather than one selected root.","To change the modulus.","To remove every nonreal solution.","To guarantee that all roots have principal argument zero."],"whyHtml":"Full turns of the original argument become distinct angular positions after division."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-complex-polynomials","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Complex numbers","title":"Factor/remainder theorems and conjugate-root polynomials","outcomes":["3.1.13","3.1.14","3.1.15"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Remainder and factor theorems · Real coefficients force conjugate pairs · Determine unknown coefficients · Report complete factorisations · Proof of the remainder and factor theorems","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Factor/remainder theorems and conjugate-root polynomials</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Remainder and factor theorems</li><li>Real coefficients force conjugate pairs</li><li>Determine unknown coefficients</li><li>Report complete factorisations</li><li>Proof of the remainder and factor theorems</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Remainder and factor theorems","text":"For a polynomial P, division by z-a leaves remainder P(a). Thus z-a is a factor exactly when P(a)=0. Substitute a into every term, including constant terms, and simplify powers of i correctly. Polynomial long division or synthetic division then reduces the degree. A stated root without a substitution or division check can leave an unsupported factorisation.","diagram":null,"html":"For a polynomial P, division by z-a leaves remainder P(a). Thus z-a is a factor exactly when P(a)=0. Substitute a into every term, including constant terms, and simplify powers of i correctly. Polynomial long division or synthetic division then reduces the degree. A stated root without a substitution or division check can leave an unsupported factorisation."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Real coefficients force conjugate pairs","text":"If all coefficients are real and P(a+bi)=0, conjugating the equation gives P(a-bi)=0. The pair produces the real quadratic $(z-a)^2+b^2$. Dividing by this quadratic often leaves a lower-degree real polynomial. This theorem fails when the coefficients are not all real; check the hypothesis explicitly.","diagram":"conjugate","html":"If all coefficients are real and P(a+bi)=0, conjugating the equation gives P(a-bi)=0. The pair produces the real quadratic <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z-a)^2+b^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Dividing by this quadratic often leaves a lower-degree real polynomial. This theorem fails when the coefficients are not all real; check the hypothesis explicitly."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Determine unknown coefficients","text":"When a complex root is supplied and unknown coefficients are real, substitute the root, collect real and imaginary components, then solve the resulting equations. Verify the root after finding the parameters. If the question gives a remainder rather than a root, set P(a) equal to that remainder, not zero.","diagram":null,"html":"When a complex root is supplied and unknown coefficients are real, substitute the root, collect real and imaginary components, then solve the resulting equations. Verify the root after finding the parameters. If the question gives a remainder rather than a root, set P(a) equal to that remainder, not zero."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Report complete factorisations","text":"Over the reals, irreducible conjugate-pair quadratics may remain. Over the complex numbers, give linear factors and retain the leading coefficient. Degree limits the number of roots counted with multiplicity. Repeated roots are different from omitted roots: list multiplicities when relevant and use expansion or coefficient comparisons as a final check.","diagram":null,"html":"Over the reals, irreducible conjugate-pair quadratics may remain. Over the complex numbers, give linear factors and retain the leading coefficient. Degree limits the number of roots counted with multiplicity. Repeated roots are different from omitted roots: list multiplicities when relevant and use expansion or coefficient comparisons as a final check."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Proof of the remainder and factor theorems","text":"Polynomial division by the monic linear expression z-a gives $P(z)=(z-a)Q(z)+R$, where the remainder R is constant because its degree must be below 1. Evaluate at z=a: the product term vanishes, leaving $P(a)=R$. Thus the remainder is P(a). The expression z-a divides P exactly if and only if this remainder is zero, equivalently if and only if P(a)=0. The proof works for real or complex coefficients. A nonzero leading coefficient in P remains in a complete factorisation; knowing one root does not determine the entire polynomial.","diagram":null,"html":"Polynomial division by the monic linear expression z-a gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(z)=(z-a)Q(z)+R</annotation></semantics></math></span>, where the remainder R is constant because its degree must be below 1. Evaluate at z=a: the product term vanishes, leaving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(a)=R</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Thus the remainder is P(a). The expression z-a divides P exactly if and only if this remainder is zero, equivalently if and only if P(a)=0. The proof works for real or complex coefficients. A nonzero leading coefficient in P remains in a complete factorisation; knowing one root does not determine the entire polynomial."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Extended exam task","q":6},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-complex-polynomials-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Factor $P(z)=z^3-3z^2+4z-2$ given root $1+i$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Real coefficients imply root $1-i$.","[1] Pair factor is $(z-1)^2+1=z^2-2z+2$.","[1] Division gives remaining factor z-1.","[1] $P(z)=(z-1)(z-1-i)(z-1+i)$, with roots $1,1\\pm i$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Factor <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(z)=z^3-3z^2+4z-2</annotation></semantics></math></span> given root <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Real coefficients imply root <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Pair factor is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z-1)^2+1=z^2-2z+2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Division gives remaining factor z-1.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(z)=(z-1)(z-1-i)(z-1+i)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with roots <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1,1\\pm i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-polynomials-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"For real a,b, $P(z)=z^3+az^2+bz+10$ has root $1+2i$. Find a,b. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $(1+2i)^2=-3+4i$, $(1+2i)^3=-11-2i$.","[1] Substitution gives real equation $-1-3a+b=0$ and imaginary equation $-2+4a+2b=0$.","[1] Thus $b=1+3a$ and $2a+b=1$, giving $a=0,b=1$.","[1] $P=z^3+z+10=(z+2)(z^2-2z+5)$ verifies the root."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For real a,b, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(z)=z^3+az^2+bz+10</annotation></semantics></math></span> has root <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find a,b. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+2i)^2=-3+4i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+2i)^3=-11-2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Substitution gives real equation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1-3a+b=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and imaginary equation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2+4a+2b=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=1+3a</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2a+b=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0,b=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=z^3+z+10=(z+2)(z^2-2z+5)</annotation></semantics></math></span> verifies the root."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-polynomials-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find the remainder when $z^3+2z+1$ is divided by z-i. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Remainder is P(i).","[1] $i^3=-i$.","[1] $P(i)=-i+2i+1=1+i$.","[1] Since the remainder is nonzero, z-i is not a factor."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the remainder when <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^3+2z+1</annotation></semantics></math></span> is divided by z-i. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Remainder is P(i).","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i^3=-i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(i)=-i+2i+1=1+i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Since the remainder is nonzero, z-i is not a factor."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-polynomials-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Factor $z^4+5z^2+4$ over the complex numbers. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Treat $z^2$ as a variable: $(z^2+1)(z^2+4)$.","[1] First factor has roots $\\pm i$.","[1] Second factor has roots $\\pm2i$.","[1] Complete factorisation is $(z-i)(z+i)(z-2i)(z+2i)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Factor <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^4+5z^2+4</annotation></semantics></math></span> over the complex numbers. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Treat <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> as a variable: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z^2+1)(z^2+4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] First factor has roots <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Second factor has roots <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Complete factorisation is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z-i)(z+i)(z-2i)(z+2i)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-polynomials-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Prove the conjugate-root theorem for a polynomial with real coefficients. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let $P(z)=\\sum c_jz^j$ with each $c_j$ real and P(r)=0.","[1] Conjugate both sides: $\\overline{P(r)}=0$.","[1] Conjugation preserves sums and products, and $\\bar c_j=c_j$, so $\\overline{P(r)}=P(\\bar r)$.","[1] Thus $P(\\bar r)=0$, proving the conjugate is a root."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove the conjugate-root theorem for a polynomial with real coefficients. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(z)=\\sum c_jz^j</annotation></semantics></math></span> with each <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_j</annotation></semantics></math></span> real and P(r)=0.","[1] Conjugate both sides: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overline{P(r)}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Conjugation preserves sums and products, and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>c</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar c_j=c_j</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overline{P(r)}=P(\\bar r)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(\\bar r)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, proving the conjugate is a root."]},{"id":"mas12-complex-polynomials-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A monic real cubic has roots $2+i$, $2-i$ and -3. Find it in expanded form. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Conjugate pair factor is $(z-2)^2+1=z^2-4z+5$.","[1] Third factor is z+3.","[1] Multiply to obtain $z^3-z^2-7z+15$.","[1] Root sum 1 agrees with the negative quadratic coefficient."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A monic real cubic has roots <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2-i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and -3. Find it in expanded form. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Conjugate pair factor is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z-2)^2+1=z^2-4z+5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Third factor is z+3.","[1] Multiply to obtain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^3-z^2-7z+15</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Root sum 1 agrees with the negative quadratic coefficient."]},{"prompt":"A monic real cubic $P(z)=z^3+az^2+bz+10$ has root $1+2i$. (a) Find a,b. (b) Factor P completely over the complex numbers. (c) Find the remainder on division by z-i. (d) Describe the Argand circle through the conjugate pair with centre 1. (8 marks)","steps":["[2] Powers of $1+2i$ are $-3+4i$ and $-11-2i$. Real and imaginary equations give $b=1+3a$ and $2a+b=1$.","[1] Hence a=0,b=1.","[2] $P=(z+2)(z^2-2z+5)=(z+2)(z-1-2i)(z-1+2i)$.","[1] Remainder is $P(i)=-i+i+10=10$.","[2] Each nonreal root lies distance 2 from 1. Circle is $|z-1|=2$, Cartesian $(x-1)^2+y^2=4$."],"id":"mas12-complex-polynomials-extended","title":"Extended exam task","allocations":[2,1,2,1,2],"marks":8,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed or exact working","promptHtml":"A monic real cubic <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(z)=z^3+az^2+bz+10</annotation></semantics></math></span> has root <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (a) Find a,b. (b) Factor P completely over the complex numbers. (c) Find the remainder on division by z-i. (d) Describe the Argand circle through the conjugate pair with centre 1. (8 marks)","stepsHtml":["[2] Powers of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+2i</annotation></semantics></math></span> are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3+4i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-11-2i</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Real and imaginary equations give <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=1+3a</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2a+b=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Hence a=0,b=1.","[2] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=(z+2)(z^2-2z+5)=(z+2)(z-1-2i)(z-1+2i)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Remainder is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(i)=-i+i+10=10</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[2] Each nonreal root lies distance 2 from 1. Circle is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z-1|=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, Cartesian <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-1)^2+y^2=4</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What is the remainder on division of P(z) by z-a?","o":["P(a).","P(-a) in every case.","Zero regardless of P.","The leading coefficient only."],"a":0,"why":"The division identity evaluated at z=a removes the factor term and leaves the remainder.","qHtml":"What is the remainder on division of P(z) by z-a?","oHtml":["P(a).","P(-a) in every case.","Zero regardless of P.","The leading coefficient only."],"whyHtml":"The division identity evaluated at z=a removes the factor term and leaves the remainder."}],"subtitle":"17 screens · 2 worked examples · 5 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-functions-composition","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Functions and sketching graphs","title":"Composition rules and exact domain restrictions","outcomes":["3.2.1","3.2.2"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Composition is an ordered evaluation · Intersect the admissible conditions · Cancellation does not restore inputs · Range is produced by the domain","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Composition rules and exact domain restrictions</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Composition is an ordered evaluation</li><li>Intersect the admissible conditions</li><li>Cancellation does not restore inputs</li><li>Range is produced by the domain</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Composition is an ordered evaluation","text":"The composite $(f\\circ g)(x)=f(g(x))$ applies g first. Substitution must replace every occurrence of the input in f with the entire expression g(x), using brackets. Generally $f\\circ g\\ne g\\circ f$. A simplified rule alone does not specify the composite function: its domain is also required.","diagram":null,"html":"The composite <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(f\\circ g)(x)=f(g(x))</annotation></semantics></math></span> applies g first. Substitution must replace every occurrence of the input in f with the entire expression g(x), using brackets. Generally <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>g</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\circ g\\ne g\\circ f</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A simplified rule alone does not specify the composite function: its domain is also required."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Intersect the admissible conditions","text":"The domain of $f\\circ g$ is $\\{x\\in D_g:g(x)\\in D_f\\}$. First require g to be defined, then require its output to be a valid input to f. Square roots demand nonnegative radicands, denominators demand nonzero values and logarithms demand positive arguments. These conditions can combine into intervals with excluded points.","diagram":"composition","html":"The domain of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\circ g</annotation></semantics></math></span> is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{x\\in D_g:g(x)\\in D_f\\}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. First require g to be defined, then require its output to be a valid input to f. Square roots demand nonnegative radicands, denominators demand nonzero values and logarithms demand positive arguments. These conditions can combine into intervals with excluded points."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Cancellation does not restore inputs","text":"An expression may simplify to a formula defined on a larger set, but the original composite keeps its restrictions. For example, a cancelled denominator remains excluded. Explicitly stated domains also matter even when the formula itself would allow more inputs. Derive the admissible domain before cancellation and retain it alongside the simplified rule.","diagram":null,"html":"An expression may simplify to a formula defined on a larger set, but the original composite keeps its restrictions. For example, a cancelled denominator remains excluded. Explicitly stated domains also matter even when the formula itself would allow more inputs. Derive the admissible domain before cancellation and retain it alongside the simplified rule."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Range is produced by the domain","text":"Find the outputs actually attained on the allowed domain. An algebraically possible output may require an excluded input. Mapping diagrams can show why the inner range must land inside the outer domain. In unfamiliar exam questions, write the two conditions before solving inequalities; this separates a function's definition from a calculator's symbolic simplification.","diagram":null,"html":"Find the outputs actually attained on the allowed domain. An algebraically possible output may require an excluded input. Mapping diagrams can show why the inner range must land inside the outer domain. In unfamiliar exam questions, write the two conditions before solving inequalities; this separates a function&#39;s definition from a calculator&#39;s symbolic simplification."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-functions-composition-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Let $f(x)=\\sqrt x$, $g(x)=x-2$. Find both composites and domains. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $f(g(x))=\\sqrt{x-2}$ requires $x-2\\ge0$.","[1] Its domain is $[2,\\infty)$.","[1] $g(f(x))=\\sqrt x-2$ requires $x\\ge0$.","[1] Its domain is $[0,\\infty)$; the composites differ."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Let <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\sqrt x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=x-2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find both composites and domains. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(g(x))=\\sqrt{x-2}</annotation></semantics></math></span> requires <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-2\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its domain is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[2,\\infty)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(f(x))=\\sqrt x-2</annotation></semantics></math></span> requires <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its domain is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,\\infty)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the composites differ."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-composition-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"For $f(x)=1/(x-1)$ and $g(x)=x^2$, find $f\\circ g$ and its domain. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] g is defined for all real x.","[1] Its output must not equal 1: $x^2\\ne1$.","[1] Rule is $1/(x^2-1)$.","[1] Domain is $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-1,1\\}$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=1/(x-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\circ g</annotation></semantics></math></span> and its domain. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] g is defined for all real x.","[1] Its output must not equal 1: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2\\ne1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Rule is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(x^2-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Domain is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mo>∖</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-1,1\\}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-composition-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"For $f(x)=\\ln x$ and $g(x)=4-x^2$, find the composite domain. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Require $4-x^2>0$.","[1] This gives $x^2<4$.","[1] Hence $-2<x<2$.","[1] Rule is $\\ln(4-x^2)$ on (-2,2), with endpoints excluded."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\ln x</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=4-x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find the composite domain. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Require <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-x^2&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2&lt;4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2&lt;x&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Rule is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(4-x^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> on (-2,2), with endpoints excluded."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-composition-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Let $f(x)=x^2$ on $[0,3]$ and $g(x)=x-1$ on $[0,5]$. Find the domain and range of $f\\circ g$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Inner input requires $0\\le x\\le5$.","[1] Outer input requires $0\\le x-1\\le3$, giving $1\\le x\\le4$.","[1] Composite is $(x-1)^2$ on [1,4].","[1] Its range is [0,9]."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Let <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> on <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,3]</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=x-1</annotation></semantics></math></span> on <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,5]</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find the domain and range of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\circ g</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Inner input requires <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le x\\le5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Outer input requires <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le x-1\\le3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\le x\\le4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Composite is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-1)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> on [1,4].","[1] Its range is [0,9]."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-composition-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"For $f(x)=1/x$ and $g(x)=1/x$, state the composite and explain its domain. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Inner g excludes x=0.","[1] For permitted x, g(x) is nonzero, so it is admissible for f.","[1] $f(g(x))=x$.","[1] Domain remains $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$ despite the simplified identity formula."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=1/x</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=1/x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, state the composite and explain its domain. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Inner g excludes x=0.","[1] For permitted x, g(x) is nonzero, so it is admissible for f.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(g(x))=x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Domain remains <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mo>∖</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}</annotation></semantics></math></span> despite the simplified identity formula."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-composition-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"For $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}$ and $g(x)=1/(x-2)$, find the composite domain. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Require $x\\ne2$ and $1/(x-2)+1\\ge0$.","[1] The inequality is $(x-1)/(x-2)\\ge0$.","[1] A sign chart with critical points 1 and 2 gives $x\\le1$ or $x>2$.","[1] Domain is $(-\\infty,1]\\cup(2,\\infty)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=1/(x-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find the composite domain. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Require <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ne2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(x-2)+1\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The inequality is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-1)/(x-2)\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] A sign chart with critical points 1 and 2 gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\le1</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Domain is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>∪</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-\\infty,1]\\cup(2,\\infty)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which condition defines the domain of f composed with g?","o":["x is in the domain of g and g(x) is in the domain of f.","x is merely in the domain of f.","The two formulas have the same degree.","Only the simplified formula matters."],"a":0,"why":"The inner evaluation must exist and its output must be accepted by the outer function.","qHtml":"Which condition defines the domain of f composed with g?","oHtml":["x is in the domain of g and g(x) is in the domain of f.","x is merely in the domain of f.","The two formulas have the same degree.","Only the simplified formula matters."],"whyHtml":"The inner evaluation must exist and its output must be accepted by the outer function."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-functions-inverse","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Functions and sketching graphs","title":"One-to-one functions, inverse restrictions and reflection","outcomes":["3.2.3","3.2.4","3.2.5"],"icon":"📐","scope":"An inverse reverses a one-to-one map · Solve for the original input · Restrict a many-to-one function · Check both identities on their domains","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>One-to-one functions, inverse restrictions and reflection</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>An inverse reverses a one-to-one map</li><li>Solve for the original input</li><li>Restrict a many-to-one function</li><li>Check both identities on their domains</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"An inverse reverses a one-to-one map","text":"A function has an inverse function on its range when each output comes from exactly one input. The horizontal-line test checks one-to-one behaviour on a graph. The inverse exchanges domain and range. The notation $f^{-1}$ denotes the inverse function, not the reciprocal 1/f. These two constructions usually have different rules and domains.","diagram":null,"html":"A function has an inverse function on its range when each output comes from exactly one input. The horizontal-line test checks one-to-one behaviour on a graph. The inverse exchanges domain and range. The notation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> denotes the inverse function, not the reciprocal 1/f. These two constructions usually have different rules and domains."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Solve for the original input","text":"Write $y=f(x)$, rearrange to express x in terms of y, then interchange variable names. Track any branch choice from the original domain. For a squared expression, taking a square root gives a sign choice that must be resolved using the restriction; automatically choosing the positive sign can yield the wrong inverse.","diagram":"inverse","html":"Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=f(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, rearrange to express x in terms of y, then interchange variable names. Track any branch choice from the original domain. For a squared expression, taking a square root gives a sign choice that must be resolved using the restriction; automatically choosing the positive sign can yield the wrong inverse."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Restrict a many-to-one function","text":"A parabola on the entire real line is not one-to-one, but either side of its vertex can give an inverse. Different restrictions produce different inverse branches. State the selected domain and resulting range before deriving the inverse. The reflection of the restricted graph in y=x then gives the inverse graph.","diagram":null,"html":"A parabola on the entire real line is not one-to-one, but either side of its vertex can give an inverse. Different restrictions produce different inverse branches. State the selected domain and resulting range before deriving the inverse. The reflection of the restricted graph in y=x then gives the inverse graph."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Check both identities on their domains","text":"$f^{-1}(f(x))=x$ holds for x in the original domain and $f(f^{-1}(y))=y$ for y in the original range. With square roots, $\\sqrt{x^2}=|x|$, not x for every real value. Fixed points lie on y=x, but inverse graphs may also intersect elsewhere in special situations; do not infer more than the algebra establishes.","diagram":null,"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{-1}(f(x))=x</annotation></semantics></math></span> holds for x in the original domain and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(f^{-1}(y))=y</annotation></semantics></math></span> for y in the original range. With square roots, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{x^2}=|x|</annotation></semantics></math></span>, not x for every real value. Fixed points lie on y=x, but inverse graphs may also intersect elsewhere in special situations; do not infer more than the algebra establishes."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-functions-inverse-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find the inverse of $f(x)=(x-2)^2+1$ on $x\\ge2$, including its domain. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let $y=(x-2)^2+1$, so $y-1=(x-2)^2$.","[1] Since $x-2\\ge0$, $x=2+\\sqrt{y-1}$.","[1] $f^{-1}(x)=2+\\sqrt{x-1}$.","[1] Its domain is [1,infinity) and range [2,infinity)."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the inverse of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=(x-2)^2+1</annotation></semantics></math></span> on <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, including its domain. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=(x-2)^2+1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y-1=(x-2)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Since <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-2\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2+\\sqrt{y-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{-1}(x)=2+\\sqrt{x-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its domain is [1,infinity) and range [2,infinity)."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-inverse-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find the inverse of the same rule restricted to $x\\le2$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Again $(x-2)^2=y-1$.","[1] Now $x-2\\le0$, so $x=2-\\sqrt{y-1}$.","[1] $f^{-1}(x)=2-\\sqrt{x-1}$, domain [1,infinity).","[1] Range is (-infinity,2]; the domain restriction changes the branch."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the inverse of the same rule restricted to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\le2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Again <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-2)^2=y-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Now <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-2\\le0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2-\\sqrt{y-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{-1}(x)=2-\\sqrt{x-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, domain [1,infinity).","[1] Range is (-infinity,2]; the domain restriction changes the branch."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-inverse-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find the inverse of $f(x)=(3x-2)/(x+1)$ and state both exclusions. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $y(x+1)=3x-2$.","[1] $(y-3)x=-y-2$, so $x=(y+2)/(3-y)$.","[1] Inverse is $(x+2)/(3-x)$, excluding x=3.","[1] Original domain excludes -1 and original range excludes 3."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the inverse of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=(3x-2)/(x+1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and state both exclusions. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(x+1)=3x-2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(y-3)x=-y-2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(y+2)/(3-y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Inverse is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x+2)/(3-x)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, excluding x=3.","[1] Original domain excludes -1 and original range excludes 3."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-inverse-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Explain why $f(x)=x^2$ on the real numbers has no inverse function, then give a suitable restriction. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $f(2)=f(-2)=4$ despite distinct inputs.","[1] It is not one-to-one on the real line.","[1] Restricting to $x\\ge0$ makes it one-to-one with range $y\\ge0$.","[1] Its inverse is $\\sqrt x$ on $x\\ge0$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> on the real numbers has no inverse function, then give a suitable restriction. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(2)=f(-2)=4</annotation></semantics></math></span> despite distinct inputs.","[1] It is not one-to-one on the real line.","[1] Restricting to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span> makes it one-to-one with range <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its inverse is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt x</annotation></semantics></math></span> on <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-inverse-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find the inverse of $f(x)=e^{2x}+3$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $y-3=e^{2x}>0$.","[1] Take logarithms: $2x=\\ln(y-3)$.","[1] $f^{-1}(x)=\\tfrac12\\ln(x-3)$.","[1] Inverse domain is (3,infinity), range all real numbers."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the inverse of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=e^{2x}+3</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y-3=e^{2x}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Take logarithms: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x=\\ln(y-3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{-1}(x)=\\tfrac12\\ln(x-3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Inverse domain is (3,infinity), range all real numbers."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-inverse-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"For $f(x)=x^2$ restricted to $x\\le0$, verify its inverse composition. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The inverse is $f^{-1}(y)=-\\sqrt y$ on $y\\ge0$.","[1] $f^{-1}(f(x))=-\\sqrt{x^2}=-|x|$.","[1] Since $x\\le0$, $|x|=-x$, so the result is x.","[1] The domain condition is necessary to justify the sign."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> restricted to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\le0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, verify its inverse composition. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The inverse is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>y</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{-1}(y)=-\\sqrt y</annotation></semantics></math></span> on <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msqrt><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{-1}(f(x))=-\\sqrt{x^2}=-|x|</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Since <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\le0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|x|=-x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so the result is x.","[1] The domain condition is necessary to justify the sign."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What is the domain of an inverse function?","o":["The range of the original one-to-one function.","Always all real numbers.","The original domain regardless of range.","Only positive integers."],"a":0,"why":"Reversing input and output exchanges the two sets.","qHtml":"What is the domain of an inverse function?","oHtml":["The range of the original one-to-one function.","Always all real numbers.","The original domain regardless of range.","Only positive integers."],"whyHtml":"Reversing input and output exchanges the two sets."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-functions-absolute","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Functions and sketching graphs","title":"Absolute-value transformations and piecewise reasoning","outcomes":["3.2.6","3.2.7"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Absolute value changes signs geometrically · Piecewise definitions justify graphs · Solve absolute equations by cases · Annotate transformed features","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Absolute-value transformations and piecewise reasoning</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Absolute value changes signs geometrically</li><li>Piecewise definitions justify graphs</li><li>Solve absolute equations by cases</li><li>Annotate transformed features</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Absolute value changes signs geometrically","text":"The graph y=|f(x)| retains the parts of y=f(x) at or above the x-axis and reflects its negative parts upward. Zeros remain zeros and the output is nonnegative. The graph y=f(|x|) instead retains the original right-hand half and mirrors it into the left-hand half. These transformations act on different parts of the function and must not be confused.","diagram":null,"html":"The graph y=|f(x)| retains the parts of y=f(x) at or above the x-axis and reflects its negative parts upward. Zeros remain zeros and the output is nonnegative. The graph y=f(|x|) instead retains the original right-hand half and mirrors it into the left-hand half. These transformations act on different parts of the function and must not be confused."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Piecewise definitions justify graphs","text":"$|u|=u$ when $u\\ge0$ and -u when u<0. Solve f(x)=0 and use signs to write |f(x)| piecewise. Corners can occur where f crosses zero with nonzero slope. Differentiability must be checked at joining points rather than assumed from continuity. A repeated root can behave differently from a crossing root.","diagram":"absolute","html":"<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|u|=u</annotation></semantics></math></span> when <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and -u when u&lt;0. Solve f(x)=0 and use signs to write |f(x)| piecewise. Corners can occur where f crosses zero with nonzero slope. Differentiability must be checked at joining points rather than assumed from continuity. A repeated root can behave differently from a crossing root."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Solve absolute equations by cases","text":"For |f(x)|=a, no solution exists if a<0; for a>=0 solve f(x)=a and f(x)=-a, combining without duplication. Inequalities also depend on the sign of the bound. |u|<a for a>0 means -a<u<a; |u|>a means u<-a or u>a. Preserve any original domain exclusions.","diagram":null,"html":"For |f(x)|=a, no solution exists if a&lt;0; for a&gt;=0 solve f(x)=a and f(x)=-a, combining without duplication. Inequalities also depend on the sign of the bound. |u|&lt;a for a&gt;0 means -a&lt;u&lt;a; |u|&gt;a means u&lt;-a or u&gt;a. Preserve any original domain exclusions."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Annotate transformed features","text":"Mark intercepts, join points, asymptotes and the surviving domain. Absolute value does not automatically remove undefined inputs. For f(|x|), both sides depend on nonnegative arguments, so an original restriction can eliminate pairs of inputs. Use test values to check which half is reflected and describe changes in symmetry and range.","diagram":null,"html":"Mark intercepts, join points, asymptotes and the surviving domain. Absolute value does not automatically remove undefined inputs. For f(|x|), both sides depend on nonnegative arguments, so an original restriction can eliminate pairs of inputs. Use test values to check which half is reflected and describe changes in symmetry and range."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-functions-absolute-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Write $|x-2|$ piecewise and solve $|x-2|=3$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Rule is $2-x$ for x<2 and x-2 for x>=2.","[1] Solve x-2=3 or x-2=-3.","[1] Roots are x=5 and x=-1.","[1] Vertex is (2,0), with a corner from slopes -1 and 1."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|x-2|</annotation></semantics></math></span> piecewise and solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|x-2|=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Rule is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2-x</annotation></semantics></math></span> for x&lt;2 and x-2 for x&gt;=2.","[1] Solve x-2=3 or x-2=-3.","[1] Roots are x=5 and x=-1.","[1] Vertex is (2,0), with a corner from slopes -1 and 1."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-absolute-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Compare $|x^2-1|$ and $(|x|)^2-1$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] For |x|<1, $x^2-1<0$, so the first rule is $1-x^2$.","[1] For |x|>=1, it is $x^2-1$.","[1] The second is $x^2-1$ everywhere because $(|x|)^2=x^2$.","[1] The graphs differ between -1 and 1; output and input absolute values act differently."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Compare <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|x^2-1|</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(|x|)^2-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] For |x|&lt;1, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2-1&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so the first rule is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] For |x|&gt;=1, it is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The second is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2-1</annotation></semantics></math></span> everywhere because <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(|x|)^2=x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The graphs differ between -1 and 1; output and input absolute values act differently."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-absolute-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Solve $|2x+1|<5$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Since 5>0, write $-5<2x+1<5$.","[1] Subtract 1 to obtain $-6<2x<4$.","[1] Divide by positive 2.","[1] Solution is $-3<x<2$, endpoints excluded."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|2x+1|&lt;5</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Since 5&gt;0, write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-5&lt;2x+1&lt;5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Subtract 1 to obtain <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-6&lt;2x&lt;4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Divide by positive 2.","[1] Solution is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3&lt;x&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, endpoints excluded."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-absolute-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Describe $f(|x|)$ for $f(x)=x^3-2$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Rule is $|x|^3-2$.","[1] It equals $x^3-2$ for x>=0 and $-x^3-2$ for x<0.","[1] The right half of the original graph is mirrored into the left.","[1] It is even, has minimum -2 at zero and range [-2,infinity)."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Describe <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(|x|)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^3-2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Rule is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|x|^3-2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] It equals <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^3-2</annotation></semantics></math></span> for x&gt;=0 and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-x^3-2</annotation></semantics></math></span> for x&lt;0.","[1] The right half of the original graph is mirrored into the left.","[1] It is even, has minimum -2 at zero and range [-2,infinity)."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-absolute-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Solve $|x^2-4|=5$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Either $x^2-4=5$ or $x^2-4=-5$.","[1] The first gives $x^2=9$, roots $\\pm3$.","[1] The second gives $x^2=-1$, no real roots.","[1] Real solution set is {-3,3}."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|x^2-4|=5</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Either <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2-4=5</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2-4=-5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The first gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2=9</annotation></semantics></math></span>, roots <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The second gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2=-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, no real roots.","[1] Real solution set is {-3,3}."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-absolute-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"For $f(x)=1/(x-1)$, find the domain and vertical asymptotes of f(|x|). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Rule is $1/(|x|-1)$.","[1] Require |x| not equal 1, so x not equal -1 or 1.","[1] Both excluded locations give vertical asymptotes.","[1] The graph is even, and y=0 is its horizontal asymptote."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=1/(x-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find the domain and vertical asymptotes of f(|x|). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Rule is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(|x|-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Require |x| not equal 1, so x not equal -1 or 1.","[1] Both excluded locations give vertical asymptotes.","[1] The graph is even, and y=0 is its horizontal asymptote."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which part of y=f(x) is reflected to obtain y=|f(x)|?","o":["The part below the x-axis is reflected upward.","Only the right half is reflected left.","The entire graph is reflected in y=x.","All positive outputs become negative."],"a":0,"why":"Absolute value of the output changes negative y-values to their positives.","qHtml":"Which part of y=f(x) is reflected to obtain y=|f(x)|?","oHtml":["The part below the x-axis is reflected upward.","Only the right half is reflected left.","The entire graph is reflected in y=x.","All positive outputs become negative."],"whyHtml":"Absolute value of the output changes negative y-values to their positives."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-functions-reciprocal","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Functions and sketching graphs","title":"Reciprocal graphs, zeros and asymptotes","outcomes":["3.2.7"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Reciprocate outputs, preserve exclusions · Use landmarks rather than guessing · Asymptotes need limits · Organise the sketch","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Reciprocal graphs, zeros and asymptotes</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Reciprocate outputs, preserve exclusions</li><li>Use landmarks rather than guessing</li><li>Asymptotes need limits</li><li>Organise the sketch</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reciprocate outputs, preserve exclusions","text":"For y=1/f(x), each permitted nonzero output becomes its reciprocal. Zeros of f become excluded inputs and often vertical asymptotes. Inputs at which f was already undefined remain excluded, even if a simplified reciprocal looks defined there. Nonzero signs are preserved: positive outputs stay positive and negative outputs stay negative.","diagram":null,"html":"For y=1/f(x), each permitted nonzero output becomes its reciprocal. Zeros of f become excluded inputs and often vertical asymptotes. Inputs at which f was already undefined remain excluded, even if a simplified reciprocal looks defined there. Nonzero signs are preserved: positive outputs stay positive and negative outputs stay negative."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use landmarks rather than guessing","text":"Points with f(x)=1 or -1 are unchanged. Large positive or negative outputs move near zero, and outputs near zero become large in magnitude. If f tends to a nonzero finite value L, its reciprocal tends to 1/L. If |f| tends to infinity, the reciprocal tends to zero. A horizontal asymptote need not forbid crossing it at finite inputs.","diagram":"rational","html":"Points with f(x)=1 or -1 are unchanged. Large positive or negative outputs move near zero, and outputs near zero become large in magnitude. If f tends to a nonzero finite value L, its reciprocal tends to 1/L. If |f| tends to infinity, the reciprocal tends to zero. A horizontal asymptote need not forbid crossing it at finite inputs."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Asymptotes need limits","text":"A zero of f is a vertical asymptote of 1/f only when the behaviour approaches zero suitably from permitted nearby inputs. A discontinuity inherited from f may instead be a hole. Check algebra and original domains together. Signs near zeros determine the direction of each infinite branch. Repeated and simple zeros can produce different same-side or opposite-side behaviour.","diagram":null,"html":"A zero of f is a vertical asymptote of 1/f only when the behaviour approaches zero suitably from permitted nearby inputs. A discontinuity inherited from f may instead be a hole. Check algebra and original domains together. Signs near zeros determine the direction of each infinite branch. Repeated and simple zeros can produce different same-side or opposite-side behaviour."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Organise the sketch","text":"Find domain, sign intervals, intercepts if any, unchanged points, asymptotes and holes. A reciprocal cannot equal zero where it is defined. Derivative $-(f^{\\prime}(x))/(f(x)^2)$ can classify monotonicity when f is differentiable and nonzero. A graph and table should explain how outputs transform rather than merely display a new curve.","diagram":null,"html":"Find domain, sign intervals, intercepts if any, unchanged points, asymptotes and holes. A reciprocal cannot equal zero where it is defined. Derivative <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-(f^{\\prime}(x))/(f(x)^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> can classify monotonicity when f is differentiable and nonzero. A graph and table should explain how outputs transform rather than merely display a new curve."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-functions-reciprocal-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Sketch the key features of $y=1/(x-2)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Domain excludes 2; vertical asymptote x=2.","[1] Horizontal asymptote y=0, with no x-intercept.","[1] y-intercept is -1/2, and points (3,1),(1,-1) remain useful landmarks.","[1] The branch is negative for x<2 and positive for x>2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Sketch the key features of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=1/(x-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Domain excludes 2; vertical asymptote x=2.","[1] Horizontal asymptote y=0, with no x-intercept.","[1] y-intercept is -1/2, and points (3,1),(1,-1) remain useful landmarks.","[1] The branch is negative for x&lt;2 and positive for x&gt;2."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-reciprocal-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"f(x)=1/(x-1) on x not equal 1. Find 1/f(x) including its inherited restriction. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] f is never zero on its domain.","[1] The reciprocal simplifies to x-1.","[1] Original x=1 remains excluded.","[1] The graph is the line y=x-1 with a hole at (1,0), not a complete line."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"f(x)=1/(x-1) on x not equal 1. Find 1/f(x) including its inherited restriction. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] f is never zero on its domain.","[1] The reciprocal simplifies to x-1.","[1] Original x=1 remains excluded.","[1] The graph is the line y=x-1 with a hole at (1,0), not a complete line."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-reciprocal-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find domain and asymptotes of $y=1/(x^2-4)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Denominator zeros are x=-2,2, excluded.","[1] Both are vertical asymptotes.","[1] As |x| grows, the reciprocal tends to 0.","[1] Horizontal asymptote is y=0; the graph is even."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find domain and asymptotes of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=1/(x^2-4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Denominator zeros are x=-2,2, excluded.","[1] Both are vertical asymptotes.","[1] As |x| grows, the reciprocal tends to 0.","[1] Horizontal asymptote is y=0; the graph is even."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-reciprocal-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find all points unchanged when y=x+1 is transformed to its reciprocal graph. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Outputs are unchanged when u=1/u.","[1] Thus $u^2=1$, giving u=1 or -1.","[1] $x+1=1$ gives x=0; $x+1=-1$ gives x=-2.","[1] Unchanged points are (0,1) and (-2,-1)."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find all points unchanged when y=x+1 is transformed to its reciprocal graph. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Outputs are unchanged when u=1/u.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u^2=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving u=1 or -1.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+1=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives x=0; <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+1=-1</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives x=-2.","[1] Unchanged points are (0,1) and (-2,-1)."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-reciprocal-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Explain why $y=1/(x^2+1)$ has no vertical asymptotes and give its range. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $x^2+1\\ge1$ never equals zero.","[1] Domain is all real numbers and there are no real poles.","[1] Outputs satisfy $0<y\\le1$, maximum 1 at x=0.","[1] Range is (0,1], approaching but never reaching zero."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=1/(x^2+1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> has no vertical asymptotes and give its range. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+1\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math></span> never equals zero.","[1] Domain is all real numbers and there are no real poles.","[1] Outputs satisfy <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;y\\le1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, maximum 1 at x=0.","[1] Range is (0,1], approaching but never reaching zero."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-reciprocal-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"For $f(x)=2+1/x$ on x not equal 0, find the reciprocal and identify both excluded inputs. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Reciprocal simplifies to $x/(2x+1)$.","[1] f was undefined at x=0, so the simplified graph has a hole at (0,0).","[1] f is zero at x=-1/2, giving a vertical asymptote there.","[1] Horizontal asymptote is y=1/2; domain excludes 0 and -1/2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=2+1/x</annotation></semantics></math></span> on x not equal 0, find the reciprocal and identify both excluded inputs. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Reciprocal simplifies to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x/(2x+1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] f was undefined at x=0, so the simplified graph has a hole at (0,0).","[1] f is zero at x=-1/2, giving a vertical asymptote there.","[1] Horizontal asymptote is y=1/2; domain excludes 0 and -1/2."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Can a defined reciprocal 1/f(x) equal zero?","o":["No.","Yes, whenever f(x)=0.","Yes, whenever f(x)=1.","Always at the origin."],"a":0,"why":"A finite nonzero denominator cannot make a numerator of 1 equal zero.","qHtml":"Can a defined reciprocal 1/f(x) equal zero?","oHtml":["No.","Yes, whenever f(x)=0.","Yes, whenever f(x)=1.","Always at the origin."],"whyHtml":"A finite nonzero denominator cannot make a numerator of 1 equal zero."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-functions-rational","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Functions and sketching graphs","title":"Rational graphs, holes and asymptotes","outcomes":["3.2.8"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Start with the original denominator · Polynomial division gives end behaviour · Build an exact sign and feature map · Keep the graph and algebra consistent","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Rational graphs, holes and asymptotes</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Start with the original denominator</li><li>Polynomial division gives end behaviour</li><li>Build an exact sign and feature map</li><li>Keep the graph and algebra consistent</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Start with the original denominator","text":"A rational function is a quotient of polynomials. Its domain excludes every original denominator zero. Factor before cancelling. A cancelled factor can give a removable discontinuity, or hole, while uncancelled denominator zeros commonly give vertical asymptotes. Compute the limiting height for a hole using the simplified rule, but keep the input excluded.","diagram":null,"html":"A rational function is a quotient of polynomials. Its domain excludes every original denominator zero. Factor before cancelling. A cancelled factor can give a removable discontinuity, or hole, while uncancelled denominator zeros commonly give vertical asymptotes. Compute the limiting height for a hole using the simplified rule, but keep the input excluded."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Polynomial division gives end behaviour","text":"If numerator degree equals denominator degree, the horizontal asymptote is the ratio of leading coefficients. A smaller numerator degree gives y=0. When the numerator degree is one larger, divide to find an oblique asymptote. The remainder term tends to zero at large |x|. Higher-degree differences can give a polynomial asymptote rather than a straight line.","diagram":"rational","html":"If numerator degree equals denominator degree, the horizontal asymptote is the ratio of leading coefficients. A smaller numerator degree gives y=0. When the numerator degree is one larger, divide to find an oblique asymptote. The remainder term tends to zero at large |x|. Higher-degree differences can give a polynomial asymptote rather than a straight line."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Build an exact sign and feature map","text":"Find intercepts from allowed numerator zeros and evaluation at x=0 when permitted. Use denominator zeros and numerator zeros in a sign chart. Derivatives can locate turning points. Near a vertical asymptote, check each side separately rather than assuming both branches go the same way. An asymptote can be crossed away from its limiting region.","diagram":null,"html":"Find intercepts from allowed numerator zeros and evaluation at x=0 when permitted. Use denominator zeros and numerator zeros in a sign chart. Derivatives can locate turning points. Near a vertical asymptote, check each side separately rather than assuming both branches go the same way. An asymptote can be crossed away from its limiting region."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Keep the graph and algebra consistent","text":"Sketch holes as open circles and vertical asymptotes as guides. Label intercepts and any turning points. A calculator plot can omit a small hole or suggest a false joining line across a pole, so the algebra determines the domain. Distinguish the equation of a simplified curve from the full function with restrictions.","diagram":null,"html":"Sketch holes as open circles and vertical asymptotes as guides. Label intercepts and any turning points. A calculator plot can omit a small hole or suggest a false joining line across a pole, so the algebra determines the domain. Distinguish the equation of a simplified curve from the full function with restrictions."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Extended exam task","q":6},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-functions-rational-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Analyse $f(x)=(x^2-1)/(x-1)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Original domain excludes x=1.","[1] Factor and cancel to give x+1 for x not equal 1.","[1] The graph is a line with a hole at (1,2).","[1] There is no vertical asymptote at 1; its finite limiting height is 2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Analyse <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=(x^2-1)/(x-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Original domain excludes x=1.","[1] Factor and cancel to give x+1 for x not equal 1.","[1] The graph is a line with a hole at (1,2).","[1] There is no vertical asymptote at 1; its finite limiting height is 2."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-rational-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find the oblique asymptote and intercepts of $f(x)=(x^2+1)/(x-1)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Division gives $x+1+2/(x-1)$.","[1] Oblique asymptote is y=x+1 and vertical asymptote x=1.","[1] No real numerator zero exists, so no x-intercept.","[1] At x=0, y=-1, giving the y-intercept."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the oblique asymptote and intercepts of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=(x^2+1)/(x-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Division gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+1+2/(x-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Oblique asymptote is y=x+1 and vertical asymptote x=1.","[1] No real numerator zero exists, so no x-intercept.","[1] At x=0, y=-1, giving the y-intercept."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-rational-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find asymptotes and intercepts of $(2x+3)/(x-2)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Domain excludes 2 and x=2 is a vertical asymptote.","[1] Equal degrees give horizontal asymptote y=2.","[1] Numerator zero gives x=-3/2.","[1] y-intercept is -3/2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find asymptotes and intercepts of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2x+3)/(x-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Domain excludes 2 and x=2 is a vertical asymptote.","[1] Equal degrees give horizontal asymptote y=2.","[1] Numerator zero gives x=-3/2.","[1] y-intercept is -3/2."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-rational-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Analyse holes and poles of $(x^2-4)/(x^2-x-2)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Factor numerator $(x-2)(x+2)$ and denominator $(x-2)(x+1)$.","[1] Domain excludes 2 and -1; simplified rule is $(x+2)/(x+1)$.","[1] At x=2 the limiting value is 4/3, giving a hole.","[1] x=-1 is a vertical asymptote and y=1 a horizontal asymptote."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Analyse holes and poles of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x^2-4)/(x^2-x-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Factor numerator <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-2)(x+2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and denominator <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-2)(x+1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Domain excludes 2 and -1; simplified rule is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x+2)/(x+1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] At x=2 the limiting value is 4/3, giving a hole.","[1] x=-1 is a vertical asymptote and y=1 a horizontal asymptote."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-rational-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find stationary points of $f(x)=x+1+2/(x-1)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $f^{\\prime}(x)=1-2/(x-1)^2$.","[1] Set zero: $(x-1)^2=2$, so $x=1\\pm\\sqrt2$.","[1] At these points, y=$2\\pm2\\sqrt2$.","[1] Derivative changes + to - at the left point (local maximum) and - to + at the right point (local minimum)."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find stationary points of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x+1+2/(x-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{\\prime}(x)=1-2/(x-1)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Set zero: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-1)^2=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1\\pm\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] At these points, y=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pm2\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Derivative changes + to - at the left point (local maximum) and - to + at the right point (local minimum)."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-rational-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Can a rational graph cross a horizontal asymptote? Show using $f(x)=1+x/(x^2+1)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The added fraction tends to zero as |x| tends to infinity.","[1] Therefore y=1 is a horizontal asymptote.","[1] At x=0, f(0)=1.","[1] The added term changes sign across zero, so the graph crosses the asymptote there."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Can a rational graph cross a horizontal asymptote? Show using <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=1+x/(x^2+1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The added fraction tends to zero as |x| tends to infinity.","[1] Therefore y=1 is a horizontal asymptote.","[1] At x=0, f(0)=1.","[1] The added term changes sign across zero, so the graph crosses the asymptote there."]},{"prompt":"For $f(x)=(x^2-4)/(x^2-x-2)$, state the original domain, all intercepts, hole and asymptotes. Find its inverse function with the exact inverse domain, including the effect of the hole. (9 marks)","steps":["[1] Domain excludes x=-1 and x=2.","[1] Simplification gives $f(x)=(x+2)/(x+1)=1+1/(x+1)$ on the retained domain.","[1] Intercepts are (-2,0) and (0,2).","[2] Hole is (2,4/3), vertical asymptote x=-1 and horizontal asymptote y=1.","[2] Solve $y=(x+2)/(x+1)$ for x: $x=(2-y)/(y-1)$. Thus inverse rule is $(2-x)/(x-1)$.","[2] Original range excludes 1 and also 4/3, whose only original input would be 2. These form the inverse domain exclusions; inverse range excludes -1,2."],"id":"mas12-functions-rational-extended","title":"Extended exam task","allocations":[1,1,1,2,2,2],"marks":9,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed or exact working","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=(x^2-4)/(x^2-x-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, state the original domain, all intercepts, hole and asymptotes. Find its inverse function with the exact inverse domain, including the effect of the hole. (9 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Domain excludes x=-1 and x=2.","[1] Simplification gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=(x+2)/(x+1)=1+1/(x+1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> on the retained domain.","[1] Intercepts are (-2,0) and (0,2).","[2] Hole is (2,4/3), vertical asymptote x=-1 and horizontal asymptote y=1.","[2] Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=(x+2)/(x+1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for x: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(2-y)/(y-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Thus inverse rule is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2-x)/(x-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[2] Original range excludes 1 and also 4/3, whose only original input would be 2. These form the inverse domain exclusions; inverse range excludes -1,2."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What remains after cancelling an original denominator factor?","o":["Its zero remains excluded from the domain.","The excluded input is automatically restored.","Every cancellation creates a vertical asymptote.","The entire function becomes undefined."],"a":0,"why":"Simplification preserves values at admissible inputs but does not redefine the original function.","qHtml":"What remains after cancelling an original denominator factor?","oHtml":["Its zero remains excluded from the domain.","The excluded input is automatically restored.","Every cancellation creates a vertical asymptote.","The entire function becomes undefined."],"whyHtml":"Simplification preserves values at admissible inputs but does not redefine the original function."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 5 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-functions-parameters","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Functions and sketching graphs","title":"Reconstructing functions and interpreting parameters","outcomes":["3.2.8"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Read geometry into parameters · Build rational functions from factors · Use constraints and check uniqueness · Link algebra with the sketch","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Reconstructing functions and interpreting parameters</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Read geometry into parameters</li><li>Build rational functions from factors</li><li>Use constraints and check uniqueness</li><li>Link algebra with the sketch</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Read geometry into parameters","text":"In $y=a/(x-h)+k$, x=h is the vertical asymptote, y=k the horizontal asymptote and a determines branch signs and scale. A point on the graph determines a once h,k are known. A condition at an excluded input cannot be substituted as if it were an ordinary point. Explain how each feature translates into an equation.","diagram":null,"html":"In <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=a/(x-h)+k</annotation></semantics></math></span>, x=h is the vertical asymptote, y=k the horizontal asymptote and a determines branch signs and scale. A point on the graph determines a once h,k are known. A condition at an excluded input cannot be substituted as if it were an ordinary point. Explain how each feature translates into an equation."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Build rational functions from factors","text":"Specified zeros produce numerator factors, while poles produce denominator factors. Additional factors may be needed for degree or end behaviour. A hole requires a common factor in the original expression and an explicit excluded input. Preserve a leading scale constant and determine it from a further point. A list of intercepts alone may not uniquely determine the function.","diagram":"rational","html":"Specified zeros produce numerator factors, while poles produce denominator factors. Additional factors may be needed for degree or end behaviour. A hole requires a common factor in the original expression and an explicit excluded input. Preserve a leading scale constant and determine it from a further point. A list of intercepts alone may not uniquely determine the function."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use constraints and check uniqueness","text":"Several equations can determine parameters, but verify they are independent and compatible. An area, derivative or tangent condition can add another constraint. After solving, check all original features, denominator exclusions and any inequalities. Extraneous candidates may appear if an absolute-value condition was squared or if a forbidden parameter creates cancellation.","diagram":null,"html":"Several equations can determine parameters, but verify they are independent and compatible. An area, derivative or tangent condition can add another constraint. After solving, check all original features, denominator exclusions and any inequalities. Extraneous candidates may appear if an absolute-value condition was squared or if a forbidden parameter creates cancellation."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Link algebra with the sketch","text":"Write a concise feature table containing domain, intercepts, asymptotes and holes. Sketch branch signs and end behaviour using the reconstructed rule. Technology can check the curve but cannot establish uniqueness unless the assumptions on the function's form are stated. In exam answers, name the assumed family before finding its parameters.","diagram":null,"html":"Write a concise feature table containing domain, intercepts, asymptotes and holes. Sketch branch signs and end behaviour using the reconstructed rule. Technology can check the curve but cannot establish uniqueness unless the assumptions on the function&#39;s form are stated. In exam answers, name the assumed family before finding its parameters."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-functions-parameters-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"A function $f(x)=a/(x-h)+k$ has asymptotes x=2,y=-1 and passes through (3,4). Find it. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Asymptotes give h=2 and k=-1.","[1] Substitute the point: $4=a/(3-2)-1$.","[1] Hence a=5.","[1] $f(x)=5/(x-2)-1$, with x not equal 2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A function <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=a/(x-h)+k</annotation></semantics></math></span> has asymptotes x=2,y=-1 and passes through (3,4). Find it. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Asymptotes give h=2 and k=-1.","[1] Substitute the point: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4=a/(3-2)-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Hence a=5.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=5/(x-2)-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with x not equal 2."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-parameters-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"A rational function has form $a(x-1)/(x+2)$ and passes through (0,3). Find a and its asymptotes. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] At x=0, $3=-a/2$.","[1] Thus a=-6.","[1] Function is $-6(x-1)/(x+2)$; vertical asymptote x=-2.","[1] Equal degrees give horizontal asymptote y=-6."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A rational function has form <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a(x-1)/(x+2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and passes through (0,3). Find a and its asymptotes. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] At x=0, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3=-a/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus a=-6.","[1] Function is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-6(x-1)/(x+2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; vertical asymptote x=-2.","[1] Equal degrees give horizontal asymptote y=-6."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-parameters-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Construct a rational function with zeros -1,3, pole 2, and f(0)=6, assuming numerator degree 2 and denominator degree 1. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Set $f(x)=a(x+1)(x-3)/(x-2)$.","[1] At zero, $6=3a/2$.","[1] Thus a=4.","[1] Rule is $4(x+1)(x-3)/(x-2)$, domain excluding 2, with no cancellation."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Construct a rational function with zeros -1,3, pole 2, and f(0)=6, assuming numerator degree 2 and denominator degree 1. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Set <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=a(x+1)(x-3)/(x-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] At zero, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6=3a/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus a=4.","[1] Rule is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4(x+1)(x-3)/(x-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, domain excluding 2, with no cancellation."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-parameters-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Write a function whose graph is y=x+2 with a hole at x=1, using an original rational expression. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Introduce common factor x-1.","[1] $f(x)=(x+2)(x-1)/(x-1)$.","[1] Original denominator excludes x=1.","[1] The hole is at (1,3), while all other points lie on the line."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Write a function whose graph is y=x+2 with a hole at x=1, using an original rational expression. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Introduce common factor x-1.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=(x+2)(x-1)/(x-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Original denominator excludes x=1.","[1] The hole is at (1,3), while all other points lie on the line."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-parameters-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"For $f(x)=a/(x-1)+2$, the tangent at x=3 has slope -2. Find a and the tangent equation. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $f^{\\prime}(x)=-a/(x-1)^2$.","[1] $f^{\\prime}(3)=-a/4=-2$, so a=8.","[1] $f(3)=8/2+2=6$.","[1] Tangent is $y-6=-2(x-3)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=a/(x-1)+2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the tangent at x=3 has slope -2. Find a and the tangent equation. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{\\prime}(x)=-a/(x-1)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{\\prime}(3)=-a/4=-2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so a=8.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(3)=8/2+2=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Tangent is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y-6=-2(x-3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-functions-parameters-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Explain why zeros 1 and 2 alone do not uniquely determine a quadratic, and find the one satisfying f(0)=4. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The family is $f(x)=a(x-1)(x-2)$ with nonzero a.","[1] Different a values retain the same zeros.","[1] At x=0, $4=2a$, giving a=2.","[1] Thus the added condition selects $f(x)=2(x-1)(x-2)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why zeros 1 and 2 alone do not uniquely determine a quadratic, and find the one satisfying f(0)=4. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The family is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=a(x-1)(x-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with nonzero a.","[1] Different a values retain the same zeros.","[1] At x=0, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4=2a</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving a=2.","[1] Thus the added condition selects <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=2(x-1)(x-2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What additional information commonly determines a scale constant after factors are fixed?","o":["A valid point on the graph.","An excluded denominator zero alone.","The same zero stated twice.","The colour of the plotted curve."],"a":0,"why":"A permitted point supplies an equation involving the remaining scale parameter.","qHtml":"What additional information commonly determines a scale constant after factors are fixed?","oHtml":["A valid point on the graph.","An excluded denominator zero alone.","The same zero stated twice.","The colour of the plotted curve."],"whyHtml":"A permitted point supplies an equation involving the remaining scale parameter."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-space-vectors","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Vectors in three dimensions","title":"Three-dimensional components, magnitude and dot product","outcomes":["3.3.1"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Components in three dimensions · Magnitude and unit direction · Dot product and angle · Projection and interpretation","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Three-dimensional components, magnitude and dot product</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Components in three dimensions</li><li>Magnitude and unit direction</li><li>Dot product and angle</li><li>Projection and interpretation</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Components in three dimensions","text":"Write $v=x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j+z\\mathbf k=(x,y,z)$ using three mutually perpendicular unit directions. A position vector depends on the chosen origin; a displacement B-A does not. Add and scale components individually. Distinguish the coordinate named z from a complex number used in the earlier topic.","diagram":null,"html":"Write <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j+z\\mathbf k=(x,y,z)</annotation></semantics></math></span> using three mutually perpendicular unit directions. A position vector depends on the chosen origin; a displacement B-A does not. Add and scale components individually. Distinguish the coordinate named z from a complex number used in the earlier topic."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Magnitude and unit direction","text":"$|v|=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}$. For nonzero v, $\\hat v=v/|v|$ has magnitude 1 and the same direction. Zero has no unit direction. Parallel nonzero vectors are scalar multiples; test all three components with the same scalar, including zero components rather than dividing by them.","diagram":"vector-add","html":"<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|v|=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For nonzero v, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat v=v/|v|</annotation></semantics></math></span> has magnitude 1 and the same direction. Zero has no unit direction. Parallel nonzero vectors are scalar multiples; test all three components with the same scalar, including zero components rather than dividing by them."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Dot product and angle","text":"$u\\cdot v=u_xv_x+u_yv_y+u_zv_z=|u||v|\\cos\\theta$. The angle between nonzero vectors lies between 0 and pi. Perpendicularity means zero dot product. A zero vector has zero dot product with every vector but cannot define a geometric angle. The acute angle between lines may require the absolute dot product to remove orientation choices.","diagram":null,"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u\\cdot v=u_xv_x+u_yv_y+u_zv_z=|u||v|\\cos\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The angle between nonzero vectors lies between 0 and pi. Perpendicularity means zero dot product. A zero vector has zero dot product with every vector but cannot define a geometric angle. The acute angle between lines may require the absolute dot product to remove orientation choices."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Projection and interpretation","text":"The vector projection of u onto nonzero v is $(u\\cdot v)v/|v|^2$. Its residual is perpendicular to v. A signed scalar projection is $u\\cdot\\hat v$; a component length uses its absolute value. Check requested vector versus scalar and include contextual units for displacement or force.","diagram":null,"html":"The vector projection of u onto nonzero v is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>v</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(u\\cdot v)v/|v|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Its residual is perpendicular to v. A signed scalar projection is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u\\cdot\\hat v</annotation></semantics></math></span>; a component length uses its absolute value. Check requested vector versus scalar and include contextual units for displacement or force."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-space-vectors-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"For A=(1,2,-1), B=(4,-2,3), find AB and its unit direction. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $B-A=(3,-4,4)$.","[1] Magnitude is $\\sqrt{9+16+16}=\\sqrt{41}$.","[1] Unit vector is $(3,-4,4)/\\sqrt{41}$.","[1] Its magnitude is 1 and it points from A to B."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For A=(1,2,-1), B=(4,-2,3), find AB and its unit direction. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B-A=(3,-4,4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Magnitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>41</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{9+16+16}=\\sqrt{41}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Unit vector is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mn>41</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,-4,4)/\\sqrt{41}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its magnitude is 1 and it points from A to B."]},{"id":"mas12-space-vectors-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find the angle between u=(1,2,2), v=(2,1,-2). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Dot product is $2+2-4=0$.","[1] Both magnitudes are 3.","[1] $\\cos\\theta=0/9=0$.","[1] Thus angle is $\\pi/2$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the angle between u=(1,2,2), v=(2,1,-2). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Dot product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+2-4=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both magnitudes are 3.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos\\theta=0/9=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus angle is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-space-vectors-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find k so (2,k,-1) is perpendicular to (1,3,5). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Dot product is $2+3k-5$.","[1] Set $3k-3=0$.","[1] k=1.","[1] Substitution gives zero with both vectors nonzero."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find k so (2,k,-1) is perpendicular to (1,3,5). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Dot product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+3k-5</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Set <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3k-3=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] k=1.","[1] Substitution gives zero with both vectors nonzero."]},{"id":"mas12-space-vectors-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Project (2,1,3) onto (1,0,1). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Dot product is 5 and squared axis magnitude is 2.","[1] Projection is $(5/2)(1,0,1)$.","[1] Residual is $(-1/2,1,1/2)$.","[1] Residual dotted with (1,0,1) is zero."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Project (2,1,3) onto (1,0,1). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Dot product is 5 and squared axis magnitude is 2.","[1] Projection is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(5/2)(1,0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Residual is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1/2,1,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Residual dotted with (1,0,1) is zero."]},{"id":"mas12-space-vectors-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Are (2,-4,6) and (-1,2,-3) parallel? Compare their directions. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The second vector is -1/2 times the first.","[1] A common nonzero scalar establishes parallelism.","[1] The scalar is negative, so directions are opposite.","[1] Their angle as directed vectors is pi, although the associated lines have angle zero."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Are (2,-4,6) and (-1,2,-3) parallel? Compare their directions. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The second vector is -1/2 times the first.","[1] A common nonzero scalar establishes parallelism.","[1] The scalar is negative, so directions are opposite.","[1] Their angle as directed vectors is pi, although the associated lines have angle zero."]},{"id":"mas12-space-vectors-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Find the angle between (1,1,0) and (0,1,1), exactly. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Dot product is 1.","[1] Both magnitudes are $\\sqrt2$.","[1] $\\cos\\theta=1/2$.","[1] $\\theta=\\pi/3$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the angle between (1,1,0) and (0,1,1), exactly. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Dot product is 1.","[1] Both magnitudes are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos\\theta=1/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which vector has no unit direction?","o":["The zero vector.","Any vector with one zero component.","Any vector with a negative component.","Every vector in three dimensions."],"a":0,"why":"Normalising requires division by a nonzero magnitude.","qHtml":"Which vector has no unit direction?","oHtml":["The zero vector.","Any vector with one zero component.","Any vector with a negative component.","Every vector in three dimensions."],"whyHtml":"Normalising requires division by a nonzero magnitude."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-space-proofs","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Vectors in three dimensions","title":"Geometric proofs with vector notation","outcomes":["3.3.2"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Use arbitrary position vectors · Parallelism and collinearity · Midpoint and diagonal arguments · Write a connected conclusion","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Geometric proofs with vector notation</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Use arbitrary position vectors</li><li>Parallelism and collinearity</li><li>Midpoint and diagonal arguments</li><li>Write a connected conclusion</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use arbitrary position vectors","text":"A vector proof should represent general vertices by a,b,c rather than choose one convenient numerical shape. Displacements such as b-a remove origin dependence. Midpoints are averages and internal division points are weighted sums. State the geometry supplied, such as noncollinearity, before using a vector equation to infer a nondegenerate shape.","diagram":null,"html":"A vector proof should represent general vertices by a,b,c rather than choose one convenient numerical shape. Displacements such as b-a remove origin dependence. Midpoints are averages and internal division points are weighted sums. State the geometry supplied, such as noncollinearity, before using a vector equation to infer a nondegenerate shape."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Parallelism and collinearity","text":"To prove two nonzero directions parallel, show one is a scalar multiple of the other. To prove three distinct points collinear, show their displacement vectors are dependent. A vector sum equal to zero can describe a closed path; it does not on its own make each vector zero. Independence must be explicitly established before equating coefficients.","diagram":"logic","html":"To prove two nonzero directions parallel, show one is a scalar multiple of the other. To prove three distinct points collinear, show their displacement vectors are dependent. A vector sum equal to zero can describe a closed path; it does not on its own make each vector zero. Independence must be explicitly established before equating coefficients."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Midpoint and diagonal arguments","text":"For any quadrilateral, joining adjacent side midpoints gives a parallelogram because each midpoint-side vector is half a corresponding diagonal. For a parallelogram, a+c=b+d expresses diagonal bisection. Equal or perpendicular diagonals can add rectangle or rhombus properties only with the appropriate parallelogram assumption.","diagram":null,"html":"For any quadrilateral, joining adjacent side midpoints gives a parallelogram because each midpoint-side vector is half a corresponding diagonal. For a parallelogram, a+c=b+d expresses diagonal bisection. Equal or perpendicular diagonals can add rectangle or rhombus properties only with the appropriate parallelogram assumption."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Write a connected conclusion","text":"Use algebra to establish the exact required property, then explain its geometric meaning. Dot products prove right angles; magnitudes prove equal lengths. Numerical checking can illustrate the theorem but cannot prove it for arbitrary positions. Include nonzero directions wherever an angle or parallel-line claim requires them.","diagram":null,"html":"Use algebra to establish the exact required property, then explain its geometric meaning. Dot products prove right angles; magnitudes prove equal lengths. Numerical checking can illustrate the theorem but cannot prove it for arbitrary positions. Include nonzero directions wherever an angle or parallel-line claim requires them."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-space-proofs-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Prove the segment joining midpoints of AB and AC is parallel to BC and half its length. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Midpoints are $m=(a+b)/2$, $n=(a+c)/2$.","[1] $n-m=(c-b)/2$.","[1] This is half the vector BC, so the directions are parallel.","[1] Its magnitude is half |BC| for distinct B,C."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove the segment joining midpoints of AB and AC is parallel to BC and half its length. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Midpoints are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=(a+b)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=(a+c)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n-m=(c-b)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This is half the vector BC, so the directions are parallel.","[1] Its magnitude is half |BC| for distinct B,C."]},{"id":"mas12-space-proofs-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Prove the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use vertices a, a+u, a+u+v, a+v.","[1] Midpoint of the first diagonal is $a+(u+v)/2$.","[1] Midpoint of the other is the same vector.","[1] The shared midpoint proves both diagonals are bisected."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use vertices a, a+u, a+u+v, a+v.","[1] Midpoint of the first diagonal is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a+(u+v)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Midpoint of the other is the same vector.","[1] The shared midpoint proves both diagonals are bisected."]},{"id":"mas12-space-proofs-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Prove adjacent-side midpoints of a nondegenerate quadrilateral form a parallelogram. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let consecutive midpoints be $(a+b)/2,(b+c)/2,(c+d)/2,(d+a)/2$.","[1] The first and third side vectors are $(c-a)/2$ and its negative.","[1] The second and fourth are $(d-b)/2$ and its negative.","[1] Both pairs of opposite sides are parallel and equal, giving a parallelogram when the midpoint figure is nondegenerate."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove adjacent-side midpoints of a nondegenerate quadrilateral form a parallelogram. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let consecutive midpoints be <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(a+b)/2,(b+c)/2,(c+d)/2,(d+a)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The first and third side vectors are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(c-a)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and its negative.","[1] The second and fourth are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(d-b)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and its negative.","[1] Both pairs of opposite sides are parallel and equal, giving a parallelogram when the midpoint figure is nondegenerate."]},{"id":"mas12-space-proofs-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"A parallelogram has side vectors u,v. Prove equal diagonals imply perpendicular adjacent sides. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Diagonals have vectors u+v and u-v.","[1] Equality of lengths gives $|u+v|^2=|u-v|^2$.","[1] Expanding gives $4u\\cdot v=0$.","[1] Nonzero adjacent sides are perpendicular, so the parallelogram is a rectangle."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A parallelogram has side vectors u,v. Prove equal diagonals imply perpendicular adjacent sides. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Diagonals have vectors u+v and u-v.","[1] Equality of lengths gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|u+v|^2=|u-v|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Expanding gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>u</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4u\\cdot v=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Nonzero adjacent sides are perpendicular, so the parallelogram is a rectangle."]},{"id":"mas12-space-proofs-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Prove perpendicular diagonals of a nondegenerate parallelogram imply equal adjacent side lengths. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Diagonal dot product is $(u+v)\\cdot(u-v)$.","[1] Cross terms cancel, leaving $|u|^2-|v|^2$.","[1] Perpendicularity makes this zero, so |u|=|v|.","[1] The parallelogram has all sides equal and is a rhombus."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove perpendicular diagonals of a nondegenerate parallelogram imply equal adjacent side lengths. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Diagonal dot product is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(u+v)\\cdot(u-v)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Cross terms cancel, leaving <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>u</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>v</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|u|^2-|v|^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Perpendicularity makes this zero, so |u|=|v|.","[1] The parallelogram has all sides equal and is a rhombus."]},{"id":"mas12-space-proofs-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"If u,v are independent and $2u+kv=mu+3v$, find m,k and explain the hypothesis. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Rearrange: $(2-m)u+(k-3)v=0$.","[1] Independence means the only zero linear combination has both coefficients zero.","[1] Thus m=2 and k=3.","[1] Without independence, different coefficients could describe the same vector."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"If u,v are independent and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2u+kv=mu+3v</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find m,k and explain the hypothesis. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Rearrange: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2-m)u+(k-3)v=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Independence means the only zero linear combination has both coefficients zero.","[1] Thus m=2 and k=3.","[1] Without independence, different coefficients could describe the same vector."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What makes a proof general rather than one numerical check?","o":["It uses arbitrary objects satisfying the stated hypotheses.","It uses a graph drawn to scale.","It uses three selected examples.","It omits the assumptions."],"a":0,"why":"General vector expressions cover every eligible configuration.","qHtml":"What makes a proof general rather than one numerical check?","oHtml":["It uses arbitrary objects satisfying the stated hypotheses.","It uses a graph drawn to scale.","It uses three selected examples.","It omits the assumptions."],"whyHtml":"General vector expressions cover every eligible configuration."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-space-spheres","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Vectors in three dimensions","title":"Coordinates, distance and sphere equations","outcomes":["3.3.3"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Distance extends Pythagoras · Sphere equation · Intersect with planes or lines · Normals and geometric checks","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Coordinates, distance and sphere equations</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Distance extends Pythagoras</li><li>Sphere equation</li><li>Intersect with planes or lines</li><li>Normals and geometric checks</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distance extends Pythagoras","text":"The distance between A and B is $\\sqrt{(b_x-a_x)^2+(b_y-a_y)^2+(b_z-a_z)^2}$. The midpoint averages all three coordinates. An equation for equal distances from two distinct points becomes a plane, the perpendicular-bisector plane, rather than the two-dimensional line from earlier work.","diagram":null,"html":"The distance between A and B is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{(b_x-a_x)^2+(b_y-a_y)^2+(b_z-a_z)^2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The midpoint averages all three coordinates. An equation for equal distances from two distinct points becomes a plane, the perpendicular-bisector plane, rather than the two-dimensional line from earlier work."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Sphere equation","text":"A sphere with centre C=(a,b,c) and radius r>0 has equation $(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2+(z-c)^2=r^2$. Expand or complete squares in all three coordinates to convert forms. A negative squared radius describes no real points; zero describes a single point, not a positive-radius sphere.","diagram":"sphere","html":"A sphere with centre C=(a,b,c) and radius r&gt;0 has equation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2+(z-c)^2=r^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Expand or complete squares in all three coordinates to convert forms. A negative squared radius describes no real points; zero describes a single point, not a positive-radius sphere."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Intersect with planes or lines","text":"Substitute a plane coordinate such as z=k to obtain a circle, point or no intersection depending on its distance from the centre. A general line substitution produces a quadratic in its parameter. Two roots indicate a secant, a repeated root tangency and no real roots no meeting. Confirm parameter restrictions if only a segment is considered.","diagram":null,"html":"Substitute a plane coordinate such as z=k to obtain a circle, point or no intersection depending on its distance from the centre. A general line substitution produces a quadratic in its parameter. Two roots indicate a secant, a repeated root tangency and no real roots no meeting. Confirm parameter restrictions if only a segment is considered."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Normals and geometric checks","text":"At a sphere point P, the radius P-C is normal to the tangent plane. Use it to build $(P-C)\\cdot(X-P)=0$. Check P lies on the sphere before using this equation. Coordinate calculations should end with the requested geometric description and units.","diagram":null,"html":"At a sphere point P, the radius P-C is normal to the tangent plane. Use it to build <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(P-C)\\cdot(X-P)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Check P lies on the sphere before using this equation. Coordinate calculations should end with the requested geometric description and units."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-space-spheres-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find centre and radius of $x^2+y^2+z^2-4x+6y-2z-11=0$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Complete squares in x,y,z.","[1] $(x-2)^2+(y+3)^2+(z-1)^2=25$.","[1] Centre is (2,-3,1).","[1] Radius is 5."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find centre and radius of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2+z^2-4x+6y-2z-11=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Complete squares in x,y,z.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-2)^2+(y+3)^2+(z-1)^2=25</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Centre is (2,-3,1).","[1] Radius is 5."]},{"id":"mas12-space-spheres-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find the intersection of $x^2+y^2+z^2=25$ with z=3. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Substitute z=3.","[1] $x^2+y^2=16$.","[1] It is a circle in the plane z=3.","[1] Centre (0,0,3), radius 4."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the intersection of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2+z^2=25</annotation></semantics></math></span> with z=3. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Substitute z=3.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2=16</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] It is a circle in the plane z=3.","[1] Centre (0,0,3), radius 4."]},{"id":"mas12-space-spheres-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find the sphere with diameter endpoints (1,2,3) and (5,2,-1). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Centre is midpoint (3,2,1).","[1] Endpoint displacement is (4,0,-4), length $4\\sqrt2$.","[1] Radius is half, $2\\sqrt2$.","[1] Equation is $(x-3)^2+(y-2)^2+(z-1)^2=8$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the sphere with diameter endpoints (1,2,3) and (5,2,-1). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Centre is midpoint (3,2,1).","[1] Endpoint displacement is (4,0,-4), length <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Radius is half, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Equation is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-3)^2+(y-2)^2+(z-1)^2=8</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-space-spheres-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find the perpendicular-bisector plane of (0,0,0) and (2,4,6). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Equate squared distances: $x^2+y^2+z^2=(x-2)^2+(y-4)^2+(z-6)^2$.","[1] Cancel squares to give $4x+8y+12z=56$.","[1] Plane is $x+2y+3z=14$.","[1] It passes through midpoint (1,2,3) with normal parallel to the endpoint displacement."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the perpendicular-bisector plane of (0,0,0) and (2,4,6). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Equate squared distances: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2+z^2=(x-2)^2+(y-4)^2+(z-6)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Cancel squares to give <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>56</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4x+8y+12z=56</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Plane is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+2y+3z=14</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] It passes through midpoint (1,2,3) with normal parallel to the endpoint displacement."]},{"id":"mas12-space-spheres-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find the tangent plane to $x^2+y^2+z^2=9$ at (1,2,2). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The point lies on the sphere because $1+4+4=9$.","[1] Radius normal is (1,2,2).","[1] Plane equation is $(x-1)+2(y-2)+2(z-2)=0$.","[1] Thus $x+2y+2z=9$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the tangent plane to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2+z^2=9</annotation></semantics></math></span> at (1,2,2). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The point lies on the sphere because <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+4+4=9</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Radius normal is (1,2,2).","[1] Plane equation is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-1)+2(y-2)+2(z-2)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+2y+2z=9</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-space-spheres-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Classify the intersections of the radius-5 sphere centred at the origin with z=5 and z=6. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] At z=5, $x^2+y^2=0$.","[1] The intersection is the single point (0,0,5), a tangent plane contact.","[1] At z=6, $x^2+y^2=-11$.","[1] No real points exist, so there is no intersection."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Classify the intersections of the radius-5 sphere centred at the origin with z=5 and z=6. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] At z=5, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The intersection is the single point (0,0,5), a tangent plane contact.","[1] At z=6, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2=-11</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] No real points exist, so there is no intersection."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What locus is equidistant from two distinct points in three dimensions?","o":["Their perpendicular-bisector plane.","Always a sphere centred at the origin.","A single point only.","The line joining the points."],"a":0,"why":"Expanding equality of squared distances produces a plane normal to their displacement.","qHtml":"What locus is equidistant from two distinct points in three dimensions?","oHtml":["Their perpendicular-bisector plane.","Always a sphere centred at the origin.","A single point only.","The line joining the points."],"whyHtml":"Expanding equality of squared distances produces a plane normal to their displacement."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-space-curves","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Vectors in three dimensions","title":"Parametric curves and Cartesian conics","outcomes":["3.3.4","3.3.12"],"icon":"📐","scope":"A parameter generates coordinates · Trigonometric parameters and conics · Gradients and singular parameters · Preserve range and interpretation · Hyperbolas require their own identity and branch checks","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Parametric curves and Cartesian conics</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>A parameter generates coordinates</li><li>Trigonometric parameters and conics</li><li>Gradients and singular parameters</li><li>Preserve range and interpretation</li><li>Hyperbolas require their own identity and branch checks</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"A parameter generates coordinates","text":"A parametric curve gives x,y and possibly z as functions of t. Its parameter interval determines the portion and direction traced. Eliminating t can produce a Cartesian equation but may lose an interval restriction or the orientation. A full conic equation may contain points the given parameter never reaches.","diagram":null,"html":"A parametric curve gives x,y and possibly z as functions of t. Its parameter interval determines the portion and direction traced. Eliminating t can produce a Cartesian equation but may lose an interval restriction or the orientation. A full conic equation may contain points the given parameter never reaches."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Trigonometric parameters and conics","text":"For x=a cos t, y=b sin t, the identity $\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t=1$ gives $x^2/a^2+y^2/b^2=1$ when a,b are nonzero. A full interval of length $2\\pi$ traces an ellipse; a shorter interval may trace an arc. Different parameter speeds can trace the same geometric curve with different motion.","diagram":"parametric","html":"For x=a cos t, y=b sin t, the identity <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2/a^2+y^2/b^2=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> when a,b are nonzero. A full interval of length <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> traces an ellipse; a shorter interval may trace an arc. Different parameter speeds can trace the same geometric curve with different motion."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Gradients and singular parameters","text":"Where dx/dt is nonzero, $dy/dx=(dy/dt)/(dx/dt)$. A vertical tangent may occur where dx/dt=0 but dy/dt is nonzero. If both vanish, further analysis is required; division by zero does not classify the curve. For space curves, position vectors can describe nonplanar paths without a single y(x) representation.","diagram":null,"html":"Where dx/dt is nonzero, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dy/dx=(dy/dt)/(dx/dt)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A vertical tangent may occur where dx/dt=0 but dy/dt is nonzero. If both vanish, further analysis is required; division by zero does not classify the curve. For space curves, position vectors can describe nonplanar paths without a single y(x) representation."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Preserve range and interpretation","text":"Use parameter values to mark endpoints, direction arrows and repeated visits. A space curve such as a helix combines circular horizontal motion with a changing vertical coordinate. A sketch is a projection and may distort distances and angles. Explain which features come from the exact coordinates.","diagram":null,"html":"Use parameter values to mark endpoints, direction arrows and repeated visits. A space curve such as a helix combines circular horizontal motion with a changing vertical coordinate. A sketch is a projection and may distort distances and angles. Explain which features come from the exact coordinates."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Hyperbolas require their own identity and branch checks","text":"For $x=a\\sec t$ and $y=b\\tan t$, with nonzero a,b and $\\cos t\\ne0$, the identity $\\sec^2t-\\tan^2t=1$ yields $x^2/a^2-y^2/b^2=1$. If a>0 and $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$, cosine is positive and only the right branch $x\\ge a$ is traced. A broader eligible parameter set can trace both branches, but the pole values remain excluded. The asymptotes are $y=\\pm(b/a)x$. Alternatively, $x=a\\sqrt{1+t^2}$, $y=bt$ with all real t gives the full right branch; negate x to obtain the left. Eliminating a parameter must preserve these branch restrictions.","diagram":null,"html":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=a\\sec t</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=b\\tan t</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with nonzero a,b and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>t</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos t\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the identity <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sec^2t-\\tan^2t=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> yields <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2/a^2-y^2/b^2=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. If a&gt;0 and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\pi/2&lt;t&lt;\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, cosine is positive and only the right branch <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge a</annotation></semantics></math></span> is traced. A broader eligible parameter set can trace both branches, but the pole values remain excluded. The asymptotes are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=\\pm(b/a)x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Alternatively, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=a\\sqrt{1+t^2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=bt</annotation></semantics></math></span> with all real t gives the full right branch; negate x to obtain the left. Eliminating a parameter must preserve these branch restrictions."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-space-curves-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Eliminate t from x=3 cos t,y=2 sin t for $0\\le t<2\\pi$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $\\cos t=x/3$, $\\sin t=y/2$.","[1] Apply the Pythagorean identity.","[1] $x^2/9+y^2/4=1$.","[1] The full ellipse is traced once anticlockwise from (3,0)."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Eliminate t from x=3 cos t,y=2 sin t for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le t&lt;2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos t=x/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin t=y/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Apply the Pythagorean identity.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2/9+y^2/4=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The full ellipse is traced once anticlockwise from (3,0)."]},{"id":"mas12-space-curves-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"For x=t,y=$t^2$ on -1<=t<=2, find the Cartesian curve and endpoints. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] t=x, so y=$x^2$.","[1] The parameter restricts x to [-1,2].","[1] Endpoints are (-1,1) and (2,4).","[1] Increasing t moves from left to right, not around the entire parabola."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For x=t,y=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> on -1&lt;=t&lt;=2, find the Cartesian curve and endpoints. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] t=x, so y=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The parameter restricts x to [-1,2].","[1] Endpoints are (-1,1) and (2,4).","[1] Increasing t moves from left to right, not around the entire parabola."]},{"id":"mas12-space-curves-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"For x=2 cos t,y=2 sin t, find dy/dx at t=pi/4. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $dx/dt=-2\\sin t$, $dy/dt=2\\cos t$.","[1] Their ratio is $-\\cot t$ when sin t is nonzero.","[1] At pi/4, slope is -1.","[1] Point is $(\\sqrt2,\\sqrt2)$, giving the tangent location."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For x=2 cos t,y=2 sin t, find dy/dx at t=pi/4. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dx/dt=-2\\sin t</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dy/dt=2\\cos t</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Their ratio is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>cot</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\cot t</annotation></semantics></math></span> when sin t is nonzero.","[1] At pi/4, slope is -1.","[1] Point is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\sqrt2,\\sqrt2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving the tangent location."]},{"id":"mas12-space-curves-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Eliminate t from x=$t^2$,y=2t and state the portion for t>=0. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] $t=y/2$.","[1] $x=y^2/4$, or $y^2=4x$.","[1] t>=0 requires y>=0.","[1] Only the upper half of the right-opening parabola is traced."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Eliminate t from x=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>,y=2t and state the portion for t&gt;=0. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=y/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=y^2/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>, or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^2=4x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] t&gt;=0 requires y&gt;=0.","[1] Only the upper half of the right-opening parabola is traced."]},{"id":"mas12-space-curves-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Describe r(t)=(cos t,sin t,t), $0\\le t\\le4\\pi$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Horizontal coordinates satisfy $x^2+y^2=1$.","[1] z=t increases uniformly.","[1] Two full horizontal turns occur over the interval.","[1] The path is a helix on the unit cylinder, rising from z=0 to z=4pi."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Describe r(t)=(cos t,sin t,t), <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le t\\le4\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Horizontal coordinates satisfy <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] z=t increases uniformly.","[1] Two full horizontal turns occur over the interval.","[1] The path is a helix on the unit cylinder, rising from z=0 to z=4pi."]},{"id":"mas12-space-curves-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"For $x=2\\sec t$, $y=3\\tan t$, $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$, derive the Cartesian equation, eligible branch and asymptotes. (5 marks)","steps":["[1] $x^2/4-y^2/9=\\sec^2t-\\tan^2t=1$.","[1] Cosine is positive on the interval, so $x\\ge2$ and only the right branch is traced.","[1] Tan t ranges over all real values; this covers the full right branch.","[2] Asymptotes are $y=\\pm(3/2)x$. Increasing t moves upward from negative y through vertex (2,0) to positive y."],"allocations":[1,1,1,2],"marks":5,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2\\sec t</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=3\\tan t</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\pi/2&lt;t&lt;\\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, derive the Cartesian equation, eligible branch and asymptotes. (5 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2/4-y^2/9=\\sec^2t-\\tan^2t=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Cosine is positive on the interval, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and only the right branch is traced.","[1] Tan t ranges over all real values; this covers the full right branch.","[2] Asymptotes are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=\\pm(3/2)x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Increasing t moves upward from negative y through vertex (2,0) to positive y."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What can be lost when eliminating a parameter?","o":["The interval restriction and direction of travel.","The existence of any coordinates.","Every point on the curve.","The ability to use algebra."],"a":0,"why":"The Cartesian equation alone may describe a larger locus without traversal information.","qHtml":"What can be lost when eliminating a parameter?","oHtml":["The interval restriction and direction of travel.","The existence of any coordinates.","Every point on the curve.","The ability to use algebra."],"whyHtml":"The Cartesian equation alone may describe a larger locus without traversal information."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-space-lines","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Vectors in three dimensions","title":"Vector equations of lines and line segments","outcomes":["3.3.5"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Point plus direction · Convert to components · Line relationships in space · Angles and distances","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Vector equations of lines and line segments</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Point plus direction</li><li>Convert to components</li><li>Line relationships in space</li><li>Angles and distances</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Point plus direction","text":"A line has vector equation $r=a+td$ for real t, where a is one position on the line and d is a nonzero direction. Different anchor points or nonzero multiples of d can describe the same line. A segment AB uses $r=a+t(b-a)$ with $0\\le t\\le1$. A ray needs its own parameter restriction.","diagram":null,"html":"A line has vector equation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=a+td</annotation></semantics></math></span> for real t, where a is one position on the line and d is a nonzero direction. Different anchor points or nonzero multiples of d can describe the same line. A segment AB uses <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=a+t(b-a)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le t\\le1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A ray needs its own parameter restriction."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Convert to components","text":"The vector equation gives x=a_x+td_x and similarly for y,z. Symmetric form divides by nonzero direction components; a zero component instead gives a fixed coordinate. Never write division by zero to force a three-fraction format. To check membership, solve for one parameter and verify all component equations.","diagram":"plane","html":"The vector equation gives x=a_x+td_x and similarly for y,z. Symmetric form divides by nonzero direction components; a zero component instead gives a fixed coordinate. Never write division by zero to force a three-fraction format. To check membership, solve for one parameter and verify all component equations."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Line relationships in space","text":"Parallel directions do not by themselves establish the same line; check an anchor displacement. Nonparallel lines may intersect or be skew. Solve $a+td=b+se$ using independent parameters and all three component equations. A solution to only two components can be inconsistent with the third.","diagram":null,"html":"Parallel directions do not by themselves establish the same line; check an anchor displacement. Nonparallel lines may intersect or be skew. Solve <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a+td=b+se</annotation></semantics></math></span> using independent parameters and all three component equations. A solution to only two components can be inconsistent with the third."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Angles and distances","text":"For an acute angle between lines, use $\\cos\\theta=|d\\cdot e|/(|d||e|)$. The shortest displacement from a point P to a line is the residual after projecting P-a onto d. Express the closest point and check perpendicularity. Both tasks depend on nonzero directions.","diagram":null,"html":"For an acute angle between lines, use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos\\theta=|d\\cdot e|/(|d||e|)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The shortest displacement from a point P to a line is the residual after projecting P-a onto d. Express the closest point and check perpendicularity. Both tasks depend on nonzero directions."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-space-lines-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find a line and segment equation through A=(1,0,2), B=(3,4,1). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Direction is B-A=(2,4,-1).","[1] Line is r=(1,0,2)+t(2,4,-1), t real.","[1] For the segment use the same equation with $0\\le t\\le1$.","[1] t=0 and t=1 give the two endpoints."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find a line and segment equation through A=(1,0,2), B=(3,4,1). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Direction is B-A=(2,4,-1).","[1] Line is r=(1,0,2)+t(2,4,-1), t real.","[1] For the segment use the same equation with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le t\\le1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] t=0 and t=1 give the two endpoints."]},{"id":"mas12-space-lines-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Is P=(5,8,0) on that line? Is it on the segment? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] x equation gives $1+2t=5$, so t=2.","[1] y=4t=8 and z=2-t=0 both check.","[1] P lies on the line.","[1] It is outside the segment because t=2 is not in [0,1]."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Is P=(5,8,0) on that line? Is it on the segment? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] x equation gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+2t=5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so t=2.","[1] y=4t=8 and z=2-t=0 both check.","[1] P lies on the line.","[1] It is outside the segment because t=2 is not in [0,1]."]},{"id":"mas12-space-lines-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Write component and symmetric forms of r=(2,1,-3)+t(0,2,4). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Components are x=2,y=1+2t,z=-3+4t.","[1] Nonzero components give $(y-1)/2=(z+3)/4$.","[1] Include the separate condition x=2.","[1] Do not divide x-2 by the zero direction component."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Write component and symmetric forms of r=(2,1,-3)+t(0,2,4). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Components are x=2,y=1+2t,z=-3+4t.","[1] Nonzero components give <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(y-1)/2=(z+3)/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Include the separate condition x=2.","[1] Do not divide x-2 by the zero direction component."]},{"id":"mas12-space-lines-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find the closest point to P=(2,3,1) on the line r=t(1,0,0). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Projection of P onto direction (1,0,0) is (2,0,0).","[1] Closest point is Q=(2,0,0).","[1] Residual P-Q=(0,3,1) is perpendicular to the line.","[1] Distance is $\\sqrt{10}$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the closest point to P=(2,3,1) on the line r=t(1,0,0). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Projection of P onto direction (1,0,0) is (2,0,0).","[1] Closest point is Q=(2,0,0).","[1] Residual P-Q=(0,3,1) is perpendicular to the line.","[1] Distance is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>10</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{10}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-space-lines-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find the acute angle between lines with directions (1,1,0) and (1,0,1). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Dot product is 1.","[1] Each magnitude is $\\sqrt2$.","[1] Acute-angle cosine is 1/2.","[1] Angle is pi/3."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the acute angle between lines with directions (1,1,0) and (1,0,1). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Dot product is 1.","[1] Each magnitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Acute-angle cosine is 1/2.","[1] Angle is pi/3."]},{"id":"mas12-space-lines-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Are r=(1,2,3)+t(1,0,1) and s=(2,2,4)+u(2,0,2) the same line? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Directions are parallel because the second is twice the first.","[1] Anchor difference is (1,0,1), also parallel to the direction.","[1] The second anchor lies on the first line at t=1.","[1] They describe the same line with different parameters."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Are r=(1,2,3)+t(1,0,1) and s=(2,2,4)+u(2,0,2) the same line? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Directions are parallel because the second is twice the first.","[1] Anchor difference is (1,0,1), also parallel to the direction.","[1] The second anchor lies on the first line at t=1.","[1] They describe the same line with different parameters."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What extra test distinguishes coincident lines from distinct parallel lines?","o":["Whether an anchor point of one lies on the other.","Whether both use the same parameter letter.","Whether both direction vectors have unit length.","Whether their x coordinates are always positive."],"a":0,"why":"Parallel directions allow either coincident or displaced lines.","qHtml":"What extra test distinguishes coincident lines from distinct parallel lines?","oHtml":["Whether an anchor point of one lies on the other.","Whether both use the same parameter letter.","Whether both direction vectors have unit length.","Whether their x coordinates are always positive."],"whyHtml":"Parallel directions allow either coincident or displaced lines."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-space-meetings","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Vectors in three dimensions","title":"Intersecting paths versus simultaneous meetings","outcomes":["3.3.6"],"icon":"📐","scope":"A path intersection is not a collision · Solve every coordinate · Relative position measures separation · Interpret the conclusion","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Intersecting paths versus simultaneous meetings</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>A path intersection is not a collision</li><li>Solve every coordinate</li><li>Relative position measures separation</li><li>Interpret the conclusion</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"A path intersection is not a collision","text":"Two parametric paths may pass through the same location at different times. To find geometric intersections, assign independent parameters t,s and solve the three coordinate equalities. To test a simultaneous meeting when both parameters represent the same clock, use one common time. State time origins and units before comparing them.","diagram":null,"html":"Two parametric paths may pass through the same location at different times. To find geometric intersections, assign independent parameters t,s and solve the three coordinate equalities. To test a simultaneous meeting when both parameters represent the same clock, use one common time. State time origins and units before comparing them."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Solve every coordinate","text":"In space, matching two coordinates does not guarantee the third. Substitute candidate parameters into all three equations and respect their time intervals. If directions are not parallel but no common point exists, straight paths can be skew. An intersection outside the allowed time intervals is not a meeting during the modelled journey.","diagram":"meeting","html":"In space, matching two coordinates does not guarantee the third. Substitute candidate parameters into all three equations and respect their time intervals. If directions are not parallel but no common point exists, straight paths can be skew. An intersection outside the allowed time intervals is not a meeting during the modelled journey."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Relative position measures separation","text":"For simultaneous time t, separation vector is $r_1(t)-r_2(t)$. Its magnitude gives distance. Minimise its squared magnitude to avoid a square root; for constant velocities it is a quadratic. Check endpoints when the unconstrained minimum lies outside the time interval. A positive minimum means no collision.","diagram":null,"html":"For simultaneous time t, separation vector is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_1(t)-r_2(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Its magnitude gives distance. Minimise its squared magnitude to avoid a square root; for constant velocities it is a quadratic. Check endpoints when the unconstrained minimum lies outside the time interval. A positive minimum means no collision."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret the conclusion","text":"Report location and both path times for an intersection, or one common time and location for a collision. Include a closest-approach distance and units if requested. Point particles ignore object size; whether physical bodies touch needs extra dimensions. Mathematical paths alone do not encode which point was occupied at a particular time.","diagram":null,"html":"Report location and both path times for an intersection, or one common time and location for a collision. Include a closest-approach distance and units if requested. Point particles ignore object size; whether physical bodies touch needs extra dimensions. Mathematical paths alone do not encode which point was occupied at a particular time."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-space-meetings-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"A has r(t)=(t,0,0), B has s(u)=(1,u-2,0), for nonnegative parameters. Find the path intersection and test a common-clock collision. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Equal coordinates give t=1,u=2, so paths intersect at (1,0,0).","[1] A arrives there at time 1 and B at time 2.","[1] A collision would require one time satisfying t=1 and t=2 simultaneously.","[1] No collision occurs despite the path intersection."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A has r(t)=(t,0,0), B has s(u)=(1,u-2,0), for nonnegative parameters. Find the path intersection and test a common-clock collision. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Equal coordinates give t=1,u=2, so paths intersect at (1,0,0).","[1] A arrives there at time 1 and B at time 2.","[1] A collision would require one time satisfying t=1 and t=2 simultaneously.","[1] No collision occurs despite the path intersection."]},{"id":"mas12-space-meetings-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find the closest simultaneous approach of r(t)=(t,0,0), s(t)=(0,t,1), t>=0. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Relative vector is (t,-t,-1).","[1] Squared distance is $2t^2+1$.","[1] Minimum on t>=0 is at t=0.","[1] Closest distance is 1; the paths never collide."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the closest simultaneous approach of r(t)=(t,0,0), s(t)=(0,t,1), t&gt;=0. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Relative vector is (t,-t,-1).","[1] Squared distance is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2t^2+1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Minimum on t&gt;=0 is at t=0.","[1] Closest distance is 1; the paths never collide."]},{"id":"mas12-space-meetings-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Particles r(t)=(1+t,2t,0), s(t)=(4-t,3,0) use the same clock. Do they meet? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] x equality gives $1+t=4-t$, so t=3/2.","[1] y equality gives $2t=3$, the same time.","[1] z coordinates both equal zero.","[1] They meet at (5/2,3,0) at time 3/2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Particles r(t)=(1+t,2t,0), s(t)=(4-t,3,0) use the same clock. Do they meet? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] x equality gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+t=4-t</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so t=3/2.","[1] y equality gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2t=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the same time.","[1] z coordinates both equal zero.","[1] They meet at (5/2,3,0) at time 3/2."]},{"id":"mas12-space-meetings-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Classify paths r(t)=(t,0,0), s(u)=(0,u,1). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Directions (1,0,0),(0,1,0) are nonparallel.","[1] An intersection would require z=0 and z=1.","[1] This is impossible.","[1] The lines are skew; crossing in an xy projection is insufficient."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Classify paths r(t)=(t,0,0), s(u)=(0,u,1). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Directions (1,0,0),(0,1,0) are nonparallel.","[1] An intersection would require z=0 and z=1.","[1] This is impossible.","[1] The lines are skew; crossing in an xy projection is insufficient."]},{"id":"mas12-space-meetings-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find the minimum distance for r(t)=(t,0), s(t)=(4,t), 0<=t<=3. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Squared separation is $(t-4)^2+t^2=2t^2-8t+16$.","[1] Derivative is 4t-8, giving t=2 inside the interval.","[1] Minimum squared distance is 8.","[1] Distance is $2\\sqrt2$ at time 2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the minimum distance for r(t)=(t,0), s(t)=(4,t), 0&lt;=t&lt;=3. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Squared separation is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(t-4)^2+t^2=2t^2-8t+16</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Derivative is 4t-8, giving t=2 inside the interval.","[1] Minimum squared distance is 8.","[1] Distance is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> at time 2."]},{"id":"mas12-space-meetings-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A path intersection gives t=-1,s=2 but both journeys start at time zero. Interpret it. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The extended mathematical lines meet.","[1] The first arrival parameter is negative.","[1] It lies outside the modelled journey of that particle.","[1] Therefore it is not an intersection of the permitted travelled paths, nor a collision in the modelled period."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A path intersection gives t=-1,s=2 but both journeys start at time zero. Interpret it. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The extended mathematical lines meet.","[1] The first arrival parameter is negative.","[1] It lies outside the modelled journey of that particle.","[1] Therefore it is not an intersection of the permitted travelled paths, nor a collision in the modelled period."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What is required for a simultaneous meeting?","o":["Equal positions at the same eligible clock time.","Equal positions at any two unrelated parameters.","Parallel velocities only.","A crossing in one projected diagram."],"a":0,"why":"A shared geometric location is insufficient without matching times.","qHtml":"What is required for a simultaneous meeting?","oHtml":["Equal positions at the same eligible clock time.","Equal positions at any two unrelated parameters.","Parallel velocities only.","A crossing in one projected diagram."],"whyHtml":"A shared geometric location is insufficient without matching times."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-space-cross","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Vectors in three dimensions","title":"Cross products, area and normal vectors","outcomes":["3.3.7"],"icon":"📐","scope":"A perpendicular vector from two directions · Area interpretation · Normals and coplanarity · Check computation and sign","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Cross products, area and normal vectors</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>A perpendicular vector from two directions</li><li>Area interpretation</li><li>Normals and coplanarity</li><li>Check computation and sign</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"A perpendicular vector from two directions","text":"For u=(a,b,c), v=(d,e,f), $u\\times v=(bf-ce,cd-af,ae-bd)$. It is perpendicular to both inputs. Swapping order reverses its sign, and parallel vectors have zero cross product. The right-hand rule sets orientation. Unlike a dot product, a cross product is a vector.","diagram":null,"html":"For u=(a,b,c), v=(d,e,f), <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>f</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u\\times v=(bf-ce,cd-af,ae-bd)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. It is perpendicular to both inputs. Swapping order reverses its sign, and parallel vectors have zero cross product. The right-hand rule sets orientation. Unlike a dot product, a cross product is a vector."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Area interpretation","text":"$|u\\times v|=|u||v|\\sin\\theta$ is the area of the parallelogram spanned by the vectors. Triangle area is half this magnitude. A zero result can indicate parallel directions or a zero input. Keep magnitude separate from the vector itself and use squared units for area.","diagram":"plane","html":"<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|u\\times v|=|u||v|\\sin\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the area of the parallelogram spanned by the vectors. Triangle area is half this magnitude. A zero result can indicate parallel directions or a zero input. Keep magnitude separate from the vector itself and use squared units for area."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Normals and coplanarity","text":"Two nonparallel plane directions produce a nonzero normal. Three points define a plane when their two displacement directions have nonzero cross product. The scalar triple product $(u\\times v)\\cdot w$ detects whether the third direction lies in the plane and gives signed parallelepiped volume; its absolute value is the geometric volume.","diagram":null,"html":"Two nonparallel plane directions produce a nonzero normal. Three points define a plane when their two displacement directions have nonzero cross product. The scalar triple product <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(u\\times v)\\cdot w</annotation></semantics></math></span> detects whether the third direction lies in the plane and gives signed parallelepiped volume; its absolute value is the geometric volume."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Check computation and sign","text":"After calculating a normal, dot it with each original direction to verify orthogonality. Reversing a normal does not change the plane it defines, but changes its orientation. A zero normal cannot define a plane equation. In an exam, show the determinant or component calculation and the geometric interpretation.","diagram":null,"html":"After calculating a normal, dot it with each original direction to verify orthogonality. Reversing a normal does not change the plane it defines, but changes its orientation. A zero normal cannot define a plane equation. In an exam, show the determinant or component calculation and the geometric interpretation."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-space-cross-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find (1,2,0) cross (0,1,3) and verify perpendicularity. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Components give $(6,-3,1)$.","[1] Dot with (1,2,0) gives 6-6=0.","[1] Dot with (0,1,3) gives -3+3=0.","[1] Both checks confirm a normal to their span."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find (1,2,0) cross (0,1,3) and verify perpendicularity. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Components give <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(6,-3,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Dot with (1,2,0) gives 6-6=0.","[1] Dot with (0,1,3) gives -3+3=0.","[1] Both checks confirm a normal to their span."]},{"id":"mas12-space-cross-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find the area of triangle with A=(0,0,0), B=(1,2,0), C=(0,1,3). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Side cross product is (6,-3,1).","[1] Its magnitude is $\\sqrt{36+9+1}=\\sqrt{46}$.","[1] This is the corresponding parallelogram area.","[1] Triangle area is $\\sqrt{46}/2$ square units."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the area of triangle with A=(0,0,0), B=(1,2,0), C=(0,1,3). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Side cross product is (6,-3,1).","[1] Its magnitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>36</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>46</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{36+9+1}=\\sqrt{46}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This is the corresponding parallelogram area.","[1] Triangle area is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>46</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{46}/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> square units."]},{"id":"mas12-space-cross-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find a normal to directions (1,0,1) and (0,1,1). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Cross product is (-1,-1,1).","[1] It is nonzero, so directions are independent.","[1] Dot products with both directions are zero.","[1] Any nonzero scalar multiple gives another valid normal."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find a normal to directions (1,0,1) and (0,1,1). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Cross product is (-1,-1,1).","[1] It is nonzero, so directions are independent.","[1] Dot products with both directions are zero.","[1] Any nonzero scalar multiple gives another valid normal."]},{"id":"mas12-space-cross-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Explain the result of (1,2,3) cross (2,4,6). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The second vector is twice the first.","[1] Components of the cross product all vanish.","[1] Spanned parallelogram area is zero.","[1] The two directions alone do not determine a unique plane."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain the result of (1,2,3) cross (2,4,6). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The second vector is twice the first.","[1] Components of the cross product all vanish.","[1] Spanned parallelogram area is zero.","[1] The two directions alone do not determine a unique plane."]},{"id":"mas12-space-cross-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find the volume spanned by u=(1,0,0), v=(0,2,0), w=(1,1,3). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] u cross v is (0,0,2).","[1] Scalar triple product with w is 6.","[1] Absolute value gives geometric volume 6.","[1] Units are cubic if the original vectors are lengths."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the volume spanned by u=(1,0,0), v=(0,2,0), w=(1,1,3). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] u cross v is (0,0,2).","[1] Scalar triple product with w is 6.","[1] Absolute value gives geometric volume 6.","[1] Units are cubic if the original vectors are lengths."]},{"id":"mas12-space-cross-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Are directions (1,0,1),(0,1,1),(1,1,2) coplanar? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Cross of the first two is (-1,-1,1).","[1] Dot with the third is -1-1+2=0.","[1] The third lies in their span; indeed it is their sum.","[1] Thus the three directions are coplanar."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Are directions (1,0,1),(0,1,1),(1,1,2) coplanar? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Cross of the first two is (-1,-1,1).","[1] Dot with the third is -1-1+2=0.","[1] The third lies in their span; indeed it is their sum.","[1] Thus the three directions are coplanar."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What does the magnitude of u cross v measure?","o":["The area of their spanned parallelogram.","The angle in radians itself.","The sum of their lengths.","The signed dot product."],"a":0,"why":"Its magnitude is base times perpendicular height.","qHtml":"What does the magnitude of u cross v measure?","oHtml":["The area of their spanned parallelogram.","The angle in radians itself.","The sum of their lengths.","The signed dot product."],"whyHtml":"Its magnitude is base times perpendicular height."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-space-planes","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Vectors in three dimensions","title":"Vector and Cartesian planes and intersections","outcomes":["3.3.8"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Two equivalent plane descriptions · Find planes from supplied geometry · Intersect a line and plane · Angles and distances","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Vector and Cartesian planes and intersections</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Two equivalent plane descriptions</li><li>Find planes from supplied geometry</li><li>Intersect a line and plane</li><li>Angles and distances</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Two equivalent plane descriptions","text":"A plane can be written $r=a+su+tv$ with independent directions u,v, or $n\\cdot(r-a)=0$ with nonzero normal n perpendicular to both. Cartesian form is $n_xx+n_yy+n_zz=d$. The normal is not a position vector for every plane point. A zero normal cannot define a unique plane.","diagram":null,"html":"A plane can be written <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=a+su+tv</annotation></semantics></math></span> with independent directions u,v, or <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\cdot(r-a)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> with nonzero normal n perpendicular to both. Cartesian form is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_xx+n_yy+n_zz=d</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The normal is not a position vector for every plane point. A zero normal cannot define a unique plane."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Find planes from supplied geometry","text":"Three noncollinear points provide two displacement directions and their cross-product normal. A point and normal give the equation immediately. Substitute each supplied point to check the result. Parallel planes have proportional normals; they can be coincident or distinct depending on constants.","diagram":"plane","html":"Three noncollinear points provide two displacement directions and their cross-product normal. A point and normal give the equation immediately. Substitute each supplied point to check the result. Parallel planes have proportional normals; they can be coincident or distinct depending on constants."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Intersect a line and plane","text":"Substitute $r=a+td$ into the plane equation. A nonzero coefficient of t gives one intersection. If that coefficient is zero, the direction is parallel to the plane; the remaining constant decides whether the whole line lies in the plane or no intersection exists. Respect segment or time restrictions afterwards.","diagram":null,"html":"Substitute <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=a+td</annotation></semantics></math></span> into the plane equation. A nonzero coefficient of t gives one intersection. If that coefficient is zero, the direction is parallel to the plane; the remaining constant decides whether the whole line lies in the plane or no intersection exists. Respect segment or time restrictions afterwards."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Angles and distances","text":"The acute angle between planes is the acute angle between normals. For a line-plane angle alpha, $\\sin\\alpha=|n\\cdot d|/(|n||d|)$. Distance from P to $n\\cdot r=d_0$ is $|n\\cdot P-d_0|/|n|$. Use nonzero normals and interpret whether the requested angle is to the plane or to its normal.","diagram":null,"html":"The acute angle between planes is the acute angle between normals. For a line-plane angle alpha, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\alpha=|n\\cdot d|/(|n||d|)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Distance from P to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\cdot r=d_0</annotation></semantics></math></span> is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|n\\cdot P-d_0|/|n|</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Use nonzero normals and interpret whether the requested angle is to the plane or to its normal."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Extended exam task","q":6},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-space-planes-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find a plane through (1,0,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,1). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Directions are (-1,1,0) and (-1,0,1).","[1] Their cross is (1,1,1).","[1] Normal form through (1,0,0) gives x-1+y+z=0.","[1] Plane is x+y+z=1, checked by all three points."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find a plane through (1,0,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,1). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Directions are (-1,1,0) and (-1,0,1).","[1] Their cross is (1,1,1).","[1] Normal form through (1,0,0) gives x-1+y+z=0.","[1] Plane is x+y+z=1, checked by all three points."]},{"id":"mas12-space-planes-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Intersect r=(1,0,2)+t(1,2,-1) with x+y+z=6. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Substitute: $1+t+2t+2-t=6$.","[1] Thus 3+2t=6, giving t=3/2.","[1] Point is (5/2,3,1/2).","[1] Coordinates sum to 6, verifying the intersection."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Intersect r=(1,0,2)+t(1,2,-1) with x+y+z=6. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Substitute: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+t+2t+2-t=6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus 3+2t=6, giving t=3/2.","[1] Point is (5/2,3,1/2).","[1] Coordinates sum to 6, verifying the intersection."]},{"id":"mas12-space-planes-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find the distance from (1,2,3) to 2x-y+2z=4. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Normal is (2,-1,2), magnitude 3.","[1] Residual is $2(1)-2+2(3)-4=2$.","[1] Distance is |2|/3.","[1] Therefore distance is 2/3 units."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the distance from (1,2,3) to 2x-y+2z=4. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Normal is (2,-1,2), magnitude 3.","[1] Residual is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(1)-2+2(3)-4=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Distance is |2|/3.","[1] Therefore distance is 2/3 units."]},{"id":"mas12-space-planes-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find a vector equation for x+y+z=1. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Choose anchor (1,0,0).","[1] Choose plane directions (-1,1,0),(-1,0,1).","[1] r=(1,0,0)+s(-1,1,0)+t(-1,0,1).","[1] Directions are independent and every generated point sums to 1."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find a vector equation for x+y+z=1. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Choose anchor (1,0,0).","[1] Choose plane directions (-1,1,0),(-1,0,1).","[1] r=(1,0,0)+s(-1,1,0)+t(-1,0,1).","[1] Directions are independent and every generated point sums to 1."]},{"id":"mas12-space-planes-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Classify r=(1,0,0)+t(1,-1,0) relative to x+y+z=1. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Direction dot normal is 1-1+0=0.","[1] Anchor satisfies the plane equation.","[1] Substitution gives 1 for every t.","[1] The entire line lies in the plane."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Classify r=(1,0,0)+t(1,-1,0) relative to x+y+z=1. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Direction dot normal is 1-1+0=0.","[1] Anchor satisfies the plane equation.","[1] Substitution gives 1 for every t.","[1] The entire line lies in the plane."]},{"id":"mas12-space-planes-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Find the angle between a line of direction (1,0,1) and plane z=0. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Normal is (0,0,1).","[1] $\\sin\\alpha=1/(1\\sqrt2)=1/\\sqrt2$.","[1] Acute line-plane angle is pi/4.","[1] The formula uses sine because the plane angle complements the normal angle."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the angle between a line of direction (1,0,1) and plane z=0. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Normal is (0,0,1).","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\alpha=1/(1\\sqrt2)=1/\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Acute line-plane angle is pi/4.","[1] The formula uses sine because the plane angle complements the normal angle."]},{"prompt":"Points A=(1,0,0),B=(0,1,0),C=(0,0,1) define a plane. Find its equation and triangle area. Find the perpendicular projection of P=(2,2,2) onto the plane and the distance from P to it. (8 marks)","steps":["[2] AB=(-1,1,0), AC=(-1,0,1), cross=(1,1,1); plane x+y+z=1.","[1] Triangle area is half the normal magnitude, $\\sqrt3/2$.","[2] A perpendicular line through P is $(2,2,2)-t(1,1,1)$. Plane substitution gives $6-3t=1$, so t=5/3.","[1] Projection is (1/3,1/3,1/3).","[2] Displacement to P is (5/3,5/3,5/3), length $5/\\sqrt3$. It is parallel to the normal and the projection lies in the plane."],"id":"mas12-space-planes-extended","title":"Extended exam task","allocations":[2,1,2,1,2],"marks":8,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed or exact working","promptHtml":"Points A=(1,0,0),B=(0,1,0),C=(0,0,1) define a plane. Find its equation and triangle area. Find the perpendicular projection of P=(2,2,2) onto the plane and the distance from P to it. (8 marks)","stepsHtml":["[2] AB=(-1,1,0), AC=(-1,0,1), cross=(1,1,1); plane x+y+z=1.","[1] Triangle area is half the normal magnitude, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt3/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[2] A perpendicular line through P is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,2,2)-t(1,1,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Plane substitution gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6-3t=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so t=5/3.","[1] Projection is (1/3,1/3,1/3).","[2] Displacement to P is (5/3,5/3,5/3), length <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5/\\sqrt3</annotation></semantics></math></span>. It is parallel to the normal and the projection lies in the plane."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What if a line direction is perpendicular to a plane normal?","o":["The line is parallel to the plane or lies wholly in it.","It must meet the plane in exactly one point.","It is perpendicular to the plane.","The plane has zero area."],"a":0,"why":"The anchor location decides between containment and no intersection.","qHtml":"What if a line direction is perpendicular to a plane normal?","oHtml":["The line is parallel to the plane or lies wholly in it.","It must meet the plane in exactly one point.","It is perpendicular to the plane.","The plane has zero area."],"whyHtml":"The anchor location decides between containment and no intersection."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 5 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-space-systems","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Vectors in three dimensions","title":"Elimination, unique solutions and degenerate systems","outcomes":["3.3.9","3.3.10"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Three equations represent three planes · Eliminate with visible operations · Interpret degenerate rows · Parameter questions need case analysis","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Elimination, unique solutions and degenerate systems</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Three equations represent three planes</li><li>Eliminate with visible operations</li><li>Interpret degenerate rows</li><li>Parameter questions need case analysis</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Three equations represent three planes","text":"A linear system in x,y,z corresponds to intersecting planes. One point is a unique solution; a line or plane of points means infinitely many solutions; incompatible equations give none. Elimination reduces the system while preserving its solution set when each operation is reversible.","diagram":null,"html":"A linear system in x,y,z corresponds to intersecting planes. One point is a unique solution; a line or plane of points means infinitely many solutions; incompatible equations give none. Elimination reduces the system while preserving its solution set when each operation is reversible."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Eliminate with visible operations","text":"Swap equations, multiply one by a nonzero scalar or add a multiple of one to another. Record operations or enough intermediate equations to show the method. Once triangular form is reached, back-substitute. Do not divide by a parameter that might be zero without splitting that case first.","diagram":"matrix-inverse","html":"Swap equations, multiply one by a nonzero scalar or add a multiple of one to another. Record operations or enough intermediate equations to show the method. Once triangular form is reached, back-substitute. Do not divide by a parameter that might be zero without splitting that case first."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret degenerate rows","text":"A row $0=0$ is redundant, leaving fewer independent constraints. A row $0=c$ with $c\\ne0$ is inconsistent and ends the solution search. A free variable can parameterise infinitely many solutions. The count of equations alone does not guarantee uniqueness because equations may be dependent.","diagram":null,"html":"A row <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> is redundant, leaving fewer independent constraints. A row <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0=c</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span> is inconsistent and ends the solution search. A free variable can parameterise infinitely many solutions. The count of equations alone does not guarantee uniqueness because equations may be dependent."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Parameter questions need case analysis","text":"If a pivot expression depends on k, identify exceptional values before dividing. Analyse each exceptional system directly and report whether it is consistent. Technology can solve a general case yet omit a special parameter case. Check the final solutions in all original equations, including the apparently redundant ones.","diagram":null,"html":"If a pivot expression depends on k, identify exceptional values before dividing. Analyse each exceptional system directly and report whether it is consistent. Technology can solve a general case yet omit a special parameter case. Check the final solutions in all original equations, including the apparently redundant ones."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-space-systems-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Solve x+y+z=6, x-y+z=2, 2x+y-z=3. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Subtract second from first: 2y=4, so y=2.","[1] First then gives x+z=4.","[1] Third gives 2x-z=1; adding yields 3x=5.","[1] x=5/3,y=2,z=7/3, satisfying all equations."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve x+y+z=6, x-y+z=2, 2x+y-z=3. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Subtract second from first: 2y=4, so y=2.","[1] First then gives x+z=4.","[1] Third gives 2x-z=1; adding yields 3x=5.","[1] x=5/3,y=2,z=7/3, satisfying all equations."]},{"id":"mas12-space-systems-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Classify x+y+z=1, 2x+2y+2z=2, x-y=0 and parameterise solutions. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The second equation is redundant.","[1] Third gives y=x.","[1] Set x=t,y=t; first gives z=1-2t.","[1] Solutions form the line (t,t,1-2t), t real."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Classify x+y+z=1, 2x+2y+2z=2, x-y=0 and parameterise solutions. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The second equation is redundant.","[1] Third gives y=x.","[1] Set x=t,y=t; first gives z=1-2t.","[1] Solutions form the line (t,t,1-2t), t real."]},{"id":"mas12-space-systems-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Classify x+y+z=1, 2x+2y+2z=3. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Double the first equation gives left side of the second.","[1] Its constant would be 2.","[1] Subtraction yields 0=1.","[1] The system is inconsistent and has no solution."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Classify x+y+z=1, 2x+2y+2z=3. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Double the first equation gives left side of the second.","[1] Its constant would be 2.","[1] Subtraction yields 0=1.","[1] The system is inconsistent and has no solution."]},{"id":"mas12-space-systems-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Solve x+y=2, y+z=3, x+z=4. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Add first and third then subtract second: 2x=3.","[1] x=3/2.","[1] y=2-x=1/2 and z=4-x=5/2.","[1] Check y+z=3; unique solution follows."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve x+y=2, y+z=3, x+z=4. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Add first and third then subtract second: 2x=3.","[1] x=3/2.","[1] y=2-x=1/2 and z=4-x=5/2.","[1] Check y+z=3; unique solution follows."]},{"id":"mas12-space-systems-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"For x+y+z=1, x+y+kz=2, x-y=0, classify k=1 and solve otherwise. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Subtract the first from second: (k-1)z=1.","[1] If k=1, this is 0=1, so no solution.","[1] If k not equal 1, z=1/(k-1) and x=y.","[1] x=y=(k-2)/[2(k-1)], giving a unique solution."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For x+y+z=1, x+y+kz=2, x-y=0, classify k=1 and solve otherwise. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Subtract the first from second: (k-1)z=1.","[1] If k=1, this is 0=1, so no solution.","[1] If k not equal 1, z=1/(k-1) and x=y.","[1] x=y=(k-2)/[2(k-1)], giving a unique solution."]},{"id":"mas12-space-systems-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Why does a row 0=0 not mean the original system has no solutions? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] It imposes no new restriction.","[1] It arises when an equation is dependent on others.","[1] Remaining independent equations may describe a point, line or plane depending on rank and consistency.","[1] Only a contradiction such as 0=1 establishes no solution."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Why does a row 0=0 not mean the original system has no solutions? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] It imposes no new restriction.","[1] It arises when an equation is dependent on others.","[1] Remaining independent equations may describe a point, line or plane depending on rank and consistency.","[1] Only a contradiction such as 0=1 establishes no solution."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which eliminated row establishes inconsistency?","o":["0=5.","0=0.","x=0.","y+z=0."],"a":0,"why":"A zero left side cannot equal a nonzero constant.","qHtml":"Which eliminated row establishes inconsistency?","oHtml":["0=5.","0=0.","x=0.","y+z=0."],"whyHtml":"A zero left side cannot equal a nonzero constant."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-space-calculus","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Vectors in three dimensions","title":"Vector calculus, displacement and distance travelled","outcomes":["3.3.11","3.3.13","3.3.14"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Differentiate position componentwise · Recover motion by integration · Displacement versus distance · Turning and acceleration","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Vector calculus, displacement and distance travelled</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Differentiate position componentwise</li><li>Recover motion by integration</li><li>Displacement versus distance</li><li>Turning and acceleration</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Differentiate position componentwise","text":"For r(t)=(x(t),y(t),z(t)), velocity is v=r derivative and acceleration is a=v derivative. Speed is |v|, a nonnegative scalar. Velocity includes direction; speed does not. Derivatives require the coordinate functions to be differentiable at the relevant time, and units change from distance to distance/time and distance/time squared.","diagram":null,"html":"For r(t)=(x(t),y(t),z(t)), velocity is v=r derivative and acceleration is a=v derivative. Speed is |v|, a nonnegative scalar. Velocity includes direction; speed does not. Derivatives require the coordinate functions to be differentiable at the relevant time, and units change from distance to distance/time and distance/time squared."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recover motion by integration","text":"Integrate acceleration to obtain velocity and use the initial velocity vector to determine all constants. Integrate again and use initial position. Each component has its own constant; one scalar constant cannot replace a vector of initial conditions. Differentiate the final position to check both the acceleration and initial data.","diagram":"motion","html":"Integrate acceleration to obtain velocity and use the initial velocity vector to determine all constants. Integrate again and use initial position. Each component has its own constant; one scalar constant cannot replace a vector of initial conditions. Differentiate the final position to check both the acceleration and initial data."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Displacement versus distance","text":"Displacement from a to b is r(b)-r(a), equivalently the integral of velocity. Distance travelled is the integral of speed $\\int_a^b|v(t)|\\,dt$. The magnitude of displacement is at most the distance and can be zero even after substantial movement. In one dimension split at velocity sign changes; in more dimensions integrate the vector magnitude.","diagram":null,"html":"Displacement from a to b is r(b)-r(a), equivalently the integral of velocity. Distance travelled is the integral of speed <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_a^b|v(t)|\\,dt</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The magnitude of displacement is at most the distance and can be zero even after substantial movement. In one dimension split at velocity sign changes; in more dimensions integrate the vector magnitude."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Turning and acceleration","text":"A changing direction produces acceleration even at constant speed. Instantaneous rest requires every velocity component to be zero simultaneously. One vanishing component does not imply rest. A speed extremum can be investigated through $d(|v|^2)/dt=2v\\cdot a$, but endpoints and zero-speed points need care. Interpret the time interval and reject ineligible roots.","diagram":null,"html":"A changing direction produces acceleration even at constant speed. Instantaneous rest requires every velocity component to be zero simultaneously. One vanishing component does not imply rest. A speed extremum can be investigated through <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>v</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>v</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(|v|^2)/dt=2v\\cdot a</annotation></semantics></math></span>, but endpoints and zero-speed points need care. Interpret the time interval and reject ineligible roots."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-space-calculus-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"For r(t)=($t^2$,2t,0), find velocity, acceleration and speed at t=1. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] v(t)=(2t,2,0).","[1] a(t)=(2,0,0).","[1] At t=1, v=(2,2,0).","[1] Speed is $\\sqrt8=2\\sqrt2$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For r(t)=(<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>,2t,0), find velocity, acceleration and speed at t=1. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] v(t)=(2t,2,0).","[1] a(t)=(2,0,0).","[1] At t=1, v=(2,2,0).","[1] Speed is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>8</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt8=2\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-space-calculus-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"A particle has a=(2,0,-2), v(0)=(1,3,0), r(0)=(0,1,2). Find r(t). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Integrate: v=(2t+C_1,C_2,-2t+C_3).","[1] Initial velocity gives v=(2t+1,3,-2t).","[1] Integrate again: r=($t^2+t$,3t+1,$2-t^2$).","[1] Initial position fixes all three constants; differentiating verifies a."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A particle has a=(2,0,-2), v(0)=(1,3,0), r(0)=(0,1,2). Find r(t). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Integrate: v=(2t+C_1,C_2,-2t+C_3).","[1] Initial velocity gives v=(2t+1,3,-2t).","[1] Integrate again: r=(<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^2+t</annotation></semantics></math></span>,3t+1,<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2-t^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>).","[1] Initial position fixes all three constants; differentiating verifies a."]},{"id":"mas12-space-calculus-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"For x(t)=$t^2-2t$ on 0<=t<=3, find displacement and distance. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Displacement x(3)-x(0)=3.","[1] Velocity 2t-2 changes sign at t=1.","[1] Positions are x(0)=0,x(1)=-1,x(3)=3.","[1] Distance is 1+4=5, greater than displacement magnitude 3."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For x(t)=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^2-2t</annotation></semantics></math></span> on 0&lt;=t&lt;=3, find displacement and distance. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Displacement x(3)-x(0)=3.","[1] Velocity 2t-2 changes sign at t=1.","[1] Positions are x(0)=0,x(1)=-1,x(3)=3.","[1] Distance is 1+4=5, greater than displacement magnitude 3."]},{"id":"mas12-space-calculus-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"For r(t)=(3t,4t,0), 0<=t<=2, find displacement and distance. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Displacement is (6,8,0).","[1] Constant velocity is (3,4,0), speed 5.","[1] Distance is integral of 5 over length 2, giving 10.","[1] Displacement magnitude is also 10 because direction never reverses."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For r(t)=(3t,4t,0), 0&lt;=t&lt;=2, find displacement and distance. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Displacement is (6,8,0).","[1] Constant velocity is (3,4,0), speed 5.","[1] Distance is integral of 5 over length 2, giving 10.","[1] Displacement magnitude is also 10 because direction never reverses."]},{"id":"mas12-space-calculus-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"For v(t)=(t-1,2t-4,0), can the particle be at rest? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Rest needs both nonconstant components zero simultaneously.","[1] First component vanishes at t=1.","[1] Second vanishes at t=2.","[1] No common time exists, so the particle is never at rest."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For v(t)=(t-1,2t-4,0), can the particle be at rest? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Rest needs both nonconstant components zero simultaneously.","[1] First component vanishes at t=1.","[1] Second vanishes at t=2.","[1] No common time exists, so the particle is never at rest."]},{"id":"mas12-space-calculus-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Find minimum speed for v(t)=(t,2-t,0), t real. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Squared speed is $t^2+(2-t)^2=2t^2-4t+4$.","[1] Its derivative is 4t-4, zero at t=1.","[1] The quadratic opens upward, giving a global minimum.","[1] Minimum speed is $\\sqrt2$ at t=1."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find minimum speed for v(t)=(t,2-t,0), t real. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Squared speed is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^2+(2-t)^2=2t^2-4t+4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Its derivative is 4t-4, zero at t=1.","[1] The quadratic opens upward, giving a global minimum.","[1] Minimum speed is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> at t=1."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which integral gives distance travelled?","o":["The integral of the magnitude of velocity.","The integral of one chosen velocity component.","The magnitude of final position in every case.","The signed integral of acceleration."],"a":0,"why":"Speed is nonnegative and accumulates the full travelled path.","qHtml":"Which integral gives distance travelled?","oHtml":["The integral of the magnitude of velocity.","The integral of one chosen velocity component.","The magnitude of final position in every case.","The signed integral of acceleration."],"whyHtml":"Speed is nonnegative and accumulates the full travelled path."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-space-motion","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Complex numbers, functions and vectors","area":"Vectors in three dimensions","title":"Projectile and circular motion in a plane","outcomes":["3.3.15"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Projectile assumptions · Choose physically eligible times · Circular motion is accelerating · Compare models with reality","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Projectile and circular motion in a plane</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Projectile assumptions</li><li>Choose physically eligible times</li><li>Circular motion is accelerating</li><li>Compare models with reality</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Projectile assumptions","text":"With constant downward gravitational acceleration and negligible air resistance, position is $r(t)=r_0+v_0t+\\tfrac12at^2$. Resolve launch velocity into horizontal and vertical components. State the value of g used, the launch height and ground level. Horizontal velocity is constant while vertical velocity changes linearly.","diagram":null,"html":"With constant downward gravitational acceleration and negligible air resistance, position is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r(t)=r_0+v_0t+\\tfrac12at^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Resolve launch velocity into horizontal and vertical components. State the value of g used, the launch height and ground level. Horizontal velocity is constant while vertical velocity changes linearly."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Choose physically eligible times","text":"Solve the vertical position equation for impact and retain eligible nonnegative times. Time zero may describe the launch rather than later landing. Range is horizontal displacement at impact, not the full trajectory length. Maximum height occurs when the vertical velocity is zero, provided that time lies in the flight interval.","diagram":"projectile","html":"Solve the vertical position equation for impact and retain eligible nonnegative times. Time zero may describe the launch rather than later landing. Range is horizontal displacement at impact, not the full trajectory length. Maximum height occurs when the vertical velocity is zero, provided that time lies in the flight interval."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Circular motion is accelerating","text":"For r(t)=(R cos omega t,R sin omega t), velocity is tangent and acceleration equals $-\\omega^2r$. Speed is constant R|omega|, yet direction changes. Period is $2\\pi/|\\omega|$ for nonzero omega. A translated circle uses acceleration toward its centre rather than toward the global origin.","diagram":null,"html":"For r(t)=(R cos omega t,R sin omega t), velocity is tangent and acceleration equals <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\omega^2r</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Speed is constant R|omega|, yet direction changes. Period is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/|\\omega|</annotation></semantics></math></span> for nonzero omega. A translated circle uses acceleration toward its centre rather than toward the global origin."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare models with reality","text":"Projectile and circular formulas describe specific assumptions. Resistance, variable acceleration or an irregular path can require a different differential equation. Explain the physical meaning of each vector and preserve units. A projected sketch should show launch, peak and impact or tangent and inward acceleration, while exact coordinates establish values.","diagram":null,"html":"Projectile and circular formulas describe specific assumptions. Resistance, variable acceleration or an irregular path can require a different differential equation. Explain the physical meaning of each vector and preserve units. A projected sketch should show launch, peak and impact or tangent and inward acceleration, while exact coordinates establish values."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Extended exam task","q":6},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-space-motion-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"A projectile starts at ground level with velocity (12,16) m/s, acceleration (0,-10) m/s squared. Find flight time and range. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Position is (12t,$16t-5t^2$).","[1] Ground equation gives t(16-5t)=0.","[1] Later impact is t=16/5=3.2 s.","[1] Range is 12(3.2)=38.4 m."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A projectile starts at ground level with velocity (12,16) m/s, acceleration (0,-10) m/s squared. Find flight time and range. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Position is (12t,<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">16t-5t^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>).","[1] Ground equation gives t(16-5t)=0.","[1] Later impact is t=16/5=3.2 s.","[1] Range is 12(3.2)=38.4 m."]},{"id":"mas12-space-motion-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"For the same projectile, find maximum height and velocity there. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Vertical velocity is 16-10t.","[1] It vanishes at t=1.6 s.","[1] Height is $16(1.6)-5(1.6)^2=12.8$ m.","[1] Velocity is (12,0) m/s; it is not at rest."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For the same projectile, find maximum height and velocity there. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Vertical velocity is 16-10t.","[1] It vanishes at t=1.6 s.","[1] Height is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.6</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>12.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">16(1.6)-5(1.6)^2=12.8</annotation></semantics></math></span> m.","[1] Velocity is (12,0) m/s; it is not at rest."]},{"id":"mas12-space-motion-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"For r(t)=(3 cos 2t,3 sin 2t), find speed and period. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] v=(-6 sin 2t,6 cos 2t).","[1] Speed is 6 from the trigonometric identity.","[1] Angular frequency is 2.","[1] Period is pi time units."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For r(t)=(3 cos 2t,3 sin 2t), find speed and period. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] v=(-6 sin 2t,6 cos 2t).","[1] Speed is 6 from the trigonometric identity.","[1] Angular frequency is 2.","[1] Period is pi time units."]},{"id":"mas12-space-motion-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find acceleration for r(t)=(1+2 cos t,-3+2 sin t). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Differentiate twice: a=(-2 cos t,-2 sin t).","[1] Centre is C=(1,-3).","[1] Thus a=-(r-C).","[1] Acceleration points inward toward C, not necessarily toward the origin."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find acceleration for r(t)=(1+2 cos t,-3+2 sin t). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Differentiate twice: a=(-2 cos t,-2 sin t).","[1] Centre is C=(1,-3).","[1] Thus a=-(r-C).","[1] Acceleration points inward toward C, not necessarily toward the origin."]},{"id":"mas12-space-motion-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"A projectile has y(t)=$5+10t-5t^2$. Find its impact time at y=0. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Equation reduces to $t^2-2t-1=0$.","[1] Roots are $1\\pm\\sqrt2$.","[1] Negative root is ineligible after launch.","[1] Impact time is $1+\\sqrt2$ time units."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A projectile has y(t)=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5+10t-5t^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find its impact time at y=0. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Equation reduces to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t^2-2t-1=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Roots are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\pm\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Negative root is ineligible after launch.","[1] Impact time is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> time units."]},{"id":"mas12-space-motion-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Prove velocity and acceleration are perpendicular for r(t)=(R cos omega t,R sin omega t) with nonzero R,omega. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] v=(-R omega sin omega t,R omega cos omega t).","[1] a=(-R omega squared cos omega t,-R omega squared sin omega t).","[1] Their dot product is the sum of equal opposite terms, zero.","[1] Nonzero tangent velocity is perpendicular to inward acceleration, explaining constant speed."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Prove velocity and acceleration are perpendicular for r(t)=(R cos omega t,R sin omega t) with nonzero R,omega. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] v=(-R omega sin omega t,R omega cos omega t).","[1] a=(-R omega squared cos omega t,-R omega squared sin omega t).","[1] Their dot product is the sum of equal opposite terms, zero.","[1] Nonzero tangent velocity is perpendicular to inward acceleration, explaining constant speed."]},{"prompt":"A projectile starts at (0,5) m with velocity (12,10) m/s under acceleration (0,-10) m/s squared. Find its position and velocity functions, maximum height, impact time and horizontal range. Is it at rest at maximum height? (8 marks)","steps":["[2] Position is $(12t,5+10t-5t^2)$ and velocity $(12,10-10t)$.","[2] Vertical velocity vanishes at t=1 s, giving maximum height 10 m.","[2] Ground equation gives $t=1\\pm\\sqrt2$; eligible impact time is $1+\\sqrt2$ s.","[1] Range is $12(1+\\sqrt2)$ m, approximately 28.971 m.","[1] At the peak velocity is (12,0), so the particle is not at rest."],"id":"mas12-space-motion-extended","title":"Extended exam task","allocations":[2,2,2,1,1],"marks":8,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed or exact working","promptHtml":"A projectile starts at (0,5) m with velocity (12,10) m/s under acceleration (0,-10) m/s squared. Find its position and velocity functions, maximum height, impact time and horizontal range. Is it at rest at maximum height? (8 marks)","stepsHtml":["[2] Position is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>t</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(12t,5+10t-5t^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and velocity <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(12,10-10t)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[2] Vertical velocity vanishes at t=1 s, giving maximum height 10 m.","[2] Ground equation gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1\\pm\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>; eligible impact time is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> s.","[1] Range is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12(1+\\sqrt2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> m, approximately 28.971 m.","[1] At the peak velocity is (12,0), so the particle is not at rest."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Why can constant-speed circular motion have nonzero acceleration?","o":["Its velocity direction changes.","Constant speed always means zero velocity.","Acceleration measures only changes in speed.","The radius must increase."],"a":0,"why":"Acceleration is the rate of change of the full velocity vector.","qHtml":"Why can constant-speed circular motion have nonzero acceleration?","oHtml":["Its velocity direction changes.","Constant speed always means zero velocity.","Acceleration measures only changes in speed.","The radius must increase."],"whyHtml":"Acceleration is the rate of change of the full velocity vector."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 5 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-integration-trig","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Integration and applications","title":"Trigonometric identities before integration","outcomes":["4.1.1"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Rewrite before integrating · Account for inner frequency · Use parity of powers · Check and interpret","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Trigonometric identities before integration</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Rewrite before integrating</li><li>Account for inner frequency</li><li>Use parity of powers</li><li>Check and interpret</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Rewrite before integrating","text":"Products and powers of trigonometric functions may not match an immediate antiderivative. Use identities such as $\\sin^2x=(1-\\cos2x)/2$, $\\cos^2x=(1+\\cos2x)/2$ and $2\\sin x\\cos x=\\sin2x$. The rewritten form exposes terms with known antiderivatives. State the identity so the method is visible.","diagram":null,"html":"Products and powers of trigonometric functions may not match an immediate antiderivative. Use identities such as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x=(1-\\cos2x)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos^2x=(1+\\cos2x)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sin x\\cos x=\\sin2x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The rewritten form exposes terms with known antiderivatives. State the identity so the method is visible."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Account for inner frequency","text":"Integrating sin(kx) gives -cos(kx)/k and integrating cos(kx) gives sin(kx)/k for nonzero k. A missing frequency factor is a common error. Integrals of sec squared and cosec squared give tan and negative cot, respectively, on intervals avoiding their poles. Always add a constant for indefinite integration.","diagram":"trig-integral","html":"Integrating sin(kx) gives -cos(kx)/k and integrating cos(kx) gives sin(kx)/k for nonzero k. A missing frequency factor is a common error. Integrals of sec squared and cosec squared give tan and negative cot, respectively, on intervals avoiding their poles. Always add a constant for indefinite integration."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use parity of powers","text":"An odd sine power can be written as sine times a polynomial in cosine, then use u=cos x. An odd cosine power similarly leaves a cosine factor with u=sin x. Even powers often use the double-angle identities. Select the identity that creates a derivative partner, rather than expand without a goal.","diagram":null,"html":"An odd sine power can be written as sine times a polynomial in cosine, then use u=cos x. An odd cosine power similarly leaves a cosine factor with u=sin x. Even powers often use the double-angle identities. Select the identity that creates a derivative partner, rather than expand without a goal."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Check and interpret","text":"Differentiate the antiderivative to recover the original integrand. For definite integrals, evaluate bounds and preserve exact pi values. Signed integral and geometric area differ when the integrand changes sign. Trigonometric arguments are in radians for calculus, because the derivative formulas assume radian measure.","diagram":null,"html":"Differentiate the antiderivative to recover the original integrand. For definite integrals, evaluate bounds and preserve exact pi values. Signed integral and geometric area differ when the integrand changes sign. Trigonometric arguments are in radians for calculus, because the derivative formulas assume radian measure."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-integration-trig-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find $\\int\\sin^2x\\,dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use $\\sin^2x=(1-\\cos2x)/2$.","[1] Integrate termwise.","[1] Result is $x/2-\\sin2x/4+C$.","[1] Differentiation gives $(1-\\cos2x)/2=\\sin^2x$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int\\sin^2x\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x=(1-\\cos2x)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Integrate termwise.","[1] Result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x/2-\\sin2x/4+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Differentiation gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-\\cos2x)/2=\\sin^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-trig-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Evaluate $\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\cos^2x\\,dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use $(1+\\cos2x)/2$.","[1] Antiderivative is $x/2+\\sin2x/4$.","[1] At pi/2 it is pi/4, and at zero it is zero.","[1] Integral is pi/4."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\cos^2x\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+\\cos2x)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Antiderivative is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x/2+\\sin2x/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] At pi/2 it is pi/4, and at zero it is zero.","[1] Integral is pi/4."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-trig-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find $\\int\\sin x\\cos x\\,dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Product is half sin 2x.","[1] Integrate to $-\\cos2x/4+C$.","[1] Equivalently $\\sin^2x/2+C$ after changing the constant.","[1] Differentiate either form to obtain sin x cos x."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int\\sin x\\cos x\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Product is half sin 2x.","[1] Integrate to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\cos2x/4+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Equivalently <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x/2+C</annotation></semantics></math></span> after changing the constant.","[1] Differentiate either form to obtain sin x cos x."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-trig-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find $\\int\\sin^3x\\,dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Write sin x(1-cos squared x).","[1] Set u=cos x, du=-sin x dx.","[1] Integral becomes $-\\int(1-u^2)du=-u+u^3/3+C$.","[1] Result is $-\\cos x+\\cos^3x/3+C$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int\\sin^3x\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Write sin x(1-cos squared x).","[1] Set u=cos x, du=-sin x dx.","[1] Integral becomes <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo>∫</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\int(1-u^2)du=-u+u^3/3+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\cos x+\\cos^3x/3+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-trig-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find $\\int\\sec^2(3x)\\,dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The derivative of tan 3x is 3 sec squared 3x.","[1] Divide by the frequency 3.","[1] Antiderivative is tan(3x)/3+C.","[1] It is valid on intervals where cos 3x is nonzero."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msup><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int\\sec^2(3x)\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The derivative of tan 3x is 3 sec squared 3x.","[1] Divide by the frequency 3.","[1] Antiderivative is tan(3x)/3+C.","[1] It is valid on intervals where cos 3x is nonzero."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-trig-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Evaluate $\\int_0^\\pi\\sin2x\\,dx$ and explain why it is not the geometric area. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Antiderivative is -cos 2x/2.","[1] Endpoint values are equal, so integral is zero.","[1] Sin 2x is positive then negative, changing sign at pi/2.","[1] Areas do not cancel; integrating absolute value gives total area 2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^\\pi\\sin2x\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span> and explain why it is not the geometric area. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Antiderivative is -cos 2x/2.","[1] Endpoint values are equal, so integral is zero.","[1] Sin 2x is positive then negative, changing sign at pi/2.","[1] Areas do not cancel; integrating absolute value gives total area 2."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which identity is useful for integrating an even sine square?","o":["$\\sin^2x=(1-\\cos2x)/2$.","$\\sin^2x=\\sin2x$.","$\\sin^2x=1+\\cos2x$.","$\\sin^2x=2\\cos x$."],"a":0,"why":"The double-angle identity converts the power to directly integrable terms.","qHtml":"Which identity is useful for integrating an even sine square?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x=(1-\\cos2x)/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x=\\sin2x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x=1+\\cos2x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2x=2\\cos x</annotation></semantics></math></span>."],"whyHtml":"The double-angle identity converts the power to directly integrable terms."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-integration-substitution","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Integration and applications","title":"Substitution and reverse chain-rule integration","outcomes":["4.1.2"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Substitution reverses a chain rule · Choose the inner function · Back-substitute for indefinite integrals · Show the differential step","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Substitution and reverse chain-rule integration</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Substitution reverses a chain rule</li><li>Choose the inner function</li><li>Back-substitute for indefinite integrals</li><li>Show the differential step</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Substitution reverses a chain rule","text":"When an integrand contains a function g(x) and its derivative, set u=g(x) and replace $g'(x)dx$ by du. The transformed integral must contain only u and du; leaving unexplained x factors behind is incomplete. A constant multiple of the derivative can be adjusted outside the integral.","diagram":null,"html":"When an integrand contains a function g(x) and its derivative, set u=g(x) and replace <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g&#x27;(x)dx</annotation></semantics></math></span> by du. The transformed integral must contain only u and du; leaving unexplained x factors behind is incomplete. A constant multiple of the derivative can be adjusted outside the integral."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Choose the inner function","text":"Powers, roots and exponentials often suggest the inner expression as u. For $\\int2x(x^2+1)^4dx$, the derivative of $x^2+1$ is exactly 2x. An integrand without the needed derivative may require a different rewrite. Substitution is a choice to simplify structure, not a rule that any inner expression automatically works.","diagram":"substitution","html":"Powers, roots and exponentials often suggest the inner expression as u. For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>4</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int2x(x^2+1)^4dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the derivative of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+1</annotation></semantics></math></span> is exactly 2x. An integrand without the needed derivative may require a different rewrite. Substitution is a choice to simplify structure, not a rule that any inner expression automatically works."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Back-substitute for indefinite integrals","text":"After integrating in u, return to x and add C. Domain restrictions in logarithms or roots remain relevant. If using a substitution that is not one-to-one on the whole interval, work on appropriate subintervals. Differentiate the final answer to confirm every chain-rule factor.","diagram":null,"html":"After integrating in u, return to x and add C. Domain restrictions in logarithms or roots remain relevant. If using a substitution that is not one-to-one on the whole interval, work on appropriate subintervals. Differentiate the final answer to confirm every chain-rule factor."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Show the differential step","text":"Write u, du and the replacement clearly. This provides method evidence even if later arithmetic fails. Exact forms are preferred over rounded coefficients. A calculator can check an integral but should not replace the substitution working when that method is requested.","diagram":null,"html":"Write u, du and the replacement clearly. This provides method evidence even if later arithmetic fails. Exact forms are preferred over rounded coefficients. A calculator can check an integral but should not replace the substitution working when that method is requested."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-integration-substitution-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find $\\int2x(x^2+1)^4dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let u=x squared+1, du=2x dx.","[1] Integral is $\\int u^4du$.","[1] This is $u^5/5+C$.","[1] Answer $(x^2+1)^5/5+C$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>4</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int2x(x^2+1)^4dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let u=x squared+1, du=2x dx.","[1] Integral is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int u^4du</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u^5/5+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Answer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>5</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x^2+1)^5/5+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-substitution-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find $\\int x\\sqrt{x^2+4}\\,dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Set u=x squared+4, so x dx=du/2.","[1] Integral is $\\tfrac12\\int u^{1/2}du$.","[1] This gives $u^{3/2}/3+C$.","[1] Back-substitute $(x^2+4)^{3/2}/3+C$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>x</mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int x\\sqrt{x^2+4}\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Set u=x squared+4, so x dx=du/2.","[1] Integral is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12\\int u^{1/2}du</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u^{3/2}/3+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Back-substitute <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x^2+4)^{3/2}/3+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-substitution-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find $\\int\\cos x\\,e^{\\sin x}dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let u=sin x.","[1] du=cos x dx.","[1] Integral is $\\int e^u du=e^u+C$.","[1] Answer $e^{\\sin x}+C$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int\\cos x\\,e^{\\sin x}dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let u=sin x.","[1] du=cos x dx.","[1] Integral is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int e^u du=e^u+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Answer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{\\sin x}+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-substitution-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find $\\int(3x+2)^5dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Set u=3x+2, du=3dx.","[1] Integral becomes $\\tfrac13\\int u^5du$.","[1] Result $u^6/18+C$.","[1] Answer $(3x+2)^6/18+C$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>5</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int(3x+2)^5dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Set u=3x+2, du=3dx.","[1] Integral becomes <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac13\\int u^5du</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u^6/18+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Answer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>6</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3x+2)^6/18+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-substitution-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find $\\int\\sin x\\cos^4x\\,dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] u=cos x, du=-sin x dx.","[1] Integral is $-\\int u^4du$.","[1] This equals $-u^5/5+C$.","[1] Answer $-\\cos^5x/5+C$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int\\sin x\\cos^4x\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] u=cos x, du=-sin x dx.","[1] Integral is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\int u^4du</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This equals <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-u^5/5+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Answer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>5</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\cos^5x/5+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-substitution-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Correct: integral of $e^{2x}$ is $e^{2x}+C$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Differentiating the proposed answer gives $2e^{2x}$.","[1] The missing chain-rule adjustment is factor 1/2.","[1] Substitution u=2x gives dx=du/2.","[1] Correct integral is $e^{2x}/2+C$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Correct: integral of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{2x}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{2x}+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Differentiating the proposed answer gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2e^{2x}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] The missing chain-rule adjustment is factor 1/2.","[1] Substitution u=2x gives dx=du/2.","[1] Correct integral is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{2x}/2+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"After substitution, what should the transformed integral contain?","o":["Only the new variable and its differential.","An arbitrary mixture of unexplained x and u terms.","No differential.","Only numerical constants in every case."],"a":0,"why":"Every original factor must be accounted for by the substitution.","qHtml":"After substitution, what should the transformed integral contain?","oHtml":["Only the new variable and its differential.","An arbitrary mixture of unexplained x and u terms.","No differential.","Only numerical constants in every case."],"whyHtml":"Every original factor must be accounted for by the substitution."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-integration-definite","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Integration and applications","title":"Substitution in definite integrals and changed bounds","outcomes":["4.1.2"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Change bounds with the variable · Reversed bounds encode direction · Domains and continuity still matter · Keep exact values and verify","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Substitution in definite integrals and changed bounds</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Change bounds with the variable</li><li>Reversed bounds encode direction</li><li>Domains and continuity still matter</li><li>Keep exact values and verify</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Change bounds with the variable","text":"For u=g(x), an original bound x=a becomes u=g(a), and x=b becomes u=g(b). Once the integrand and differential are fully in u, evaluate using u-bounds. Alternatively integrate in u, back-substitute and use x-bounds. Mixing a u-antiderivative with unchanged x-bounds gives the wrong result.","diagram":null,"html":"For u=g(x), an original bound x=a becomes u=g(a), and x=b becomes u=g(b). Once the integrand and differential are fully in u, evaluate using u-bounds. Alternatively integrate in u, back-substitute and use x-bounds. Mixing a u-antiderivative with unchanged x-bounds gives the wrong result."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reversed bounds encode direction","text":"If the substitution decreases across the interval, the transformed lower bound can exceed the upper bound. The negative differential factor or reversed orientation must be handled consistently. Do not sort transformed bounds automatically without changing the sign. For nonmonotone substitutions, reason carefully or split the interval.","diagram":"substitution","html":"If the substitution decreases across the interval, the transformed lower bound can exceed the upper bound. The negative differential factor or reversed orientation must be handled consistently. Do not sort transformed bounds automatically without changing the sign. For nonmonotone substitutions, reason carefully or split the interval."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Domains and continuity still matter","text":"A definite integral across a pole is not justified by merely evaluating an antiderivative at endpoints. Check the integrand is defined and the required integral exists on the interval. A substitution may expose but cannot remove an original singularity. This course's ordinary definite-integral calculations need an appropriate continuous integrand or a separately justified treatment.","diagram":null,"html":"A definite integral across a pole is not justified by merely evaluating an antiderivative at endpoints. Check the integrand is defined and the required integral exists on the interval. A substitution may expose but cannot remove an original singularity. This course&#39;s ordinary definite-integral calculations need an appropriate continuous integrand or a separately justified treatment."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Keep exact values and verify","text":"Show the transformed limits and integrand, then evaluate. A numeric check can detect a sign or factor error, especially for a nonnegative original integrand whose integral must be nonnegative. Where bounds contain exact roots or angles, preserve them through the substitution.","diagram":null,"html":"Show the transformed limits and integrand, then evaluate. A numeric check can detect a sign or factor error, especially for a nonnegative original integrand whose integral must be nonnegative. Where bounds contain exact roots or angles, preserve them through the substitution."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-integration-definite-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Evaluate $\\int_0^1 2x(x^2+1)^2dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] u=x squared+1, du=2x dx.","[1] Bounds become u=1 to u=2.","[1] Integral is $[u^3/3]_1^2$.","[1] Value is 7/3."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^1 2x(x^2+1)^2dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] u=x squared+1, du=2x dx.","[1] Bounds become u=1 to u=2.","[1] Integral is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[u^3/3]_1^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Value is 7/3."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-definite-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Evaluate $\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin x\\,e^{\\cos x}dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] u=cos x, du=-sin x dx.","[1] Bounds change from u=1 to u=0.","[1] Integral is $-\\int_1^0e^u du$.","[1] Value is e-1, positive as expected."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin x\\,e^{\\cos x}dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] u=cos x, du=-sin x dx.","[1] Bounds change from u=1 to u=0.","[1] Integral is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\int_1^0e^u du</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Value is e-1, positive as expected."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-definite-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Evaluate $\\int_1^2 x/(x^2+1)\\,dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] u=x squared+1, du=2x dx.","[1] Bounds are 2 and 5.","[1] Integral is half integral of 1/u from 2 to 5.","[1] Result is $\\tfrac12\\ln(5/2)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_1^2 x/(x^2+1)\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] u=x squared+1, du=2x dx.","[1] Bounds are 2 and 5.","[1] Integral is half integral of 1/u from 2 to 5.","[1] Result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12\\ln(5/2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-definite-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Evaluate $\\int_0^3\\sqrt{2x+1}\\,dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] u=2x+1, dx=du/2.","[1] Bounds become 1 and 7.","[1] Integral is $[u^{3/2}/3]_1^7$.","[1] Value $(7\\sqrt7-1)/3$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^3\\sqrt{2x+1}\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] u=2x+1, dx=du/2.","[1] Bounds become 1 and 7.","[1] Integral is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>1</mn><mn>7</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[u^{3/2}/3]_1^7</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Value <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>7</mn><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(7\\sqrt7-1)/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-definite-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Evaluate $\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\tan x\\sec^2x\\,dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] u=tan x, du=sec squared x dx.","[1] Bounds become 0 and 1.","[1] Integral is $\\int_0^1u\\,du$.","[1] Result is 1/2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\tan x\\sec^2x\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] u=tan x, du=sec squared x dx.","[1] Bounds become 0 and 1.","[1] Integral is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mi>u</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^1u\\,du</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result is 1/2."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-definite-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Explain why evaluating $[\\ln|x|]_{-1}^1=0$ does not establish $\\int_{-1}^1dx/x=0$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The integrand is undefined at x=0 inside the interval.","[1] The antiderivative is not valid across one continuous interval containing zero.","[1] The ordinary improper integral has divergent one-sided pieces.","[1] A symmetric principal value is a different concept and cannot justify that ordinary integral."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why evaluating <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\ln|x|]_{-1}^1=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> does not establish <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_{-1}^1dx/x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The integrand is undefined at x=0 inside the interval.","[1] The antiderivative is not valid across one continuous interval containing zero.","[1] The ordinary improper integral has divergent one-sided pieces.","[1] A symmetric principal value is a different concept and cannot justify that ordinary integral."]}],"bank":[{"q":"When integrating entirely in u, which bounds must be used?","o":["The transformed u-values of the original endpoints.","The original x-values regardless of substitution.","Always zero and one.","No bounds for a definite integral."],"a":0,"why":"Bounds must refer to the variable used in the antiderivative.","qHtml":"When integrating entirely in u, which bounds must be used?","oHtml":["The transformed u-values of the original endpoints.","The original x-values regardless of substitution.","Always zero and one.","No bounds for a definite integral."],"whyHtml":"Bounds must refer to the variable used in the antiderivative."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-integration-log","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Integration and applications","title":"Logarithmic integrals and absolute values","outcomes":["4.1.3"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Recognise the logarithmic derivative · Linear denominators · Definite logarithmic integrals · Compare similar integrands","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Logarithmic integrals and absolute values</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Recognise the logarithmic derivative</li><li>Linear denominators</li><li>Definite logarithmic integrals</li><li>Compare similar integrands</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recognise the logarithmic derivative","text":"The integral of $f'(x)/f(x)$ is $\\ln|f(x)|+C$ on an interval where f is nonzero. The absolute value permits negative f while keeping the real logarithm defined. If a problem guarantees f>0, it can be omitted on that interval. Check the derivative partner and any constant factor before applying the rule.","diagram":null,"html":"The integral of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#x27;(x)/f(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln|f(x)|+C</annotation></semantics></math></span> on an interval where f is nonzero. The absolute value permits negative f while keeping the real logarithm defined. If a problem guarantees f&gt;0, it can be omitted on that interval. Check the derivative partner and any constant factor before applying the rule."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Linear denominators","text":"For nonzero a, $\\int dx/(ax+b)=\\ln|ax+b|/a+C$. A missing 1/a gives a chain-rule error. The pole x=-b/a separates intervals of validity. Writing a logarithm does not make the original pole admissible. Different interval components can have different arbitrary constants.","diagram":"substitution","html":"For nonzero a, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int dx/(ax+b)=\\ln|ax+b|/a+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A missing 1/a gives a chain-rule error. The pole x=-b/a separates intervals of validity. Writing a logarithm does not make the original pole admissible. Different interval components can have different arbitrary constants."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Definite logarithmic integrals","text":"Evaluate only on an interval without an unhandled singularity. Subtract endpoint logarithms and simplify to a logarithm of a positive ratio when appropriate. The sign of the integral depends on the original integrand; absolute values inside the logarithm do not make every integral positive.","diagram":null,"html":"Evaluate only on an interval without an unhandled singularity. Subtract endpoint logarithms and simplify to a logarithm of a positive ratio when appropriate. The sign of the integral depends on the original integrand; absolute values inside the logarithm do not make every integral positive."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare similar integrands","text":"$\\int1/x\\,dx$ is logarithmic, but $\\int1/x^2\\,dx$ is a power integral. $\\int x/(x^2+1)dx$ uses a logarithm with a half factor, whereas $\\int1/(x^2+1)dx$ has no x derivative partner and is an arctangent integral. Choose by structure, not by the presence of a denominator alone.","diagram":null,"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int1/x\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span> is logarithmic, but <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int1/x^2\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a power integral. <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int x/(x^2+1)dx</annotation></semantics></math></span> uses a logarithm with a half factor, whereas <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int1/(x^2+1)dx</annotation></semantics></math></span> has no x derivative partner and is an arctangent integral. Choose by structure, not by the presence of a denominator alone."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-integration-log-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find $\\int(2x+3)/(x^2+3x+5)dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Denominator derivative is exactly 2x+3.","[1] Set u=x squared+3x+5.","[1] Integral is ln|u|+C.","[1] The denominator is always positive, so answer $\\ln(x^2+3x+5)+C$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int(2x+3)/(x^2+3x+5)dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Denominator derivative is exactly 2x+3.","[1] Set u=x squared+3x+5.","[1] Integral is ln|u|+C.","[1] The denominator is always positive, so answer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(x^2+3x+5)+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-log-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Evaluate $\\int_0^1dx/(2x+3)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Antiderivative is half ln|2x+3|.","[1] Denominator is positive throughout [0,1].","[1] Endpoint subtraction is half (ln5-ln3).","[1] Value is $\\tfrac12\\ln(5/3)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^1dx/(2x+3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Antiderivative is half ln|2x+3|.","[1] Denominator is positive throughout [0,1].","[1] Endpoint subtraction is half (ln5-ln3).","[1] Value is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12\\ln(5/3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-log-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find $\\int dx/(3x-6)$ with its interval restriction. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Denominator derivative is 3.","[1] Antiderivative is $\\tfrac13\\ln|3x-6|+C$.","[1] Exclude x=2.","[1] Use the formula on an interval entirely to one side of 2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int dx/(3x-6)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with its interval restriction. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Denominator derivative is 3.","[1] Antiderivative is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac13\\ln|3x-6|+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Exclude x=2.","[1] Use the formula on an interval entirely to one side of 2."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-log-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find $\\int\\tan x\\,dx$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Write tan x=sin x/cos x.","[1] Denominator derivative is -sin x.","[1] Integral is $-\\ln|\\cos x|+C$.","[1] Valid intervals exclude zeros of cos x."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int\\tan x\\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Write tan x=sin x/cos x.","[1] Denominator derivative is -sin x.","[1] Integral is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\ln|\\cos x|+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Valid intervals exclude zeros of cos x."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-log-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Compare integrals of 1/x and $1/x^2$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] For 1/x, the power rule would divide by zero, so use ln|x|+C.","[1] For $x^{-2}$, power rule gives $-x^{-1}+C$.","[1] Both exclude x=0.","[1] Differentiation verifies the two distinct forms."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Compare integrals of 1/x and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] For 1/x, the power rule would divide by zero, so use ln|x|+C.","[1] For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, power rule gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-x^{-1}+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Both exclude x=0.","[1] Differentiation verifies the two distinct forms."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-log-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Evaluate $\\int_{-2}^{-1}dx/x$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] No zero lies in the interval, so a logarithmic antiderivative is valid.","[1] Use ln|x| at endpoints.","[1] Value is ln1-ln2=-ln2.","[1] It is negative, agreeing with the negative integrand."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_{-2}^{-1}dx/x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] No zero lies in the interval, so a logarithmic antiderivative is valid.","[1] Use ln|x| at endpoints.","[1] Value is ln1-ln2=-ln2.","[1] It is negative, agreeing with the negative integrand."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What is the antiderivative of f derivative divided by nonzero f?","o":["$\\ln|f(x)|+C$.","$\\ln f(x)$ without any domain condition.","$f(x)^2+C$.","$1/f(x)+C$ always."],"a":0,"why":"The absolute value supplies a real logarithm on either sign interval.","qHtml":"What is the antiderivative of f derivative divided by nonzero f?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln|f(x)|+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln f(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span> without any domain condition.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)^2+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/f(x)+C</annotation></semantics></math></span> always."],"whyHtml":"The absolute value supplies a real logarithm on either sign interval."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-integration-fractions","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Integration and applications","title":"Partial fractions and rational integration","outcomes":["4.1.4"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Decompose a proper rational expression · Repeated factors need every power · Find coefficients and integrate · Verify the identity and interval","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Partial fractions and rational integration</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Decompose a proper rational expression</li><li>Repeated factors need every power</li><li>Find coefficients and integrate</li><li>Verify the identity and interval</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Decompose a proper rational expression","text":"Partial fractions replace a rational expression by simpler terms whose denominators are factors of the original denominator. First use polynomial division if the numerator degree is not smaller. For distinct linear factors, use one constant numerator per factor. Solve coefficients by an identity valid for all permitted x, not just by one arbitrary numerical check.","diagram":null,"html":"Partial fractions replace a rational expression by simpler terms whose denominators are factors of the original denominator. First use polynomial division if the numerator degree is not smaller. For distinct linear factors, use one constant numerator per factor. Solve coefficients by an identity valid for all permitted x, not just by one arbitrary numerical check."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Repeated factors need every power","text":"A denominator containing $(x-a)^2$ needs terms A/(x-a)+B/(x-a) squared. Omitting the lower power may make the proposed decomposition impossible. An irreducible quadratic needs a linear numerator, although the required integration technique depends on its form. Factor accurately before choosing the template.","diagram":"substitution","html":"A denominator containing <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-a)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> needs terms A/(x-a)+B/(x-a) squared. Omitting the lower power may make the proposed decomposition impossible. An irreducible quadratic needs a linear numerator, although the required integration technique depends on its form. Factor accurately before choosing the template."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Find coefficients and integrate","text":"Multiply through by the common denominator. Substitute convenient roots to find some coefficients, then compare remaining coefficients or use extra values. Integrate linear-denominator terms logarithmically with their slope factors. Higher reciprocal powers use the power rule. Keep the original excluded inputs.","diagram":null,"html":"Multiply through by the common denominator. Substitute convenient roots to find some coefficients, then compare remaining coefficients or use extra values. Integrate linear-denominator terms logarithmically with their slope factors. Higher reciprocal powers use the power rule. Keep the original excluded inputs."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Verify the identity and interval","text":"Recombine the proposed fractions to check the numerator. For definite integrals, ensure no denominator zero lies inside the interval. A decomposition does not turn a divergent ordinary integral into a finite one. Show enough algebra to justify the coefficients and distinguish logarithmic terms from inverse-power terms.","diagram":null,"html":"Recombine the proposed fractions to check the numerator. For definite integrals, ensure no denominator zero lies inside the interval. A decomposition does not turn a divergent ordinary integral into a finite one. Show enough algebra to justify the coefficients and distinguish logarithmic terms from inverse-power terms."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-integration-fractions-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Integrate $(3x+5)/[(x+1)(x+2)]$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Set A/(x+1)+B/(x+2); numerator identity is $3x+5=A(x+2)+B(x+1)$.","[1] At x=-1, A=2; at x=-2, B=1.","[1] Integrate the two terms.","[1] Answer $2\\ln|x+1|+\\ln|x+2|+C$, excluding -1,-2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Integrate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3x+5)/[(x+1)(x+2)]</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Set A/(x+1)+B/(x+2); numerator identity is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3x+5=A(x+2)+B(x+1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] At x=-1, A=2; at x=-2, B=1.","[1] Integrate the two terms.","[1] Answer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\ln|x+1|+\\ln|x+2|+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>, excluding -1,-2."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-fractions-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Decompose and integrate $(2x+1)/(x-1)^2$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Template is A/(x-1)+B/(x-1) squared.","[1] $2x+1=A(x-1)+B$ gives A=2,B=3.","[1] Integrate 2/(x-1) and $3(x-1)^{-2}$.","[1] Answer $2\\ln|x-1|-3/(x-1)+C$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Decompose and integrate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2x+1)/(x-1)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Template is A/(x-1)+B/(x-1) squared.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x+1=A(x-1)+B</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives A=2,B=3.","[1] Integrate 2/(x-1) and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3(x-1)^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Answer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\ln|x-1|-3/(x-1)+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-fractions-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Integrate $1/(x^2-1)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Factor denominator (x-1)(x+1).","[1] Decomposition is half/(x-1) minus half/(x+1).","[1] Integrate logarithmically.","[1] Result $\\tfrac12\\ln|x-1|-\\tfrac12\\ln|x+1|+C$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Integrate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(x^2-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Factor denominator (x-1)(x+1).","[1] Decomposition is half/(x-1) minus half/(x+1).","[1] Integrate logarithmically.","[1] Result <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>−</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12\\ln|x-1|-\\tfrac12\\ln|x+1|+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-fractions-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Integrate $x^2/(x-1)$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Divide to get x+1+1/(x-1).","[1] Integrate the polynomial part.","[1] Integrate the last term as ln|x-1|.","[1] Answer $x^2/2+x+\\ln|x-1|+C$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Integrate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2/(x-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Divide to get x+1+1/(x-1).","[1] Integrate the polynomial part.","[1] Integrate the last term as ln|x-1|.","[1] Answer <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2/2+x+\\ln|x-1|+C</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-fractions-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Evaluate $\\int_0^1dx/[(x+1)(x+2)]$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Decompose as 1/(x+1)-1/(x+2).","[1] Antiderivative is ln(x+1)-ln(x+2) on [0,1].","[1] Endpoint subtraction is (ln2-ln3)-(ln1-ln2).","[1] Result is ln(4/3)."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^1dx/[(x+1)(x+2)]</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Decompose as 1/(x+1)-1/(x+2).","[1] Antiderivative is ln(x+1)-ln(x+2) on [0,1].","[1] Endpoint subtraction is (ln2-ln3)-(ln1-ln2).","[1] Result is ln(4/3)."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-fractions-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Why is A/(x-2) squared alone generally insufficient for a repeated factor $(x-2)^2$? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Multiplying through would give only a constant numerator A.","[1] A general linear numerator cannot be represented that way.","[1] Adding B/(x-2) produces a linear contribution B(x-2).","[1] Both powers are needed in the general template."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Why is A/(x-2) squared alone generally insufficient for a repeated factor <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-2)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Multiplying through would give only a constant numerator A.","[1] A general linear numerator cannot be represented that way.","[1] Adding B/(x-2) produces a linear contribution B(x-2).","[1] Both powers are needed in the general template."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What should precede partial fractions for an improper rational expression?","o":["Polynomial division.","Cancelling every denominator zero without checking.","Ignoring the polynomial part.","Replacing all factors by a single constant."],"a":0,"why":"Division separates a polynomial and a proper rational remainder.","qHtml":"What should precede partial fractions for an improper rational expression?","oHtml":["Polynomial division.","Cancelling every denominator zero without checking.","Ignoring the polynomial part.","Replacing all factors by a single constant."],"whyHtml":"Division separates a polynomial and a proper rational remainder."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-integration-area","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Integration and applications","title":"Areas between curves with either variable","outcomes":["4.1.5"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Area uses positive separation · Integrate with respect to y when simpler · Separate signed integrals from geometry · Check the region","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Areas between curves with either variable</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Area uses positive separation</li><li>Integrate with respect to y when simpler</li><li>Separate signed integrals from geometry</li><li>Check the region</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Area uses positive separation","text":"For upper y=f(x) and lower y=g(x), area is $\\int_a^b[f(x)-g(x)]dx$. Find intersections to establish bounds and identify which curve is above on each interval. If the order changes, split the integral. Taking an absolute value only after adding signed pieces can conceal cancellation and lose area.","diagram":null,"html":"For upper y=f(x) and lower y=g(x), area is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_a^b[f(x)-g(x)]dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find intersections to establish bounds and identify which curve is above on each interval. If the order changes, split the integral. Taking an absolute value only after adding signed pieces can conceal cancellation and lose area."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Integrate with respect to y when simpler","text":"If curves are x=F(y),x=G(y), use right minus left and dy. This can avoid solving a sideways parabola into separate square-root branches. Bounds are y-coordinates, not x-coordinates. A sketch helps choose the slicing direction and identify every boundary.","diagram":"area","html":"If curves are x=F(y),x=G(y), use right minus left and dy. This can avoid solving a sideways parabola into separate square-root branches. Bounds are y-coordinates, not x-coordinates. A sketch helps choose the slicing direction and identify every boundary."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate signed integrals from geometry","text":"A definite integral of f can measure signed area relative to the x-axis. Geometric area is nonnegative and uses |f| or sign-split pieces. Units are squared length. An intersection outside the specified region need not become a bound; interpret the problem's full boundary description.","diagram":null,"html":"A definite integral of f can measure signed area relative to the x-axis. Geometric area is nonnegative and uses |f| or sign-split pieces. Units are squared length. An intersection outside the specified region need not become a bound; interpret the problem&#39;s full boundary description."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Check the region","text":"Label the curves and representative vertical or horizontal strip. A numeric graph can help identify order but exact intersection algebra gives the intended boundaries. Verify a positive area and sensible size. For complex shapes, partition into regions whose slicing description remains consistent.","diagram":null,"html":"Label the curves and representative vertical or horizontal strip. A numeric graph can help identify order but exact intersection algebra gives the intended boundaries. Verify a positive area and sensible size. For complex shapes, partition into regions whose slicing description remains consistent."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-integration-area-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find the area between y=x and y=$x^2$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Intersections satisfy x=x squared, so x=0,1.","[1] On (0,1), x is above x squared.","[1] Area is $\\int_0^1(x-x^2)dx$.","[1] Value is 1/2-1/3=1/6 square units."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the area between y=x and y=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Intersections satisfy x=x squared, so x=0,1.","[1] On (0,1), x is above x squared.","[1] Area is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^1(x-x^2)dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Value is 1/2-1/3=1/6 square units."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-area-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find area between x=y+2 and x=$y^2$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Intersections give y squared=y+2, so y=-1,2.","[1] The line is right of the parabola between these bounds.","[1] Area is $\\int_{-1}^2(y+2-y^2)dy$.","[1] Value is 9/2 square units."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find area between x=y+2 and x=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Intersections give y squared=y+2, so y=-1,2.","[1] The line is right of the parabola between these bounds.","[1] Area is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_{-1}^2(y+2-y^2)dy</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Value is 9/2 square units."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-area-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find area enclosed by y=$4-x^2$ and y=0. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Zeros are x=-2,2.","[1] The parabola is nonnegative between them.","[1] Area is $\\int_{-2}^2(4-x^2)dx$.","[1] Value is 32/3 square units."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find area enclosed by y=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and y=0. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Zeros are x=-2,2.","[1] The parabola is nonnegative between them.","[1] Area is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_{-2}^2(4-x^2)dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Value is 32/3 square units."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-area-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find area between y=sin x and the x-axis on [0,2pi]. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Sin x changes sign at pi.","[1] Area is integral sin x from 0 to pi minus integral sin x from pi to 2pi.","[1] Each positive area is 2.","[1] Total is 4, although the signed integral over the full interval is zero."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find area between y=sin x and the x-axis on [0,2pi]. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Sin x changes sign at pi.","[1] Area is integral sin x from 0 to pi minus integral sin x from pi to 2pi.","[1] Each positive area is 2.","[1] Total is 4, although the signed integral over the full interval is zero."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-area-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find area between y=$x^3$ and y=x on [-1,1]. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Intersections are -1,0,1.","[1] Order changes at zero.","[1] By symmetry area is $2\\int_0^1(x-x^3)dx$.","[1] Result is 2(1/2-1/4)=1/2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find area between y=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^3</annotation></semantics></math></span> and y=x on [-1,1]. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Intersections are -1,0,1.","[1] Order changes at zero.","[1] By symmetry area is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\int_0^1(x-x^3)dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result is 2(1/2-1/4)=1/2."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-area-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Find area bounded by x=0,x=$4-y^2$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Intersections with x=0 give y=-2,2.","[1] Right minus left is 4-y squared.","[1] Area is $\\int_{-2}^2(4-y^2)dy$.","[1] Value is 32/3 square units; horizontal slicing is direct."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find area bounded by x=0,x=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-y^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Intersections with x=0 give y=-2,2.","[1] Right minus left is 4-y squared.","[1] Area is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_{-2}^2(4-y^2)dy</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Value is 32/3 square units; horizontal slicing is direct."]}],"bank":[{"q":"For integration with respect to y, which difference gives strip width?","o":["Right boundary minus left boundary.","Upper boundary minus lower boundary regardless of variable.","Always the product of boundaries.","Left minus right for positive width."],"a":0,"why":"Horizontal strips have width measured along the x direction.","qHtml":"For integration with respect to y, which difference gives strip width?","oHtml":["Right boundary minus left boundary.","Upper boundary minus lower boundary regardless of variable.","Always the product of boundaries.","Left minus right for positive width."],"whyHtml":"Horizontal strips have width measured along the x direction."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-integration-volumes","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Integration and applications","title":"Solids of revolution about either axis","outcomes":["4.1.6"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Disks and washers · Rotation about the y-axis · Shifted axes and overlapping regions · Verify dimensions and reasonableness","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Solids of revolution about either axis</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Disks and washers</li><li>Rotation about the y-axis</li><li>Shifted axes and overlapping regions</li><li>Verify dimensions and reasonableness</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Disks and washers","text":"Rotating y=f(x) about the x-axis creates disks with cross-sectional area pi f(x) squared when the region extends to the axis. For a region between radii R and r, washer area is $\\pi(R^2-r^2)$. Subtract squared radii, not square the difference. Identify distances from the rotation axis, which are nonnegative lengths.","diagram":null,"html":"Rotating y=f(x) about the x-axis creates disks with cross-sectional area pi f(x) squared when the region extends to the axis. For a region between radii R and r, washer area is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(R^2-r^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Subtract squared radii, not square the difference. Identify distances from the rotation axis, which are nonnegative lengths."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Rotation about the y-axis","text":"With x=F(y), disks perpendicular to the y-axis give volume $\\pi\\int F(y)^2dy$. Washers again use outer minus inner squared radii. Choose bounds in the integration variable. Do not rotate a drawing mentally without specifying the original region and cross-section.","diagram":"washer","html":"With x=F(y), disks perpendicular to the y-axis give volume <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∫</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi\\int F(y)^2dy</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Washers again use outer minus inner squared radii. Choose bounds in the integration variable. Do not rotate a drawing mentally without specifying the original region and cross-section."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Shifted axes and overlapping regions","text":"For an axis y=c, radii are distances from that line, not simply the original function values. If the region crosses the axis, the farther boundary determines the outer radius and an inner hole may disappear. Split when the cross-section formula changes. A graph supports the setup, but the radius equations justify it.","diagram":null,"html":"For an axis y=c, radii are distances from that line, not simply the original function values. If the region crosses the axis, the farther boundary determines the outer radius and an inner hole may disappear. Split when the cross-section formula changes. A graph supports the setup, but the radius equations justify it."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Verify dimensions and reasonableness","text":"Volume has cubic units. A graph length factor does not automatically equal a volume factor; the disk area involves a square. Check the interval, positivity of cross-sectional area and plausible bounds such as a containing cylinder. Differentiate an antiderivative or use numeric integration to verify arithmetic.","diagram":null,"html":"Volume has cubic units. A graph length factor does not automatically equal a volume factor; the disk area involves a square. Check the interval, positivity of cross-sectional area and plausible bounds such as a containing cylinder. Differentiate an antiderivative or use numeric integration to verify arithmetic."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Extended exam task","q":6},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-integration-volumes-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Rotate the region under y=x on 0<=x<=2 about the x-axis. Find volume. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Radius is x.","[1] Disk area is pi x squared.","[1] Volume is $\\pi\\int_0^2x^2dx$.","[1] Result is $8\\pi/3$ cubic units."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Rotate the region under y=x on 0&lt;=x&lt;=2 about the x-axis. Find volume. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Radius is x.","[1] Disk area is pi x squared.","[1] Volume is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi\\int_0^2x^2dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span> cubic units."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-volumes-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Rotate the region between y=2 and y=x on 0<=x<=2 about the x-axis. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Outer radius is 2, inner radius x.","[1] Washer area is pi(4-x squared).","[1] Integrate from 0 to 2.","[1] Volume is $\\pi(8-8/3)=16\\pi/3$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Rotate the region between y=2 and y=x on 0&lt;=x&lt;=2 about the x-axis. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Outer radius is 2, inner radius x.","[1] Washer area is pi(4-x squared).","[1] Integrate from 0 to 2.","[1] Volume is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(8-8/3)=16\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-volumes-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Rotate the region between x=0 and x=$\\sqrt y$ for 0<=y<=4 about the y-axis. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Radius is square root y.","[1] Disk area is pi y.","[1] Volume is $\\pi\\int_0^4y\\,dy$.","[1] Result is $8\\pi$ cubic units."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Rotate the region between x=0 and x=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>y</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt y</annotation></semantics></math></span> for 0&lt;=y&lt;=4 about the y-axis. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Radius is square root y.","[1] Disk area is pi y.","[1] Volume is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi\\int_0^4y\\,dy</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> cubic units."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-volumes-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Rotate the region between y=x and y=1, 0<=x<=1, about y=1. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Distance to the lower boundary is 1-x.","[1] The region touches the axis, so there is no inner hole.","[1] Volume is $\\pi\\int_0^1(1-x)^2dx$.","[1] Result is pi/3."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Rotate the region between y=x and y=1, 0&lt;=x&lt;=1, about y=1. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Distance to the lower boundary is 1-x.","[1] The region touches the axis, so there is no inner hole.","[1] Volume is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi\\int_0^1(1-x)^2dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result is pi/3."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-volumes-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Rotate the region under y=$\\sqrt{9-x^2}$ on [-3,3] about the x-axis. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Squared radius is 9-x squared.","[1] Volume is $\\pi\\int_{-3}^3(9-x^2)dx$.","[1] Result is $36\\pi$.","[1] This agrees with the sphere formula for radius 3."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Rotate the region under y=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{9-x^2}</annotation></semantics></math></span> on [-3,3] about the x-axis. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Squared radius is 9-x squared.","[1] Volume is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi\\int_{-3}^3(9-x^2)dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>36</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">36\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] This agrees with the sphere formula for radius 3."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-volumes-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Explain the error in using pi(3-1) squared for a washer with outer radius 3 and inner radius 1. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The cross-section is an outer disk with an inner disk removed.","[1] Their areas are 9pi and pi.","[1] Correct washer area is 8pi.","[1] Squaring the radial thickness gives 4pi and does not represent the annulus."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain the error in using pi(3-1) squared for a washer with outer radius 3 and inner radius 1. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The cross-section is an outer disk with an inner disk removed.","[1] Their areas are 9pi and pi.","[1] Correct washer area is 8pi.","[1] Squaring the radial thickness gives 4pi and does not represent the annulus."]},{"prompt":"The region between y=x and $y=x^2$ is rotated about the x-axis. Find its original area and resulting volume. Then rotate the same region about the y-axis using horizontal washers and compare the two volumes. (8 marks)","steps":["[1] Intersections are x=0,1, and the line is above the parabola between them.","[1] Original area is $\\int_0^1(x-x^2)dx=1/6$.","[2] About the x-axis, $V_x=\\pi\\int_0^1(x^2-x^4)dx=2\\pi/15$.","[2] At a height y between 0 and 1, the region extends from x=y to $x=\\sqrt y$.","[2] About the y-axis, $V_y=\\pi\\int_0^1(y-y^2)dy=\\pi/6$, larger than $2\\pi/15$ by $\\pi/30$."],"id":"mas12-integration-volumes-extended","title":"Extended exam task","allocations":[1,1,2,2,2],"marks":8,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed or exact working","promptHtml":"The region between y=x and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is rotated about the x-axis. Find its original area and resulting volume. Then rotate the same region about the y-axis using horizontal washers and compare the two volumes. (8 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Intersections are x=0,1, and the line is above the parabola between them.","[1] Original area is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^1(x-x^2)dx=1/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[2] About the x-axis, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_x=\\pi\\int_0^1(x^2-x^4)dx=2\\pi/15</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[2] At a height y between 0 and 1, the region extends from x=y to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>y</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\sqrt y</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[2] About the y-axis, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_y=\\pi\\int_0^1(y-y^2)dy=\\pi/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>, larger than <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/15</annotation></semantics></math></span> by <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/30</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What is a washer cross-sectional area?","o":["$\\pi(R^2-r^2)$.","$\\pi(R-r)^2$.","$2\\pi(R-r)$.","$\\pi(R+r)$."],"a":0,"why":"It subtracts the inner disk area from the outer disk area.","qHtml":"What is a washer cross-sectional area?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(R^2-r^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(R-r)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi(R-r)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi(R+r)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."],"whyHtml":"It subtracts the inner disk area from the outer disk area."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 5 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-integration-numerical","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Integration and applications","title":"Numerical integration, technology and model checks","outcomes":["4.1.7"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Technology estimates a definite integral · Approximation has accuracy limits · Check the reported number · Apply to area and distance","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Numerical integration, technology and model checks</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Technology estimates a definite integral</li><li>Approximation has accuracy limits</li><li>Check the reported number</li><li>Apply to area and distance</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Technology estimates a definite integral","text":"Some integrands have no elementary antiderivative suitable for the course. A calculator or software numerical-integral command uses a specified function, variable and bounds. Enter brackets and units carefully, and record the definite integral before the decimal answer. Check that the chosen interval does not contain an unhandled singularity.","diagram":null,"html":"Some integrands have no elementary antiderivative suitable for the course. A calculator or software numerical-integral command uses a specified function, variable and bounds. Enter brackets and units carefully, and record the definite integral before the decimal answer. Check that the chosen interval does not contain an unhandled singularity."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Approximation has accuracy limits","text":"Numerical quadrature samples and combines function values to estimate area. More subdivisions can improve simple methods but do not guarantee accuracy for every rapidly changing or discontinuous function. The trapezoidal rule illustrates the idea: endpoints have weight one and interior points weight two, with factor h/2. SCSA requires technology evaluation; this rule supplies an explanation of approximation rather than a substitute for that skill.","diagram":"numerical","html":"Numerical quadrature samples and combines function values to estimate area. More subdivisions can improve simple methods but do not guarantee accuracy for every rapidly changing or discontinuous function. The trapezoidal rule illustrates the idea: endpoints have weight one and interior points weight two, with factor h/2. SCSA requires technology evaluation; this rule supplies an explanation of approximation rather than a substitute for that skill."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Check the reported number","text":"Compare with sign, upper and lower bounds and a rough graph. A nonnegative integrand must give a nonnegative ordinary integral. Retain guard digits until a requested final rounding. A calculator can produce a value with many digits whose justified accuracy is less. Changing the window on a graph is not itself a numeric integration method.","diagram":null,"html":"Compare with sign, upper and lower bounds and a rough graph. A nonnegative integrand must give a nonnegative ordinary integral. Retain guard digits until a requested final rounding. A calculator can produce a value with many digits whose justified accuracy is less. Changing the window on a graph is not itself a numeric integration method."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply to area and distance","text":"For geometric area, enter the correct positive separation or split at intersections. For distance, integrate speed, not a signed velocity component. State model assumptions and units with the final approximation. Different devices use different menus; the mathematical setup should remain reproducible across them.","diagram":null,"html":"For geometric area, enter the correct positive separation or split at intersections. For distance, integrate speed, not a signed velocity component. State model assumptions and units with the final approximation. Different devices use different menus; the mathematical setup should remain reproducible across them."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-integration-numerical-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Use numerical integration for $\\int_0^1e^{-x^2}dx$, to four decimals, and give a rough bound. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Enter the integrand and bounds 0,1 in a numerical-integral command.","[1] Computation gives approximately 0.7468241328.","[1] Rounded value is 0.7468.","[1] Since $e^{-1}\\le e^{-x^2}\\le1$, the integral lies between e inverse and 1."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Use numerical integration for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^1e^{-x^2}dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>, to four decimals, and give a rough bound. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Enter the integrand and bounds 0,1 in a numerical-integral command.","[1] Computation gives approximately 0.7468241328.","[1] Rounded value is 0.7468.","[1] Since <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-1}\\le e^{-x^2}\\le1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the integral lies between e inverse and 1."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-numerical-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Approximate $\\int_0^1x^2dx$ using two equal trapezoids and compare with its exact value. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] h=1/2, sampled values are 0,1/4,1.","[1] Trapezoidal estimate is $(h/2)[0+2(1/4)+1]=3/8$.","[1] Exact integral is 1/3.","[1] The estimate is high by 1/24, illustrating that numerical estimates are not exact identities."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Approximate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^1x^2dx</annotation></semantics></math></span> using two equal trapezoids and compare with its exact value. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] h=1/2, sampled values are 0,1/4,1.","[1] Trapezoidal estimate is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(h/2)[0+2(1/4)+1]=3/8</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Exact integral is 1/3.","[1] The estimate is high by 1/24, illustrating that numerical estimates are not exact identities."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-numerical-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Numerically evaluate $\\int_0^2\\sqrt{1+x^2}dx$ to three decimals. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Set up the definite integral with a continuous positive integrand.","[1] Numerical value is approximately 2.957885715.","[1] Rounded answer is 2.958.","[1] It lies between 2 and $2\\sqrt5$, providing a coarse check."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Numerically evaluate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^2\\sqrt{1+x^2}dx</annotation></semantics></math></span> to three decimals. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Set up the definite integral with a continuous positive integrand.","[1] Numerical value is approximately 2.957885715.","[1] Rounded answer is 2.958.","[1] It lies between 2 and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sqrt5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, providing a coarse check."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-numerical-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"A particle has velocity (1,t,0) on 0<=t<=2. Set up and numerically find distance. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Speed is square root(1+t squared).","[1] Distance is $\\int_0^2\\sqrt{1+t^2}dt$.","[1] Numerical value is approximately 2.958 distance units.","[1] Displacement magnitude is $\\sqrt8$, smaller than this curved-path distance."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"A particle has velocity (1,t,0) on 0&lt;=t&lt;=2. Set up and numerically find distance. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Speed is square root(1+t squared).","[1] Distance is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^2\\sqrt{1+t^2}dt</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Numerical value is approximately 2.958 distance units.","[1] Displacement magnitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>8</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt8</annotation></semantics></math></span>, smaller than this curved-path distance."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-numerical-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Give upper and lower bounds for $\\int_1^2dx/(1+x)$ without finding an antiderivative. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The integrand decreases from 1/2 to 1/3.","[1] Interval width is 1.","[1] Rectangle comparisons give bounds 1/3 and 1/2.","[1] A numerical answer outside this interval signals an input or computation error."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Give upper and lower bounds for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_1^2dx/(1+x)</annotation></semantics></math></span> without finding an antiderivative. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The integrand decreases from 1/2 to 1/3.","[1] Interval width is 1.","[1] Rectangle comparisons give bounds 1/3 and 1/2.","[1] A numerical answer outside this interval signals an input or computation error."]},{"id":"mas12-integration-numerical-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A calculator returns a small finite value for an integral across a pole. What must be checked before accepting it? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Inspect the integrand domain and locate denominator zeros.","[1] Determine whether any lies inside the interval.","[1] Ordinary endpoint-antiderivative or numeric evaluation may be invalid across that singularity.","[1] A software output alone does not establish existence of the ordinary integral."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"A calculator returns a small finite value for an integral across a pole. What must be checked before accepting it? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Inspect the integrand domain and locate denominator zeros.","[1] Determine whether any lies inside the interval.","[1] Ordinary endpoint-antiderivative or numeric evaluation may be invalid across that singularity.","[1] A software output alone does not establish existence of the ordinary integral."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What should accompany a numerical integral answer?","o":["The mathematical integral setup, rounding and contextual units.","Only the calculator model name.","Only a graph-window screenshot.","An assertion that every displayed digit is exact."],"a":0,"why":"The setup communicates what was computed and allows independent checking.","qHtml":"What should accompany a numerical integral answer?","oHtml":["The mathematical integral setup, rounding and contextual units.","Only the calculator model name.","Only a graph-window screenshot.","An assertion that every displayed digit is exact."],"whyHtml":"The setup communicates what was computed and allows independent checking."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-implicit-first","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Rates and differential equations","title":"Implicit differentiation and tangent gradients","outcomes":["4.2.1"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Differentiate with y depending on x · Collect the gradient terms · Find tangents and normals · Preserve curve restrictions","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Implicit differentiation and tangent gradients</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Differentiate with y depending on x</li><li>Collect the gradient terms</li><li>Find tangents and normals</li><li>Preserve curve restrictions</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Differentiate with y depending on x","text":"An implicit relation F(x,y)=0 can describe a curve without an explicit y(x) formula. When differentiating with respect to x, treat y as y(x): the derivative of $y^2$ is $2y\\,dy/dx$. Terms such as xy need the product rule, giving y+x dy/dx. Omitting the y derivative changes the meaning of the equation.","diagram":null,"html":"An implicit relation F(x,y)=0 can describe a curve without an explicit y(x) formula. When differentiating with respect to x, treat y as y(x): the derivative of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2y\\,dy/dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Terms such as xy need the product rule, giving y+x dy/dx. Omitting the y derivative changes the meaning of the equation."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Collect the gradient terms","text":"After differentiating, gather terms involving dy/dx and solve where their coefficient is nonzero. For $x^2+y^2=R^2$, the slope is -x/y when y is nonzero. At y=0 this formula is undefined; the circle has a vertical tangent there when x is nonzero. An undefined quotient alone does not settle every singular case.","diagram":"implicit","html":"After differentiating, gather terms involving dy/dx and solve where their coefficient is nonzero. For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2=R^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the slope is -x/y when y is nonzero. At y=0 this formula is undefined; the circle has a vertical tangent there when x is nonzero. An undefined quotient alone does not settle every singular case."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Find tangents and normals","text":"Check the supplied point lies on the original curve, then evaluate the derivative there. A finite gradient m gives tangent y-y0=m(x-x0). A nonzero m gives normal gradient -1/m. Horizontal and vertical special cases need their corresponding vertical or horizontal normals rather than dividing by zero.","diagram":null,"html":"Check the supplied point lies on the original curve, then evaluate the derivative there. A finite gradient m gives tangent y-y0=m(x-x0). A nonzero m gives normal gradient -1/m. Horizontal and vertical special cases need their corresponding vertical or horizontal normals rather than dividing by zero."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Preserve curve restrictions","text":"A candidate point found from a gradient condition must also satisfy the original implicit equation. Squaring or algebraic rearrangement can introduce extra candidates. A curve may have multiple branches or a point where it cannot be represented by one smooth y(x). State the eligible branch and point where required.","diagram":null,"html":"A candidate point found from a gradient condition must also satisfy the original implicit equation. Squaring or algebraic rearrangement can introduce extra candidates. A curve may have multiple branches or a point where it cannot be represented by one smooth y(x). State the eligible branch and point where required."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-implicit-first-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find the tangent to $x^2+y^2=25$ at (3,4). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The point satisfies 9+16=25.","[1] Differentiate: 2x+2y dy/dx=0.","[1] Slope at the point is -3/4.","[1] Tangent is $y-4=-(3/4)(x-3)$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the tangent to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2=25</annotation></semantics></math></span> at (3,4). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The point satisfies 9+16=25.","[1] Differentiate: 2x+2y dy/dx=0.","[1] Slope at the point is -3/4.","[1] Tangent is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y-4=-(3/4)(x-3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-implicit-first-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find dy/dx for $x^2+xy+y^2=7$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Differentiate all terms: 2x+y+x dy/dx+2y dy/dx=0.","[1] Collect: (x+2y)dy/dx=-(2x+y).","[1] Gradient is $-(2x+y)/(x+2y)$.","[1] Formula requires x+2y nonzero; exceptional points need separate analysis."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find dy/dx for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+xy+y^2=7</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Differentiate all terms: 2x+y+x dy/dx+2y dy/dx=0.","[1] Collect: (x+2y)dy/dx=-(2x+y).","[1] Gradient is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-(2x+y)/(x+2y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Formula requires x+2y nonzero; exceptional points need separate analysis."]},{"id":"mas12-implicit-first-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find the normal to $x^2+y^2=25$ at (3,4). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Tangent gradient is -3/4.","[1] Normal gradient is 4/3.","[1] Equation is $y-4=(4/3)(x-3)$.","[1] It passes through the centre, as the radius is normal to a circle."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the normal to <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2=25</annotation></semantics></math></span> at (3,4). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Tangent gradient is -3/4.","[1] Normal gradient is 4/3.","[1] Equation is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y-4=(4/3)(x-3)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] It passes through the centre, as the radius is normal to a circle."]},{"id":"mas12-implicit-first-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Find the tangent to the same circle at (5,0). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The point lies on the circle.","[1] Differentiated equation gives 10+0 times dy/dx=0, so no finite slope exists there.","[1] The radius is horizontal.","[1] Tangent is vertical: x=5."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the tangent to the same circle at (5,0). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The point lies on the circle.","[1] Differentiated equation gives 10+0 times dy/dx=0, so no finite slope exists there.","[1] The radius is horizontal.","[1] Tangent is vertical: x=5."]},{"id":"mas12-implicit-first-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Find dy/dx for $y^3+x^2y=8$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Differentiate y cubed as 3y squared dy/dx.","[1] Product term gives 2xy+x squared dy/dx.","[1] Collect: $(3y^2+x^2)dy/dx=-2xy$.","[1] Gradient is $-2xy/(3y^2+x^2)$ where the denominator is nonzero."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find dy/dx for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^3+x^2y=8</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Differentiate y cubed as 3y squared dy/dx.","[1] Product term gives 2xy+x squared dy/dx.","[1] Collect: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3y^2+x^2)dy/dx=-2xy</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Gradient is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2xy/(3y^2+x^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> where the denominator is nonzero."]},{"id":"mas12-implicit-first-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Find horizontal tangents on $x^2+y^2=9$. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] For a finite zero slope, -x/y=0 requires x=0,y nonzero.","[1] Original equation gives y=3 or -3.","[1] Points are (0,3),(0,-3).","[1] Tangents are y=3 and y=-3."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find horizontal tangents on <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2=9</annotation></semantics></math></span>. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] For a finite zero slope, -x/y=0 requires x=0,y nonzero.","[1] Original equation gives y=3 or -3.","[1] Points are (0,3),(0,-3).","[1] Tangents are y=3 and y=-3."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What is the x-derivative of $y^2$ when y depends on x?","o":["$2y\\,dy/dx$.","2y only.","2x.","Zero for all implicit curves."],"a":0,"why":"The chain rule includes the derivative of the dependent variable.","qHtml":"What is the x-derivative of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> when y depends on x?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2y\\,dy/dx</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","2y only.","2x.","Zero for all implicit curves."],"whyHtml":"The chain rule includes the derivative of the dependent variable."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-implicit-second","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Rates and differential equations","title":"Second implicit derivatives and local shape","outcomes":["4.2.1"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Differentiate the first derivative as a function · The differentiated equation can be simpler · Evaluate locally and classify · Check branches and exceptional points","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Second implicit derivatives and local shape</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Differentiate the first derivative as a function</li><li>The differentiated equation can be simpler</li><li>Evaluate locally and classify</li><li>Check branches and exceptional points</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Differentiate the first derivative as a function","text":"A first derivative expression may contain both x and y. Its derivative therefore uses dy/dx again. The second derivative describes how the tangent gradient changes with x, not the derivative with respect to y alone. Use product or quotient rules with full chain factors.","diagram":null,"html":"A first derivative expression may contain both x and y. Its derivative therefore uses dy/dx again. The second derivative describes how the tangent gradient changes with x, not the derivative with respect to y alone. Use product or quotient rules with full chain factors."},{"kind":"learn","title":"The differentiated equation can be simpler","text":"From $x^2+y^2=R^2$, differentiate $2x+2yy^{\\prime}=0$ again to get $2+2(y^{\\prime})^2+2yy^{\\prime\\prime}=0$. Hence $y^{\\prime\\prime}=-(1+(y^{\\prime})^2)/y$ where y is nonzero. Substitute the first derivative and original curve relation to simplify. This method can be clearer than a large quotient expansion.","diagram":"implicit","html":"From <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2=R^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, differentiate <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x+2yy^{\\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> again to get <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+2(y^{\\prime})^2+2yy^{\\prime\\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^{\\prime\\prime}=-(1+(y^{\\prime})^2)/y</annotation></semantics></math></span> where y is nonzero. Substitute the first derivative and original curve relation to simplify. This method can be clearer than a large quotient expansion."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Evaluate locally and classify","text":"At a valid point, evaluate y derivative and then second derivative. Positive y second derivative means local concavity upward and negative means downward where a smooth graph exists. A zero second derivative alone does not establish an inflection; the concavity must change. A stationary point also requires the first derivative to vanish.","diagram":null,"html":"At a valid point, evaluate y derivative and then second derivative. Positive y second derivative means local concavity upward and negative means downward where a smooth graph exists. A zero second derivative alone does not establish an inflection; the concavity must change. A stationary point also requires the first derivative to vanish."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Check branches and exceptional points","text":"An implicit curve can have upper and lower branches with different concavity. If the coefficient of y second derivative is zero, the formula may not apply. Do not divide through a zero and present an infinite second derivative as an ordinary number. A vertical tangent calls for a different local coordinate description.","diagram":null,"html":"An implicit curve can have upper and lower branches with different concavity. If the coefficient of y second derivative is zero, the formula may not apply. Do not divide through a zero and present an infinite second derivative as an ordinary number. A vertical tangent calls for a different local coordinate description."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-implicit-second-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Find the second derivative of the radius-5 circle at (3,4). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] First derivative is -x/y, so at the point y derivative=-3/4.","[1] Differentiate again: $1+(y^{\\prime})^2+yy^{\\prime\\prime}=0$.","[1] $y^{\\prime\\prime}=-(1+9/16)/4$.","[1] Result is -25/64, so the local upper branch is concave downward."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find the second derivative of the radius-5 circle at (3,4). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] First derivative is -x/y, so at the point y derivative=-3/4.","[1] Differentiate again: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+(y^{\\prime})^2+yy^{\\prime\\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^{\\prime\\prime}=-(1+9/16)/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Result is -25/64, so the local upper branch is concave downward."]},{"id":"mas12-implicit-second-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Find a general second derivative for $xy=1$ on x nonzero. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] First differentiation gives y+x y derivative=0.","[1] Differentiate again: 2y derivative+x y second derivative=0.","[1] Use y=1/x and y derivative=-1/x squared.","[1] Second derivative is 2/x cubed, matching the explicit reciprocal rule."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find a general second derivative for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">xy=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> on x nonzero. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] First differentiation gives y+x y derivative=0.","[1] Differentiate again: 2y derivative+x y second derivative=0.","[1] Use y=1/x and y derivative=-1/x squared.","[1] Second derivative is 2/x cubed, matching the explicit reciprocal rule."]},{"id":"mas12-implicit-second-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"For $x^2+y^2=9$, find y second derivative at (0,-3). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] First derivative at the point is zero.","[1] Relation is $1+(y^{\\prime})^2+yy^{\\prime\\prime}=0$.","[1] Thus $1-3y^{\\prime\\prime}=0$.","[1] Second derivative is 1/3, consistent with a local minimum on the lower branch."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2=9</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find y second derivative at (0,-3). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] First derivative at the point is zero.","[1] Relation is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+(y^{\\prime})^2+yy^{\\prime\\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Thus <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-3y^{\\prime\\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Second derivative is 1/3, consistent with a local minimum on the lower branch."]},{"id":"mas12-implicit-second-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"For $x^2+xy+y^2=3$, find first and second derivatives at (1,1). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Point satisfies the curve; first derivative is -(2x+y)/(x+2y)=-1.","[1] Differentiate the first implicit equation: $2+2y^{\\prime}+2(y^{\\prime})^2+(x+2y)y^{\\prime\\prime}=0$.","[1] At (1,1), this is 2-2+2+3y second derivative=0.","[1] Hence second derivative is -2/3."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+xy+y^2=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find first and second derivatives at (1,1). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Point satisfies the curve; first derivative is -(2x+y)/(x+2y)=-1.","[1] Differentiate the first implicit equation: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+2y^{\\prime}+2(y^{\\prime})^2+(x+2y)y^{\\prime\\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] At (1,1), this is 2-2+2+3y second derivative=0.","[1] Hence second derivative is -2/3."]},{"id":"mas12-implicit-second-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Explain why y second derivative=0 at a point does not by itself prove an inflection. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] An inflection requires a change of concavity across the point.","[1] A zero second derivative can occur without a sign change.","[1] For example, y=x fourth power has second derivative 12x squared, zero at 0 but nonnegative on both sides.","[1] Thus zero is only a candidate test requiring further evidence."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why y second derivative=0 at a point does not by itself prove an inflection. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] An inflection requires a change of concavity across the point.","[1] A zero second derivative can occur without a sign change.","[1] For example, y=x fourth power has second derivative 12x squared, zero at 0 but nonnegative on both sides.","[1] Thus zero is only a candidate test requiring further evidence."]},{"id":"mas12-implicit-second-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Derive y second derivative for $y^2=x$ on its positive branch. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Differentiate: 2y y derivative=1, so y derivative=1/(2y).","[1] Differentiate again: $2(y^{\\prime})^2+2yy^{\\prime\\prime}=0$.","[1] Therefore y second derivative=-1/(4y cubed).","[1] On y>0 this is negative, agreeing with y=square root x."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Derive y second derivative for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^2=x</annotation></semantics></math></span> on its positive branch. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Differentiate: 2y y derivative=1, so y derivative=1/(2y).","[1] Differentiate again: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(y^{\\prime})^2+2yy^{\\prime\\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Therefore y second derivative=-1/(4y cubed).","[1] On y&gt;0 this is negative, agreeing with y=square root x."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which term appears when differentiating y times y derivative?","o":["The square of y derivative plus y times y second derivative.","Only y second derivative.","Only the square of y.","Zero."],"a":0,"why":"The product rule differentiates both factors.","qHtml":"Which term appears when differentiating y times y derivative?","oHtml":["The square of y derivative plus y times y second derivative.","Only y second derivative.","Only the square of y.","Zero."],"whyHtml":"The product rule differentiates both factors."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-related-rates","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Rates and differential equations","title":"Related rates, chain rules and signed changes","outcomes":["4.2.2"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Choose a relation valid throughout the motion · Apply the chain rule · Use geometry to eliminate extra variables · Finish with a contextual conclusion","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Related rates, chain rules and signed changes</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Choose a relation valid throughout the motion</li><li>Apply the chain rule</li><li>Use geometry to eliminate extra variables</li><li>Finish with a contextual conclusion</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Choose a relation valid throughout the motion","text":"Define the changing quantities and connect them by geometry or another model equation. Differentiate with respect to common time before substituting instantaneous values. Replacing a changing length by its current value before differentiation can incorrectly treat it as constant. State units and assumptions such as a fixed ladder length or fixed cone shape.","diagram":null,"html":"Define the changing quantities and connect them by geometry or another model equation. Differentiate with respect to common time before substituting instantaneous values. Replacing a changing length by its current value before differentiation can incorrectly treat it as constant. State units and assumptions such as a fixed ladder length or fixed cone shape."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply the chain rule","text":"If y=f(x(t)), then dy/dt=(dy/dx)(dx/dt). A square produces 2x dx/dt, and a volume can combine several changing dimensions through the product rule. Use given rates with their signs: decreasing radius means a negative radial rate. A question asking how quickly something decreases may want the positive magnitude with the decrease stated.","diagram":"ladder","html":"If y=f(x(t)), then dy/dt=(dy/dx)(dx/dt). A square produces 2x dx/dt, and a volume can combine several changing dimensions through the product rule. Use given rates with their signs: decreasing radius means a negative radial rate. A question asking how quickly something decreases may want the positive magnitude with the decrease stated."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use geometry to eliminate extra variables","text":"Similar triangles can express a tank radius as a constant multiple of water depth. Pythagoras can relate ladder-foot and ladder-top positions. Work with the correct quantities: a tank's fixed full radius is different from the changing surface radius. Calculate the missing instantaneous value before evaluating the derived rate formula.","diagram":null,"html":"Similar triangles can express a tank radius as a constant multiple of water depth. Pythagoras can relate ladder-foot and ladder-top positions. Work with the correct quantities: a tank&#39;s fixed full radius is different from the changing surface radius. Calculate the missing instantaneous value before evaluating the derived rate formula."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Finish with a contextual conclusion","text":"A bare number is incomplete. Identify what is increasing or decreasing and provide length/time, area/time or volume/time units. Check the sign against the physical situation and note model boundaries. Near a limiting configuration, a rate may become large because the idealised geometry changes quickly; this does not guarantee physical validity there.","diagram":null,"html":"A bare number is incomplete. Identify what is increasing or decreasing and provide length/time, area/time or volume/time units. Check the sign against the physical situation and note model boundaries. Near a limiting configuration, a rate may become large because the idealised geometry changes quickly; this does not guarantee physical validity there."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Extended exam task","q":6},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-related-rates-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"A 5 m ladder has its foot 3 m from a wall, moving outward at 0.2 m/s. Find the top movement rate. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let horizontal x and vertical y obey x squared+y squared=25; current y=4.","[1] Differentiate: 2x dx/dt+2y dy/dt=0.","[1] dy/dt=-(3/4)(0.2)=-0.15 m/s.","[1] The top is moving down at 0.15 m/s."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A 5 m ladder has its foot 3 m from a wall, moving outward at 0.2 m/s. Find the top movement rate. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let horizontal x and vertical y obey x squared+y squared=25; current y=4.","[1] Differentiate: 2x dx/dt+2y dy/dt=0.","[1] dy/dt=-(3/4)(0.2)=-0.15 m/s.","[1] The top is moving down at 0.15 m/s."]},{"id":"mas12-related-rates-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"A spherical balloon radius is 2 cm and increasing at 0.5 cm/s. Find volume rate. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Volume is $V=4\\pi r^3/3$.","[1] Differentiate: dV/dt=4pi r squared dr/dt.","[1] Substitute r=2 and rate 0.5.","[1] Volume increases at $8\\pi$ cubic cm/s."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A spherical balloon radius is 2 cm and increasing at 0.5 cm/s. Find volume rate. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Volume is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=4\\pi r^3/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Differentiate: dV/dt=4pi r squared dr/dt.","[1] Substitute r=2 and rate 0.5.","[1] Volume increases at <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> cubic cm/s."]},{"id":"mas12-related-rates-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"A circle area area grows at 6 square cm/s. Find radial rate when r=3 cm. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] A=pi r squared.","[1] dA/dt=2pi r dr/dt.","[1] dr/dt=6/(6pi).","[1] Radius increases at 1/pi cm/s."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A circle area area grows at 6 square cm/s. Find radial rate when r=3 cm. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] A=pi r squared.","[1] dA/dt=2pi r dr/dt.","[1] dr/dt=6/(6pi).","[1] Radius increases at 1/pi cm/s."]},{"id":"mas12-related-rates-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Water in a cone has surface radius half its depth. It enters at 3 cubic m/min. Find depth rate at h=2 m. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] r=h/2, so V=pi h cubed/12.","[1] dV/dt=(pi h squared/4)dh/dt.","[1] At h=2, 3=pi dh/dt.","[1] Depth rises at 3/pi m/min."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Water in a cone has surface radius half its depth. It enters at 3 cubic m/min. Find depth rate at h=2 m. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] r=h/2, so V=pi h cubed/12.","[1] dV/dt=(pi h squared/4)dh/dt.","[1] At h=2, 3=pi dh/dt.","[1] Depth rises at 3/pi m/min."]},{"id":"mas12-related-rates-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"A square side side decreases at 0.1 cm/s. Find area rate when side is 4 cm. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] A=s squared.","[1] dA/dt=2s ds/dt.","[1] At s=4, rate is 2(4)(-0.1)=-0.8.","[1] Area decreases at 0.8 square cm/s."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A square side side decreases at 0.1 cm/s. Find area rate when side is 4 cm. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] A=s squared.","[1] dA/dt=2s ds/dt.","[1] At s=4, rate is 2(4)(-0.1)=-0.8.","[1] Area decreases at 0.8 square cm/s."]},{"id":"mas12-related-rates-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Why must a ladder current horizontal distance not be substituted as a constant before differentiating its constraint? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The distance is changing with time.","[1] Substitution as a fixed number would remove its derivative term.","[1] Differentiate the general relation with both quantities variable.","[1] Then insert the instantaneous values and rates."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Why must a ladder current horizontal distance not be substituted as a constant before differentiating its constraint? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The distance is changing with time.","[1] Substitution as a fixed number would remove its derivative term.","[1] Differentiate the general relation with both quantities variable.","[1] Then insert the instantaneous values and rates."]},{"prompt":"A conical tank has height 6 m and top radius 3 m. Water enters at 2 cubic m/min and drains at a rate equal to the current water depth h in cubic m/min. Form a differential equation for h(t). Find the depth rate at h=1 and h=3, identify equilibrium and interpret its stability for positive depths. (8 marks)","steps":["[2] Similar triangles give r=h/2 and water volume $V=\\pi h^3/12$.","[1] Net volume rate is $dV/dt=2-h$.","[2] Differentiation gives $(\\pi h^2/4)dh/dt=2-h$, so $dh/dt=4(2-h)/(\\pi h^2)$ for h>0.","[2] At h=1, rate is $4/\\pi$ m/min; at h=3, rate is $-4/(9\\pi)$ m/min.","[1] Equilibrium h=2 m is stable: positive depths below rise and those above fall. The formula is singular at an empty tank and the model needs separate treatment there."],"id":"mas12-related-rates-extended","title":"Extended exam task","allocations":[2,1,2,2,1],"marks":8,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed or exact working","promptHtml":"A conical tank has height 6 m and top radius 3 m. Water enters at 2 cubic m/min and drains at a rate equal to the current water depth h in cubic m/min. Form a differential equation for h(t). Find the depth rate at h=1 and h=3, identify equilibrium and interpret its stability for positive depths. (8 marks)","stepsHtml":["[2] Similar triangles give r=h/2 and water volume <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=\\pi h^3/12</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Net volume rate is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dV/dt=2-h</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[2] Differentiation gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\pi h^2/4)dh/dt=2-h</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dh/dt=4(2-h)/(\\pi h^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for h&gt;0.","[2] At h=1, rate is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4/\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> m/min; at h=3, rate is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4/(9\\pi)</annotation></semantics></math></span> m/min.","[1] Equilibrium h=2 m is stable: positive depths below rise and those above fall. The formula is singular at an empty tank and the model needs separate treatment there."]}],"bank":[{"q":"When should instantaneous values normally be substituted in a related-rates equation?","o":["After differentiating the general changing relation.","Before differentiation, treating every current quantity as constant.","Never.","Only after discarding all signs."],"a":0,"why":"The derivatives must retain the time dependence of each changing quantity.","qHtml":"When should instantaneous values normally be substituted in a related-rates equation?","oHtml":["After differentiating the general changing relation.","Before differentiation, treating every current quantity as constant.","Never.","Only after discarding all signs."],"whyHtml":"The derivatives must retain the time dependence of each changing quantity."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 5 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-increments-euler","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Rates and differential equations","title":"Small increments and Euler approximation","outcomes":["4.2.3"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Linear increments approximate change · Euler applies local slopes repeatedly · Track and compare approximation · Interpret restrictions","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Small increments and Euler approximation</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Linear increments approximate change</li><li>Euler applies local slopes repeatedly</li><li>Track and compare approximation</li><li>Interpret restrictions</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Linear increments approximate change","text":"For a small change delta x, $\\delta y\\approx f'(x)\\delta x$. The tangent predicts the new value $f(x+\\delta x)\\approx f(x)+f'(x)\\delta x$. This is an approximation, not a finite-change identity. Its quality depends on step size and curvature; large changes can make it poor.","diagram":null,"html":"For a small change delta x, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta y\\approx f&#x27;(x)\\delta x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The tangent predicts the new value <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x+\\delta x)\\approx f(x)+f&#x27;(x)\\delta x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This is an approximation, not a finite-change identity. Its quality depends on step size and curvature; large changes can make it poor."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Euler applies local slopes repeatedly","text":"For dy/dx=F(x,y), start from (x0,y0) and use $x_{n+1}=x_n+h$, $y_{n+1}=y_n+hF(x_n,y_n)$. Each step uses the slope at the beginning of that step. Calculate the slope again after updating both coordinates. Using the final point slope is a different numerical method.","diagram":"euler","html":"For dy/dx=F(x,y), start from (x0,y0) and use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{n+1}=x_n+h</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_{n+1}=y_n+hF(x_n,y_n)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Each step uses the slope at the beginning of that step. Calculate the slope again after updating both coordinates. Using the final point slope is a different numerical method."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Track and compare approximation","text":"Make a table of x,y,slope and next y. Smaller steps often improve convergence for a well-behaved equation, but rounding and numerical stability still matter. Compare with a known exact solution or repeat with a smaller step to assess plausibility. Local tangent segments explain why an approximation can systematically lie above or below a curved solution.","diagram":null,"html":"Make a table of x,y,slope and next y. Smaller steps often improve convergence for a well-behaved equation, but rounding and numerical stability still matter. Compare with a known exact solution or repeat with a smaller step to assess plausibility. Local tangent segments explain why an approximation can systematically lie above or below a curved solution."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret restrictions","text":"Keep the model's domain, including denominators or nonnegative populations. A numerical step can overshoot a physically meaningful boundary even if the true solution does not. State step size, starting condition and final rounding so the answer is reproducible. A direction field shows local slopes while Euler chooses one approximate path.","diagram":null,"html":"Keep the model&#39;s domain, including denominators or nonnegative populations. A numerical step can overshoot a physically meaningful boundary even if the true solution does not. State step size, starting condition and final rounding so the answer is reproducible. A direction field shows local slopes while Euler chooses one approximate path."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore the effect of Euler step size","model":"euler"},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-increments-euler-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Estimate square root(4.1) using a small increment from x=4. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] f(4)=2 and derivative is 1/(2 square root x).","[1] At 4 the derivative is 1/4.","[1] Delta x=0.1 gives delta y approximately 0.025.","[1] Estimate is 2.025, slightly above the true concave-down curve."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Estimate square root(4.1) using a small increment from x=4. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] f(4)=2 and derivative is 1/(2 square root x).","[1] At 4 the derivative is 1/4.","[1] Delta x=0.1 gives delta y approximately 0.025.","[1] Estimate is 2.025, slightly above the true concave-down curve."]},{"id":"mas12-increments-euler-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Use Euler with h=0.1 for y derivative=y, y(0)=1, to estimate y(0.2). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] First slope is 1; y1=1+0.1(1)=1.1.","[1] At x=0.1 the new slope is 1.1.","[1] y2=1.1+0.1(1.1)=1.21.","[1] Estimate is 1.21; exact e power 0.2 is approximately 1.2214."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Use Euler with h=0.1 for y derivative=y, y(0)=1, to estimate y(0.2). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] First slope is 1; y1=1+0.1(1)=1.1.","[1] At x=0.1 the new slope is 1.1.","[1] y2=1.1+0.1(1.1)=1.21.","[1] Estimate is 1.21; exact e power 0.2 is approximately 1.2214."]},{"id":"mas12-increments-euler-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Use one Euler step h=0.2 for y derivative=x+y, y(0)=2. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Initial slope is 0+2=2.","[1] Increase is 0.2(2)=0.4.","[1] New x=0.2 and y=2.4.","[1] The slope for a following step would be 0.2+2.4=2.6."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Use one Euler step h=0.2 for y derivative=x+y, y(0)=2. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Initial slope is 0+2=2.","[1] Increase is 0.2(2)=0.4.","[1] New x=0.2 and y=2.4.","[1] The slope for a following step would be 0.2+2.4=2.6."]},{"id":"mas12-increments-euler-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Use two h=0.5 steps for y derivative=2x, y(0)=0. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] At x=0, slope=0, so y1=0 at x=0.5.","[1] New slope is 1.","[1] Second step gives y2=0.5 at x=1.","[1] Exact y=x squared gives 1; the coarse left-slope approximation underestimates."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Use two h=0.5 steps for y derivative=2x, y(0)=0. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] At x=0, slope=0, so y1=0 at x=0.5.","[1] New slope is 1.","[1] Second step gives y2=0.5 at x=1.","[1] Exact y=x squared gives 1; the coarse left-slope approximation underestimates."]},{"id":"mas12-increments-euler-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Estimate the change in sphere volume for r=3 and delta r=0.01. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] V derivative with respect to r is 4pi r squared.","[1] At r=3 this is 36pi.","[1] Delta V approximately 36pi(0.01)=0.36pi.","[1] The result has cubic units and is an approximation for the small radius increase."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Estimate the change in sphere volume for r=3 and delta r=0.01. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] V derivative with respect to r is 4pi r squared.","[1] At r=3 this is 36pi.","[1] Delta V approximately 36pi(0.01)=0.36pi.","[1] The result has cubic units and is an approximation for the small radius increase."]},{"id":"mas12-increments-euler-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Explain why Euler y next=y+hF(x next,y next) is not the standard explicit Euler update. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Standard Euler evaluates F at the known starting point.","[1] The proposed rule involves the unknown next y inside F.","[1] It requires solving an equation for that next value.","[1] It describes an implicit method, not the specified explicit update."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why Euler y next=y+hF(x next,y next) is not the standard explicit Euler update. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Standard Euler evaluates F at the known starting point.","[1] The proposed rule involves the unknown next y inside F.","[1] It requires solving an equation for that next value.","[1] It describes an implicit method, not the specified explicit update."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which slope does standard explicit Euler use for a step?","o":["The slope at the current starting point.","The unknown end-point slope.","Always the original slope for every step.","Zero regardless of the equation."],"a":0,"why":"The current coordinates determine the local tangent used to advance.","qHtml":"Which slope does standard explicit Euler use for a step?","oHtml":["The slope at the current starting point.","The unknown end-point slope.","Always the original slope for every step.","Zero regardless of the equation."],"whyHtml":"The current coordinates determine the local tangent used to advance."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-separable-equations","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Rates and differential equations","title":"Separation, initial conditions and lost equilibria","outcomes":["4.2.4"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Separate variables with care · Initial conditions determine constants · First-order equations beyond growth · Check the final solution","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Separation, initial conditions and lost equilibria</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Separate variables with care</li><li>Initial conditions determine constants</li><li>First-order equations beyond growth</li><li>Check the final solution</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate variables with care","text":"For dy/dx=f(x)g(y), rearrange to dy/g(y)=f(x)dx where g(y) is nonzero. Integrate both sides, then solve explicitly if practical. Division can lose constant solutions at roots of g, so check those equilibria in the original equation separately. A general implicit solution may be sufficient when algebra does not isolate y neatly.","diagram":null,"html":"For dy/dx=f(x)g(y), rearrange to dy/g(y)=f(x)dx where g(y) is nonzero. Integrate both sides, then solve explicitly if practical. Division can lose constant solutions at roots of g, so check those equilibria in the original equation separately. A general implicit solution may be sufficient when algebra does not isolate y neatly."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Initial conditions determine constants","text":"Substitute the supplied initial point into a solution to find the constant. If exponentiating a logarithm, absorb constants correctly and preserve sign possibilities. For y derivative=ky, all solutions are $y=Ce^{kx}$ including C=0. Initial data select a particular solution, not a new differential equation.","diagram":"separation","html":"Substitute the supplied initial point into a solution to find the constant. If exponentiating a logarithm, absorb constants correctly and preserve sign possibilities. For y derivative=ky, all solutions are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=Ce^{kx}</annotation></semantics></math></span> including C=0. Initial data select a particular solution, not a new differential equation."},{"kind":"learn","title":"First-order equations beyond growth","text":"An equation y derivative=f(x) integrates directly. A separated equation can involve rational, trigonometric or power expressions; use the relevant antiderivative and domain. A blow-up time or denominator zero can limit the interval containing the initial point. Report the interval when it matters physically or mathematically.","diagram":null,"html":"An equation y derivative=f(x) integrates directly. A separated equation can involve rational, trigonometric or power expressions; use the relevant antiderivative and domain. A blow-up time or denominator zero can limit the interval containing the initial point. Report the interval when it matters physically or mathematically."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Check the final solution","text":"Differentiate and substitute into the original equation and verify the initial condition. A family obtained after dividing by y must not silently omit y=0. Squaring or extracting roots can produce branch restrictions. Keep exact constants until a contextual approximation is requested.","diagram":null,"html":"Differentiate and substitute into the original equation and verify the initial condition. A family obtained after dividing by y must not silently omit y=0. Squaring or extracting roots can produce branch restrictions. Keep exact constants until a contextual approximation is requested."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-separable-equations-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Solve y derivative=3y, y(0)=2. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] For nonzero y, dy/y=3dx.","[1] Integrate: ln|y|=3x+C.","[1] General family is y=A e power 3x.","[1] Initial value gives A=2, so $y=2e^{3x}$."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve y derivative=3y, y(0)=2. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] For nonzero y, dy/y=3dx.","[1] Integrate: ln|y|=3x+C.","[1] General family is y=A e power 3x.","[1] Initial value gives A=2, so <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=2e^{3x}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."]},{"id":"mas12-separable-equations-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Solve y derivative=x/y, y(0)=2. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Separate: y dy=x dx, requiring y nonzero.","[1] Integrate: y squared/2=x squared/2+C.","[1] Initial value gives y squared=x squared+4.","[1] Positive initial branch is $y=\\sqrt{x^2+4}$, valid for all real x."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve y derivative=x/y, y(0)=2. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Separate: y dy=x dx, requiring y nonzero.","[1] Integrate: y squared/2=x squared/2+C.","[1] Initial value gives y squared=x squared+4.","[1] Positive initial branch is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=\\sqrt{x^2+4}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, valid for all real x."]},{"id":"mas12-separable-equations-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find all solutions of y derivative=$y^2$, including equilibria. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] For y nonzero, integrate y inverse squared dy=dx.","[1] -1/y=x+C, so y=1/(K-x).","[1] These solutions exclude x=K and apply on one side of that pole.","[1] The additional constant solution y=0 was lost by division and also satisfies the equation."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find all solutions of y derivative=<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, including equilibria. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] For y nonzero, integrate y inverse squared dy=dx.","[1] -1/y=x+C, so y=1/(K-x).","[1] These solutions exclude x=K and apply on one side of that pole.","[1] The additional constant solution y=0 was lost by division and also satisfies the equation."]},{"id":"mas12-separable-equations-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Solve y derivative=2x+1 with y(1)=4. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Direct integration gives y=x squared+x+C.","[1] At x=1, 4=2+C.","[1] C=2.","[1] Solution y=x squared+x+2 differentiates to 2x+1."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve y derivative=2x+1 with y(1)=4. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Direct integration gives y=x squared+x+C.","[1] At x=1, 4=2+C.","[1] C=2.","[1] Solution y=x squared+x+2 differentiates to 2x+1."]},{"id":"mas12-separable-equations-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Solve y derivative=xy with y(0)=-3. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Separate dy/y=x dx.","[1] ln|y|=x squared/2+C.","[1] y=A exp(x squared/2).","[1] A=-3 from the initial condition; the solution remains negative."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve y derivative=xy with y(0)=-3. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Separate dy/y=x dx.","[1] ln|y|=x squared/2+C.","[1] y=A exp(x squared/2).","[1] A=-3 from the initial condition; the solution remains negative."]},{"id":"mas12-separable-equations-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Solve y derivative=1+y with y(0)=0. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Separate dy/(1+y)=dx, handling y=-1 separately.","[1] ln|1+y|=x+C, so 1+y=A e power x.","[1] Initial data give A=1.","[1] Particular solution is y=e power x-1; y=-1 is an equilibrium of the general equation but does not fit these data."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve y derivative=1+y with y(0)=0. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Separate dy/(1+y)=dx, handling y=-1 separately.","[1] ln|1+y|=x+C, so 1+y=A e power x.","[1] Initial data give A=1.","[1] Particular solution is y=e power x-1; y=-1 is an equilibrium of the general equation but does not fit these data."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What must be checked after dividing a differential equation by g(y)?","o":["Constant solutions where g(y)=0.","Only values where x=0.","Whether every solution is positive.","Whether the equation becomes second order."],"a":0,"why":"Dividing by a factor excludes its zeros and can remove valid equilibria.","qHtml":"What must be checked after dividing a differential equation by g(y)?","oHtml":["Constant solutions where g(y)=0.","Only values where x=0.","Whether every solution is positive.","Whether the equation becomes second order."],"whyHtml":"Dividing by a factor excludes its zeros and can remove valid equilibria."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-direction-fields","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Rates and differential equations","title":"Direction fields, equilibria and solution curves","outcomes":["4.2.5"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Each segment represents a local slope · Autonomous equations simplify the field · Read solutions and compare predictions · Link to Euler approximation","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Direction fields, equilibria and solution curves</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Each segment represents a local slope</li><li>Autonomous equations simplify the field</li><li>Read solutions and compare predictions</li><li>Link to Euler approximation</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Each segment represents a local slope","text":"For dy/dx=F(x,y), a short segment at (x,y) has gradient F(x,y). A solution curve follows these local directions. The field does not give one unique curve without an initial condition. Zero-slope locations have horizontal segments; undefined F locations should not be assigned ordinary finite slopes.","diagram":null,"html":"For dy/dx=F(x,y), a short segment at (x,y) has gradient F(x,y). A solution curve follows these local directions. The field does not give one unique curve without an initial condition. Zero-slope locations have horizontal segments; undefined F locations should not be assigned ordinary finite slopes."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Autonomous equations simplify the field","text":"When F depends only on y, slopes are the same along each horizontal row. Equilibrium solutions occur where F(y)=0. A sign chart between equilibria shows whether solutions rise or fall. Stable equilibria attract nearby solutions in forward x-time, whereas unstable ones repel them, subject to the equation's regularity.","diagram":"direction","html":"When F depends only on y, slopes are the same along each horizontal row. Equilibrium solutions occur where F(y)=0. A sign chart between equilibria shows whether solutions rise or fall. Stable equilibria attract nearby solutions in forward x-time, whereas unstable ones repel them, subject to the equation&#39;s regularity."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Read solutions and compare predictions","text":"Choose a starting point, then trace a curve tangent to the field while preserving the indicated slopes. For a smooth equation with uniqueness, solution curves cannot cross at a shared point. This assertion requires regularity; it is not universal for every possible differential equation. A direction field can reveal trends without a closed-form solution.","diagram":null,"html":"Choose a starting point, then trace a curve tangent to the field while preserving the indicated slopes. For a smooth equation with uniqueness, solution curves cannot cross at a shared point. This assertion requires regularity; it is not universal for every possible differential equation. A direction field can reveal trends without a closed-form solution."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Link to Euler approximation","text":"Euler takes repeated tangent steps through the field. Smaller steps better follow curved trajectories in suitable cases. Check equilibrium solutions separately from varying curves and state eligible domains. A sampled field is schematic: segment density and length do not measure the solution value or its uncertainty.","diagram":null,"html":"Euler takes repeated tangent steps through the field. Smaller steps better follow curved trajectories in suitable cases. Check equilibrium solutions separately from varying curves and state eligible domains. A sampled field is schematic: segment density and length do not measure the solution value or its uncertainty."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-direction-fields-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"For y derivative=2-y, identify equilibrium and slope signs above and below it. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Set 2-y=0, giving y=2.","[1] Below 2 the slope is positive.","[1] Above 2 the slope is negative.","[1] Nearby solutions move toward 2 in forward time, so it is stable."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For y derivative=2-y, identify equilibrium and slope signs above and below it. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Set 2-y=0, giving y=2.","[1] Below 2 the slope is positive.","[1] Above 2 the slope is negative.","[1] Nearby solutions move toward 2 in forward time, so it is stable."]},{"id":"mas12-direction-fields-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"For y derivative=y(y-3), classify both equilibria by signs. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Equilibria are y=0,3.","[1] For y<0 slopes are positive; for 0<y<3 negative; for y>3 positive.","[1] Both sides near 0 point toward 0, so it is stable.","[1] Both sides near 3 point away, so it is unstable."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For y derivative=y(y-3), classify both equilibria by signs. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Equilibria are y=0,3.","[1] For y&lt;0 slopes are positive; for 0&lt;y&lt;3 negative; for y&gt;3 positive.","[1] Both sides near 0 point toward 0, so it is stable.","[1] Both sides near 3 point away, so it is unstable."]},{"id":"mas12-direction-fields-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Find slopes of y derivative=x-y at (0,0),(1,0),(0,1). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] At (0,0), slope is 0.","[1] At (1,0), slope is 1.","[1] At (0,1), slope is -1.","[1] Horizontal segments occur on y=x, but that line is not itself automatically a solution."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Find slopes of y derivative=x-y at (0,0),(1,0),(0,1). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] At (0,0), slope is 0.","[1] At (1,0), slope is 1.","[1] At (0,1), slope is -1.","[1] Horizontal segments occur on y=x, but that line is not itself automatically a solution."]},{"id":"mas12-direction-fields-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Explain why y=x is not a solution of y derivative=x-y. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] On y=x the right side is zero.","[1] The derivative of the proposed y=x is 1.","[1] The two sides disagree.","[1] A zero-slope locus need not be a constant equilibrium or a solution curve."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why y=x is not a solution of y derivative=x-y. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] On y=x the right side is zero.","[1] The derivative of the proposed y=x is 1.","[1] The two sides disagree.","[1] A zero-slope locus need not be a constant equilibrium or a solution curve."]},{"id":"mas12-direction-fields-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"For y derivative=y, describe curves starting at y(0)=1 and y(0)=-1. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Solutions are e power x and negative e power x.","[1] Positive values rise as x increases.","[1] Negative values fall farther below zero.","[1] The equilibrium y=0 is unstable, and neither nonzero solution crosses it."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For y derivative=y, describe curves starting at y(0)=1 and y(0)=-1. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Solutions are e power x and negative e power x.","[1] Positive values rise as x increases.","[1] Negative values fall farther below zero.","[1] The equilibrium y=0 is unstable, and neither nonzero solution crosses it."]},{"id":"mas12-direction-fields-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"What information does an initial point add to a direction field? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The field gives local gradients for many possible curves.","[1] An initial point selects curves passing through that location.","[1] For a suitable smooth equation it determines one local solution.","[1] It does not change the underlying slope field."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"What information does an initial point add to a direction field? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The field gives local gradients for many possible curves.","[1] An initial point selects curves passing through that location.","[1] For a suitable smooth equation it determines one local solution.","[1] It does not change the underlying slope field."]}],"bank":[{"q":"For an autonomous equation, where are slopes identical?","o":["Along each horizontal row of equal y.","Along every vertical column regardless of y.","Only at the origin.","Everywhere for all autonomous equations."],"a":0,"why":"The slope depends only on y, not x.","qHtml":"For an autonomous equation, where are slopes identical?","oHtml":["Along each horizontal row of equal y.","Along every vertical column regardless of y.","Only at the origin.","Everywhere for all autonomous equations."],"whyHtml":"The slope depends only on y, not x."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-growth-models","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Rates and differential equations","title":"Exponential, logistic and unfamiliar growth models","outcomes":["4.2.6"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Translate rates into equations · Exponential and limiting growth · Logistic equation and parameters · Assess unfamiliar models","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Exponential, logistic and unfamiliar growth models</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Translate rates into equations</li><li>Exponential and limiting growth</li><li>Logistic equation and parameters</li><li>Assess unfamiliar models</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Translate rates into equations","text":"A rate proportional to population P gives dP/dt=kP. Constant input with proportional loss gives dP/dt=a-bP. The coefficients have units determined by the variables. Define the quantity, clock and assumptions before solving. A context may require a model different from the familiar exponential or logistic forms.","diagram":null,"html":"A rate proportional to population P gives dP/dt=kP. Constant input with proportional loss gives dP/dt=a-bP. The coefficients have units determined by the variables. Define the quantity, clock and assumptions before solving. A context may require a model different from the familiar exponential or logistic forms."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Exponential and limiting growth","text":"Exponential growth has solution $P=P_0e^{kt}$; negative k models proportional decay. For a-bP with b>0, the equilibrium is a/b and the solution approaches it from either side. Initial value affects the constant. Exponential growth lacks a finite carrying capacity, so may cease to be appropriate when resources become limiting.","diagram":"growth","html":"Exponential growth has solution <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=P_0e^{kt}</annotation></semantics></math></span>; negative k models proportional decay. For a-bP with b&gt;0, the equilibrium is a/b and the solution approaches it from either side. Initial value affects the constant. Exponential growth lacks a finite carrying capacity, so may cease to be appropriate when resources become limiting."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Logistic equation and parameters","text":"The SCSA-style logistic equation $dP/dt=rP(K-P)$ with positive r,K has equilibria 0 and K. Slopes are positive between them and negative above K. For P0>0 its nonconstant solution is $P=K/(1+Ae^{-rKt})$, with $A=K/P_0-1$. The alternative parameterisation kP(1-P/K) has k=rK; do not confuse the two rate constants.","diagram":null,"html":"The SCSA-style logistic equation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dP/dt=rP(K-P)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with positive r,K has equilibria 0 and K. Slopes are positive between them and negative above K. For P0&gt;0 its nonconstant solution is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=K/(1+Ae^{-rKt})</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=K/P_0-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The alternative parameterisation kP(1-P/K) has k=rK; do not confuse the two rate constants."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Assess unfamiliar models","text":"Use units, equilibrium roots and sign intervals to interpret a proposed equation before solving. Maximum logistic growth occurs at P=K/2 because P(K-P) is a downward-opening quadratic. A physical model may restrict P>=0 even if algebra allows negative values. Explain both calculated predictions and assumptions such as constant coefficients or uniform mixing.","diagram":null,"html":"Use units, equilibrium roots and sign intervals to interpret a proposed equation before solving. Maximum logistic growth occurs at P=K/2 because P(K-P) is a downward-opening quadratic. A physical model may restrict P&gt;=0 even if algebra allows negative values. Explain both calculated predictions and assumptions such as constant coefficients or uniform mixing."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Extended exam task","q":6},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-growth-models-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Population obeys dP/dt=0.2P, P(0)=100. Find P(5) and doubling time. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Solution is $P=100e^{0.2t}$.","[1] P(5)=100e, approximately 271.83.","[1] Doubling gives e power 0.2t=2.","[1] Time is ln2/0.2, approximately 3.466 time units."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Population obeys dP/dt=0.2P, P(0)=100. Find P(5) and doubling time. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Solution is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=100e^{0.2t}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] P(5)=100e, approximately 271.83.","[1] Doubling gives e power 0.2t=2.","[1] Time is ln2/0.2, approximately 3.466 time units."]},{"id":"mas12-growth-models-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"For dP/dt=0.01P(100-P), P(0)=20, find P(t). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] K=100,r=0.01, so rK=1.","[1] A=100/20-1=4.","[1] $P(t)=100/(1+4e^{-t})$.","[1] It starts at 20 and approaches 100, with positive growth below capacity."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For dP/dt=0.01P(100-P), P(0)=20, find P(t). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] K=100,r=0.01, so rK=1.","[1] A=100/20-1=4.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(t)=100/(1+4e^{-t})</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] It starts at 20 and approaches 100, with positive growth below capacity."]},{"id":"mas12-growth-models-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"For the same logistic equation, find population and rate at maximum growth. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Rate is 0.01P(100-P).","[1] The quadratic peaks at P=50.","[1] Rate is 0.01(50)(50)=25.","[1] Maximum increase is 25 population units per time unit."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For the same logistic equation, find population and rate at maximum growth. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Rate is 0.01P(100-P).","[1] The quadratic peaks at P=50.","[1] Rate is 0.01(50)(50)=25.","[1] Maximum increase is 25 population units per time unit."]},{"id":"mas12-growth-models-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Solve dQ/dt=6-2Q, Q(0)=1. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Equilibrium is 3.","[1] Set U=Q-3, giving dU/dt=-2U.","[1] U=C e power -2t and C=-2.","[1] $Q=3-2e^{-2t}$, approaching 3 from below."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve dQ/dt=6-2Q, Q(0)=1. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Equilibrium is 3.","[1] Set U=Q-3, giving dU/dt=-2U.","[1] U=C e power -2t and C=-2.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=3-2e^{-2t}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, approaching 3 from below."]},{"id":"mas12-growth-models-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"A well-mixed tank has fixed volume 100 L, solute input 2 g/min and outflow 5 L/min with matching solvent inflow. Form the solute-mass equation. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let M(t) be grams of solute.","[1] Concentration is M/100 g/L.","[1] Solute removal rate is 5(M/100)=0.05M g/min.","[1] Equation is dM/dt=2-0.05M, with equilibrium 40 g; uniform mixing is assumed."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A well-mixed tank has fixed volume 100 L, solute input 2 g/min and outflow 5 L/min with matching solvent inflow. Form the solute-mass equation. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let M(t) be grams of solute.","[1] Concentration is M/100 g/L.","[1] Solute removal rate is 5(M/100)=0.05M g/min.","[1] Equation is dM/dt=2-0.05M, with equilibrium 40 g; uniform mixing is assumed."]},{"id":"mas12-growth-models-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Compare r in rP(K-P) with k in kP(1-P/K). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Expand the second form as (k/K)P(K-P).","[1] Equality requires r=k/K.","[1] Thus k=rK.","[1] Their units differ: k has inverse-time units, r inverse-population-time units."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Compare r in rP(K-P) with k in kP(1-P/K). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Expand the second form as (k/K)P(K-P).","[1] Equality requires r=k/K.","[1] Thus k=rK.","[1] Their units differ: k has inverse-time units, r inverse-population-time units."]},{"prompt":"A quantity follows $dP/dt=0.02P(80-P)$ with P(0)=10. Find the solution and time to reach 40. Find the largest growth rate and explain why the quantity does not reach 80 at a finite time in this solution. (8 marks)","steps":["[2] K=80,r=0.02, so rK=1.6 and A=80/10-1=7.","[1] $P=80/(1+7e^{-1.6t})$.","[2] At P=40, $7e^{-1.6t}=1$, hence $t=\\ln7/1.6$, approximately 1.216.","[1] Maximum growth occurs at P=40.","[1] Maximum rate is 0.02(40)(40)=32 quantity units per time.","[1] The exponential stays positive for finite t, so the denominator remains greater than 1; 80 is approached asymptotically."],"id":"mas12-growth-models-extended","title":"Extended exam task","allocations":[2,1,2,1,1,1],"marks":8,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed or exact working","promptHtml":"A quantity follows <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.02</mn><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>80</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dP/dt=0.02P(80-P)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with P(0)=10. Find the solution and time to reach 40. Find the largest growth rate and explain why the quantity does not reach 80 at a finite time in this solution. (8 marks)","stepsHtml":["[2] K=80,r=0.02, so rK=1.6 and A=80/10-1=7.","[1] <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.6</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=80/(1+7e^{-1.6t})</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[2] At P=40, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.6</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7e^{-1.6t}=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, hence <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>7</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\ln7/1.6</annotation></semantics></math></span>, approximately 1.216.","[1] Maximum growth occurs at P=40.","[1] Maximum rate is 0.02(40)(40)=32 quantity units per time.","[1] The exponential stays positive for finite t, so the denominator remains greater than 1; 80 is approached asymptotically."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Where is positive logistic growth fastest for capacity K?","o":["At P=K/2.","At P=K.","At P=0.","At every population equally."],"a":0,"why":"The growth-rate quadratic reaches its maximum halfway between its zeros.","qHtml":"Where is positive logistic growth fastest for capacity K?","oHtml":["At P=K/2.","At P=K.","At P=0.","At every population equally."],"whyHtml":"The growth-rate quadratic reaches its maximum halfway between its zeros."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 5 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-motion-acceleration","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Rates and differential equations","title":"Variable acceleration and velocity–position methods","outcomes":["4.2.7"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Choose the variable describing acceleration · Apply initial conditions and signs · Displacement and distance · Limit the physical model","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Variable acceleration and velocity–position methods</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Choose the variable describing acceleration</li><li>Apply initial conditions and signs</li><li>Displacement and distance</li><li>Limit the physical model</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Choose the variable describing acceleration","text":"Straight-line velocity v=dx/dt and acceleration a=dv/dt. If a is given as a function of time, integrate with respect to time. If it is a function of position, use $a=v\\,dv/dx=d(v^2/2)/dx$ where the chain-rule description is valid. This can determine speed squared without first obtaining x(t).","diagram":null,"html":"Straight-line velocity v=dx/dt and acceleration a=dv/dt. If a is given as a function of time, integrate with respect to time. If it is a function of position, use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=v\\,dv/dx=d(v^2/2)/dx</annotation></semantics></math></span> where the chain-rule description is valid. This can determine speed squared without first obtaining x(t)."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply initial conditions and signs","text":"Integrating acceleration in time requires initial velocity; integrating velocity then requires initial position. A v squared equation gives speed magnitude but not direction. Choose the velocity sign from the initial condition and motion interval. Turning points can require changing the sign branch rather than continuing one square root mechanically.","diagram":"motion","html":"Integrating acceleration in time requires initial velocity; integrating velocity then requires initial position. A v squared equation gives speed magnitude but not direction. Choose the velocity sign from the initial condition and motion interval. Turning points can require changing the sign branch rather than continuing one square root mechanically."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Displacement and distance","text":"Signed velocity accumulates displacement. Distance needs |v| and splits at changes of direction. A positive acceleration can accompany negative velocity, making speed decrease. Acceleration and velocity signs together determine whether speed grows or shrinks in straight-line motion away from rest.","diagram":null,"html":"Signed velocity accumulates displacement. Distance needs |v| and splits at changes of direction. A positive acceleration can accompany negative velocity, making speed decrease. Acceleration and velocity signs together determine whether speed grows or shrinks in straight-line motion away from rest."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Limit the physical model","text":"Check radicands and eligible positions when using v squared. A negative value indicates a location the modelled motion cannot reach under the stated data. Discuss rest, reversal and domain rather than treating every algebraic root as an arrival. Include time, position and rate units.","diagram":null,"html":"Check radicands and eligible positions when using v squared. A negative value indicates a location the modelled motion cannot reach under the stated data. Discuss rest, reversal and domain rather than treating every algebraic root as an arrival. Include time, position and rate units."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-motion-acceleration-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"a(t)=6t, v(0)=2,x(0)=1. Find v(t),x(t). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Integrate acceleration: v=3t squared+C.","[1] Initial velocity gives v=3t squared+2.","[1] Integrate again: x=t cubed+2t+D.","[1] Initial position gives x=t cubed+2t+1."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"a(t)=6t, v(0)=2,x(0)=1. Find v(t),x(t). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Integrate acceleration: v=3t squared+C.","[1] Initial velocity gives v=3t squared+2.","[1] Integrate again: x=t cubed+2t+D.","[1] Initial position gives x=t cubed+2t+1."]},{"id":"mas12-motion-acceleration-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"a(x)=-2x, and v=4 at x=0. Find v squared as a function of x and limiting positions. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use v dv/dx=-2x.","[1] Integrate: v squared/2=-x squared+C.","[1] Initial condition gives C=8, so v squared=16-2x squared.","[1] Reachable positions satisfy |x|<=2 square root2; endpoints have v=0."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"a(x)=-2x, and v=4 at x=0. Find v squared as a function of x and limiting positions. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use v dv/dx=-2x.","[1] Integrate: v squared/2=-x squared+C.","[1] Initial condition gives C=8, so v squared=16-2x squared.","[1] Reachable positions satisfy |x|&lt;=2 square root2; endpoints have v=0."]},{"id":"mas12-motion-acceleration-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"For v(t)=3-2t on [0,3], find displacement and distance. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Displacement is integral of 3-2t, giving 0.","[1] Velocity reverses at t=1.5.","[1] Position change to that time is 9/4, followed by an equal return.","[1] Distance is 9/2 despite zero net displacement."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For v(t)=3-2t on [0,3], find displacement and distance. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Displacement is integral of 3-2t, giving 0.","[1] Velocity reverses at t=1.5.","[1] Position change to that time is 9/4, followed by an equal return.","[1] Distance is 9/2 despite zero net displacement."]},{"id":"mas12-motion-acceleration-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"a=4x and v=2 at x=1. Find v squared. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] v dv/dx=4x.","[1] Integrate to v squared/2=2x squared+C.","[1] At x=1,v=2 gives C=0.","[1] v squared=4x squared; the sign of v still requires motion information."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"a=4x and v=2 at x=1. Find v squared. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] v dv/dx=4x.","[1] Integrate to v squared/2=2x squared+C.","[1] At x=1,v=2 gives C=0.","[1] v squared=4x squared; the sign of v still requires motion information."]},{"id":"mas12-motion-acceleration-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"A particle has v=-3 m/s and a=2 m/s squared. Is it initially speeding up? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Velocity points in the negative direction.","[1] Acceleration points oppositely.","[1] Its speed initially decreases.","[1] Away from rest, speed derivative is sign(v) times a=-2 m/s squared."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A particle has v=-3 m/s and a=2 m/s squared. Is it initially speeding up? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Velocity points in the negative direction.","[1] Acceleration points oppositely.","[1] Its speed initially decreases.","[1] Away from rest, speed derivative is sign(v) times a=-2 m/s squared."]},{"id":"mas12-motion-acceleration-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Derive a=v dv/dx using the chain rule. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Begin with a=dv/dt.","[1] Regard v as a differentiable function of x along the motion.","[1] Chain rule gives dv/dt=(dv/dx)(dx/dt).","[1] Since dx/dt=v, a=v dv/dx; branch and turning-point issues still need care."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Derive a=v dv/dx using the chain rule. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Begin with a=dv/dt.","[1] Regard v as a differentiable function of x along the motion.","[1] Chain rule gives dv/dt=(dv/dx)(dx/dt).","[1] Since dx/dt=v, a=v dv/dx; branch and turning-point issues still need care."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What information is missing from a formula for v squared alone?","o":["The direction or sign of velocity.","The speed magnitude.","The eligible radicand.","The squared velocity value."],"a":0,"why":"Both positive and negative velocities share the same square.","qHtml":"What information is missing from a formula for v squared alone?","oHtml":["The direction or sign of velocity.","The speed magnitude.","The eligible radicand.","The squared velocity value."],"whyHtml":"Both positive and negative velocities share the same square."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-motion-shm","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Rates and differential equations","title":"Simple harmonic motion, phase and energy relation","outcomes":["4.2.7"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Acceleration toward equilibrium · Amplitude, phase and period · Velocity-position relation · Interpret points in the cycle","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Simple harmonic motion, phase and energy relation</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Acceleration toward equilibrium</li><li>Amplitude, phase and period</li><li>Velocity-position relation</li><li>Interpret points in the cycle</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Acceleration toward equilibrium","text":"Simple harmonic motion satisfies $d^2x/dt^2=-\\omega^2x$ with omega>0 when x is displacement from equilibrium. Its solutions are $x=A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\sin(\\omega t)$, or a phase-shifted sinusoid. A force or acceleration proportional to position with the positive sign does not give oscillatory SHM.","diagram":null,"html":"Simple harmonic motion satisfies <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d^2x/dt^2=-\\omega^2x</annotation></semantics></math></span> with omega&gt;0 when x is displacement from equilibrium. Its solutions are <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\sin(\\omega t)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, or a phase-shifted sinusoid. A force or acceleration proportional to position with the positive sign does not give oscillatory SHM."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Amplitude, phase and period","text":"Amplitude is $\\sqrt{A^2+B^2}$. Period is $2\\pi/\\omega$ and frequency omega/(2pi). Initial position and velocity determine A and B. In phase form R cos(omega t-phi), A=R cos phi and B=R sin phi; choose phi using both signs. The coordinate must measure displacement from the actual equilibrium.","diagram":"shm","html":"Amplitude is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{A^2+B^2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Period is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/\\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span> and frequency omega/(2pi). Initial position and velocity determine A and B. In phase form R cos(omega t-phi), A=R cos phi and B=R sin phi; choose phi using both signs. The coordinate must measure displacement from the actual equilibrium."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Velocity-position relation","text":"Using a=v dv/dx gives $v^2=\\omega^2(R^2-x^2)$ for amplitude R. Maximum speed is omega R at equilibrium and speed is zero at x=+-R. This equation gives two direction branches, so use motion context to decide the sign. Maximum acceleration magnitude is omega squared R at the extremes.","diagram":null,"html":"Using a=v dv/dx gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^2=\\omega^2(R^2-x^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for amplitude R. Maximum speed is omega R at equilibrium and speed is zero at x=+-R. This equation gives two direction branches, so use motion context to decide the sign. Maximum acceleration magnitude is omega squared R at the extremes."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret points in the cycle","text":"At a turning point the particle is instantaneously at rest but acceleration is generally nonzero. At equilibrium acceleration is zero but speed is maximal. A phase diagram in x,v is an ellipse and helps relate position and direction. Real oscillations may have damping or changing forcing; those require a modified model.","diagram":null,"html":"At a turning point the particle is instantaneously at rest but acceleration is generally nonzero. At equilibrium acceleration is zero but speed is maximal. A phase diagram in x,v is an ellipse and helps relate position and direction. Real oscillations may have damping or changing forcing; those require a modified model."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Extended exam task","q":6},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-motion-shm-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"x(t)=3 cos 2t. Find amplitude, period and maximum speed. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Amplitude is 3, omega=2.","[1] Period is pi.","[1] v=-6 sin 2t.","[1] Maximum speed is 6."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"x(t)=3 cos 2t. Find amplitude, period and maximum speed. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Amplitude is 3, omega=2.","[1] Period is pi.","[1] v=-6 sin 2t.","[1] Maximum speed is 6."]},{"id":"mas12-motion-shm-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Solve x second derivative=-4x with x(0)=1,v(0)=4. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] General solution is A cos2t+B sin2t.","[1] Initial position gives A=1.","[1] v(0)=2B=4, so B=2.","[1] x=cos2t+2sin2t, amplitude square root5 and period pi."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Solve x second derivative=-4x with x(0)=1,v(0)=4. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] General solution is A cos2t+B sin2t.","[1] Initial position gives A=1.","[1] v(0)=2B=4, so B=2.","[1] x=cos2t+2sin2t, amplitude square root5 and period pi."]},{"id":"mas12-motion-shm-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"For amplitude 5 and omega=3, find speed when x=4. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] v squared=9(25-16).","[1] v squared=81.","[1] Speed is 9.","[1] Velocity may be +9 or -9 depending on the direction of travel."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For amplitude 5 and omega=3, find speed when x=4. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] v squared=9(25-16).","[1] v squared=81.","[1] Speed is 9.","[1] Velocity may be +9 or -9 depending on the direction of travel."]},{"id":"mas12-motion-shm-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"For x=2 sin t, find the first positive turning time and acceleration there. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] v=2 cos t.","[1] First positive zero is t=pi/2.","[1] Position there is 2.","[1] Acceleration=-2 sin t=-2, nonzero despite rest."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For x=2 sin t, find the first positive turning time and acceleration there. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] v=2 cos t.","[1] First positive zero is t=pi/2.","[1] Position there is 2.","[1] Acceleration=-2 sin t=-2, nonzero despite rest."]},{"id":"mas12-motion-shm-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"A system has period 4pi and amplitude 6. Find omega and maximum acceleration magnitude. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] omega=2pi/(4pi)=1/2.","[1] Acceleration magnitude is omega squared times |x|.","[1] Maximum occurs at |x|=6.","[1] Maximum magnitude is (1/4)(6)=3/2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A system has period 4pi and amplitude 6. Find omega and maximum acceleration magnitude. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] omega=2pi/(4pi)=1/2.","[1] Acceleration magnitude is omega squared times |x|.","[1] Maximum occurs at |x|=6.","[1] Maximum magnitude is (1/4)(6)=3/2."]},{"id":"mas12-motion-shm-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Explain why x second derivative=+4x is not simple harmonic motion about zero. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] SHM requires acceleration opposite to displacement.","[1] Here acceleration has the same sign as x.","[1] Solutions involve exponentials rather than a restoring sinusoid.","[1] The sign creates departure from equilibrium, not the stated restoring motion."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain why x second derivative=+4x is not simple harmonic motion about zero. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] SHM requires acceleration opposite to displacement.","[1] Here acceleration has the same sign as x.","[1] Solutions involve exponentials rather than a restoring sinusoid.","[1] The sign creates departure from equilibrium, not the stated restoring motion."]},{"prompt":"A particle satisfies $x^{\\prime\\prime}=-9x$, x(0)=4 and v(0)=-9. Find its position, amplitude and period. Find its speed at x=0 and the first positive time it reaches x=0, giving a correct phase or quadrant argument. (8 marks)","steps":["[2] Use $x=A\\cos3t+B\\sin3t$. Initial data give A=4 and 3B=-9, so B=-3.","[1] Amplitude is 5 and period $2\\pi/3$.","[1] Maximum speed at equilibrium is omega times amplitude=15.","[2] Equation $4\\cos3t-3\\sin3t=0$ gives tan3t=4/3. Initial motion is toward equilibrium and the first positive angle is $\\tan^{-1}(4/3)$ in quadrant I.","[2] First arrival time is $\\tan^{-1}(4/3)/3$, approximately 0.3091; velocity there is negative, while speed is 15."],"id":"mas12-motion-shm-extended","title":"Extended exam task","allocations":[2,1,1,2,2],"marks":8,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed or exact working","promptHtml":"A particle satisfies <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^{\\prime\\prime}=-9x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, x(0)=4 and v(0)=-9. Find its position, amplitude and period. Find its speed at x=0 and the first positive time it reaches x=0, giving a correct phase or quadrant argument. (8 marks)","stepsHtml":["[2] Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=A\\cos3t+B\\sin3t</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Initial data give A=4 and 3B=-9, so B=-3.","[1] Amplitude is 5 and period <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","[1] Maximum speed at equilibrium is omega times amplitude=15.","[2] Equation <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\cos3t-3\\sin3t=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives tan3t=4/3. Initial motion is toward equilibrium and the first positive angle is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tan^{-1}(4/3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> in quadrant I.","[2] First arrival time is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tan^{-1}(4/3)/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, approximately 0.3091; velocity there is negative, while speed is 15."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Where is SHM speed largest?","o":["At equilibrium.","At either turning point.","Only when acceleration magnitude is largest.","At every position equally."],"a":0,"why":"The relation v squared=omega squared times amplitude squared minus displacement squared peaks at zero displacement.","qHtml":"Where is SHM speed largest?","oHtml":["At equilibrium.","At either turning point.","Only when acceleration magnitude is largest.","At every position equally."],"whyHtml":"The relation v squared=omega squared times amplitude squared minus displacement squared peaks at zero displacement."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 5 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-sampling-mean","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Statistical inference","title":"A sample mean is a random variable","outcomes":["4.3.1"],"icon":"📐","scope":"A statistic varies from sample to sample · Expectation of the mean · Variation and independence · Sampling design matters","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>A sample mean is a random variable</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>A statistic varies from sample to sample</li><li>Expectation of the mean</li><li>Variation and independence</li><li>Sampling design matters</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"A statistic varies from sample to sample","text":"For observations $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$, the sample mean is $\\bar X=(X_1+\\cdots+X_n)/n$. Before sampling it is a random variable; after sampling its realised value is written x bar. The population mean mu is a fixed unknown parameter, distinct from a variable sample statistic. Different random samples usually produce different estimates.","diagram":null,"html":"For observations <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_1,\\ldots,X_n</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the sample mean is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>X</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar X=(X_1+\\cdots+X_n)/n</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Before sampling it is a random variable; after sampling its realised value is written x bar. The population mean mu is a fixed unknown parameter, distinct from a variable sample statistic. Different random samples usually produce different estimates."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Expectation of the mean","text":"If each observation has mean mu, linearity gives $E(\\bar X)=\\mu$. Thus the sample mean is unbiased under the sampling model. Unbiased does not mean each sample mean equals mu; it concerns the average over repeated sampling. A biased sampling method can invalidate the assumption that observations represent the intended population.","diagram":"sampling","html":"If each observation has mean mu, linearity gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>X</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(\\bar X)=\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Thus the sample mean is unbiased under the sampling model. Unbiased does not mean each sample mean equals mu; it concerns the average over repeated sampling. A biased sampling method can invalidate the assumption that observations represent the intended population."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Variation and independence","text":"For independent identically distributed observations with variance sigma squared, $\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\sigma^2/n$. Independence removes covariance terms. Standard deviation of the sampling mean, or standard error, is sigma divided by square root n. It is not the same as the spread of individual observations.","diagram":null,"html":"For independent identically distributed observations with variance sigma squared, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>X</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\sigma^2/n</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Independence removes covariance terms. Standard deviation of the sampling mean, or standard error, is sigma divided by square root n. It is not the same as the spread of individual observations."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Sampling design matters","text":"A random sample supports inference; a convenient group can systematically differ from the population. Repeated measurements on one person do not necessarily supply independent people. State population, sampling unit and independence assumptions. Without-replacement sampling from a small finite population may need an adjustment outside the simple independent model.","diagram":null,"html":"A random sample supports inference; a convenient group can systematically differ from the population. Repeated measurements on one person do not necessarily supply independent people. State population, sampling unit and independence assumptions. Without-replacement sampling from a small finite population may need an adjustment outside the simple independent model."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-sampling-mean-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"A population has mean 50 and standard deviation 12. For independent samples of size 36, find mean and standard deviation of the sample mean. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Expected sample mean equals population mean 50.","[1] Standard error is sigma/square root n.","[1] 12/square root36=2.","[1] Thus the sampling distribution has mean 50 and standard deviation 2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A population has mean 50 and standard deviation 12. For independent samples of size 36, find mean and standard deviation of the sample mean. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Expected sample mean equals population mean 50.","[1] Standard error is sigma/square root n.","[1] 12/square root36=2.","[1] Thus the sampling distribution has mean 50 and standard deviation 2."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-mean-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Explain unbiasedness when a particular sample mean is 48 but mu=50. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] An individual sample can differ from the parameter through sampling variation.","[1] Unbiasedness states E(sample mean)=mu.","[1] Repeated random sample means average toward 50 under the model.","[1] The single observed 48 does not disprove unbiasedness."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Explain unbiasedness when a particular sample mean is 48 but mu=50. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] An individual sample can differ from the parameter through sampling variation.","[1] Unbiasedness states E(sample mean)=mu.","[1] Repeated random sample means average toward 50 under the model.","[1] The single observed 48 does not disprove unbiasedness."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-mean-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"For independent X,Y with mean 10 and variance 4 each, find expectation and variance of (X+Y)/2. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Expectation is (10+10)/2=10.","[1] Independence gives Var(X+Y)=4+4=8.","[1] Scaling by 1/2 multiplies variance by 1/4.","[1] Variance is 2 and standard deviation square root2."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For independent X,Y with mean 10 and variance 4 each, find expectation and variance of (X+Y)/2. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Expectation is (10+10)/2=10.","[1] Independence gives Var(X+Y)=4+4=8.","[1] Scaling by 1/2 multiplies variance by 1/4.","[1] Variance is 2 and standard deviation square root2."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-mean-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"A sample contains 8,10,12,14. Calculate its mean and distinguish it from mu. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Sum is 44.","[1] Divide by sample size 4.","[1] Observed mean is 11.","[1] Mu is the population mean, which is not determined exactly by this sample alone."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A sample contains 8,10,12,14. Calculate its mean and distinguish it from mu. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Sum is 44.","[1] Divide by sample size 4.","[1] Observed mean is 11.","[1] Mu is the population mean, which is not determined exactly by this sample alone."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-mean-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Why do 100 repeated measurements from one student not automatically form an independent random sample of 100 students? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The sampling unit differs: measurements are not distinct randomly selected students.","[1] Repeats on the same individual may be correlated.","[1] Between-student variability is not adequately represented.","[1] The usual independent sample-mean model needs a justified design."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Why do 100 repeated measurements from one student not automatically form an independent random sample of 100 students? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The sampling unit differs: measurements are not distinct randomly selected students.","[1] Repeats on the same individual may be correlated.","[1] Between-student variability is not adequately represented.","[1] The usual independent sample-mean model needs a justified design."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-mean-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"If sample size changes from 25 to 100 with the same sigma, compare standard errors. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Old SE is sigma/5.","[1] New SE is sigma/10.","[1] The new SE is half the old.","[1] Quadrupling sample size halves SE; it does not divide SE by four."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"If sample size changes from 25 to 100 with the same sigma, compare standard errors. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Old SE is sigma/5.","[1] New SE is sigma/10.","[1] The new SE is half the old.","[1] Quadrupling sample size halves SE; it does not divide SE by four."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What does unbiasedness of the sample mean assert?","o":["Its expectation over the sampling model equals mu.","Every sample mean equals mu exactly.","Every sample contains the entire population.","The population has zero variance."],"a":0,"why":"Unbiasedness is a repeated-sampling property, not an exactness claim for one sample.","qHtml":"What does unbiasedness of the sample mean assert?","oHtml":["Its expectation over the sampling model equals mu.","Every sample mean equals mu exactly.","Every sample contains the entire population.","The population has zero variance."],"whyHtml":"Unbiasedness is a repeated-sampling property, not an exactness claim for one sample."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-sampling-distribution","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Statistical inference","title":"Sampling distributions and standard error","outcomes":["4.3.2"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Distribution of a statistic · Normal populations give exact normal means · Estimate standard error from data · Compare sample sizes graphically","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Sampling distributions and standard error</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Distribution of a statistic</li><li>Normal populations give exact normal means</li><li>Estimate standard error from data</li><li>Compare sample sizes graphically</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distribution of a statistic","text":"The sampling distribution of the mean describes values of the statistic over repeated samples of the same size from the same population. It is different from the distribution of individual observations. A histogram of one sample estimates individual variation; a histogram of many simulated sample means estimates sampling variation.","diagram":null,"html":"The sampling distribution of the mean describes values of the statistic over repeated samples of the same size from the same population. It is different from the distribution of individual observations. A histogram of one sample estimates individual variation; a histogram of many simulated sample means estimates sampling variation."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Normal populations give exact normal means","text":"If independent observations are normally distributed with mean mu and variance sigma squared, then the sample mean is normal with mean mu and variance sigma squared/n for every positive n. Keep variance and standard deviation notation explicit. A calculator's normal command generally asks for standard deviation, not variance.","diagram":"sampling","html":"If independent observations are normally distributed with mean mu and variance sigma squared, then the sample mean is normal with mean mu and variance sigma squared/n for every positive n. Keep variance and standard deviation notation explicit. A calculator&#39;s normal command generally asks for standard deviation, not variance."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Estimate standard error from data","text":"When sigma is unknown, sample standard deviation s estimates individual spread and s/square root n estimates standard error. Increasing n narrows the sampling distribution but does not make individual people less variable. Changing the population or sampling process can change both mu and sigma.","diagram":null,"html":"When sigma is unknown, sample standard deviation s estimates individual spread and s/square root n estimates standard error. Increasing n narrows the sampling distribution but does not make individual people less variable. Changing the population or sampling process can change both mu and sigma."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare sample sizes graphically","text":"Sampling distributions centred at the same mu can have different widths. For the same population, larger n gives a narrower mean distribution. Repeated-sample simulations should draw independent representative samples and calculate one mean per sample. A finite simulation histogram approximates the theoretical distribution and depends on the number of repetitions.","diagram":null,"html":"Sampling distributions centred at the same mu can have different widths. For the same population, larger n gives a narrower mean distribution. Repeated-sample simulations should draw independent representative samples and calculate one mean per sample. A finite simulation histogram approximates the theoretical distribution and depends on the number of repetitions."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore repeated samples and interval capture","model":"sampling"},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-sampling-distribution-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Individual masses are normal with mean 70 kg, sigma=8 kg. Describe the mean of n=16 independent observations. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Normality of the population gives an exact normal sample mean.","[1] Mean is 70 kg.","[1] Standard deviation is 8/4=2 kg.","[1] Variance is 4 square kg; do not enter 4 as the normal-command standard deviation."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Individual masses are normal with mean 70 kg, sigma=8 kg. Describe the mean of n=16 independent observations. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Normality of the population gives an exact normal sample mean.","[1] Mean is 70 kg.","[1] Standard deviation is 8/4=2 kg.","[1] Variance is 4 square kg; do not enter 4 as the normal-command standard deviation."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-distribution-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"If s=15 and n=100, estimate standard error and explain its meaning. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Estimated SE is s/square root n.","[1] This is 15/10=1.5.","[1] It estimates the spread of sample means under repeated sampling.","[1] It does not estimate the standard deviation of individual values, which is s=15."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"If s=15 and n=100, estimate standard error and explain its meaning. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Estimated SE is s/square root n.","[1] This is 15/10=1.5.","[1] It estimates the spread of sample means under repeated sampling.","[1] It does not estimate the standard deviation of individual values, which is s=15."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-distribution-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"A normal population has variance 81. Find sample-mean variance and SD for n=9. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Population sigma is 9.","[1] Mean variance is 81/9=9.","[1] Mean SD is square root9=3.","[1] State the distinction clearly: variance 9, standard deviation 3."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"A normal population has variance 81. Find sample-mean variance and SD for n=9. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Population sigma is 9.","[1] Mean variance is 81/9=9.","[1] Mean SD is square root9=3.","[1] State the distinction clearly: variance 9, standard deviation 3."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-distribution-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"For sigma=20, compare sampling-mean SD for n=25 and n=400. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] At n=25, SE=20/5=4.","[1] At n=400, SE=20/20=1.","[1] The larger sample distribution is one-quarter as wide by SD.","[1] Its centre remains mu if the sampling model is unchanged."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"For sigma=20, compare sampling-mean SD for n=25 and n=400. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] At n=25, SE=20/5=4.","[1] At n=400, SE=20/20=1.","[1] The larger sample distribution is one-quarter as wide by SD.","[1] Its centre remains mu if the sampling model is unchanged."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-distribution-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Outline a simulation of a sampling-mean distribution with n=30. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Specify a population distribution.","[1] Generate 30 independent observations and calculate their mean.","[1] Repeat many times, storing one mean per repetition.","[1] Plot those means and compare their centre and spread with mu and sigma/square root30."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Outline a simulation of a sampling-mean distribution with n=30. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Specify a population distribution.","[1] Generate 30 independent observations and calculate their mean.","[1] Repeat many times, storing one mean per repetition.","[1] Plot those means and compare their centre and spread with mu and sigma/square root30."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-distribution-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Why can a narrow sampling-mean distribution coexist with widely spread individual values? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Individual spread is measured by sigma.","[1] Averaging independent observations reduces variance to sigma squared/n.","[1] The standard error can be small for large n even when sigma is large.","[1] Precision of a mean estimate does not imply uniform individual outcomes."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-free","promptHtml":"Why can a narrow sampling-mean distribution coexist with widely spread individual values? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Individual spread is measured by sigma.","[1] Averaging independent observations reduces variance to sigma squared/n.","[1] The standard error can be small for large n even when sigma is large.","[1] Precision of a mean estimate does not imply uniform individual outcomes."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What is a histogram of repeated sample means displaying?","o":["A sampling distribution of the mean.","The distribution of the individual population values directly.","The distribution of all possible population means.","Only one sample sorted into order."],"a":0,"why":"Each plotted value is a statistic calculated from a separate sample.","qHtml":"What is a histogram of repeated sample means displaying?","oHtml":["A sampling distribution of the mean.","The distribution of the individual population values directly.","The distribution of all possible population means.","Only one sample sorted into order."],"whyHtml":"Each plotted value is a statistic calculated from a separate sample."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-sampling-clt","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Statistical inference","title":"Normal approximation, standardisation and simulation","outcomes":["4.3.2","4.3.3"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Large samples can give approximately normal means · Standardise with standard error · Translate tails and intervals · Simulation assesses the approximation","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Normal approximation, standardisation and simulation</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Large samples can give approximately normal means</li><li>Standardise with standard error</li><li>Translate tails and intervals</li><li>Simulation assesses the approximation</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Large samples can give approximately normal means","text":"For independent identically distributed observations with finite mean and variance, the central limit theorem describes the standardised sample mean approaching a standard normal distribution as n grows. The original population need not be normal. Accuracy depends on skewness, tails and sample size; n=30 is not a universal guarantee.","diagram":null,"html":"For independent identically distributed observations with finite mean and variance, the central limit theorem describes the standardised sample mean approaching a standard normal distribution as n grows. The original population need not be normal. Accuracy depends on skewness, tails and sample size; n=30 is not a universal guarantee."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Standardise with standard error","text":"Use $Z=(\\bar X-\\mu)/(\\sigma/\\sqrt n)$. Probabilities for a sample mean use this standard error, whereas probabilities for one individual use sigma. Identify which random variable the question concerns before entering a normal cumulative probability. When sigma is unknown, replace it by sample standard deviation s: $(\\bar X-\\mu)/(s/\\sqrt n)$ is approximately standard normal for suitable large samples. Simulate this standardised statistic as well as the unstandardised means; the course uses large samples of at least 30, but distribution shape and independence still matter. Include the approximation sign when normality comes from a large-sample argument.","diagram":"sampling","html":"Use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>X</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=(\\bar X-\\mu)/(\\sigma/\\sqrt n)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Probabilities for a sample mean use this standard error, whereas probabilities for one individual use sigma. Identify which random variable the question concerns before entering a normal cumulative probability. When sigma is unknown, replace it by sample standard deviation s: <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>X</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\bar X-\\mu)/(s/\\sqrt n)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is approximately standard normal for suitable large samples. Simulate this standardised statistic as well as the unstandardised means; the course uses large samples of at least 30, but distribution shape and independence still matter. Include the approximation sign when normality comes from a large-sample argument."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Translate tails and intervals","text":"For a mean threshold a, compute its z-score then use the appropriate lower or upper tail. For an interval, subtract cumulative probabilities at its endpoints. Check whether the answer should be near zero, one-half or one. Use sufficient precision before final rounding and state assumptions alongside the probability.","diagram":null,"html":"For a mean threshold a, compute its z-score then use the appropriate lower or upper tail. For an interval, subtract cumulative probabilities at its endpoints. Check whether the answer should be near zero, one-half or one. Use sufficient precision before final rounding and state assumptions alongside the probability."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Simulation assesses the approximation","text":"Compare repeated sample-mean histograms for small and large n from a skewed population. Larger n commonly produces a more symmetric shape, but dependent observations or extremely heavy tails can defeat the simple model. A simulation supports understanding; it does not prove the theorem or fix a biased sampling method.","diagram":null,"html":"Compare repeated sample-mean histograms for small and large n from a skewed population. Larger n commonly produces a more symmetric shape, but dependent observations or extremely heavy tails can defeat the simple model. A simulation supports understanding; it does not prove the theorem or fix a biased sampling method."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore repeated samples and interval capture","model":"sampling"},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-sampling-clt-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Mu=100,sigma=20,n=25, and population is normal. Find P(sample mean>108). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] SE=20/5=4.","[1] Threshold z=(108-100)/4=2.","[1] Upper standard-normal tail above 2 is approximately 0.02275.","[1] Probability is 0.0228 to four decimals, exact normal model apart from rounding."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Mu=100,sigma=20,n=25, and population is normal. Find P(sample mean&gt;108). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] SE=20/5=4.","[1] Threshold z=(108-100)/4=2.","[1] Upper standard-normal tail above 2 is approximately 0.02275.","[1] Probability is 0.0228 to four decimals, exact normal model apart from rounding."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-clt-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"A nonnormal population has mu=50,sigma=12. For n=144 independent observations, approximate P(48<sample mean<52). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Large-sample SE is 12/12=1.","[1] Bounds standardise to -2 and 2.","[1] Normal probability between them is approximately 0.9545.","[1] This is a CLT approximation subject to suitable finite-variance independent sampling."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"A nonnormal population has mu=50,sigma=12. For n=144 independent observations, approximate P(48&lt;sample mean&lt;52). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Large-sample SE is 12/12=1.","[1] Bounds standardise to -2 and 2.","[1] Normal probability between them is approximately 0.9545.","[1] This is a CLT approximation subject to suitable finite-variance independent sampling."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-clt-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Mu=40,sigma=10,n=100. Approximate P(sample mean<39). (4 marks)","steps":["[1] SE=1.","[1] z=(39-40)/1=-1.","[1] Standard-normal lower-tail probability is approximately 0.1587.","[1] Use the large-sample approximation unless the population is already normal."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Mu=40,sigma=10,n=100. Approximate P(sample mean&lt;39). (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] SE=1.","[1] z=(39-40)/1=-1.","[1] Standard-normal lower-tail probability is approximately 0.1587.","[1] Use the large-sample approximation unless the population is already normal."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-clt-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"For a normal population mu=0,sigma=6, find the central 95% interval of sample means for n=36. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] SE=1.","[1] Central standard-normal bounds are approximately +-1.96.","[1] Sampling-mean interval is (-1.96,1.96).","[1] This describes the random sample mean with known mu, not a confidence interval calculated from observed data."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"For a normal population mu=0,sigma=6, find the central 95% interval of sample means for n=36. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] SE=1.","[1] Central standard-normal bounds are approximately +-1.96.","[1] Sampling-mean interval is (-1.96,1.96).","[1] This describes the random sample mean with known mu, not a confidence interval calculated from observed data."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-clt-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"Why is a normal approximation for n=30 not automatically valid for every population? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Approximation quality depends on the underlying distribution.","[1] Strong skewness or heavy tails can require much larger samples.","[1] Independence and finite variance are assumptions of the stated CLT model.","[1] A sample-size rule alone does not establish them."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Why is a normal approximation for n=30 not automatically valid for every population? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Approximation quality depends on the underlying distribution.","[1] Strong skewness or heavy tails can require much larger samples.","[1] Independence and finite variance are assumptions of the stated CLT model.","[1] A sample-size rule alone does not establish them."]},{"id":"mas12-sampling-clt-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A question asks P(X>110) for one individual but uses sigma/square root100. Explain the error. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The random variable is an individual X, not a mean of 100 observations.","[1] Its standard deviation is sigma.","[1] Dividing by square root100 incorrectly narrows its distribution.","[1] Standardise using (110-mu)/sigma for the individual question."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"A question asks P(X&gt;110) for one individual but uses sigma/square root100. Explain the error. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The random variable is an individual X, not a mean of 100 observations.","[1] Its standard deviation is sigma.","[1] Dividing by square root100 incorrectly narrows its distribution.","[1] Standardise using (110-mu)/sigma for the individual question."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which SD belongs in a probability about the mean of n independent observations?","o":["Sigma divided by square root n.","Sigma times n.","Sigma squared without a square root.","The individual sigma in every mean question."],"a":0,"why":"Averaging changes the variance and therefore the standardisation.","qHtml":"Which SD belongs in a probability about the mean of n independent observations?","oHtml":["Sigma divided by square root n.","Sigma times n.","Sigma squared without a square root.","The individual sigma in every mean question."],"whyHtml":"Averaging changes the variance and therefore the standardisation."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-confidence-intervals","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Statistical inference","title":"Approximate confidence intervals for a population mean","outcomes":["4.3.4","4.3.5"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Estimate an unknown population mean · Choose the critical value · Calculate margin and endpoints · Interpret in context","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Approximate confidence intervals for a population mean</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Estimate an unknown population mean</li><li>Choose the critical value</li><li>Calculate margin and endpoints</li><li>Interpret in context</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Estimate an unknown population mean","text":"An approximate large-sample confidence interval is $\\bar x\\pm z^*s/\\sqrt n$, using sample mean, sample standard deviation and an appropriate normal critical value. The interval is generated from random data to estimate fixed mu. For the course's large-sample method, justify representative independent sampling and an adequate normal approximation.","diagram":null,"html":"An approximate large-sample confidence interval is <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>±</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x\\pm z^*s/\\sqrt n</annotation></semantics></math></span>, using sample mean, sample standard deviation and an appropriate normal critical value. The interval is generated from random data to estimate fixed mu. For the course&#39;s large-sample method, justify representative independent sampling and an adequate normal approximation."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Choose the critical value","text":"A two-sided confidence level C leaves probability (1-C)/2 in each normal tail. Common critical values are approximately 1.645 for 90%, 1.96 for 95% and 2.576 for 99%. Do not use the confidence percentage itself as z. If sigma is explicitly known, it replaces s in the standard-error formula.","diagram":"ci-formula","html":"A two-sided confidence level C leaves probability (1-C)/2 in each normal tail. Common critical values are approximately 1.645 for 90%, 1.96 for 95% and 2.576 for 99%. Do not use the confidence percentage itself as z. If sigma is explicitly known, it replaces s in the standard-error formula."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate margin and endpoints","text":"The margin of error is z star times standard error. Show the mean, SE, margin and endpoints, then round suitably for the measurement precision. More confidence increases interval width for unchanged data. A narrower interval can come from a larger sample, a smaller spread or a lower confidence level; state which factor changed.","diagram":null,"html":"The margin of error is z star times standard error. Show the mean, SE, margin and endpoints, then round suitably for the measurement precision. More confidence increases interval width for unchanged data. A narrower interval can come from a larger sample, a smaller spread or a lower confidence level; state which factor changed."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret in context","text":"A 95% procedure captures mu in about 95% of repeated eligible samples. The realised interval either contains the fixed mu or does not. It is not an interval containing 95% of individual observations. Poor sampling design can make a numerically correct formula misleading, and higher confidence does not remove bias.","diagram":null,"html":"A 95% procedure captures mu in about 95% of repeated eligible samples. The realised interval either contains the fixed mu or does not. It is not an interval containing 95% of individual observations. Poor sampling design can make a numerically correct formula misleading, and higher confidence does not remove bias."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-confidence-intervals-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"n=100,x bar=50,s=10. Find an approximate 95% confidence interval for mu. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Estimated SE is 10/10=1.","[1] Critical value is 1.96.","[1] Margin is 1.96.","[1] Interval is [48.04,51.96], in the original measurement units."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"n=100,x bar=50,s=10. Find an approximate 95% confidence interval for mu. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Estimated SE is 10/10=1.","[1] Critical value is 1.96.","[1] Margin is 1.96.","[1] Interval is [48.04,51.96], in the original measurement units."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-intervals-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"n=64,x bar=120,s=16. Find a 90% interval using z=1.645. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] SE=16/8=2.","[1] Margin=1.645(2)=3.29.","[1] Endpoints are 120+-3.29.","[1] Approximate interval is [116.71,123.29], assuming the normal mean approximation is suitable."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"n=64,x bar=120,s=16. Find a 90% interval using z=1.645. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] SE=16/8=2.","[1] Margin=1.645(2)=3.29.","[1] Endpoints are 120+-3.29.","[1] Approximate interval is [116.71,123.29], assuming the normal mean approximation is suitable."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-intervals-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"n=400,x bar=30,s=8. Find a 99% interval using z=2.576. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] SE=8/20=0.4.","[1] Margin=2.576(0.4)=1.0304.","[1] Endpoints are 28.9696 and 31.0304.","[1] Rounded interval [28.970,31.030] uses consistent precision."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"n=400,x bar=30,s=8. Find a 99% interval using z=2.576. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] SE=8/20=0.4.","[1] Margin=2.576(0.4)=1.0304.","[1] Endpoints are 28.9696 and 31.0304.","[1] Rounded interval [28.970,31.030] uses consistent precision."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-intervals-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"A 95% interval is [18,22] with x bar=20,n=100. Estimate s from the stated rounded interval. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Margin is 2.","[1] Use 2 approximately equal to 1.96s/10.","[1] s approximately equals 20/1.96.","[1] Estimated s is 10.204; interval rounding makes this an approximate reconstruction."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"A 95% interval is [18,22] with x bar=20,n=100. Estimate s from the stated rounded interval. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Margin is 2.","[1] Use 2 approximately equal to 1.96s/10.","[1] s approximately equals 20/1.96.","[1] Estimated s is 10.204; interval rounding makes this an approximate reconstruction."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-intervals-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"How does a 99% interval compare with a 95% interval from the same data? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The centre and estimated SE are unchanged.","[1] Critical value increases from 1.96 to about 2.576.","[1] Margin grows by factor 2.576/1.96, about 1.314.","[1] The 99% interval is wider, trading precision for a higher capture rate."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"How does a 99% interval compare with a 95% interval from the same data? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The centre and estimated SE are unchanged.","[1] Critical value increases from 1.96 to about 2.576.","[1] Margin grows by factor 2.576/1.96, about 1.314.","[1] The 99% interval is wider, trading precision for a higher capture rate."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-intervals-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Why does a confidence interval from a voluntary website poll need caution even with a large n? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Volunteers can systematically differ from the intended population.","[1] The representative random-sampling assumption may fail.","[1] A large n reduces formula-based sampling error but does not remove selection bias.","[1] State the design limitation before making a population inference."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Why does a confidence interval from a voluntary website poll need caution even with a large n? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Volunteers can systematically differ from the intended population.","[1] The representative random-sampling assumption may fail.","[1] A large n reduces formula-based sampling error but does not remove selection bias.","[1] State the design limitation before making a population inference."]}],"bank":[{"q":"What is the margin of error in the large-sample mean interval?","o":["$z^*s/\\sqrt n$.","$z^*s\\sqrt n$.","Only x bar.","95% of s for every confidence level."],"a":0,"why":"The normal critical multiplier scales the estimated standard error.","qHtml":"What is the margin of error in the large-sample mean interval?","oHtml":["<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^*s/\\sqrt n</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>s</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^*s\\sqrt n</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Only x bar.","95% of s for every confidence level."],"whyHtml":"The normal critical multiplier scales the estimated standard error."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-confidence-coverage","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Statistical inference","title":"Repeated-sampling confidence interpretation","outcomes":["4.3.6"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Confidence is a property of the procedure · Distinguish common misinterpretations · Simulate coverage transparently · Coverage depends on assumptions","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Repeated-sampling confidence interpretation</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Confidence is a property of the procedure</li><li>Distinguish common misinterpretations</li><li>Simulate coverage transparently</li><li>Coverage depends on assumptions</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Confidence is a property of the procedure","text":"Imagine repeatedly drawing samples and applying the same interval rule. A 95% rule captures the fixed population mean in approximately 95% of those repetitions when assumptions hold. The interval endpoints vary; mu does not. An observed interval either covers mu or misses it, although we usually do not know which.","diagram":null,"html":"Imagine repeatedly drawing samples and applying the same interval rule. A 95% rule captures the fixed population mean in approximately 95% of those repetitions when assumptions hold. The interval endpoints vary; mu does not. An observed interval either covers mu or misses it, although we usually do not know which."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish common misinterpretations","text":"A 95% confidence interval does not assert that 95% of the population lies inside, nor that 95% of sample observations lie inside. It is not a claim that 95% of all possible mu values are equally plausible under this frequentist model. Describe confidence through the repeated-sampling capture rate and the specified parameter.","diagram":"coverage","html":"A 95% confidence interval does not assert that 95% of the population lies inside, nor that 95% of sample observations lie inside. It is not a claim that 95% of all possible mu values are equally plausible under this frequentist model. Describe confidence through the repeated-sampling capture rate and the specified parameter."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Simulate coverage transparently","text":"Choose a known population, draw many samples, calculate each x bar and s, build the interval and record whether its endpoints contain the known mu. Plot intervals with a vertical reference line at mu, distinguishing misses. The simulated proportion fluctuates around the nominal rate rather than equalling it exactly in every run.","diagram":null,"html":"Choose a known population, draw many samples, calculate each x bar and s, build the interval and record whether its endpoints contain the known mu. Plot intervals with a vertical reference line at mu, distinguishing misses. The simulated proportion fluctuates around the nominal rate rather than equalling it exactly in every run."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Coverage depends on assumptions","text":"A short simulation can fluctuate noticeably, and a poorly justified normal approximation can give a capture rate different from the nominal one. Bias or dependence can also undermine coverage. More simulation repetitions improve the estimate of the procedure's rate but do not change the sample size within each interval or fix an invalid design.","diagram":null,"html":"A short simulation can fluctuate noticeably, and a poorly justified normal approximation can give a capture rate different from the nominal one. Bias or dependence can also undermine coverage. More simulation repetitions improve the estimate of the procedure&#39;s rate but do not change the sample size within each interval or fix an invalid design."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"interactive","title":"Explore repeated samples and interval capture","model":"sampling"},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-confidence-coverage-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"In 1000 simulations, 947 nominal 95% intervals contain the known mu. Interpret the result. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Observed coverage is 947/1000=0.947.","[1] This is close to nominal 0.95.","[1] Simulation variation explains why the count need not be exactly 950.","[1] The result supports this approximate coverage under the model, not universal validity for every population."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"In 1000 simulations, 947 nominal 95% intervals contain the known mu. Interpret the result. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Observed coverage is 947/1000=0.947.","[1] This is close to nominal 0.95.","[1] Simulation variation explains why the count need not be exactly 950.","[1] The result supports this approximate coverage under the model, not universal validity for every population."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-coverage-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Correct: there is a 95% probability that the fixed mu is inside our already calculated interval. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] In the frequentist model mu is fixed.","[1] Randomness generated the endpoints through the sample.","[1] The realised interval either contains mu or does not.","[1] State instead that the procedure captures mu in about 95% of repeated eligible samples."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Correct: there is a 95% probability that the fixed mu is inside our already calculated interval. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] In the frequentist model mu is fixed.","[1] Randomness generated the endpoints through the sample.","[1] The realised interval either contains mu or does not.","[1] State instead that the procedure captures mu in about 95% of repeated eligible samples."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-coverage-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"A 95% interval for mean height is [169,171] cm. Does it predict that 95% of people are this tall? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] No: the target parameter is mean height.","[1] Individual heights have a different spread.","[1] The mean interval uses standard error s/square root n.","[1] An individual-value interval uses the individual SD s and will usually be much wider."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"A 95% interval for mean height is [169,171] cm. Does it predict that 95% of people are this tall? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] No: the target parameter is mean height.","[1] Individual heights have a different spread.","[1] The mean interval uses standard error s/square root n.","[1] An individual-value interval uses the individual SD s and will usually be much wider."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-coverage-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Outline an honest coverage simulation for n=50 from a normal population with known mean. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Draw 50 independent observations per repetition.","[1] Calculate x bar,s and the selected interval.","[1] Test whether the known mu lies between its endpoints.","[1] Repeat many times and report the coverage fraction and simulation conditions."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Outline an honest coverage simulation for n=50 from a normal population with known mean. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Draw 50 independent observations per repetition.","[1] Calculate x bar,s and the selected interval.","[1] Test whether the known mu lies between its endpoints.","[1] Repeat many times and report the coverage fraction and simulation conditions."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-coverage-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"If only 20 intervals are simulated, must exactly 19 cover mu for a 95% procedure? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] No, the capture count is random.","[1] Its expected count is approximately 20(0.95)=19.","[1] Actual counts can differ.","[1] More repetitions make the observed fraction more stable, without guaranteeing an exact nominal fraction."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"If only 20 intervals are simulated, must exactly 19 cover mu for a 95% procedure? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] No, the capture count is random.","[1] Its expected count is approximately 20(0.95)=19.","[1] Actual counts can differ.","[1] More repetitions make the observed fraction more stable, without guaranteeing an exact nominal fraction."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-coverage-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"Why may nominal 95% coverage fail for strongly dependent observations treated as independent? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The formula assumes the variance of the mean is sigma squared/n.","[1] Dependence adds covariance terms.","[1] The calculated SE may be too small or otherwise incorrect.","[1] The advertised capture rate is therefore not justified by the nominal percentage alone."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Why may nominal 95% coverage fail for strongly dependent observations treated as independent? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The formula assumes the variance of the mean is sigma squared/n.","[1] Dependence adds covariance terms.","[1] The calculated SE may be too small or otherwise incorrect.","[1] The advertised capture rate is therefore not justified by the nominal percentage alone."]}],"bank":[{"q":"In repeated confidence-interval sampling, what varies?","o":["The sample statistics and interval endpoints.","The fixed population mean changes after every sample.","Only the confidence percentage changes.","Nothing changes."],"a":0,"why":"Random samples generate different intervals around one fixed parameter.","qHtml":"In repeated confidence-interval sampling, what varies?","oHtml":["The sample statistics and interval endpoints.","The fixed population mean changes after every sample.","Only the confidence percentage changes.","Nothing changes."],"whyHtml":"Random samples generate different intervals around one fixed parameter."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-confidence-design","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Statistical inference","title":"Margin of error, sample size and sampling limits","outcomes":["4.3.5"],"icon":"📐","scope":"Plan a desired margin · Understand square-root scaling · Account for design limits · Communicate feasibility and precision","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Margin of error, sample size and sampling limits</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>Plan a desired margin</li><li>Understand square-root scaling</li><li>Account for design limits</li><li>Communicate feasibility and precision</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Plan a desired margin","text":"For estimated individual SD s and critical value z star, margin d is approximately z star s/square root n. Solving gives $n\\ge(z^*s/d)^2$. Round the required sample size upward to a whole number, then check the margin. Rounding down can fail the stated target.","diagram":null,"html":"For estimated individual SD s and critical value z star, margin d is approximately z star s/square root n. Solving gives <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge(z^*s/d)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Round the required sample size upward to a whole number, then check the margin. Rounding down can fail the stated target."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Understand square-root scaling","text":"Halving margin requires four times the sample size when confidence level and spread stay fixed. Increasing confidence increases the critical value and therefore the required n. A sample-size calculation relies on a reasonable prior estimate of spread; if that estimate is too low, the realised interval can be wider than planned.","diagram":"ci-formula","html":"Halving margin requires four times the sample size when confidence level and spread stay fixed. Increasing confidence increases the critical value and therefore the required n. A sample-size calculation relies on a reasonable prior estimate of spread; if that estimate is too low, the realised interval can be wider than planned."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Account for design limits","text":"Random representative recruitment, independence and valid measurements are essential. Nonresponse and voluntary participation can create bias. If recruiting people who may not respond, the required completed sample size differs from the initial invitations. The formula is not a substitute for a sound sampling plan.","diagram":null,"html":"Random representative recruitment, independence and valid measurements are essential. Nonresponse and voluntary participation can create bias. If recruiting people who may not respond, the required completed sample size differs from the initial invitations. The formula is not a substitute for a sound sampling plan."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Communicate feasibility and precision","text":"State confidence level, planning SD, desired margin and required completed observations. Compare practical cost against improvement from larger n. Avoid describing a narrow interval as accurate if systematic error is unaddressed. A preliminary pilot can inform s, but its uncertainty and representativeness should be acknowledged.","diagram":null,"html":"State confidence level, planning SD, desired margin and required completed observations. Compare practical cost against improvement from larger n. Avoid describing a narrow interval as accurate if systematic error is unaddressed. A preliminary pilot can inform s, but its uncertainty and representativeness should be acknowledged."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Extended exam task","q":6},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-confidence-design-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"Plan a 95% interval with margin at most 2 and estimated s=10. Find minimum n. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Use z=1.96 and n at least (1.96(10)/2) squared.","[1] This is 96.04.","[1] Round upward to n=97.","[1] At 97, margin is approximately 1.990, meeting the target."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Plan a 95% interval with margin at most 2 and estimated s=10. Find minimum n. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Use z=1.96 and n at least (1.96(10)/2) squared.","[1] This is 96.04.","[1] Round upward to n=97.","[1] At 97, margin is approximately 1.990, meeting the target."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-design-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Current margin is 4 at n=100. What n gives margin 1 with unchanged confidence and spread? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Desired margin is one-quarter of the current margin.","[1] Since margin is proportional to n inverse square root, n must multiply by 16.","[1] New n=1600.","[1] Check: 4 square root(100/1600)=1."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Current margin is 4 at n=100. What n gives margin 1 with unchanged confidence and spread? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Desired margin is one-quarter of the current margin.","[1] Since margin is proportional to n inverse square root, n must multiply by 16.","[1] New n=1600.","[1] Check: 4 square root(100/1600)=1."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-design-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"For 90% confidence, s=12 and target d=1.5, use z=1.645 to find n. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] n at least (1.645(12)/1.5) squared.","[1] The ratio is 13.16 and square is 173.1856.","[1] Round upward to 174.","[1] This assumes completed independent representative observations and an adequate mean approximation."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"For 90% confidence, s=12 and target d=1.5, use z=1.645 to find n. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] n at least (1.645(12)/1.5) squared.","[1] The ratio is 13.16 and square is 173.1856.","[1] Round upward to 174.","[1] This assumes completed independent representative observations and an adequate mean approximation."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-design-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"If 200 completed responses are needed and expected response rate is 80%, estimate invitations. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Let N be invitations; expected completions are 0.8N.","[1] Require expected 0.8N at least 200.","[1] N at least 250.","[1] This is an expectation-based planning estimate, not a guarantee of exactly 200 responses or absence of nonresponse bias."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"If 200 completed responses are needed and expected response rate is 80%, estimate invitations. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Let N be invitations; expected completions are 0.8N.","[1] Require expected 0.8N at least 200.","[1] N at least 250.","[1] This is an expectation-based planning estimate, not a guarantee of exactly 200 responses or absence of nonresponse bias."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-design-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"How does doubling s affect required n at fixed margin and confidence? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Required n is proportional to s squared.","[1] Replacing s by 2s multiplies the squared expression by 4.","[1] Required n quadruples.","[1] Uncertainty in planning spread can therefore materially affect recruitment needs."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"How does doubling s affect required n at fixed margin and confidence? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Required n is proportional to s squared.","[1] Replacing s by 2s multiplies the squared expression by 4.","[1] Required n quadruples.","[1] Uncertainty in planning spread can therefore materially affect recruitment needs."]},{"id":"mas12-confidence-design-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A planner calculates n=45.01 and chooses 45. Explain the error. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The inequality requires n at least 45.01.","[1] n must be a whole number.","[1] 45 is below the threshold and fails the theoretical margin target.","[1] Choose 46 and verify the resulting margin."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"A planner calculates n=45.01 and chooses 45. Explain the error. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The inequality requires n at least 45.01.","[1] n must be a whole number.","[1] 45 is below the threshold and fails the theoretical margin target.","[1] Choose 46 and verify the resulting margin."]},{"prompt":"A random independent sample of 144 measurements has mean 72 and s=12. Calculate a 95% mean confidence interval and an approximate central 95% individual-value interval under a normal-population assumption. Plan the minimum completed sample size for a 99% mean interval with margin at most 1, using s=12 and z=2.576. Explain one limitation of the plan. (8 marks)","steps":["[1] SE=12/12=1.","[2] Mean interval is 72±1.96, or [70.04,73.96].","[2] Individual interval is 72±1.96(12), or [48.48,95.52], targeting individual spread rather than mu.","[2] Required n is at least $(2.576\\times12)^2=955.551744$, so choose 956.","[1] The planning SD is an estimate and actual spread may differ; representative independent sampling and suitable approximations still matter."],"id":"mas12-confidence-design-extended","title":"Extended exam task","allocations":[1,2,2,2,1],"marks":8,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed or exact working","promptHtml":"A random independent sample of 144 measurements has mean 72 and s=12. Calculate a 95% mean confidence interval and an approximate central 95% individual-value interval under a normal-population assumption. Plan the minimum completed sample size for a 99% mean interval with margin at most 1, using s=12 and z=2.576. Explain one limitation of the plan. (8 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] SE=12/12=1.","[2] Mean interval is 72±1.96, or [70.04,73.96].","[2] Individual interval is 72±1.96(12), or [48.48,95.52], targeting individual spread rather than mu.","[2] Required n is at least <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2.576</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>12</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>955.551744</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2.576\\times12)^2=955.551744</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so choose 956.","[1] The planning SD is an estimate and actual spread may differ; representative independent sampling and suitable approximations still matter."]}],"bank":[{"q":"How should a noninteger minimum sample size be rounded?","o":["Upward.","Downward to save observations.","To the nearest integer regardless of the inequality.","Always to 100."],"a":0,"why":"The completed sample size must meet or exceed the calculated lower bound.","qHtml":"How should a noninteger minimum sample size be rounded?","oHtml":["Upward.","Downward to save observations.","To the nearest integer regardless of the inequality.","Always to 100."],"whyHtml":"The completed sample size must meet or exceed the calculated lower bound."}],"subtitle":"16 screens · 2 worked examples · 5 independent tasks"},{"id":"mas12-population-intervals","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Calculus, differential equations and inference","area":"Statistical inference","title":"Intervals for individual values versus the mean","outcomes":["4.3.7"],"icon":"📐","scope":"An interval for individual values has a different target · Compare with a mean confidence interval · Use normality and context · Interpret approximate coverage","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Learning goals and prerequisites","html":"<h3>Intervals for individual values versus the mean</h3><p>Work through the explanations and models, then attempt the questions before revealing their solutions. Show equations, reasoning, domains and units where relevant. Draw requested diagrams on paper.</p><p>Specialist builds on Mathematics Methods; revise the relevant algebra and calculus when needed.</p><ul><li>An interval for individual values has a different target</li><li>Compare with a mean confidence interval</li><li>Use normality and context</li><li>Interpret approximate coverage</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"An interval for individual values has a different target","text":"For a normal population, the central proportion C lies approximately between mu+-z star sigma. Estimate this using x bar+-z star s when population parameters are unknown. It describes the spread of individual values, rather than the uncertainty in estimating mu. Parameter estimation adds uncertainty, so this plug-in interval is approximate.","diagram":null,"html":"For a normal population, the central proportion C lies approximately between mu+-z star sigma. Estimate this using x bar+-z star s when population parameters are unknown. It describes the spread of individual values, rather than the uncertainty in estimating mu. Parameter estimation adds uncertainty, so this plug-in interval is approximate."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare with a mean confidence interval","text":"A large-sample confidence interval for mu uses x bar+-z star s/square root n. Its width shrinks with n, while the individual-value spread remains approximately z star s. The same confidence-style percentage and critical value do not make the two targets equivalent. State whether the question concerns individuals or the population mean.","diagram":"individual","html":"A large-sample confidence interval for mu uses x bar+-z star s/square root n. Its width shrinks with n, while the individual-value spread remains approximately z star s. The same confidence-style percentage and critical value do not make the two targets equivalent. State whether the question concerns individuals or the population mean."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use normality and context","text":"A central individual interval relies on an approximately normal population. With skewed data, symmetric normal limits can have wrong coverage or impossible values. A large-sample CLT can justify a mean interval without making the individual population normal. Distinguish that assumption explicitly.","diagram":null,"html":"A central individual interval relies on an approximately normal population. With skewed data, symmetric normal limits can have wrong coverage or impossible values. A large-sample CLT can justify a mean interval without making the individual population normal. Distinguish that assumption explicitly."},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret approximate coverage","text":"An estimated interval x bar+-1.96s aims to cover roughly 95% of normal individual values if the estimated parameters are close. It is not a formal exact prediction or tolerance interval with an additional guarantee about estimation uncertainty. Explain the intended course-level approximation, units and any impossible physical endpoint.","diagram":null,"html":"An estimated interval x bar+-1.96s aims to cover roughly 95% of normal individual values if the estimated parameters are close. It is not a formal exact prediction or tolerance interval with an additional guarantee about estimation uncertainty. Explain the intended course-level approximation, units and any impossible physical endpoint."},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 1","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Worked example: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"solution","title":"Step-by-step solution: Worked example 2","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 1","q":2},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 2","q":3},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 3","q":4},{"kind":"question","title":"Practice: Independent practice 4","q":5},{"kind":"check","title":"Check the key idea","q":0},{"kind":"sources","title":"SCSA sources and further practice","html":"<p>These are original ATAR-style questions and suggested allocations. Use official papers and keys for additional timed practice. Full reasoning remains important in calculator-assumed work.</p><p>Checked 8 October 2026 against Year 11 teaching from 2026 and Year 12 teaching from 2025, and compared with the published Year 12 syllabus for 2027.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/mathematics/mathematics-specialist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA Mathematics Specialist syllabus, formula sheets, examination papers and marking keys</a></p><p>2026 section structure: calculator-free 50 minutes, 48 raw marks and 35%; calculator-assumed 100 minutes, 86 raw marks and 65%. Reading time is 5 and 10 minutes respectively. Raw marks differ from section weights. Lesson marks are original practice allocations.</p>"}],"questions":[{"id":"mas12-population-intervals-q1","title":"Worked example 1","prompt":"With n=100,x bar=50,s=10, compare the approximate central 95% individual interval and the 95% mean confidence interval. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Individual interval is 50+-1.96(10).","[1] It is [30.4,69.6].","[1] Mean confidence interval uses SE=1, giving [48.04,51.96].","[1] They target individual spread and uncertainty in mu respectively."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"With n=100,x bar=50,s=10, compare the approximate central 95% individual interval and the 95% mean confidence interval. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Individual interval is 50+-1.96(10).","[1] It is [30.4,69.6].","[1] Mean confidence interval uses SE=1, giving [48.04,51.96].","[1] They target individual spread and uncertainty in mu respectively."]},{"id":"mas12-population-intervals-q2","title":"Worked example 2","prompt":"Normal measurements have estimated mean 120 and s=8. Find an approximate central 90% individual interval using z=1.645. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Margin for individuals is 1.645(8)=13.16.","[1] Endpoints are 120+-13.16.","[1] Interval is [106.84,133.16].","[1] This is a plug-in normal-population value interval, not a confidence interval for mu."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Normal measurements have estimated mean 120 and s=8. Find an approximate central 90% individual interval using z=1.645. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Margin for individuals is 1.645(8)=13.16.","[1] Endpoints are 120+-13.16.","[1] Interval is [106.84,133.16].","[1] This is a plug-in normal-population value interval, not a confidence interval for mu."]},{"id":"mas12-population-intervals-q3","title":"Independent practice 1","prompt":"Why does quadrupling n halve a mean interval margin but not the estimated individual spread margin when s is unchanged? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Mean margin contains s/square root n.","[1] Quadrupling n doubles the denominator.","[1] Individual margin contains s without n.","[1] More observations improve mean precision rather than making the population inherently less variable."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Why does quadrupling n halve a mean interval margin but not the estimated individual spread margin when s is unchanged? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Mean margin contains s/square root n.","[1] Quadrupling n doubles the denominator.","[1] Individual margin contains s without n.","[1] More observations improve mean precision rather than making the population inherently less variable."]},{"id":"mas12-population-intervals-q4","title":"Independent practice 2","prompt":"Population values are highly skewed, but n=1000 gives a useful mean approximation. Can x bar+-1.96s automatically cover 95% of individuals? (4 marks)","steps":["[1] No: the CLT concerns sample means.","[1] It does not transform the individual population into a normal distribution.","[1] Individual coverage from symmetric normal limits can be poor.","[1] A justified distributional method for individual values is needed."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"Population values are highly skewed, but n=1000 gives a useful mean approximation. Can x bar+-1.96s automatically cover 95% of individuals? (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] No: the CLT concerns sample means.","[1] It does not transform the individual population into a normal distribution.","[1] Individual coverage from symmetric normal limits can be poor.","[1] A justified distributional method for individual values is needed."]},{"id":"mas12-population-intervals-q5","title":"Independent practice 3","prompt":"For a normal population mu=10,sigma=2, find the exact-model central 95% interval using z=1.96. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] Individual interval is mu+-z sigma.","[1] Margin is 3.92.","[1] Interval is [6.08,13.92].","[1] Approximately 95% of individual values lie within it under the stated normal model; the small approximation is the rounded z value."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"For a normal population mu=10,sigma=2, find the exact-model central 95% interval using z=1.96. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] Individual interval is mu+-z sigma.","[1] Margin is 3.92.","[1] Interval is [6.08,13.92].","[1] Approximately 95% of individual values lie within it under the stated normal model; the small approximation is the rounded z value."]},{"id":"mas12-population-intervals-q6","title":"Independent practice 4","prompt":"A report calls [98,102] a range for 95% of people because it is a 95% confidence interval for the mean. Correct its wording. (4 marks)","steps":["[1] The interval estimates the population mean, not the range of individual people.","[1] Its procedure has about 95% repeated-sampling capture of mu under assumptions.","[1] Individual spread requires a separate calculation using the individual SD.","[1] Report the target parameter clearly rather than attaching the percentage to individuals."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"mode":"calculator-assumed","promptHtml":"A report calls [98,102] a range for 95% of people because it is a 95% confidence interval for the mean. Correct its wording. (4 marks)","stepsHtml":["[1] The interval estimates the population mean, not the range of individual people.","[1] Its procedure has about 95% repeated-sampling capture of mu under assumptions.","[1] Individual spread requires a separate calculation using the individual SD.","[1] Report the target parameter clearly rather than attaching the percentage to individuals."]}],"bank":[{"q":"Which spread is used for an approximate normal individual-value interval?","o":["The individual standard deviation s.","s divided by square root n.","The sample mean alone.","Zero once n is large."],"a":0,"why":"Individual variation is not reduced by averaging because the target is one population value.","qHtml":"Which spread is used for an approximate normal individual-value interval?","oHtml":["The individual standard deviation s.","s divided by square root n.","The sample mean alone.","Zero once n is large."],"whyHtml":"Individual variation is not reduced by averaging because the target is one population value."}],"subtitle":"15 screens · 2 worked examples · 4 independent tasks"}];
